// Built by bin/docs-flows from the charts that docs pages place. Do not edit.
window.orbitFlows = {
 "charts": {
  "ai-router/task-guard": {
   "kind": "ai_router.task_guard",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: Nick 2026-11-07, one fixed model key for all task VMs, usable only inside the task VM network; the guard pattern of /reference/tasks#credentials-in-task-vms.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: ai-router:task-guard · also each release when the guard's config changed, and `ai-router:task-guard` on the Gateway\n  %% kind: ai_router.task_guard\n  autonumber\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Router Node<br/>services, over ssh\n  Note over GW: Admit the guard<br/>the router's Node\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - renders the guard's Caddyfile and its UFW rules from its sources and the router's port: the task VM WireGuard range and the WireGuard address of each Node whose last forwarder run left a forwarder\n  %%> - pushes vault copies of the task key and the anchor key; the intent holds neither\n  %%> - `--remove` records the guard's removal instead: the same run takes it off the Node, with each forwarder Node's allow\n  %%> - takes the lock `ai_router_guard:<router Node>`, which forwarder runs share\n  %%> - records the digest of what it applies, without a secret: the guard's Caddyfile, its UFW rules, its state and the bytes of its playbooks\n  %%> - each release's `ai_router_guard` phase admits this run by itself, as `release:<commit>`, when the guard is in place and its digest differs from the one the last guard or forwarder run applied\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the ai-router extension is set up on its Node\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-ai-router-task-guard\n  SW->>N: Apply the guard<br/>Ansible\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - writes the two keys as root-only files and the Caddyfile into /etc/orbit/ai-router-guard, validated by `caddy validate`\n  %%> - runs its own Caddy, `orbit-ai-router-guard.service`, on 10.44.0.3:8318: a dynamic user that loads the keys with `LoadCredential`, no admin endpoint, no saved config\n  %%> - passes a POST with the task key to an inference route, with no query or only `beta=true` on the two messages routes, for Claude Code; answers 401 to any other request from its sources and 403 to every other source\n  %%> - places the UFW rules above the WireGuard member trust, re-placing a pair out of order: allow 8318 from the task VM range and from each forwarder Node, deny it from every other member; each forwarder Node's allow moves with the pair, and the allow of a Node the rules do not name goes\n  %%> - from the Node itself, outside the range: the task key gets 403\n  SW->>N: Prove the refusal<br/>from Node 209\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the swamp Node is a WireGuard member outside the task VM range: the Node answers on SSH, and port 8318 does not\n  alt the checks pass\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded\n    Note over GW: Record the origin<br/>http://10.44.0.3:8318\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Pi on every task VM calls this origin with the one task key; the guard swaps in the anchor key\n  else a step failed\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step\n  end\n"
  },
  "analytics/update": {
   "kind": "analytics.update",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, rule 5 (merge into node.role_converge: a version pin is a role setting), DECISIONS R1-R4. docs/reference/analytics.md.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rule 22 (env file via cfgmgmt file, docker run argv, cfgmgmt docker_container, curl check).\n  %% Decided: Nick 2026-11-02, a Docker Process runs as one plain docker run through @swamp/ssh exec; its check is the docker_container check.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: analytics:update\n  %% kind: analytics.update\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Node 21<br/>analytics role\n  CLI->>GW: analytics:update\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit analytics:update`\n  %%> - syntax: `[version] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Plausible Community Edition version as three numbers; the image tag is `v` plus the version\n  %%> - the optional version argument supplies the version directly, for example `orbit analytics:update 3.2.1`\n  %%> - `--json` returns machine-readable output\n  %%> - in a terminal the CLI asks for a missing version; with `--json` it refuses (`analytics.version_invalid`)\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - changes the `analytics` role's version setting and converges the role\n  Note over GW: Validate the version<br/>role setting · role lock\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Gateway Node\n  %%> - a Node has the active `analytics` role (`analytics.role_missing`)\n  %%> - no other change runs for that role; the Gateway holds one lock\n  %%> Decides:\n  %%> - whether the version differs from the one that runs\n  alt same version\n    Note over GW: Nothing to change<br/>R1: no run\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - nothing changes; no Swamp run starts (R1)\n    GW-->>CLI: Version unchanged<br/>3.2.1 on Node 21\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the final result at once\n  else another version\n    GW->>SW: Start role converge<br/>operation-node-role · analytics\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - records task #5128 as `node.role_converge` for Node 21's `analytics` role, with the new version as its setting\n    %%> - pushes the Plausible environment to the Swamp vault after commit (R3) and starts one run on serve (Node 209)\n    %%> Inputs:\n    %%> - the new and the current `plausible` Process definitions, and the vault key name of the environment\n    %%> - Node 21's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n    %%> Why no own kind:\n    %%> - a version pin is a role setting; the role converge already replaces the Process (rule 5)\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5128\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the task number, at once\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n    SW->>N: Prepare the database<br/>docker run --rm · --env-file\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - over SSH from 209 with `@swamp/ssh`; the environment comes from `vault.get` into a root-only 0600 env file written with `@swamp/cfgmgmt/file`\n    %%> - one `docker run --rm --env-file` argv each for `createdb` and `migrate`; nothing is copied to the Node\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - runs the new image's `createdb` and `migrate` as one-off containers; the running Process keeps serving\n    %%> If it fails:\n    %%> - the old container keeps running unchanged\n    SW->>N: Replace the Process<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · docker run\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - one `docker rm -f plausible` argv, then one plain `docker run -d --name plausible --env-file … <image> <cmd>` argv from the new image, with the same environment and mounts\n    %%> - the same `docker run` as every Docker Process (`process:create`)\n    %%> Why it is safe:\n    %%> - Plausible keeps no data in its container\n    SW->>N: Check Plausible is healthy<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · curl -fsS\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the `docker_container` check: the container runs the new image\n    %%> - one bounded wait (`timeout 60`): `curl` retries Plausible's health URL until the new container answers\n    alt the check passes\n      N-->>SW: Plausible ready\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - Plausible runs the new version\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n      %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n      Note over GW: Record the version<br/>3.2.1 · task succeeded\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the role's version becomes 3.2.1 and the Process definition is updated\n      %%> - task #5128 succeeds\n    else replacing or the check fails\n      N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or no answer\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - the new container does not start or Plausible does not answer\n      SW->>N: Restore the old Process<br/>previous image and spec\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - removes the failed container and creates it again from the captured previous definition, then checks it answers\n      %%> Not reversed:\n      %%> - the database migrations\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      %%> The Gateway reads:\n      %%> - which step failed, its error code, and whether the restore passed\n      Note over GW: Keep the old version<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the role keeps its previous version; task #5128 fails\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show #5128\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "cluster/activate": {
   "kind": "cluster.activate",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run with the shared route reconcile child (route.reconcile). docs/reference/routes.md#change-a-tld-cluster-state-or-cluster-membership.\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: cluster:activate\n  %% kind: cluster.activate\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane V icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Cluster Nodes<br/>Router · members\n  participant V as VPN Node<br/>private DNS\n  CLI->>GW: cluster:activate<br/>CLUSTER\n  Note over GW: Validate, lock, plan<br/>transitions stored\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to a member Node, or to the Gateway Node when the Cluster has none\n  %%> - a Cluster with a TLD has one active Router (`cluster.router_required`) and no non-member Node owns the TLD (`cluster.tld_conflict`); a Cluster without a TLD that has affected Routes needs a Router too (`route.router_required`)\n  %%> - every affected Route keeps a valid domain, scope and target; one refusal leaves every Route in place; an active public Route that the change would move is refused (`route.reconciliation_required`)\n  %%> - no other Cluster change runs; the Gateway waits up to 30 seconds, then refuses with `cluster.router_busy`\n  %%> Stored:\n  %%> - the Cluster stays inactive; a pending replacement is stored for each domain change and the old placement for each scope change\n  alt Cluster is inactive, or an earlier change is unfinished\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-cluster-activate\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    SW->>N: Open LAN ingress<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall\n    %%> Why first:\n    %%> - LAN members must reach the Router before private DNS sends them there; identical lines stay\n    opt Routes change\n      SW->>N: Reconcile the Routes<br/>child route-reconcile\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - moves every affected Route to the Cluster scope and namespace; the cutover is inside the child\n    end\n    SW->>V: Select the Router in DNS<br/>records + catalog, then reload\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - private DNS answers Cluster names with the Router's LAN address for LAN members and its WireGuard address for the rest\n    SW->>N: Prune unused rules<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall absent\n    %%> Why it always runs:\n    %%> - after a failure before the cutover it removes the LAN rules and DNS selection the stored state does not need\n    alt every step succeeds\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      Note over GW: Record the Cluster active<br/>Routes cut over\n    else a step fails\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      Note over GW: Read the failed step<br/>cutover or not\n      %%> Result before the cutover:\n      %%> - the Cluster stays inactive and every Route keeps its domain and scope\n      %%> Result after the cutover:\n      %%> - the Cluster is active; a rerun finishes the withdrawal and the prune\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same command again; every step runs again and skips what already matches\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    end\n  else Cluster is active, nothing unfinished\n    GW-->>CLI: 200 OK<br/>already active\n    %%> Skipped:\n    %%> - nothing runs and the result reports `Skipped — already active (cluster.state_unchanged)`\n  end\n"
  },
  "cluster/deactivate": {
   "kind": "cluster.deactivate",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run with the shared route reconcile child (route.reconcile). docs/reference/routes.md#change-a-tld-cluster-state-or-cluster-membership.\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: cluster:deactivate\n  %% kind: cluster.deactivate\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane V icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Cluster Nodes<br/>Router · members\n  participant V as VPN Node<br/>private DNS\n  CLI->>GW: cluster:deactivate<br/>CLUSTER\n  %%> Consent:\n  %%> - the CLI resolves the Cluster, then asks to confirm with its name and the effect; `--force` skips the prompt (`cluster.confirmation_required` without it)\n  Note over GW: Validate, lock, plan<br/>transitions stored\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to a member Node, or to the Gateway Node when the Cluster has none\n  %%> - every affected Route keeps a valid domain, scope and target, and a generated Route still has an effective TLD from its Node; one refusal leaves every Route in place; an active public Route that the change would move is refused (`route.reconciliation_required`)\n  %%> - no other Cluster change runs; the Gateway waits up to 30 seconds, then refuses with `cluster.router_busy`\n  %%> Stored:\n  %%> - the Cluster stays active; a pending replacement is stored for each domain change and the old placement for each scope change\n  alt Cluster is active, or an earlier change is unfinished\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-cluster-deactivate\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    opt Routes change\n      SW->>N: Reconcile the Routes<br/>child route-reconcile\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - returns every affected Route to its Node scope, with generated domains on the Node TLDs; the cutover is inside the child\n    end\n    SW->>V: Drop the Router from DNS<br/>records + catalog, then reload\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - private DNS stops answering Cluster names with the Router\n    SW->>N: Prune unused rules<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall absent\n    %%> Why last:\n    %%> - members keep their LAN path to the Router until the Routes and DNS have moved\n    %%> Why it always runs:\n    %%> - a rerun after a failure past the cutover prunes from the stored state; before the cutover the Cluster is still active and nothing is pruned\n    alt every step succeeds\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      Note over GW: Record Cluster inactive<br/>Routes cut over\n    else a step fails\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      Note over GW: Read the failed step<br/>cutover or not\n      %%> Result before the cutover:\n      %%> - the Cluster stays active and every Route keeps its domain and scope\n      %%> Result after the cutover:\n      %%> - the Cluster is inactive; a rerun finishes the withdrawal and the prune\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same command again; every step runs again and skips what already matches\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    end\n  else Cluster is inactive, nothing unfinished\n    GW-->>CLI: 200 OK<br/>already inactive\n    %%> Skipped:\n    %%> - nothing runs and the result reports `Skipped — already inactive (cluster.state_unchanged)`\n  end\n"
  },
  "cluster/node-add": {
   "kind": "cluster.node_add",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run with the shared route reconcile child (route.reconcile). docs/reference/routes.md#change-a-tld-cluster-state-or-cluster-membership.\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: cluster:node:add\n  %% kind: cluster.node_add\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane V icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Cluster Nodes<br/>Router · members\n  participant V as VPN Node<br/>private DNS\n  CLI->>GW: cluster:node:add<br/>CLUSTER NODE\n  Note over GW: Validate, lock, plan<br/>transitions stored\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the Node being attached\n  %%> - the Node is active (`cluster.node_inactive`), belongs to no other Cluster (`cluster.membership_conflict`), and its LAN address is free in the Cluster (`cluster.lan_ip_conflict`); a Route that would take Cluster scope needs an active Router (`route.router_required`)\n  %%> - every affected Route keeps a valid domain, scope and target; one refusal leaves every Route in place; an active public Route that the change would move is refused (`route.reconciliation_required`)\n  %%> - no other Cluster change runs; the Gateway waits up to 30 seconds, then refuses with `cluster.router_busy`\n  %%> Stored:\n  %%> - the Node stays outside the Cluster with a pending membership; a pending replacement is stored for each domain change and the old placement for each scope change\n  alt Cluster is active, or the Node is already a member\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-cluster-node-add\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    SW->>N: Open LAN ingress<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall\n    %%> Why first:\n    %%> - the Router opens HTTPS and DNS to the member's LAN address before private DNS sends the member there; identical lines stay\n    %%> Without a LAN address:\n    %%> - the member gets no LAN rule and is reached over WireGuard\n    opt Routes change\n      SW->>N: Reconcile the Routes<br/>child route-reconcile\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - moves the Node's Routes to the Cluster scope, with generated domains in the Cluster TLD when it has one; custom proxy Routes keep their Node scope; the cutover is inside the child\n    end\n    SW->>V: Select the Router in DNS<br/>records + catalog, then reload\n    SW->>N: Prune unused rules<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall absent\n    %%> Why it always runs:\n    %%> - after a failure before the cutover it removes the LAN rules and DNS selection the stored state does not need\n    alt every step succeeds\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      Note over GW: Record the membership<br/>Routes cut over\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - at the cutover when Routes change, and when the run succeeds when none do\n    else a step fails\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      Note over GW: Read the failed step<br/>cutover or not\n      %%> Result before the cutover:\n      %%> - the Node stays outside the Cluster and every Route keeps its domain and scope\n      %%> Result after the cutover:\n      %%> - the Node is a member; a rerun finishes the withdrawal, the DNS selection and the prune\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same command again; every step runs again and skips what already matches\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    end\n  else Cluster is inactive and the Node is not a member\n    GW-->>CLI: 200 OK<br/>Node attached\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the membership is recorded at once; no Node is contacted and no traffic changes\n  end\n"
  },
  "cluster/node-remove": {
   "kind": "cluster.node_remove",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run with the shared route reconcile child (route.reconcile). docs/reference/routes.md#change-a-tld-cluster-state-or-cluster-membership.\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: cluster:node:remove\n  %% kind: cluster.node_remove\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane V icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Cluster Nodes<br/>Router · members\n  participant V as VPN Node<br/>private DNS\n  CLI->>GW: cluster:node:remove<br/>CLUSTER NODE\n  %%> Consent:\n  %%> - the CLI resolves the Cluster and the Node, then asks to confirm with their names and the effect; `--force` skips the prompt (`cluster.confirmation_required` without it)\n  Note over GW: Validate, lock, plan<br/>transitions stored\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the Node being detached\n  %%> - the Node is a member of this Cluster, or its detach is unfinished (`cluster.membership_missing`), has no `router` assignment that is active, being removed or failed (`cluster.router_detach_forbidden`), has no `ingress` role (`cluster.ingress_detach_forbidden`), and does not share the active Cluster TLD (`cluster.tld_conflict`)\n  %%> - every affected Route keeps a valid domain, scope and target; one refusal leaves every Route in place; an active public Route that the change would move is refused (`route.reconciliation_required`)\n  %%> - no other Cluster change runs; the Gateway waits up to 30 seconds, then refuses with `cluster.router_busy`\n  %%> Stored:\n  %%> - the Node stays a member with a pending detach, which lasts until a run succeeds; a pending replacement is stored for each domain change and the old placement for each scope change\n  alt Cluster is active, or an earlier detach is unfinished\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-cluster-node-remove\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    opt Routes change\n      SW->>N: Reconcile the Routes<br/>child route-reconcile\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - returns the Node's Routes to Node scope, with generated domains on the Node TLD; custom proxy Routes keep their Node scope; the cutover is inside the child\n      %%> - the child moves the Routes' own DNS records and removes the Router's LAN rules to the workload; the steps below change only the per-requester answers and the member's LAN rules\n    end\n    SW->>V: Select the Node in DNS<br/>records + catalog, then reload\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - private DNS answers the Node's names with the Node instead of the Router\n    SW->>N: Prune unused rules<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall absent\n    %%> Why last:\n    %%> - the Node keeps its LAN path to the Router until its Routes and DNS have moved\n    %%> Why it always runs:\n    %%> - it removes only what the stored state does not need, so after a failure before the cutover it removes nothing the member uses\n    alt every step succeeds\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      Note over GW: End the membership<br/>Routes cut over\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - at the cutover when Routes change, and when the run succeeds when none do\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the Node leaves the Cluster, and the Gateway deletes its `router` assignment that never became active in the same transaction; the pending detach ends\n    else a step fails\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      Note over GW: Read the failed step<br/>cutover or not\n      %%> Result before the cutover:\n      %%> - the Node stays a member and every Route keeps its domain and scope\n      %%> Result after the cutover:\n      %%> - the Node has left and the pending detach stays; a rerun for the same Cluster finishes the withdrawal, the DNS selection and the prune\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same command again; every step runs again and skips what already matches\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    end\n  else Cluster is inactive, nothing unfinished\n    GW-->>CLI: 200 OK<br/>Node detached\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the membership ends at once and a `router` assignment that never became active is deleted; no Node is contacted and no traffic changes\n  end\n"
  },
  "cluster/router-set": {
   "kind": "cluster.router_set",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): only the Route-publish step of this run holds the Route-serving Nodes, for its seconds, through the Gateway's `route-hold` call; the rest of the run is not held (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS 1 (cert signing is the one step→Gateway call), R2-R4. docs/reference/native-cluster-router.md, docs/cli/cluster/index.mdx.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 17, 18, 22 (certificates installed in the publish step, as route:create does; cfgmgmt behaviors named).\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Node work of the site child is one Ansible phase (see route:create).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: cluster:router:set\n  %% kind: cluster.router_set\n  %% Pinned Caddy downloads follow admitted candidates; transport failures may try upstream, integrity failures stop.\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane M icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as New Router<br/>candidate Node\n  participant M as Member Nodes<br/>incl. old Router\n  CLI->>GW: cluster:router:set<br/>CLUSTER NODE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit cluster:router:set CLUSTER NODE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<cluster> <node> [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Cluster and the active member that becomes the Router\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - makes the candidate the Cluster Router: members on the LAN reach Cluster Routes through it over HTTPS (Cluster DNS on the Router is not built yet)\n  %%> - replaces the current Router, if any, without a gap\n  Note over GW: Validate, record candidate<br/>Cluster lock\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the candidate\n  %%> - the candidate is an active member of the Cluster and a Linux Node with a valid account (`cluster.router_node_invalid`); a candidate without a LAN address is reached by its WireGuard address\n  %%> - no other Router change runs for the Cluster; the Gateway waits up to 30 seconds, then refuses with `cluster.router_busy`\n  %%> - no unfinished replacement from another Router change, and no running `node:role:remove` of a stale Router assignment of the Cluster (`cluster.router_transition_conflict`)\n  %%> Builds:\n  %%> - the Router's Caddy sites for every Cluster Route, its certificate requests, and the LAN firewall rules\n  %%> - the before and after state, so a failure can restore the before state\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the candidate's `router` assignment is recorded as pending; the old Router stays active\n  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent\n  alt candidate is not yet the Router\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-cluster-router\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - starts one Swamp run\n    %%> Inputs:\n    %%> - the sites, certificate requests, LAN rules, and old Router assignments\n    %%> - the addresses and SSH host keys of the candidate and every member; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the task ID, at once\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n    SW->>N: Prepare the Router<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt user · apt\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - over SSH with `@swamp/ssh` and `@swamp/cfgmgmt` behaviors\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the `router` role setup: the managed account, the required packages and the pinned Caddy package\n    %%> - an existing, matching install is left as it is\n    SW->>N: Request the certificates<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · openssl req\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - makes a key and a CSR on the candidate for each Cluster Route domain that changed\n    %%> - the private keys never leave the candidate\n    SW->>GW: Sign the CSRs<br/>certificate-sign primitive\n    %%> Why a call to the Gateway:\n    %%> - certificate signing is the one allowed step→Gateway call; the Orbit CA stays in the Gateway\n    GW-->>SW: Signed certificates<br/>one batch\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the signed certificates; Swamp installs them on the candidate\n    SW->>M: Open LAN paths<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall apply\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - on the candidate: allows 443/tcp and 53/tcp+udp from each eligible member's LAN address\n    %%> - on each workload member: allows the candidate's LAN address to its Route ports\n    %%> - identical lines are left as they are\n    %%> - one `forEach` over the members; it runs after the certificates are signed\n    SW->>GW: Hold the Route Nodes<br/>operation:direct:route-hold\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks the Gateway for the hold of each Node that serves the Route's site, for the seconds of this step only, not the whole operation\n    %%> - the Gateway reads the Nodes from the admitted intent; the call carries the run ID\n    %%> - a Node that another Route write holds is waited for, up to 5 s per call and 2 minutes in all; then the step fails with `route_hold.busy` and the Route step does not run\n    %%> - the hold has a 5-minute lease and ends with the Operation, so a lost run cannot keep a Node\n    SW->>N: Install certs, publish<br/>child route-create-site · Ansible\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - installs the signed certificates next to their keys and writes the Router site list with `@swamp/cfgmgmt/file`, in one step as `route:create` does\n    %%> - validates the full Router site list in a same-directory candidate, atomically publishes it, then reloads the running Caddy; no restart\n    SW->>GW: Release the Route Nodes<br/>operation:direct:route-release\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - gives the Nodes back as soon as the Route step ends, whatever its result; the Route writes that wait for them then start\n    %%> - runs beside the next step, so it adds no time\n    SW->>M: Probe the workloads<br/>TLS from the Router over the LAN\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - checks from the candidate that every Cluster Route's workload answers with a valid certificate\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the candidate only becomes the Router when it can serve every Route\n    Note over SW: Select the Router in DNS<br/>VPN Node over @swamp/ssh\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - private DNS answers members' Cluster names with the candidate's LAN address, then waits the 31-second DNS grace\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - over SSH to the VPN Node, with the `swamp` role's key\n    opt a Router is replaced\n      SW->>GW: Hold the Nodes to retire<br/>operation:direct:route-hold\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - asks the Gateway for the hold of each Node that serves the Route's site, for the seconds of this step only, not the whole operation\n      %%> - the Gateway reads the Nodes from the admitted intent; the call carries the run ID\n      %%> - a Node that another Route write holds is waited for, up to 5 s per call and 2 minutes in all; then the step fails with `route_hold.busy` and the Route step does not run\n      %%> - the hold has a 5-minute lease and ends with the Operation, so a lost run cannot keep a Node\n      SW->>M: Retire the old Router<br/>withdraw sites · LAN rules\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - only when the run replaces a Router, after the DNS grace\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - removes the Router sites and certificates from the old Router and reloads its Caddy\n      %%> - removes the LAN rules that only the old Router needed; it never removes WireGuard baseline rules\n      %%> If it fails:\n      %%> - the candidate stays the Router; the old assignment stays recorded as unfinished, and a rerun finishes it\n      SW->>GW: Release the retired Nodes<br/>operation:direct:route-release\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - gives the Nodes back as soon as the Route step ends, whatever its result; the Route writes that wait for them then start\n      %%> - runs beside the next step, so it adds no time\n    end\n    alt every step passes\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n      %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n      Note over GW: Record the Router<br/>candidate active\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the candidate's `router` assignment becomes active; the old one is deleted\n      %%> - the task succeeds\n    else preparing or publishing fails\n      M--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or probe fails\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - a setup, certificate, firewall or Caddy step fails, a workload probe fails, or the DNS selection fails\n      SW->>M: Restore the before state<br/>Caddy · LAN rules · DNS\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - puts back the captured Caddy, firewall and DNS state, so the old Router keeps serving\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      %%> The Gateway reads:\n      %%> - which step failed, its error code, and whether the restore passed\n      %%> - if the restore fails too, the candidate is marked failed, the old assignment stays active, and the result says so; `doctor` shows the drift\n      Note over GW: Keep the old Router<br/>candidate failed · rerun\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the candidate's assignment is marked failed; the old Router stays active\n      %%> - the task fails\n      %%> On the Node:\n      %%> - packages and Caddy on the candidate stay; that is harmless\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n    end\n  else candidate is already the Router\n    GW-->>CLI: 200 OK<br/>already the Router\n    %%> Skipped:\n    %%> - the candidate is the active Router and no replacement is pending, so nothing runs and the result reports `Skipped — already the Router (cluster.router_unchanged)`\n  end\n"
  },
  "cluster/router-unset": {
   "kind": "cluster.router_unset",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): only the Route-publish step of this run holds the Route-serving Nodes, for its seconds, through the Gateway's `route-hold` call; the rest of the run is not held (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R2-R4, same shape as cluster.router_set. docs/reference/native-cluster-router.md, docs/cli/cluster/index.mdx.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 1, 22 (no empty DNS note step; cfgmgmt file for the sites).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: cluster:router:unset\n  %% kind: cluster.router_unset\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane M icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Current Router<br/>member Node\n  participant M as Member Nodes<br/>workloads\n  CLI->>GW: cluster:router:unset<br/>CLUSTER\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit cluster:router:unset CLUSTER`\n  %%> - syntax: `<cluster> [--force] [--json]`\n  %%> Confirmation:\n  %%> - without `--force`, the CLI asks first; the default is No; without a terminal, or with `--json`, `--force` is required\n  Note over CLI: Inspect Cluster, confirm\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - reads the Cluster and asks for confirmation; the default is No\n  Note over GW: Validate, plan the cleanup<br/>Cluster lock\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to a member Node of the Cluster\n  %%> - the Cluster is inactive or has no TLD (`cluster.active_router_required`)\n  %%> - the Cluster owns no Route (`cluster.routes_require_router`, or `route.reconciliation_required` for an active Route)\n  %%> - no other Router change runs for the Cluster; the Gateway waits up to 30 seconds, then refuses with `cluster.router_busy`\n  %%> - no `node:role:remove` of a stale Router assignment of the Cluster runs (`cluster.router_transition_conflict`)\n  %%> Builds:\n  %%> - the Router state to remove: Router sites and certificates, and the LAN rules only the Router needed\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the Router's `router` assignment is recorded as retiring\n  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent\n  %%> Decides:\n  %%> - whether any Node holds Router state: a Router site, a Router certificate, or a LAN rule only the Router needed\n  alt no Node holds Router state\n    Note over GW: Clear the Router<br/>R1: no run\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the `router` assignment is deleted and the task succeeds at once; no Swamp run starts (R1)\n    GW-->>CLI: Router cleared<br/>task succeeded\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the final result at once; there is nothing to follow\n  else Router state on a Node\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-cluster-router\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - the state to remove, and the addresses and SSH host keys of the Router and the members\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task ID, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>GW: Hold the Route Nodes<br/>operation:direct:route-hold\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - asks the Gateway for the hold of each Node that serves the Route's site, for the seconds of this step only, not the whole operation\n  %%> - the Gateway reads the Nodes from the admitted intent; the call carries the run ID\n  %%> - a Node that another Route write holds is waited for, up to 5 s per call and 2 minutes in all; then the step fails with `route_hold.busy` and the Route step does not run\n  %%> - the hold has a 5-minute lease and ends with the Operation, so a lost run cannot keep a Node\n  SW->>N: Withdraw the Router sites<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · reload\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - over SSH with `@swamp/ssh` and `@swamp/cfgmgmt/file`; `caddy validate`, then a reload\n  %%> No DNS change:\n  %%> - an inactive Cluster, or one without a TLD and Routes, gets no Router answers from private DNS\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - removes the Router sites and their certificates, validates the rest, and reloads Caddy; no restart\n  %%> - Caddy and the packages stay, as for every role removal that keeps shared packages\n  SW->>GW: Release the Route Nodes<br/>operation:direct:route-release\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - gives the Nodes back as soon as the Route step ends, whatever its result; the Route writes that wait for them then start\n  %%> - runs beside the next step, so it adds no time\n  SW->>M: Remove the LAN rules<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall apply\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - on the Router: removes the 443 and 53 allows for member LAN addresses\n  %%> - on each member: removes the allows for the Router's LAN address that no other Route needs\n  %%> - never removes WireGuard baseline rules\n  SW->>M: Check the rules are gone<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall check\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - reads ufw back on every Node and checks the Router lines are absent\n  alt every step passes\n    M-->>SW: Router lines absent\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the check passed on every Node\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Clear the Router<br/>assignment gone\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the `router` assignment is deleted; the Cluster has no Router\n    %%> - the task succeeds\n  else a step fails\n    M--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or line remains\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - Caddy refuses the config, SSH fails, or a rule is still present\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code\n    Note over GW: Keep the assignment<br/>failed · rerun after a fix\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the `router` assignment stays, marked failed\n    %%> - the task fails\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - steps that finished stay done; there is no rollback, because the Cluster serves no traffic through the Router\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again; it skips what is already removed\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n  end\n"
  },
  "cluster/update": {
   "kind": "cluster.update",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run with the shared route reconcile child (route.reconcile). docs/reference/routes.md#change-a-tld-cluster-state-or-cluster-membership.\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: cluster:update\n  %% kind: cluster.update\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane V icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Cluster Nodes<br/>Router · members\n  participant V as VPN Node<br/>private DNS\n  CLI->>GW: cluster:update<br/>CLUSTER options\n  Note over GW: Validate, lock, plan<br/>transitions stored\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to a member Node, or to the Gateway Node when the Cluster has none\n  %%> - at least one of `--name` and `--tld` (`cluster.update_required`); the name and TLD are valid and free\n  %%> - a new TLD on an active Cluster needs one active Router (`cluster.router_required`) and no non-member Node that owns it (`cluster.tld_conflict`), and keeps the private names within the DNS policy limit (`vpn.peer_dns_policy_invalid`)\n  %%> - every affected Route keeps a valid domain, scope and target, and a generated Route keeps an effective TLD; one refusal leaves every Route in place; an active public Route that the change would move is refused (`route.reconciliation_required`)\n  %%> - no other Cluster change runs; the Gateway waits up to 30 seconds, then refuses with `cluster.router_busy`\n  %%> Stored:\n  %%> - the Cluster keeps its name and TLD; a pending replacement is stored for each domain change and the old placement for each scope change\n  alt active Cluster and the TLD changes, or an earlier change is unfinished\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-cluster-update\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    SW->>V: Add the new TLD in DNS<br/>records + catalog, then reload\n    %%> Why first:\n    %%> - the new names must resolve before the child withdraws the old sites; the old TLD keeps answering\n    %%> Managed peers:\n    %%> - they learn the new TLD from the peer DNS policy in a separate run, one peer at a time; until then a peer resolves the new name through its public resolver\n    opt Routes change\n      SW->>N: Reconcile the Routes<br/>child route-reconcile\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - moves every generated Route to the new TLD, or back to its Node TLD when the TLD is unset; the cutover is inside the child\n    end\n    SW->>V: Prune old DNS answers<br/>records + catalog, then reload\n    %%> Why it always runs:\n    %%> - it removes only what the stored state does not need, so after a failure before the cutover it removes the new TLD again\n    alt every step succeeds\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      Note over GW: Record name and TLD<br/>Routes cut over\n    else a step fails\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      Note over GW: Read the failed step<br/>cutover or not\n      %%> Result before the cutover:\n      %%> - the Cluster keeps its name and TLD and every Route keeps its domain and scope\n      %%> Result after the cutover:\n      %%> - the new name and TLD are in force; a rerun finishes the withdrawal and the prune\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same command again; every step runs again and skips what already matches\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    end\n  else name only, inactive Cluster, or no change, nothing unfinished\n    GW-->>CLI: 200 OK<br/>Cluster updated\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the name and TLD are recorded at once; no Node is contacted and no traffic changes\n    %%> Skipped:\n    %%> - when the values already match and nothing is unfinished, the result reports `Skipped — nothing to change (cluster.update_unchanged)`\n  end\n"
  },
  "conn/environment-restart": {
   "kind": "conn.environment_restart",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Container runtime (docs/reference/development-containers.md#existing-instances, docs/reference/conn.md#restart-for-the-machine-name): restart an idle container T3 server whose label is not yet the machine name. Stock T3 reads PRETTY_HOSTNAME only at its start. The Gateway reads every label at once, then each candidate's threads right before that Instance's own `conn.environment_restart` admission. The Node form walks its containers by Instance ID.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: orbit conn:environment-restart <instance> · orbit conn:node-environments-restart <node>\n  %% kind: conn.environment_restart\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>API callers\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Conn layer\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Node<br/>the container\n  CLI->>GW: conn:environment-restart<br/>INSTANCE · or a Node's\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit conn:environment-restart INSTANCE`, syntax `<instance-id> [--json]`\n  %%> - `orbit conn:node-environments-restart NODE`, syntax `<node-id> [--json]`: the same steps for each container Instance of the Node\n  Note over GW: Read every T3 label<br/>at once · 5 s · no session\n  %%> Answers without a run:\n  %%> - `skipped`: `frozen` or `stopped` (it reads the name at its next start), `no_t3` (no Conn server row), `t3_unreadable`\n  %%> - `current`: the label already equals `<instance>.<project slug>`\n  loop each candidate, by Instance ID\n    Note over GW: Read its threads<br/>shell · admin session\n    %%> Busy, answered `busy` without a run:\n    %%> - a working run (the idle sleep's rule), or any pending runtime request, blocking or not: `t3_working`\n    %%> - another Conn Operation on the server: `conn_operation`; other work on the Instance, or an Instance that is not active: the refusal code\n    %%> Limits:\n    %%> - at most four restarts a call, later ones `skipped` `next_run`; past 120 s the rest `skipped` `deadline`\n    %%> Why the read is here:\n    %%> - one busy rule in one place; a read in the run would need the admin session as a vault copy and the rule a second time outside PHP\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-conn-environment-restart\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - one Operation holding the Instance's lock; the intent has no session\n  end\n  GW-->>CLI: 200 · one answer each<br/>restarting has operation_id\n  SW->>N: Restart T3<br/>name · processes · restart\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - stops with `skipped` when the container no longer runs, or `name_not_written` when /etc/machine-info lacks the name\n  %%> - right before the restart, reads T3's service members once (the rule: docs/reference/development-containers.md#existing-instances); a terminal or command answers `busy` `processes`, an unreadable read `processes_unreadable`\n  %%> - `systemctl try-restart t3.service`; nothing else restarts, and the kernel hostname stays\n  %%> - then reads the label inside the container on T3's port until it is the name, about 84 s at most\n  %%> What a user sees:\n  %%> - a turn with no live tool process outside the provider's session is cut (Grep, Read or Edit, or an ended Bash command); T3 continues it\n  %%> - an open T3 terminal is not ended unless it opens in the moment (about a second) between that read and the restart; Orbit Processes do not restart\n  %%> - a connected client shows Reconnecting… and reconnects; an unsent draft stays in the browser\n  alt T3 answers with its name\n    %% path: restarted\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>restarted · window_ms\n  else T3 runs a terminal or a turn's command\n    %% path: processes\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>busy · processes\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the counts of terminal shells and commands; nothing restarted, and a later run takes it\n  else T3 does not come back\n    %% path: not-back\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>conn.t3_unready\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - `failed`, or `conn.t3_label_mismatch` when T3 answers with another label; the Operation does not retry\n  end\n  loop until terminal, per restart\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: Status and result\n  end\n"
  },
  "conn/engine/environment-restart": {
   "kind": "conn.environment_restart",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% conn.environment_restart on the Gateway engine: the `Restart T3` step of conn/environment-restart.mmd (workflow operation-conn-environment-restart) as one `instance` family primitive. Design for ADR 0267 and ADR 0271; no engine flow exists yet. Step names describe effects; the implementation settles action names unless an existing action is named.\n  %% Desired state: T3 answers with the machine name. A lost receipt never restarts T3 twice: the journal repeats a finished result, and an `attempt` journaled before the restart call stops an interrupted replay from calling it again; it reports the original `restart_at`.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: conn:environment-restart\n  %% kind: conn.environment_restart\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane EN icon=workflow pill=engine tone=purple\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=executor tone=amber\n  %% lane C icon=server pill=copy tone=green\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant EN as Engine<br/>Gateway and NATS\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>executor\n  participant C as Container<br/>via executor\n  CLI->>GW: conn:environment-restart<br/>INSTANCE · or a Node's\n  Note over GW: Read every T3 label<br/>at once · 5 s · no session\n  %%> Same answers without an Operation as conn/environment-restart.mmd: skipped (frozen, stopped, no_t3, t3_unreadable) or current\n  loop each candidate, by Instance ID\n    Note over GW: Read its threads<br/>shell · admin session\n    %%> The busy rule stays in the Gateway: a working run or pending runtime request answers `busy` `t3_working`; another Conn Operation `conn_operation`; at most four restarts a call, 120 s in all\n    Note over GW: Admit the restart<br/>one Operation per Instance\n    %%> The Operation holds the Instance's lock; its intent has no session\n  end\n  GW-->>CLI: 200 · one answer each<br/>restarting has operation_id\n  alt the engine admits the whole Operation\n    %%> Admission, one decision per Operation (EngineKinds::routes): `conn.environment_restart` is in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`, the Node reports the T3 restart action, and the flow accepts the plan, else `EngineUnsupported`\n    EN->>C: Restart T3 when idle<br/>name · processes · restart\n    %%> Answers `skipped` `stopped` or `frozen` when the container no longer runs, `name_not_written` when /etc/machine-info lacks the name, nothing for a right label: the Gateway admitted the restart because the label was not the name, and the step restarts as the Swamp step does. Its only no-action answers are those ConnEnvironmentRestartProjection accepts: `skipped` (`frozen`, `stopped`, `name_not_written`) and `busy` (`processes`, `processes_unreadable`)\n    %%> Right before the restart, reads T3's service members once: a terminal or command answers `busy` `processes` (unreadable: `processes_unreadable`) and restarts nothing\n    %%> Appends an `attempt` record with `restart_at` and syncs it, then `systemctl try-restart t3.service`; nothing else restarts, the kernel hostname stays\n    %%> The receipt returns the journaled `restart_at`; the Gateway computes `window_ms` from the intent's thread `read_at` to that `restart_at` (ConnEnvironmentRestartProjection::window), so a recovered result reports the original window\n    C-->>EN: Label read back<br/>restarted · restart_at\n    %%> Readiness: reads the label on T3's port until it is the name, about 84 s at most; else `conn.t3_unready`, or `conn.t3_label_mismatch` for another label\n    opt the receipt is lost\n      EN->>C: Resend the same step<br/>fresh nonce · same step ID\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - the step passes its resend deadline with no receipt; two sends, one execution\n      %%> - the Operation keeps its lock, so Instance 42 stays fenced; no other Operation and no Swamp run acts on it meanwhile\n      %%> On the Node:\n      %%> - the journal holds `done` for this Operation step, action and target: the executor answers the stored result with `repeat: true` and does not act again\n      %%> - an `attempt` without `done` (the executor died after the restart call), on a resend or the startup replay of the `open` order: no second restart; it reads the label until the bound and answers `restarted` with the original journaled `restart_at`, or `conn.t3_unready`\n      %%> - an order still `open` replays under the target lock first; an order that expired before the lock did nothing (`executor.order_expired`), and the step is sent again\n      C-->>EN: Stored result<br/>repeat: true\n    end\n    alt every step verified\n      EN->>GW: Record the outcome<br/>restarted · busy · skipped\n      %%> - the kind's own projection records the result, the same rows a Swamp run records\n    else a step fails\n      EN--xGW: Failed step<br/>error_code · redacted detail\n      %%> - a deterministic refusal is a terminal receipt too; the journal keeps it and a resend repeats it\n      Note over GW: Record the failure<br/>conn.t3_unready\n      %%> - the Operation does not retry; a later call takes the Instance again\n    end\n  else the engine does not admit it\n    Note over GW: Start the Swamp run<br/>operation-conn-environment-restart\n    %%> - the kind is not enabled, a Node lacks the action, `$KV.NODES` is unreadable, or the flow throws `EngineUnsupported`: the whole Operation runs operation-conn-environment-restart (conn/environment-restart.mmd)\n    %%> - no step of it runs on the engine; an Operation never splits across engines\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "database/create": {
   "kind": "database.create",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4, 5 (swamp role SSH identity), round 2 bugs (server admin password by its own vault key; the owner password is staged and always removed), flow-tighten. docs/reference/database-servers.md.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 14, 16, 22 (one docker exec -i with SQL on stdin, no staged password or cleanup, no script read, report or assert step).\n  %% Verified: the parallel test database pool applies to MySQL and PostgreSQL in CreateServerDatabaseAction.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: database:create\n  %% kind: database.create\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Node 12<br/>beast-mysql server\n  CLI->>GW: database:create shop\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit database:create shop --driver=sqlite --path=/srv/shop.sqlite`\n  %%> - syntax: `<slug> [--server=SERVER] [--instance=ID] [--locale=C|C.UTF-8] [--test-processes=N] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> - or, to register an existing database: `<slug> --driver=DRIVER [--host=HOST] [--port=PORT] [--database=NAME] [--path=PATH] [--username=USER] [--password=SECRET] [--node=NODE]`\n  %%> - on a Valkey server: `<slug> --server=SERVER --pattern=PATTERN ... --allow=RULE ... [--database=INDEX]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the connection slug as the argument: lowercase kebab, at most 63 characters\n  %%> - with `--server`: the Database server that gets the new database\n  %%> - with `--instance`: the Instance that owns the database and gets it attached under prefix `DB`\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate registration\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - only the slug is a required signature argument; registration also needs a driver and that driver's fields\n  %%> - slug alone is refused with `database.driver_invalid` by the CLI\n  %%> - the default path records an existing database; it creates no database or owner on a Node\n  opt --driver\n    Note over GW: Select the driver\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Choose mysql, pgsql, sqlite or redis; required for registration.\n  end\n  opt --node\n    Note over GW: Associate a Node\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Resolve the supplied Node for the connection record.\n  end\n  opt --host\n    Note over GW: Set the host\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Required for mysql, pgsql and redis registration.\n  end\n  opt --port\n    Note over GW: Set the port\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Replace the driver default TCP port.\n  end\n  opt --database\n    Note over GW: Set the database\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Required name for SQL registration; optional index for redis.\n  end\n  opt --path\n    Note over GW: Set the SQLite path\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Required for sqlite registration.\n  end\n  opt --username\n    Note over GW: Set the username\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Required for mysql and pgsql registration.\n  end\n  opt --password\n    Note over GW: Set the password\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Required for mysql and pgsql registration; encrypt it in the record.\n  end\n  Note over GW: Record the connection<br/>R1: no run · password encrypted\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - connection `shop` is recorded, with its password encrypted\n  %%> - no task and no Swamp run start (R1); nothing changes on any Node\n  GW-->>CLI: 201 Created<br/>connection shop\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the connection record at once; there is nothing to follow\n  opt --server\n    Note over GW: Validate, reserve the name<br/>connection reserved · task #5121\n    %%> Requirements:\n    %%> - the slug `shop` is free (`database.slug_conflict`)\n    %%> - with `--server`: Database server `beast-mysql` is active, and your Node has an access grant to Node 12\n    %%> - `--locale` only on PostgreSQL; `--test-processes` on MySQL and PostgreSQL; `--pattern` and `--allow` only on Valkey\n    %%> - no database removal runs on `beast-mysql`; the Gateway holds one lock per Database server\n    %%> Builds:\n    %%> - without an Instance: database `shop` and its own owner `shop`\n    %%> - a new password for the standalone owner\n    opt --instance\n      Note over GW: Use the Instance owner\n      %%> What it changes:\n      %%> - Requires a MySQL or PostgreSQL server and existing Instance 42.\n      %%> - Use the Instance owner and password, reusing them when already recorded on this server.\n      %%> - Add the test database and default eight-worker pool to the same create batch.\n      %%> - After native success, project the attachment to Instance 42 under DB; run `env:sync` to install its keys (on a container Instance it writes only the connection's keys in place).\n    end\n    opt --test-processes\n      Note over GW: Set the test pool size\n      %%> What it changes:\n      %%> - Requires an Instance; replace the default 8 with 1–32 workers.\n    end\n    opt --locale\n      Note over GW: Set PostgreSQL locale\n      %%> What it changes:\n      %%> - Use C or C.UTF-8 and template0 instead of inheriting the image default.\n    end\n    opt --pattern\n      Note over GW: Set Valkey key patterns\n      %%> What it changes:\n      %%> - Required for a Valkey server; restrict the ACL user to these key patterns.\n    end\n    opt --allow\n      Note over GW: Set Valkey commands\n      %%> What it changes:\n      %%> - Required for a Valkey server; replace the allowed command list.\n    end\n    opt --database\n      Note over GW: Set the Valkey index\n      %%> What it changes:\n      %%> - On a server, only Valkey accepts this option; check it against the live database count.\n    end\n    GW->>SW: Store the owner password<br/>database-create-intent-5121\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - puts two whole values into the Swamp vault on Node 209, after the task is committed (R3)\n    %%> - `database-create-intent-5121`: the admin password line, then the create batch with the owner password inside\n    %%> - `database-create-owner-5121`: the owner password line, then the owner check\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - secrets go Gateway → vault → step; workflow inputs carry only vault key names\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-database-create\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n    %%> Inputs:\n    %%> - the driver, database names, owner name, test pool size and locale\n    %%> - the vault key names `database-create-intent-5121` and `database-create-owner-5121`\n    %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5121\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the task number, at once\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n    SW->>N: Create owner and databases<br/>docker exec -i · SQL on stdin\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209 with the official `@swamp/ssh` behavior and the `swamp` role's SSH identity; nothing is copied to the Node\n    %%> - one `docker exec -i` into the server container per statement batch, with the statements on stdin; the owner password from `vault.get` sits inside the SQL, never in argv or a file\n    %%> - admin login: inside the container, the first stdin line is the admin password, read into the client's password variable, never in argv or a file\n    %%> What it does, in order:\n    %%> - creates the owner login, or keeps it when it already exists with this password\n    %%> - creates the primary database and grants its standalone owner access\n    %%> - grants the owner only the databases in the admitted batch\n    %%> Refuses:\n    %%> - a name the server already has for something else (`database.name_conflict`); the batch refuses by itself before any change, so no read runs first, and prints the refusal for the Gateway\n    %%> Per driver:\n    %%> - MySQL: one grant per database; the Instance block adds the test prefix grant\n    %%> - PostgreSQL: the chosen locale and `TEMPLATE template0`; revokes PUBLIC access, then grants the public schema to the owner\n    %%> - Valkey: `CONFIG GET databases`, then `ACL SETUSER shop reset on #<hash> resetchannels ~<pattern> -@all +<rule> -@admin -@dangerous -function -script`, then `ACL SAVE`; the Gateway checks index 1 against the live count\n    %%> - Valkey with `--allow=flushdb`: `+flushdb` follows the final deny; give the user its own `--database` index\n    SW->>N: Check the owner login<br/>docker exec -i · as the owner\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - logs in as the owner, password as the first stdin line, and reads its own name; the Gateway checks the answer\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the connection only counts as active when the server accepts it; this is the one end check\n    %%> Not included:\n    %%> - no staged password file, so no cleanup step; no report or assert step\n    alt every step passes\n      N-->>SW: owner logs in\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the check passed for every database\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n      %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n      Note over GW: Record create success<br/>connection shop · task succeeded\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - connection `shop` stays linked to `beast-mysql`, with Node 12's address and port\n      %%> - task #5121 succeeds\n    else a step fails\n      N--xSW: name taken or exit ≠ 0\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - a name is taken, SQL fails, SSH fails, or the owner cannot log in\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      %%> The Gateway reads:\n      %%> - which step failed and its exit code, mapped to `error_code` such as `database.name_conflict`\n      Note over GW: Keep the connection<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - connection `shop` stays reserved with its encrypted owner password\n      %%> - an invalid Valkey index removes an unattached connection and its recorded user; the ACL user can remain on the server\n      %%> - task #5121 fails\n      %%> On the Node:\n      %%> - an owner or database created before the failure stays; there is no rollback\n      %%> - that is safe: the name check accepts objects that already belong to this connection\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same command again; it reuses the reservation and finishes the missing steps\n      %%> - a changed request is a conflict (`database.slug_conflict`)\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show #5121\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "database/destroy": {
   "kind": "database.destroy",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4, 5 (swamp role SSH identity), round 2 bugs (server admin password by its own vault key, cleanup on pass or fail). docs/reference/database-servers.md#remove-a-database.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 6, 16, 19, 22 (no copied scripts or cleanup, no ownership read, blocker check and drops in one SQL batch on stdin).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: database:destroy\n  %% kind: database.destroy\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Node 12<br/>beast-mysql server\n  CLI->>GW: database:destroy shop\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit database:destroy shop --force`\n  %%> - syntax: `<slug> [--force] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the connection slug, as `database:list` shows it\n  %%> Confirmation:\n  %%> - without `--force`, the CLI asks first; the default is No\n  %%> - with `--json` or without a terminal, `--force` is required (`database.confirmation_required`)\n  Note over CLI: Confirm destruction<br/>default No\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - ask on a terminal before sending the request; decline cancels\n  Note over GW: Check, plan the removal<br/>task #5124 · lock beast-mysql\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12\n  %%> - no Instance attaches `shop` (`database.connection_attached`); detach with `instance:database:remove` first\n  %%> - AI router setup and Analytics do not use it\n  %%> - no database creation or other removal runs on `beast-mysql`; the Gateway holds one lock per Database server\n  %%> Plans:\n  %%> - the databases: `shop`, its test database and the recorded test pool on MySQL or PostgreSQL\n  %%> - the users Orbit created for `shop` that no other connection on the server uses\n  %%> - system databases such as `mysql` or `postgres` are refused before any run\n  %%> Decides:\n  %%> - whether only the record goes, or the database must leave the server\n  alt connection without a server\n    Note over GW: Delete only the record<br/>R1: no run, database stays\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a registered external database or SQLite file\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - connection `shop` is deleted\n    %%> - the database itself stays where it is; Orbit never created it\n    %%> - no Swamp run starts (R1)\n    GW-->>CLI: Connection destroyed<br/>database kept\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the final result at once; there is nothing to follow\n  else database on a server\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-database-destroy\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n    %%> Inputs:\n    %%> - the planned databases (with the recorded test database and pool) and unshared users\n    %%> - four vault key names, one per batch (`database-destroy-5124-fence`, `-drop`, `-gone`, `-restore`); each value is the admin password line and its statements, so the password never enters the intent\n    %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5124\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the task number, at once\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n    opt PostgreSQL server\n      SW->>N: Fence new clients<br/>docker exec -i · SQL on stdin\n      %%> How:\n      %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209 with the official `@swamp/ssh` behavior; nothing is copied to the Node\n      %%> - one `docker exec -i` into the server container, the admin password as the first stdin line, then the statements\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - stops new clients from connecting to every planned database\n      %%> - journals each database's prior value in `orbit_admin.database_removal_fences`, in the same transaction\n      %%> Not included:\n      %%> - existing clients stay connected; Orbit never force-disconnects them\n    end\n    SW->>N: Drop databases and users<br/>docker exec -i · SQL on stdin\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - one statement batch on stdin: first the blocker query, then each planned database, then each planned user that no other connection on `beast-mysql` uses\n    %%> - a missing database or user counts as removed\n    %%> Blocks:\n    %%> - PostgreSQL: sessions, replication slots, prepared transactions, subscriptions, outside dependencies of a removed role\n    %%> - MySQL: connected clients, metadata locks, outside definers, cross-schema foreign keys\n    %%> - any blocker ends the batch with `database.in_use` before the first drop\n    %%> Per driver:\n    %%> - admin login: the admin password as the first stdin line, from the vault, read inside the container\n    %%> - Valkey: no database; `ACL DELUSER`, then `ACL SAVE`; the user's keys stay\n    %%> - Valkey default login: the removal plan excludes `default`; its credentials and keys stay\n    %%> Not included:\n    %%> - no ownership read first: the plan comes from the Gateway's records\n    SW->>N: Check they are gone<br/>docker exec -i · SELECT\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - checks that no planned database or user is left on the server; the check fails the run while one remains\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the removal only counts when the server shows it; this is the one end check, and a parent run such as `instance:destroy` sees a refusal too\n    opt a step failed\n      SW->>N: Restore the fence<br/>prior ALLOW_CONNECTIONS\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - only after a failed step on a fenced PostgreSQL server: a step with `dependsOn` and the condition `failed`\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - gives each surviving database its exact prior value from the journal\n      %%> If it fails:\n      %%> - the journal stays; the next `database:destroy` restores or finishes\n    end\n    alt every step passes\n      N-->>SW: set absent\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the check passed for every database and user\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n      %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n      Note over GW: Delete the connection<br/>users too · task succeeded\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - connection `shop` and its recorded users are deleted\n      %%> - task #5124 succeeds\n    else blocked or a drop fails\n      N--xSW: in use or exit ≠ 0\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - a blocker, an ownership refusal, a failed drop, or a lost SSH connection\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      %%> The Gateway reads:\n      %%> - which step failed and its error code, for example `database.in_use`\n      Note over GW: Keep the connection<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - connection `shop` stays with its users and attachments; the task records the failure\n      %%> - task #5124 fails\n      %%> On the Node:\n      %%> - after a blocker, nothing was dropped\n      %%> - after a failed drop, the dropped objects stay gone; there is no rollback\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - stop the clients or remove the blockers, then run the same command again\n      %%> - a rerun repeats the checks and counts missing objects as removed\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show #5124\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "database/import": {
   "kind": "database.import",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: main orchestrator 2026-11-13 (load a dump as the owner, no password shown). Secret shape of database.create (Q-DBAUTH, R3); client in a throwaway container on the server's network.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: database:import\n  %% kind: database.import\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Server Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: database:import shop<br/>--file=/home/USER/shop.sql.gz\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit database:import shop --file=/home/USER/shop.sql.gz`\n  %%> - syntax: `<slug> --file=PATH [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the slug of a MySQL or PostgreSQL connection on a Database server\n  %%> - the absolute path of a `.sql` or `.sql.gz` file on the server's Node\n  Note over GW: Validate, record import<br/>task #5181 · lock on shop\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the connection is on an active MySQL or PostgreSQL Database server (`database.import_unsupported`)\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Gateway Node, which reaches NODE (`node_access.required`)\n  %%> - the path is absolute, ends in `.sql` or `.sql.gz`, has no `.` or `..` part (`database.import_path_invalid`)\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5181 with the path; the owner password stays encrypted in the connection\n  GW->>SW: Store the owner password<br/>database-import-intent-5181\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - puts the owner password line into the Swamp vault after the task is committed (R3)\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the password never sits in argv, a file on the Node, the task input or result, or a log\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-database-import\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - the client argv the Gateway built, the server's address and SSH host keys\n  %%> - the vault key name, never the value\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5181\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>N: Load the dump<br/>as the Node's SSH user\n  %%> Runs as:\n  %%> - the Node's SSH user, without sudo; the dump is read only when that user owns it\n  %%> The file:\n  %%> - opened once; its real path must equal the given path (no symlink), a regular file\n  %%> - otherwise `database.import_file_refused`, and nothing is loaded\n  %%> The client:\n  %%> - a throwaway container of the server's image on the server's Docker network, read-only, no capabilities\n  %%> - it logs in as the owner over TCP at the server container's address, never its trusted loopback\n  %%> - the password is the first stdin line\n  %%> - no client command from the dump runs: psql reads Orbit's own `\\restrict <random key>` first (the dump's own pg_dump pair is dropped); mysql runs with `--skip-commands --skip-system-command`\n  %%> What it does, in order:\n  %%> - counts the tables; more than 0 refuses with `database.not_empty`\n  %%> - streams the file (gunzip for `.sql.gz`) into the client; the first error stops it\n  %%> - counts the tables again\n  %%> Limits:\n  %%> - 3600 s for the step\n  alt the dump loads\n    N-->>SW: bytes read, tables\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - bytes read from the file, the client's exit code, the table count; no names, no data\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - task #5181 succeeds with `bytes_read`, `exit_code` and `tables`\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: refused or SQL error\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the file or the database is refused, the owner cannot log in, or a statement fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, database_state\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - `database_state` is `unchanged`, `empty`, `partial`, or `unknown` when the run ends without its result\n    %%> - PostgreSQL loads in one transaction, so a failed load leaves it empty\n    %%> - MySQL commits each table, so a failed load can leave it partly filled\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - from `partial`: empty the database first, then import again\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5181\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "database/read": {
   "kind": "database.read",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: coordinator 2026-11-02. Every effect runs in Swamp; a read takes the same path as a write. Decided 2026-11-02 (orchestrator): a read on a Database server runs as docker exec in its container (Q-DBAUTH); an external connection reads from 209 with the swamp role's clients; SQLite runs sqlite3 on its Node (R4). One step per placement; the Gateway picks it. No shipped script; the Gateway checks the statement and shapes the rows.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: database:query\n  %% kind: database.read\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Database host<br/>WireGuard or ssh\n  CLI->>GW: database:query shop<br/>\"SELECT id, email FROM users\"\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit database:query shop \"SELECT id, email FROM users\"`\n  %%> - syntax: `<slug> <sql> [--write] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the slug of a registered Database connection, and one SQL statement of at most 16384 characters\n  %%> Same kind:\n  %%> - `database:tables`, `database:schema` and `database:describe` send a fixed catalog read through the same flow\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - write permission belongs to the `opt --write` block in the write chart; that flow also applies to SELECT with --write\n  Note over GW: Validate, record the read<br/>connection shop · task #5170\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs Database server app-mysql\n  %%> - the connection's driver is mysql, pgsql or sqlite; redis has no SQL\n  %%> - exactly one statement (`database.sql_multiple_statements`)\n  %%> - its leading verb doesn't write, or the Gateway refuses with `database.write_required`\n  %%> - for sqlite: the connection has a Node, or the Gateway refuses with `database.sqlite_node_required`\n  %%> Where the credential lives:\n  %%> - the Gateway stores the connection's password encrypted; at start it copies the password line and the statement into the Swamp vault as one value, `database-read-intent-5170` (R3)\n  %%> - SQLite has no credential\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5170 recorded with the full intent; no lock, because a read changes nothing\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-database-read\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - the client argv the Gateway built for the driver and mode, and the read host's address and SSH host keys\n  %%> - the vault key name of the password and statement, never the value\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5170\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI waits with progress, as for other tasks; a read is a short run, usually a few seconds\n  alt a Database server or SQLite\n    SW->>N: Run the read<br/>@swamp/ssh · one client\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - Swamp reaches the host over SSH from 209 with the official `@swamp/ssh` behavior; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n    %%> - stdin is one vault value: the password as the first line, then the statement (Q-DBAUTH); SQLite has no password line\n    %%> - the client runs no client command from the statement: psql gets it as `-c`, mysql runs with `--skip-commands --skip-system-command`\n    %%> Where it runs:\n    %%> - on a Database server: one `docker exec -i` into its container on Node 12; the client in the image logs in as the connection's own user, never root\n    %%> - on SQLite: `sqlite3 -readonly -safe` on the file's Node, as the file's owner; it cannot write, attach or run shell commands\n  else a registered external database\n    SW->>N: Run the read on 209<br/>mysql or psql client\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the `mysql` or `psql` client on 209, which the `swamp` role installs; 209 reaches the host directly, with no SSH hop\n    %%> - the same vault value reaches the client on stdin\n  end\n  %%> Both:\n  %%> - MySQL and PostgreSQL run the statement inside a READ ONLY transaction\n  %%> - a 30 s limit; MySQL prints XML, PostgreSQL and SQLite print CSV; the output stops at 64 KiB\n  alt the read succeeds\n    N-->>SW: rows\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the client output; the Gateway keeps up to 500 rows, and more sets `truncated`\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event + rows\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - the rows come back as the run's terminal result; its shared run reader sees it; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record result<br/>task succeeded · rows\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - task #5170 succeeds with the rows; no password or connection string in it\n  else the server refuses or times out\n    N--xSW: SQL error or timeout\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a syntax error, a missing table, a refused write, or no answer within 30 s\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>database.query_failed\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - a bounded error code and message, without SQL output\n    Note over GW: Mark the task failed<br/>nothing changed · rerun is safe\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - task #5170 fails; nothing changed on the server\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the command again; a read is safe to repeat\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5170\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + rows\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the rows once the task has ended; SQL null shows as `NULL`\n  end\n"
  },
  "database/write": {
   "kind": "database.write",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: coordinator 2026-11-02. Every effect runs in Swamp; same path as database.read. Decided 2026-11-02 (orchestrator, database.read): a Database server's connection runs in its container (Q-DBAUTH), an external connection from 209 with the swamp role's clients, SQLite with sqlite3 on its Node (R4). No shipped script.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: database:query\n  %% kind: database.write\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Database host<br/>WireGuard or ssh\n  CLI->>GW: database:query shop<br/>\"SELECT id FROM users\"\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit database:query shop \"DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 1\" --write`\n  %%> - syntax: `<slug> <sql> [--write] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the slug of a registered Database connection, and one SQL statement of at most 16384 characters\n  %%> - `--write` allows a write statement; it is permission, not proof that rows changed\n  %%> Lost answer:\n  %%> - repeat with the same `--idempotency-key`; the Gateway returns the same task and doesn't write again\n  Note over GW: Use the read flow<br/>database.read\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - without write permission, use the read chart; a write statement is refused with `database.write_required`\n  opt --write\n    Note over GW: Validate, record the write<br/>receipt · task #5171\n    %%> Requirements:\n    %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs Database server app-mysql\n    %%> - `--write` is set; write SQL without it is refused with `database.write_required`\n    %%> - exactly one statement (`database.sql_multiple_statements`)\n    %%> - the driver is mysql, pgsql or sqlite; redis has no SQL\n    %%> - for sqlite: the connection has a Node, or the Gateway refuses with `database.sqlite_node_required`\n    %%> Where the credential lives:\n    %%> - the Gateway stores the connection's password encrypted; at start it copies the password line and the statement into the Swamp vault as one value, `database-write-intent-5171` (R3)\n    %%> - SQLite has no credential\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the idempotency receipt is recorded before anything is sent\n    %%> - task #5171 recorded with the full intent; the lock for connection shop, so two writes don't overlap\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-database-write\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n    %%> Inputs:\n    %%> - the client argv the Gateway built for the driver, and the host's address and SSH host keys\n    %%> - the vault key name of the password and statement, never the value\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5171\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the task number, at once\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - the CLI waits with progress, as for other tasks; a write is a short run, usually a few seconds\n    alt a Database server or SQLite\n      SW->>N: Run the statement<br/>@swamp/ssh · one client\n      %%> How:\n      %%> - Swamp reaches the host over SSH from 209 with the official `@swamp/ssh` behavior; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n      %%> - stdin is one vault value: the password as the first line, then the statement (Q-DBAUTH); SQLite has no password line\n      %%> - the client runs no client command from the statement: psql gets it as `-c`, mysql runs with `--skip-commands --skip-system-command`\n      %%> Where it runs:\n      %%> - on a Database server: one `docker exec -i` into its container on Node 12, as the connection's own user, never root\n      %%> - on SQLite: `sqlite3 -safe` on the file's Node, as the file's owner; it cannot attach, write other files or run shell commands\n    else a registered external database\n      SW->>N: Run the statement on 209<br/>mysql or psql client\n      %%> How:\n      %%> - the `mysql` or `psql` client on 209, which the `swamp` role installs; 209 reaches the host directly, with no SSH hop\n    end\n    %%> Both:\n    %%> - a 30 s limit; the client prints the affected row count after the statement, and its output stops at 64 KiB\n    alt the statement succeeds\n      N-->>SW: affected row count\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the number of rows the server reports; no result rows\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event + row count\n      %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n      %%> - the row count comes back as the run's terminal result; its shared run reader sees it; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n      Note over GW: Record the write<br/>Activity · task succeeded\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - task #5171 succeeds with `write: true` and the row count\n      %%> - an Activity entry with the connection, your Node, the leading verb, the statement's SHA-256 and the row count\n      %%> - the SQL text is not stored, because it can hold data or secrets\n    else it fails or times out\n      N--xSW: SQL error or no answer\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - the server refuses the statement, or gives no answer within 30 s\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code\n      %%> The Gateway reads:\n      %%> - a bounded error code, without SQL output or the password\n      Note over GW: Mark the task failed<br/>Activity · receipt: failed\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - `database.query_failed` when the server refused it; nothing changed\n      %%> - `database.write_outcome_unknown` after a timeout or a lost connection; the write may have happened\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - Swamp never repeats a write by itself\n      %%> - check the data first after an unknown outcome; a new key runs the statement again\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show #5171\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n      GW-->>CLI: status + row count\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the current status, and the row count once the task has ended\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "database/server-create": {
   "kind": "database_server.create",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, single-swamp-node, DECISIONS R1-R4 (root password by vault key), docs/reference/database-servers.md. Valkey adoption (--process, --connection) removed.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 14, 22 (secrets from the vault on stdin, one bounded timeout wait, docker exec -i login; no script read, report or assert step).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: database:server:create\n  %% kind: database_server.create\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: database:server:create<br/>app-mysql --node=12\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit database:server:create app-mysql --node=12`\n  %%> - syntax: `<slug> --node=NODE [--driver=mysql|pgsql|redis] [--tag=TAG] [--port=PORT] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the slug of the Database server as the argument, lowercase kebab, at most 63 characters\n  %%> - the Node, and optionally the driver, the image tag and the port\n  %%> Defaults:\n  %%> - driver `mysql`; a `redis` server runs Valkey\n  %%> - tag `8.4` for mysql, `18` for pgsql, `8.1` for redis\n  %%> - port `3306`, `5432` or `6379`\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - adopting an existing Valkey Process with `--process` and `--connection`; both adoptions are done, so the option is removed\n  opt --tag\n    Note over GW: Set the image tag\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Replace the selected driver's default image tag.\n  end\n  opt --port\n    Note over GW: Set the private port\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Replace the selected driver's default published port.\n  end\n  Note over GW: Validate, record server<br/>app-mysql: provisioning · #5150\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12\n  %%> - Node 12 is an active Linux Node with Docker and a WireGuard address\n  %%> - no other Database server has the slug, or this is the same request again (then it continues)\n  %%> - no other Process on Node 12 publishes port 3306\n  %%> - no removal of app-mysql is unfinished\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - generates the root password and stores it encrypted on the Database server record\n  %%> - records the Docker Node Process `app-mysql` with image `mysql:8.4` and volume `orbit-app-mysql-data`\n  %%> - takes the lock for app-mysql, so no database change runs on it at the same time\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - Database server app-mysql recorded with status provisioning\n  %%> - task #5150 recorded with the full intent\n  %%> - nothing has happened on the Node yet\n  GW->>SW: Store the root password<br/>vault orbit-control-inputs\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - writes the first-start line `MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=…` under `database-server-create-start-5150`\n  %%> - writes the login check, the root password line and `SELECT 1`, under `database-server-create-login-5150`\n  %%> - for redis: also writes the ACL file, which holds only password hashes, under `database-server-create-intent-5150`\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the password never enters the intent, the run inputs, the Process record, a file or a command argument (R3)\n  %%> - steps read each value whole with `vault.get` and pass it on standard input\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-database-server-create\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - driver, image, port, container name and volume\n  %%> - the private bindings: Node 12's WireGuard address, and its LAN address when it has one\n  %%> - the vault key names of the password and the ACL file, never the values\n  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5150\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>N: Pull the image<br/>cfgmgmt docker_image present\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 from 209 with the official `@swamp/cfgmgmt/docker_image` behavior, `ensure: present`\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - pulls `mysql:8.4`; an image that is already present is kept\n  opt --driver\n    Note over GW: Select the server driver\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - replace default mysql with pgsql or redis; choose that driver's image, port and login check\n    %%> - Valkey writes the ACL file and skips the first-start environment and its removal\n    opt selected driver is redis\n      SW->>N: Write the ACL file<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · 0600\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - writes `users.acl` into the data volume with one line for the user `default` and the SHA-256 hash of the admin password\n      %%> - it holds only hashes\n      %%> Why:\n      %%> - Valkey starts only from this file; without it Valkey refuses to start, so a damaged server stays closed\n      %%> Repair:\n      %%> - on an active Valkey server, the same command rebuilds the file from the records, with every ACL user\n    end\n  end\n  SW->>N: Start the container<br/>stdin env · docker run\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts `app-mysql` with the volume, the health probe and the port bound only on the private addresses, as one `docker run` argv\n  %%> - for mysql and pgsql: `--env-file /dev/stdin` reads `MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD` or `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` from `vault.get`; no file holds it\n  %%> - for pgsql it also reads `POSTGRES_INITDB_ARGS=--auth-local=peer --auth-host=scram-sha-256`: the image's initdb would trust the socket and the loopback for every user\n  %%> - for redis: no environment; Valkey runs with `--aclfile /data/users.acl`\n  %%> - a rerun keeps an existing container and continues from it\n  %%> Never:\n  %%> - binds `0.0.0.0`, an IPv6 wildcard or a public address\n  SW->>N: Wait until healthy<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · timeout 60\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - one bounded command: `timeout 60` around Docker's health status, read until it reports `healthy`\n  %%> - Docker owns the probe, which uses TCP: MySQL and PostgreSQL accept it only after first-start initialization; 60 s without `healthy` fails the step\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - Swamp has no wait primitive, so one bounded command waits (Q-WAIT)\n  SW->>N: Check the admin login<br/>docker exec -i · SELECT 1\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - one `docker exec -i` that reads the root password from the first stdin line inside the container, logs in over TCP and runs `SELECT 1`\n  %%> - for redis: logs in as `default` and sends `PING`\n  %%> - the Gateway checks the answer, `1` or `PONG`, in the run's native result\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - only the final server accepts this login, so the next step never recreates a container that is still initializing\n  SW->>N: Remove the first-start env<br/>recreate · wait again\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - the default MySQL create, or PostgreSQL initialization; skipped for Valkey and an active SQL server retry\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - one bounded command removes `app-mysql` and starts it again without `--env-file`; the data volume keeps the password\n  %%> - waits for Docker to report it healthy again; this is the end check for mysql and pgsql\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the password is absent from the container environment and `docker inspect`\n  alt every step succeeds\n    N-->>SW: healthy, login works\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the container is healthy and the admin login works\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record result<br/>app-mysql: active · task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Database server app-mysql becomes active, and its Process runs\n    %%> - task #5150 succeeds\n    %%> - `database:create --server=app-mysql` can now create databases on it\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or unhealthy\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the pull fails, the container does not become healthy, or the admin login fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code, such as `database.server_start_failed`\n    Note over GW: Mark the server failed<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Database server app-mysql is marked failed, with the failed step\n    %%> - task #5150 fails\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - the container and the volume stay as they are; there is no rollback\n    %%> - that is safe: a rerun reuses the recorded password, Process and volume\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it is a new task and continues from the current state\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5150\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "database/server-destroy": {
   "kind": "database_server.destroy",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, single-swamp-node, DECISIONS R1 (record-only change starts no run), R2, R4, flow-tighten rule 22 (one docker command per step as exec argv; cfgmgmt docker_container stores its env as sensitive output the swamp role cannot write). Same shape as tool.remove.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 6, 14, 17, 22 (remove with one docker rm --force; inspect end check; no report or assert step).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: database:server:destroy\n  %% kind: database_server.destroy\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: database:server:destroy<br/>app-mysql\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit database:server:destroy app-mysql --force`\n  %%> - syntax: `<slug> [--force] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the slug of the Database server as the argument\n  %%> Confirmation:\n  %%> - without `--force`, the CLI asks first; the default is No\n  %%> - a JSON or non-interactive call without `--force` returns `database.confirmation_required`\n  Note over CLI: Confirm destruction<br/>default No\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - ask on a terminal before sending the request; decline cancels\n  Note over GW: Validate, mark removing<br/>app-mysql · task #5160\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs app-mysql\n  %%> - no Database connection points to app-mysql (`database.server_in_use`)\n  %%> - no database create, user change or other task runs on app-mysql (`database.server_busy`)\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - takes the lock for app-mysql and marks it removing, so new databases on it are refused\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5160 recorded with the full intent\n  alt the server has no Process\n    Note over GW: Delete only the record<br/>R1: no run, nothing on a Node\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a create failed before it recorded the Process\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Database server record is deleted; task #5160 succeeds at once\n    GW-->>CLI: Server removed<br/>nothing on Node 12\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the result at once; there is nothing to follow\n  else a Process runs it\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-database-server-destroy\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n    %%> Inputs:\n    %%> - the Process and its container names, including the candidate and original names a create can leave\n    %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n    %%> Not included:\n    %%> - credentials; removal needs no database login\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5160\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the task number, at once\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n    SW->>N: Remove the container<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · docker rm\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209 with `@swamp/ssh` and the `swamp` role's SSH identity; one `docker rm --force` argv\n    %%> - `@swamp/cfgmgmt/docker_container` would store the container env as sensitive output in a vault the `swamp` role cannot write\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - removes the server's container; one that is already gone counts as removed\n    %%> - the database recovers from its volume on a later start, as after a crash\n    %%> Not removed:\n    %%> - the volume `orbit-app-mysql-data`, so the data survives\n    SW->>N: Check it is gone<br/>docker container inspect\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - `docker container inspect` must exit 1, no such container; that is the end check\n    %%> - the behavior's own `check` reports drift but cannot fail a run, so a plain inspect does it\n    %%> Not included:\n    %%> - no report or assert step: a failed step ends the run with its own exit code, mapped to `database.server_remove_failed`\n    alt containers are gone\n      N-->>SW: no container left\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the check passed for every container name\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n      %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n      Note over GW: Delete server + Process<br/>volume stays · task succeeded\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the Database server and Process records are deleted\n      %%> - task #5160 succeeds\n      %%> On the Node:\n      %%> - the volume `orbit-app-mysql-data` stays; remove it by hand when its data must go\n      %%> - a new server with the same slug finds the old volume\n    else a step fails\n      N--xSW: exit ≠ 0\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - SSH fails, or Docker refuses to stop or remove a container\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      %%> The Gateway reads:\n      %%> - which step failed and its error code\n      Note over GW: Mark the server failed<br/>records kept · rerun after a fix\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - Database server app-mysql and its Process stay recorded, marked failed\n      %%> - task #5160 fails\n      %%> On the Node:\n      %%> - containers removed before the failure stay removed; there is no rollback\n      %%> - that is safe: a rerun inspects again and skips containers that are gone\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it is a new task\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show #5160\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "database/server-update": {
   "kind": "database_server.close",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R2, R4, flow-tighten rule 22 and Q-DBAUTH (one valkey-cli call per step, AUTH as the first stdin line from the vault; no rollback). docs/reference/database-servers.md#close-an-adopted-server.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: database:server:update\n  %% kind: database_server.close\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: database:server:update<br/>--require-password\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit database:server:update beast-valkey --require-password`\n  %%> - syntax: `<slug> --require-password [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the slug of an adopted Valkey server\n  %%> - `--require-password` is required; missing or false input is refused\n  Note over GW: Validate, write two inputs<br/>task #5170 · vault\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the server is an active Valkey server\n  %%> - every connection on it has a password (`database.server_in_use`)\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - writes two vault inputs, each an `AUTH default` line with the admin password and the commands of one step\n  %%> - records task #5170 with the host, port and whether `default` is still open; never the password\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-database-server-close\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5170\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  opt default is still open\n    SW->>N: List default clients<br/>exec · valkey-cli\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - one `valkey-cli` call: `AUTH default`, then `CLIENT LIST USER default`\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - `docker run --rm -i --network=host valkey/valkey:8.1 valkey-cli -h <host> -p <port>`, with the vault input on stdin, so the password is never in argv\n    Note over SW: Prove no other client<br/>assert · one line\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the list holds only this call's own connection; another client fails the run with `database.server_in_use`\n    SW->>N: Close default access<br/>exec · valkey-cli\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - one `valkey-cli` call: `AUTH default`, `ACL SETUSER default resetpass #<sha256>`, `ACL SAVE`, `CLIENT KILL USER default SKIPME yes`\n    %%> No rollback:\n    %%> - a failed save leaves the live ACL closed but not saved; a rerun repeats the close\n    Note over SW: Prove the ACL is saved<br/>assert · three OK\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - `AUTH`, `ACL SETUSER` and `ACL SAVE` each answered `OK`\n  end\n  SW->>N: Check default access<br/>exec · valkey-cli PING\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - one `valkey-cli PING` with no password; the end check\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - access only counts as closed when a client without a password gets `NOAUTH`\n  Note over SW: Prove default is closed<br/>assert · NOAUTH\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the `PING` answer starts with `NOAUTH`\n  alt default is closed\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record default closed<br/>task succeeded · vault cleared\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the server's `default_open` is false; both vault inputs are deleted\n  else a step fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step\n    Note over GW: Keep the record<br/>task failed · rerun is safe\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - `database.server_in_use` when another client is logged in as `default`; otherwise `database.server_command_failed`\n    %%> - both vault inputs are deleted\n  end\n"
  },
  "database/update": {
   "kind": "database.update",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1 (record-only update starts no run), R2-R4, 5 (swamp role SSH identity), round 2 bugs (refuse with no server, server admin password by its own vault key, cleanup on pass or fail).\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rule 22 (one docker exec -i read; no copied scripts or cleanup).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: database:update\n  %% kind: database.update\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Node 12<br/>services-valkey\n  CLI->>GW: database:update cache\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit database:update cache --database=3`\n  %%> - syntax: `<slug> [--driver=DRIVER] [--node=NODE] [--host=HOST] [--port=PORT] [--database=NAME] [--path=PATH] [--username=USER] [--password=SECRET] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the connection slug, and at least one field to replace\n  %%> - `--node=` with an empty value clears the Node association\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - the keys already stored on attached Instances; run `instance:database:add` again, then `env:sync`\n  Note over CLI: Require a field option\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - slug alone returns `database.update_required` before a request; supply at least one field below\n  opt --driver\n    Note over GW: Change driver\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Replace the driver and normalize its connection fields.\n  end\n  opt --node\n    Note over GW: Change Node association\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Resolve the Node, or clear it with an empty value; SQLite must stay on every attached Instance's Node.\n  end\n  opt --host\n    Note over GW: Change the host\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Replace the recorded hostname or IP.\n  end\n  opt --port\n    Note over GW: Change the port\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Replace the recorded TCP port.\n  end\n  opt --path\n    Note over GW: Change the SQLite path\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Replace the recorded absolute path.\n  end\n  opt --username\n    Note over GW: Change the username\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Replace the recorded username.\n  end\n  opt --password\n    Note over GW: Change the password\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Encrypt and replace the recorded password.\n  end\n  Note over GW: Validate the change<br/>connection cache\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the fields fit the driver (`validation.failed`)\n  %%> - a managed connection keeps its identity and credentials (`database.server_connection_managed`); on a Valkey server only the index may change\n  %%> - a SQLite connection stays on the same Node as every attached Instance\n  %%> Decides:\n  %%> - a change that needs a live check runs on its Database server's Node only: today, a new index on a managed Valkey server\n  %%> - every other change is record-only\n  %%> Refuses:\n  %%> - a run never targets the Gateway Node\n  opt fields need no live check\n    Note over GW: Write the change<br/>R1: no run · DatabaseUpdated\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - any change that needs no live check, for example a new password on a registered external database\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - connection `cache` is updated, its password encrypted\n    %%> - record a succeeded task without a Swamp run (R1); nothing changes on any Node\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>succeeded task · no run\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the succeeded task at once; there is no run to follow\n  end\n  opt --database\n    Note over GW: Select the database field\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - replace the database name or redis index; an external connection or unchanged or cleared managed index uses the record-only path\n    opt new managed Valkey index\n      Note over GW: Hold the change<br/>task #5122 · lock services-valkey\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the change is recorded in task #5122; connection `cache` keeps its current values\n      %%> - no other change runs on `services-valkey`; the Gateway holds one lock per Database server\n      GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>…-database-connection-update\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - starts one Swamp run of `operation-database-connection-update` on serve (Node 209)\n      %%> Inputs:\n      %%> - the requested index 3, and the server's WireGuard address and port\n      %%> - the vault key name `database-update-intent-5122`, which holds the `AUTH` line and the command; the admin password itself never enters the intent\n      %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n      GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5122\n      %%> What you get:\n      %%> - the task number, at once\n      %%> Next:\n      %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n      SW->>N: Check the index fits<br/>docker exec -i · CONFIG GET\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - reads the live server's database count; the Gateway applies index 3 only when it is below that count\n      %%> How:\n      %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209 with `@swamp/ssh`, the `swamp` role's SSH identity; nothing is copied to the Node\n      %%> - one `docker exec -i … valkey-cli` with `AUTH` from `vault.get` as the first stdin line, then `CONFIG GET databases`\n      %%> Not included:\n      %%> - no write on the server; the index is a client setting\n      alt index fits\n        N-->>SW: 16 databases\n        %%> Returns:\n        %%> - the server's database count; index 3 fits\n        SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n        %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n        %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n        Note over GW: Apply the change<br/>index 3 · task succeeded\n        %%> Result:\n        %%> - connection `cache` records index 3\n        %%> - task #5122 succeeds\n      else index too high or check fails\n        N--xSW: out of range or exit ≠ 0\n        %%> When:\n        %%> - index 3 is not below the server's count, or SSH or the Valkey login fails\n        SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n        %%> The Gateway reads:\n        %%> - which step failed and its error code, for example `database.index_invalid`\n        Note over GW: Keep the connection<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix\n        %%> Result:\n        %%> - connection `cache` keeps its old index\n        %%> - task #5122 fails, with the change it held\n        %%> On the Node:\n        %%> - nothing changed; the check only reads\n        %%> Retry:\n        %%> - run the command again with a valid index; it is a new task\n      end\n      loop every 2 s until terminal\n        CLI->>GW: operation:show #5122\n        %%> What it does:\n        %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n        GW-->>CLI: status + result\n        %%> Returns:\n        %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n      end\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "database/user-create": {
   "kind": "database_user.create",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4, 5 (swamp role SSH identity), round 2 bugs (server admin password by its own vault key, cleanup on pass or fail). docs/reference/database-servers.md#add-a-user.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 6, 22 (WAL read before any change; login and grants in one SQL batch on stdin; the user reads back its grants; no copied scripts or cleanup).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: database:user:create\n  %% kind: database_user.create\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Node 12<br/>beast-mysql server\n  CLI->>GW: database:user:create shop<br/>--username=reporting\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `printf '%s' \"$PASSWORD\" | orbit database:user:create shop --username=reporting`\n  %%> - syntax: `<slug> --username=USER [--read-only] [--replication] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the connection slug of a database on a MySQL or PostgreSQL server\n  %%> - the username: a 1-32 character identifier\n  %%> - the password from piped standard input, or a hidden `Password` prompt on a terminal; never an argument\n  %%> Grants:\n  %%> - `--read-only`: `SELECT` on the database\n  %%> - none: all database privileges on MySQL, data-write grants on PostgreSQL\n  %%> - `--replication`: PostgreSQL only, `LOGIN REPLICATION` with read-only grants\n  Note over GW: Validate, check conflicts<br/>task #5123 · lock beast-mysql\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12\n  %%> - connection `shop` has a Database server (`database.server_required`)\n  %%> - the server is MySQL or PostgreSQL (`database.driver_unsupported`); on Valkey each user is its own connection\n  %%> - `reporting` is not the server's admin login or a system role, such as `root`, `postgres` or `pg_*` (`database.user_reserved`)\n  %%> - `reporting` is not the owner of a connection on the server, nor a user another connection recorded (`database.name_conflict`)\n  %%> - no database removal runs on `beast-mysql`; the Gateway holds one lock per Database server\n  %%> - no other user create runs on `beast-mysql` (`operation.target_busy`), so two creates of one new name never pass the checks together\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5123 recorded with the database, owner, username and grant policy\n  %%> - the password stored encrypted; nothing has happened on the Node yet\n  opt --read-only\n    Note over GW: Select reader grants\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - Replace default write grants with reader grants in the apply batch; the end check verifies them.\n  end\n  GW->>SW: Store the batches<br/>database-user-create-apply-5123\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - puts three whole values into the Swamp vault on Node 209 (R3)\n  %%> - `database-user-create-wal-5123`: the admin password line, then `SHOW wal_level`\n  %%> - `database-user-create-apply-5123`: the admin password line, then the batch with the user's password (a SCRAM verifier on PostgreSQL)\n  %%> - `database-user-create-check-5123`: the user's password line, then the check\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-database-user-create\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - the driver, database, owner, username, grant policy and replication flag\n  %%> - the vault key names `database-user-create-wal-5123`, `database-user-create-apply-5123` and `database-user-create-check-5123`\n  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5123\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  opt --replication\n    Note over GW: Select replication grants\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - PostgreSQL only: set LOGIN REPLICATION and reader grants, including owner defaults for future public tables.\n    %%> - Require logical WAL before applying the batch; without the flag, PostgreSQL gets NOREPLICATION.\n    SW->>N: Check logical WAL<br/>docker exec -i · SHOW\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209 with `@swamp/ssh` and the `swamp` role's SSH identity; nothing is copied to the Node\n    %%> - one `docker exec -i … psql`; the first stdin line is the admin password, then `SHOW wal_level`\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - reads the live WAL level before any change\n    %%> Refuses:\n    %%> - another level than `logical`: the next steps skip, and the Gateway fails the task with `database.wal_level_not_logical`; nothing changes\n    %%> Not included:\n    %%> - Orbit never changes the server configuration\n  end\n  SW->>N: Apply login and grants<br/>docker exec -i · SQL on stdin\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209 with `@swamp/ssh` and the `swamp` role's SSH identity; nothing is copied to the Node\n  %%> - one `docker exec -i`; inside the container the first stdin line is the admin password, read into the client's password variable, then the batch; never in argv or a file\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - creates the login, or sets the password of a user Orbit recorded before\n  %%> - PostgreSQL: adds or removes the replication attribute\n  %%> - replaces the user's privileges with exactly the requested ones, in the same batch\n  %%> Refuses:\n  %%> - an existing role or account (MySQL: any host) that this connection did not record (`database.name_conflict`); the batch stops before any change and prints the code for the Gateway\n  %%> - MySQL: escapes underscores, so the grant covers only `shop`\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - Orbit never changes the server configuration or creates publications\n  SW->>N: Check the user<br/>docker exec -i · login, grants\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the user logs in over TCP with its own password, from the first stdin line, and reads back its name, attributes and grants\n  %%> - MySQL: `SHOW GRANTS` lists exactly the requested grant on its database\n  %%> - PostgreSQL: the replication attribute, connect and schema use, and the owner's default table privileges for the user\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the user only counts as created when the server shows it; the Gateway compares the answer in the native result\n  alt every step passes\n    N-->>SW: login and grants match\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the check passed\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record the user<br/>grants · task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - user `reporting` recorded for `shop`, with its privileges and encrypted password\n    %%> - `database:user:list shop` shows it; `vault:sync` can publish its password to Vaultwarden\n    %%> - task #5123 succeeds\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: conflict or exit ≠ 0\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the wrong WAL level, a name conflict, failed SQL, other grants, or a lost SSH connection\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code\n    Note over GW: Keep the records<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the user records stay as before; a new user is not recorded\n    %%> - task #5123 fails\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - a login created before the failure stays; there is no rollback\n    %%> - that is safe: a rerun sets the password and replaces the grants again\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it is a new task\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5123\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "doctor/doctor": {
   "kind": "doctor.inspect",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: Nick 2026-11-01, liveness 2026-11-02. Only the Node agent collects facts; doctor reads Gateway state. No Swamp run, no SSH (R1).\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: doctor\n  %% kind: doctor.inspect\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane A icon=server pill=observer tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant A as Node agent<br/>orbit-agent\n  loop on a short interval\n    A->>GW: Heartbeat\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Gateway marks the Node online\n    %%> - when heartbeats stop, it marks the Node offline, shown in the web dashboard and in node:list and node:show\n  end\n  loop continuously, on change\n    Note over A: Observe Node state<br/>disk, services, UFW, packages\n    A->>GW: Report observations<br/>with observed_at\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - at start, on change, and on recurring confirmation\n    %%> - a report the Gateway does not accept is sent again after 1, 2 and 4 seconds, and then every 4 seconds until it is accepted\n    %%> - the same holds for the request that asks what to watch\n    %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06, research thread b8654275):\n    %%> - a Node that boots before the Gateway answers reported only at its next read, up to a minute later; with a prompt report Doctor was healthy 15.8 s after boot\n    Note over GW: Store latest facts<br/>per Node, per check\n  end\n  CLI->>GW: doctor\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit doctor`\n  %%> - syntax: `doctor [--node=ID] [--family=FAMILY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - without filters, checks all accessible registered Nodes and all families\n  Note over GW: Compare facts with intent<br/>exact, drift, missing\n  %%> Doctor always answers from the Gateway's state:\n  %%> - it never starts a run and never SSHes into the Node\n  %%> - compare each managed package with its active Tool coordinate or constraint; stale or missing inventory is unverifiable\n  alt agent online\n    GW-->>CLI: Report<br/>agent online · findings\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - first line: agent online\n    %%> - then the findings; exit 0 with no drift, 1 with drift\n  else agent offline\n    GW-->>CLI: Report<br/>agent offline since 12:04\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - first line: agent offline since <time>\n    %%> - the findings still show, each marked \"possibly outdated, agent silent since <time>\"\n    %%> - a nudge to look at the Node agent first\n  end\n"
  },
  "env/import": {
   "kind": "instance.env_import",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: env:import\n  %% kind: instance.env_import\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP\n  participant GW as Gateway\n  participant SW as Swamp\n  participant N as Instance Node\n  CLI->>GW: env:import --instance=42\n  Note over GW: Validate, lock Instance\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Task accepted\n  SW->>N: Stage .env for the copy\n  %%> Refuse a missing, linked, foreign-owned or oversized file.\n  SW->>N: Copy .env to 209\n  SW->>N: Remove the staged copy\n  Note over SW: Read .env into the vault\n  Note over SW: Remove the local copy\n  %%> Also after a failed copy or vault write.\n  alt read and stored\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>size · sha256\n    GW->>SW: Read the vault key once\n    Note over GW: Parse, check, store\n    GW->>SW: Delete the vault key\n    GW-->>CLI: Operation succeeded\n  else read or import fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed\n    Note over GW: Store nothing<br/>delete the vault key\n    GW--xCLI: Operation failed\n  end\n"
  },
  "env/sync": {
   "kind": "instance.sync_environment",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, R1-R4, decision swamp-vault-secrets (vault.get), orbit-gateway-renders-create-env (Gateway owns .env, no read-back), DECISIONS instance.sync_environment (vault after commit, @swamp/ssh from 209, no private-material fetch, no per-Node worker). docs/reference/environment-variables.md.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 14, 22 (cfgmgmt file for both files, its check as the end check; no script read, foreign-key check, report or assert step).\n  %% Decided: Nick 2026-11-02, foreign-key protection at admission from the key names the Node agent reports; .env.testing only for an owned MySQL/PostgreSQL database, in the same file step.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% Decided: Nick 2026-11-03, option 1: Orbit writes a managed .env as 0640 with group orbit-env, the agent's unit holds that group and reports key names only; a .env the agent can't read is not observed.\n  %% command: env:sync\n  %% kind: instance.sync_environment\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Instance Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  alt Confirmed AppProd container\n    CLI->>GW: env:sync<br/>Instance 42\n    GW->>SW: Start environment sync<br/>private Operation input\n    Note over SW: Check the environment<br/>owner\n    SW->>N: Apply the live environment\n    N-->>SW: Release and changes<br/>Restarted Processes\n    SW-->>GW: Record the result\n  else Host or development Instance\n  CLI->>GW: env:sync --instance=42\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit env:sync --instance=42`\n  %%> - syntax: `env:sync --instance=SELECTOR [--force] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Instance: a numeric Instance ID or the exact domain of its Route\n  %%> - `--force` overwrites keys that only the Node's `.env` holds\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, render .env<br/>Instance 42 · task #5131\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, the Node of Instance 42\n  %%> - Instance 42 is active, on a Node with exactly one active `app-dev` or `app-prod` role\n  %%> - Instance 42 has stored configuration (`env.configuration_missing` otherwise)\n  %%> - a stored `{{instance.domain}}` needs a settled Route (`env.reference_unavailable` otherwise)\n  %%> - no other task holds Instance 42; the Gateway holds one lock per Instance and waits briefly for a busy one\n  %%> Checks foreign keys:\n  %%> - compares the `.env` key names the Node agent reported with the stored and owned keys; no values are read\n  %%> - Orbit writes a managed `.env` as 0640 with group `orbit-env`, which the agent's unit holds\n  %%> - a `.env` the agent can't read, such as a hand-made 0600 file or a link, or a silent agent, leaves the names not observed\n  %%> - when the agent reports that Git tracks `.env.testing`, the plan writes none\n  %%> Renders .env:\n  %%> - every stored key in sorted order, as a quoted value\n  %%> - `{{instance.domain}}` from the Route, `{{instance.environment}}` from the Node role\n  %%> Renders .env.testing:\n  %%> - only when Instance 42 owns a database on a MySQL or PostgreSQL server\n  %%> - the same `.env` with `APP_ENV=testing` and the test database credentials\n  %%> - SQLite: none; `phpunit.xml` (`:memory:`) decides\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5131 recorded with the full intent and the sha256 of each rendered file\n  %%> - nothing has happened on the Node yet\n  opt keys not observed, or .env holds foreign keys\n    GW--xCLI: Refused<br/>keys not observed or foreign\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - `env.keys_not_observed`: the Node agent has not reported the `.env` key names, and `--force` is not set\n    %%> - `env.sync_would_drop_keys`: the Node's `.env` holds keys that Orbit neither stores nor wrote, and `--force` is not set\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the refusal names the keys; no task and no run start\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - foreign keys: `env:import` adopts them first, or `env:sync --force` replaces the file\n    %%> - not observed, as for a `.env` written 0600 before this mode: `env:import`, then `env:sync --force`, which drops nothing and writes the file 0640\n  end\n  GW->>SW: Push .env to the vault<br/>vault: env-5131, env-testing-5131\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - `.env` holds secrets, which travel only through the vault (R3)\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - after the task commits, pushes the rendered files into Swamp's vault on Node 209\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the run reads them with `vault.get`; no step calls the Gateway back\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-environment\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  %%> - the account, the application directory, the file paths and the expected sha256 of each file\n  %%> - the key names Orbit wrote last time, which it may remove\n  %%> - production only: the release path and the Processes with `restart_on_deploy`\n  %%> - secrets only as vault key names\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5131\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>N: Create the orbit-env group<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/group\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - creates the system group when the Node lacks it; the Node agent's unit holds it\n  SW->>N: Write .env, .env.testing<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · 0640\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209 with `@swamp/ssh` and the official `@swamp/cfgmgmt/file` behavior; nothing is copied to the Node\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - writes the rendered `.env` from `vault.get env-5131`, mode `0640`\n  %%> - with an owned MySQL or PostgreSQL database, writes `.env.testing` in the same step, replacing its content; no merge\n  %%> - a Git-tracked `.env.testing` stays untouched: admission planned none\n  %%> - leaves a file that already matches in place\n  %%> Runs as:\n  %%> - the Instance account, in its own directory, so no root write follows a link the account placed\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - renders nothing; the Gateway already rendered the files\n  SW->>N: Set the group orbit-env<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · chgrp -h\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - root runs `chgrp -h orbit-env` on the written files; `-h` never follows a link\n  %%> - the Node agent's unit holds `orbit-env`, so it may read the `.env` key names; it never reads `.env.testing`\n  Note over SW: Check the written file<br/>assert · file state\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - compares the sha256 the file behavior recorded for each file with the rendered one\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the sync only counts when the Node holds exactly what the Gateway rendered\n  %%> - a rerun writes and checks again\n  opt production Instance\n    SW->>N: Refresh the runtime<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · in order\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - on every production sync, also when `.env` did not change\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - runs `php artisan config:cache` as the Instance account in the release, as one `@swamp/ssh exec`, when the release has a config cache; an uncached release stays uncached\n    %%> - reloads the PHP-FPM pool to reset OPcache\n    %%> - restarts the running Processes that have `restart_on_deploy`\n    %%> - each action is one `@swamp/ssh exec` command, one at a time\n    %%> Not included:\n    %%> - development Instances: they only get the file\n  end\n  alt every file matches\n    N-->>SW: files match, refreshed\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - whether each file changed, and the runtime refresh status\n    %%> Not returned:\n    %%> - no values and no file content\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event · changed flags\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event and reads the run's outputs; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record result<br/>owned keys · task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the synced key names become the keys Orbit owns on Instance 42\n    %%> - task #5131 succeeds with `changed`, the testing status and the refresh status\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0<br/>file write or check fails\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - SSH fails, a write fails, or a file does not match after the write\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the failing step ends the run with its own exit code; there is no report step\n    %%> - production: the config cache or the Process restart fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code\n    Note over GW: Keep stored values<br/>task failed · rerun is safe\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - stored values and the owned-key list stay as they were\n    %%> - task #5131 fails\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - a refused file stays unchanged; a failed cache build keeps the old cache\n    %%> - a file already written stays; there is no rollback\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - fix the cause, then run `env:sync` again; it is a new task\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5131\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n  end\n"
  },
  "env/connection-environment": {
   "kind": "instance.connection_environment",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% env:sync on a container Instance: Orbit never renders an existing container .env again, so it writes only the attached connections' keys and the keys env:update stored since the last sync, in place.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: env:sync\n  %% kind: instance.connection_environment\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>Instance container\n  CLI->>GW: env:sync --instance=24\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit env:sync --instance=24`; Instance 24 runs in a container\n  Note over GW: Admit the connection keys<br/>key names · paths · units\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - a running container, active, with no unfinished lifecycle work; hold its Instance lock\n  %%> - key names of every attached connection, of the websocket app, and of each key that `env:update` stored since the last sync; a connection's own line wins\n  %%> - each path's ACL egress rule, bridge endpoint and UFW rule, the private names, and the Instance's running units\n  %%> - with no key to write, the run still starts: it withdraws a removed websocket app's path\n  %%> On failure:\n  %%> - a stopped container or busy work start no run\n  GW->>SW: Push lines to the vault<br/>env-5140\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the rendered lines hold the password; only the vault key name goes in the run input (R3)\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>connection-environment\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID\n  SW->>N: Write the connection keys<br/>Ansible · tags environment\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - adds the missing database egress rules, bridge endpoints and UFW rules; removes none of them\n  %%> - reconciles the websocket path and a clone's own-site rules by description: keeps only the rules and the UFW rule the plan names, writes the plan's private names into `/etc/hosts`\n  %%> - replaces each key's line in `.env`, adds a missing key at the end; a `${...}` value stays and is named\n  %%> - every other byte stays; the lines reach the Node script on stdin, never argv or a log\n  %%> - a changed file clears Laravel's config cache and try-restarts the Instance's running units\n  alt keys written\n    %% path: main\n    N-->>SW: Key names per outcome<br/>written · added · kept · unchanged\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the written keys become Orbit-owned keys of Instance 24\n    %%> - each key `env:update` stored leaves the wait list: the next sync writes it no more, so a later hand edit stays\n  else file refused or phase failed\n    %% path: failed\n    N--xSW: env.configuration_invalid\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - a missing file, a link, or a quoted value that never closes leaves the file as it was; paths already added stay; a rerun is safe\n    %%> - the keys `env:update` stored stay on the wait list for the next sync\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error code\n  end\n  loop until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: Status and result\n  end\n"
  },
  "firewall/allow": {
   "kind": "firewall.allow",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: docs/cli/firewall/index.mdx; ADR 0244 and ADR 0245 (rules, admission); ADR 0271 and docs/reference/gateway-engine.md (execution).\n  %% Engine flow: the kind runs on the Gateway state machine when ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS lists it and the target Node's executor reported `ufw.ensure_rule`; otherwise it still runs the Swamp workflow operation-firewall-direct, which goes once every Node reports.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: firewall:allow\n  %% kind: firewall.allow\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=executes tone=blue\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>state machine\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>agent executor\n  CLI->>GW: firewall:allow PORT<br/>--node=NODE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit firewall:allow PORT --node=NODE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<port> --node=ID [--from=SOURCE] [--protocol=tcp|udp] [--label=TEXT]`\n  Note over GW: Validate, record the rule\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target (`node_access.required`); the target is Linux (`firewall.platform_unsupported`)\n  %%> - the port, source, protocol and label pass validation; admission refusals create no rule or Operation and change nothing on the Node\n  %%> - no other firewall change holds the target (`operation.target_busy`); one lock per Node, including the protected-access skip\n  alt repeats protected access\n    Note over GW: Skipped — already applied<br/>firewall.rule_exists\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the ports lie inside one protected port or range Orbit opens, and the source is one that entry admits\n    %%> - a range extending beyond that entry or a source it does not admit takes the operator-rule path\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - records a succeeded Operation without an operator rule, order or Node contact; this branch uses recorded intent, not a UFW check\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>succeeded Operation\n  else operator rule\n    Note over GW: Record step, start event<br/>ensure-0 · orbit.engine.start\n    %%> Requirements:\n    %%> - an opposite action for the same port, protocol and normalized source is refused (`firewall.rule_conflict`)\n    %%> - a both-protocol rule beside a single-protocol rule on the same port and source is refused, for either action (`firewall.rule_overlap`)\n    %%> Allowed:\n    %%> - different source scopes may overlap; narrower sources take precedence regardless of action\n    %%> - a repeat reuses the identity record and checks the Node in a new Operation; a label-only repeat updates the recorded label without rewriting correct UFW comments\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - records the new or reused rule as provisioning, the full non-secret intent, and the engine step `ensure-0`\n    %%> - publishes the start event on JetStream; the state machine takes it from there; nothing has happened on the Node yet\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID\n    GW->>N: Send the signed order<br/>ufw.ensure_rule · NATS\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - signs one order for the target Node: operation `<id>:ensure-0`, target the Node itself, so its firewall changes run one at a time\n    %%> - publishes it to the Node's durable consumer on the ORDERS stream, over NATS on WireGuard\n    %%> Inputs:\n    %%> - `action`, `port`, `protocol`, `source`, `comment`, `ensure: present`, `retain`\n    %%> Deadline:\n    %%> - a JetStream schedule wakes the step after 10 s; with no receipt by then, a new order goes out; after 120 s the step fails with `engine.step_budget_exceeded`\n    %%> - an order older than its 8 s expiry is refused as `expired`, and the Gateway sends a new one\n    %%> Why the engine:\n    %%> - measured on the nested fleet: 0.55–0.67 s per Operation, against 2.8–3.7 s on Swamp; a repeat takes 62 ms on the Node\n    Note over N: Read the rules<br/>ufw status numbered\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the executor checks the order's signature, then reads numbered UFW rows to plan insert positions and detect drift on a repeat\n    %%> - inactive UFW fails with `firewall.backend_inactive` before mutation; never enables the firewall\n    Note over N: Plan the changes\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - plans missing lines and moves misplaced lines; leaves identical, well-placed lines and their comments untouched\n    %%> - only overlapping ports, protocols and source networks can make an existing line misplaced; unrelated managed trust does not force a move\n    %%> - managed allows, including interface- and destination-scoped trust, do not force an existing allow to move: they cannot change its verdict; an overlapping deny still requires a move\n    %%> - places new operator lines above Orbit's broad managed rules and narrower sources above broader ones; each address family is ordered separately\n    %%> - plans one allow per selected protocol and applicable address family; a port range stays a range\n    %%> - numbered positions account for earlier planned changes\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the typed primitive `ufw.ensure_rule` in the executor (crate `firewall`, a port of the former Swamp planner)\n    alt changes needed\n      Note over N: Change the rules<br/>one ufw argv per change\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - runs the planned ufw commands one at a time; moves a misplaced line by deleting and reinserting it; preserves unrelated rules\n      Note over N: Read the result<br/>ufw status numbered\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - collects the final rows for the one end-state check\n    else already applied\n      Note over N: Skipped — already applied<br/>firewall.rule_exists\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - no mutation or second UFW read; the check uses the first read and must pass before a successful skip is reported\n    end\n    Note over N: Check the rules\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - compares every expected line and its narrow-first position\n    %%> Failure:\n    %%> - a missing or misplaced line fails with `firewall.verify_failed`\n    alt rules present and ordered\n      N-->>GW: Receipt: applied<br/>changes N\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the receipt goes to the RECEIPTS stream; the order is acknowledged only after JetStream stored it\n      %%> - the state machine marks the step done and calls the kind's projection: the rule becomes active and the Operation succeeds\n      %%> - `changes: 0` reports `Skipped — already applied (firewall.rule_exists)` in the successful result\n    else read, apply or check fails\n      N--xGW: Receipt: failed<br/>error_code\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the rule stays visible as failed and the Operation fails with `failed_step: ensure-0`; the rule records the `error_code` for `firewall:list`\n      %%> On the Node:\n      %%> - previously added lines remain, and a line deleted for reordering may be absent; no rollback\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - repeat after fixing the cause; reuse the record, keep correct lines and repair missing or misplaced ones without duplicates\n    end\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show OPERATION\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the Operation's status until it has ended; the steps list shows `ensure-0`\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the Operation has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "firewall/deny": {
   "kind": "firewall.deny",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, ADR 0241 allowlists, DECISIONS R1-R4 (rules, admission); ADR 0271 and docs/reference/gateway-engine.md (execution). Same shape as firewall.allow.\n  %% Engine flow: the kind runs on the Gateway state machine when ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS lists it and the target Node's executor reported `ufw.ensure_rule`; otherwise it still runs the Swamp workflow operation-firewall-direct, which goes once every Node reports.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: firewall:deny\n  %% kind: firewall.deny\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=executes tone=blue\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>state machine\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>agent executor\n  CLI->>GW: firewall:deny PORT<br/>--node=NODE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit firewall:deny PORT --node=NODE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<port> --node=ID [--from=SOURCE] [--protocol=tcp|udp] [--label=TEXT]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the destination port or port range\n  %%> - the Node, and optionally the source address and the protocol\n  %%> - optionally a label or short description\n  %%> Not accepted:\n  %%> - service names such as `smtp` as the port\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, record the rule\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target Node\n  %%> - the target is an active Linux Node\n  %%> - the port and source are valid\n  %%> - the port or range includes no protected port, with any `--from`: SSH 22, the recovery SSH port, the WireGuard port or a port one of the target's roles needs (`firewall.protected_port`), so a deny can never cut the Node off\n  %%> - no opposite rule for the same port, protocol and source; an allow and a deny for exactly the same scope is refused (`firewall.rule_conflict`)\n  %%> - no other firewall change runs on the target; the Gateway holds one lock per Node\n  %%> Allowed:\n  %%> - repeat an active or failed deny rule: reuse its record and check the Node in a new Operation\n  %%> - overlapping rules with a different scope, such as a broad allow with a deny for one source\n  Note over GW: Record step, start event<br/>ensure-0 · orbit.engine.start\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the new or reused rule is recorded with status provisioning, with the full intent and the engine step `ensure-0`\n  %%> - the start event goes to JetStream; the state machine takes it from there\n  %%> - nothing has happened on the Node yet\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the Operation ID, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the Operation with `operation:show`\n  GW->>N: Send the signed order<br/>ufw.ensure_rule · NATS\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - signs one order for the target Node: operation `<id>:ensure-0`, target the Node itself, so its firewall changes run one at a time\n  %%> - publishes it to the Node's durable consumer on the ORDERS stream, over NATS on WireGuard\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - `action: deny`, `port`, `protocol`, `source`, `comment`, `ensure: present`, `retain`\n  %%> Deadline:\n  %%> - a JetStream schedule wakes the step after 10 s; with no receipt by then, a new order goes out; after 120 s the step fails with `engine.step_budget_exceeded`\n  %%> - an order older than its 8 s expiry is refused as `expired`, and the Gateway sends a new one\n  Note over N: Read the rules<br/>ufw status numbered\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - the executor checks the order's signature, then reads ufw's numbered rows\n  %%> - refuses an inactive firewall (`firewall.backend_inactive`) before any change\n  Note over N: Plan the changes\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - turns ufw's numbered rows into the ufw commands this rule needs, in order\n  %%> - an insert position counts the rows as they will be after the earlier commands\n  %%> - plans no changes when every expected line already exists in the correct position\n  %%> Order:\n  %%> - narrower rules go above broader ones, allow or deny, so an exception such as one IP wins over a rule for anywhere\n  %%> - ufw stops at the first match, so Orbit sets this order on every change\n  %%> - all operator rules sit above Orbit's own broad rules, such as WireGuard peer trust, so a deny on a port also applies to WireGuard peers\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - the typed primitive `ufw.ensure_rule` in the executor (crate `firewall`, a port of the former Swamp planner)\n  alt changes needed\n    Note over N: Change the rules<br/>one ufw argv per change\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - adds one ufw deny line per port, protocol and family, commented with its label\n    %%> - inserts each line at the position the plan names; an existing identical, well-placed line is left as it is\n    %%> - moves a line that sits in the wrong place: deletes it, then inserts it again\n    %%> - runs the planned commands one at a time\n    Note over N: Read the result<br/>ufw status numbered\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the rule only counts as done when ufw shows it after the changes\n  else already applied\n    Note over N: Skipped — already applied<br/>firewall.rule_exists\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the plan contains no changes\n    %%> - there is no second read of ufw: the check runs on the first read, which nothing has changed since\n  end\n  Note over N: Check the rules\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - checks every expected line is present, in narrow-first order\n  %%> - after changes, a line that ufw changed in between fails the check, and a rerun repairs it\n  alt rules present and ordered\n    N-->>GW: Receipt: applied<br/>changes N\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - the receipt goes to the RECEIPTS stream; the order is acknowledged only after JetStream stored it\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the rule becomes active and the Operation succeeds, through the kind's projection\n    %%> - `changes: 0` reports `Skipped — already applied (firewall.rule_exists)`, part of the successful result, not an error\n  else adding or checking fails\n    N--xGW: Receipt: failed<br/>error_code\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - ufw refuses the line, or a line is missing after the add (`firewall.verify_failed`)\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the rule is marked failed with the Operation's `error_code` and `failed_step: ensure-0`, so `firewall:list` shows the failure\n    %%> - the Operation fails\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - lines added before the failure stay; there is no rollback\n    %%> - that is safe: the rule is marked failed, and a rerun skips lines that already exist\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it is a new Operation\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show OPERATION\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the Operation's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the Operation has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "firewall/list": {
   "kind": "firewall.list",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: Nick 2026-11-05. firewall:list is the one read of firewall rules; it absorbs the three API reads that only the web app and MCP used (fleet list, managed list, live list) as flags. It reads Gateway state only: no Swamp run, no SSH (R1).\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: firewall:list\n  %% kind: firewall.list\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane A icon=server pill=observer tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · web app\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant A as Node agent<br/>orbit-agent\n  loop every 60 s, sent on change\n    A->>GW: Report ufw<br/>with observed_at\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the agent reads the UFW rules files on its 60 s host tick and sends them only when they changed, plus a full resend every 10 minutes; it does not listen for file changes\n    %%> - so a new rule can take up to 60 s to show in `--live`\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Gateway stores the latest `ufw` fact per Node; the agent heartbeat says whether the fact is current\n  end\n  CLI->>GW: List the rules<br/>firewall:list\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit firewall:list [--node=ID] [--managed] [--live] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - without flags: the operator rules of every Node the caller may address, in one response (replaces the fleet list `GET /firewall-rules`)\n  %%> Refuses:\n  %%> - `firewall.node_id_invalid`: a `--node` that is not a positive integer\n  %%> - `node_access.required`: a Node the calling Node has no access grant to\n  Note over GW: Read the recorded rules<br/>per Node, in UFW order\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - reads the operator rules Orbit recorded: port, protocol, source, action, label, status\n  %%> - in the order UFW checks them, so the first matching row wins\n  %%> - never reads the Node; works while the Node is unreachable\n  opt --node=ID\n    %% path: node\n    Note over GW: One Node only<br/>the per-Node list\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the rules of that one Node, as `firewall:list --node=ID` today\n  end\n  opt --managed\n    %% path: managed\n    Note over GW: Add Orbit's own rules<br/>orbit: · locked rows\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - adds the `orbit:` rules Orbit intends on each Node: WireGuard member trust, public SSH recovery while no active role closed it, each active role's rules, the Metrics rules; each names its role\n    %%> - shown as locked rows; no command changes them (replaces `GET /nodes/{node}/managed-firewall-rules`)\n  end\n  opt --live\n    %% path: live\n    Note over GW: Compare with the agent<br/>exact · drift · missing\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - matches the agent's last `ufw` fact against the intended rules: each live rule is `exact`, `drift` or `unmanaged`, and an intended rule the Node lacks is `missing` (replaces `GET /nodes/{node}/live-firewall-rules`)\n    %%> - exact tuples win before label-based drift; a label reused on another live line cannot make an exact operator rule drift\n    %%> - shows the backend status (`active`, `inactive`, `absent`) and the fact's `observed_at`\n    %%> - `unverifiable` until the agent reports UFW, or for a rule Orbit cannot compare\n    %%> - with `--managed`, the managed rules are matched too\n    %%> - exit 0 even with drift: this is a list; Doctor is the check that fails on drift\n  end\n  alt agents online, or no --live\n    %% path: agents-online\n    GW-->>CLI: Rules<br/>table or JSON\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - columns PORT, PROTOCOL, SOURCE, ACTION, LABEL, STATUS; with `--live` also MATCH; with several Nodes also NODE\n    %%> - a failed rule has the failed Operation's `failed_step` and `error_code` in JSON, including a native change that fails before verification; a retry clears both fields while provisioning\n  else --live and an agent offline\n    %% path: agent-offline\n    GW-->>CLI: Rules<br/>some possibly outdated\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - per Node: a Node whose agent is silent gets the line \"agent offline since <time>\", and its live matches are marked \"possibly outdated\", as Doctor does; the other Nodes show as normal\n    %%> - exit 0\n  end\n  %%> Not built:\n  %%> - the flags and the Node-less default; today the CLI has only `--node`, and the three other reads exist only as API endpoints\n  %%> - one API read with the same options; the web app moves to it, and the three endpoints go\n"
  },
  "firewall/remove": {
   "kind": "firewall.remove",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, ADR 0241 allowlists, DECISIONS R1-R4 (rules, admission); ADR 0271 and docs/reference/gateway-engine.md (execution). Same shape as firewall.allow.\n  %% Engine flow: the kind runs on the Gateway state machine when ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS lists it and the target Node's executor reported `ufw.ensure_rule`; otherwise it still runs the Swamp workflow operation-firewall-direct, which goes once every Node reports.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: firewall:remove\n  %% kind: firewall.remove\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=executes tone=blue\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>state machine\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>agent executor\n  CLI->>GW: firewall:remove PORT<br/>--node=NODE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit firewall:remove PORT --node=NODE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<port> --node=ID [--from=SOURCE] [--protocol=tcp|udp]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the destination port or port range of the rule, and the Node\n  %%> - optionally the source and the protocol, to pick one rule when several share the port\n  %%> Confirmation:\n  %%> - the CLI asks before removal; `--yes` confirms without a prompt\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, mark removing\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target Node\n  %%> - the target is an active Linux Node\n  %%> - exactly one rule on the target matches the port, source and protocol: none is `firewall.rule_not_found`, several is `firewall.rule_ambiguous` and lists each match\n  %%> - no other firewall change runs on the target; the Gateway holds one lock per Node\n  %%> Works out what to keep:\n  %%> - a ufw line that another rule, or a rule Orbit itself needs, also uses stays on the Node (`retain`)\n  %%> - only lines that belong to this rule alone are removed\n  Note over GW: Record step, start event<br/>ensure-0 · orbit.engine.start\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the rule is recorded as removing, with the full intent and the engine step `ensure-0`\n  %%> - the start event goes to JetStream; the state machine takes it from there\n  %%> - nothing has happened on the Node yet\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the Operation ID, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the Operation with `operation:show`\n  GW->>N: Send the signed order<br/>ufw.ensure_rule · NATS\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - signs one order for the target Node: operation `<id>:ensure-0`, target the Node itself, so its firewall changes run one at a time\n  %%> - publishes it to the Node's durable consumer on the ORDERS stream, over NATS on WireGuard\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - `action`, `port`, `protocol`, `source`, `comment`, `ensure: absent`, `retain`\n  %%> Deadline:\n  %%> - a JetStream schedule wakes the step after 10 s; with no receipt by then, a new order goes out; after 120 s the step fails with `engine.step_budget_exceeded`\n  %%> - an order older than its 8 s expiry is refused as `expired`, and the Gateway sends a new one\n  Note over N: Read the rules<br/>ufw status numbered\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - the executor checks the order's signature, then reads ufw's numbered rows\n  %%> - refuses an inactive firewall (`firewall.backend_inactive`) before any change\n  Note over N: Plan the changes\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - turns the rule into one ufw delete command per protocol and address family\n  %%> - skips a line that is already gone and a line that must stay\n  %%> - plans no changes when no line is left to delete\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - the typed primitive `ufw.ensure_rule` in the executor (crate `firewall`, a port of the former Swamp planner)\n  alt changes needed\n    Note over N: Change the rules<br/>one ufw argv per change\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - deletes each line that belongs to this rule alone; a line that is already gone is skipped\n    %%> - a line that a role also needs (`retain`) stays\n    %%> - runs the planned commands one at a time\n    Note over N: Read the result<br/>ufw status numbered\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the rule only counts as done when ufw shows it after the changes\n  else already removed\n    Note over N: Skipped — already removed<br/>firewall.rule_absent\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the plan contains no changes\n    %%> - there is no second read of ufw: the check runs on the first read, which nothing has changed since\n  end\n  Note over N: Check the rules\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - checks every removed line is gone, and retained lines are still there\n  %%> - after changes, a line that ufw changed in between fails the check, and a rerun repairs it\n  alt end state is right\n    N-->>GW: Receipt: applied<br/>changes N\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - the receipt goes to the RECEIPTS stream; the order is acknowledged only after JetStream stored it\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the rule record is deleted from the Gateway and the Operation succeeds, through the kind's projection\n    %%> - `changes: 0` reports `Skipped — already removed (firewall.rule_absent)`, part of the successful result, not an error\n  else removing or checking fails\n    N--xGW: Receipt: failed<br/>error_code\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - ufw refuses the change, or the end state is wrong (`firewall.verify_failed`)\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the rule stays, marked failed with `failed_step: ensure-0`, so you can see it\n    %%> - the Operation fails\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - lines removed before the failure stay removed; there is no rollback\n    %%> - that is safe: the rule stays marked failed, and a rerun skips lines that are already gone\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it is a new Operation\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show OPERATION\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the Operation's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the Operation has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "fleet/rollout": {
   "kind": "fleet.rollout",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Each Node visit runs fleet-release-node: gate, CLI install when needed, record, pinned observer. It applies no role; a role changes only through node:converge. R3c: no rollout records, Swamp run history is the record. docs/reference/gateway-recovery.md#fleet-rollout.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: orbit fleet:rollout\n  %% kind: fleet.rollout\n  autonumber\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  Note over GW: Pick Nodes when no Node<br/>IDs are given\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - when the Gateway records a release `verified`, at once and at any hour: the release’s fleet pass uses this Operation when a Node needs agent or CLI delivery\n  %%> - once a day at 03:05 Europe/Amsterdam, to catch membership changes, drift and late CLI releases between releases; the schedule only queues the admission\n  %%> - when an operator runs `orbit fleet:rollout`\n  %%> - always while `ORBIT_FLEET_ROLLOUT=true`, and only for a commit whose Gateway release is verified\n  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review:\n  %%> - the verified release admits the rollout, as `gateway:release:deploy` says; the deleted `fleet-maintenance` schedule did it each minute until 2026-11-06\n  %%> - the fleet always rolls out right after each release; there is no time window (Nick, 2026-11-18)\n  %%> - the release pass confirms the serving release under the writer lock before admitting a visit, including for Nodes with no CLI caller\n  %%> - the admission waits while another rollout runs; Nodes without CLI intent need no CLI release\n  %%> - a Node with CLI intent waits for its CLI release; the admission keeps its one-minute retry after admitting other Nodes\n  %%> Picks:\n  %%> - a Node whose last visit for this commit did not converge it, a drifted Node, or a Node not visited yet\n  %%> - at most once per 5 minutes per Node; the last visit is read from earlier rollout results\n  %%> - no Node after a failed Node that still waits its 5 minutes\n  %%> - in risk order: `app-dev`, then `ci-runner` `metrics` `analytics`, then `database` `websocket` `vpn` `router`, then `app-prod` `ingress`\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-fleet-rollout\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - each picked Node with its agent pin; CLI intent only for Nodes that require it\n  %%> - the commit stays on the Gateway for visit history and alerts; steps never fetch intent or post results\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - no other rollout is running\n  loop each Node, in risk order\n    Note over SW: Earlier Nodes succeeded<br/>gate\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - one Node at a time: a Node acts only after every earlier Node succeeded\n    %%> - every Node goes at any hour; a busy publication lock defers the Node to a later pass, it is not a failure\n    opt Node carries CLI intent\n      SW->>N: Publish the CLI<br/>cli · node-cli-install\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - the Gateway uses the shared CLI eligibility rule; failed publication stops this Node\n    end\n    SW->>N: Publish the observer<br/>observer\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - a Node without CLI intent skips the CLI child and publishes its observer independently\n    %%> - the running digest and active unit prove the pinned observer\n  end\n  Note over SW: Return each Node's outcome<br/>finish\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - shapes one bounded result from child outputs; a native assertion fails the run when a Node failed\n  %%> - the Gateway's shared terminal reader captures the result, including when the run failed\n  alt every Node converged or deferred\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Operation result lists each Node's outcome; the next pass reads it\n  else a Node fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>NODE · error_code\n    %%> Why stop:\n    %%> - later Nodes are not visited, so one bad release breaks at most one Node\n    Note over GW: Alert once<br/>rollout_halted\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - fix the cause; the next pass (a verified release, the daily pass or an operator) starts again at this Node\n    %%> - retries raise no new alert for the same Node, commit and error\n  end\n"
  },
  "fleet/cli-converge": {
   "kind": "fleet.cli_converge",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4, rule 22 (official cfgmgmt, no shipped script). docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#orbit-cli.\n  %% Real steps 2026-11-02: prepare, node-N (fleet-release-node: gate, cli, record), finish.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: none · POST /api/v1/fleet/cli/converge\n  %% kind: fleet.cli_converge\n  autonumber\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  Note over GW: Converge the CLI<br/>task #5170\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - an operator or a tool calls `POST /api/v1/fleet/cli/converge`, for the CLI-eligible Nodes of the rollout set\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - optional API field `node_ids`, for example `[12]`, limits convergence to those eligible Nodes\n  %%> - without it, the whole rollout set is selected; an ineligible listed Node is rejected before admission\n  %%> What it decides:\n  %%> - per Node: the CLI release with its SHA-256, the root profile, and the public SSH descriptor of each Node that installs\n  %%> - a Node without a CLI caller is skipped (`cli_not_required`); a Node whose release is unpublished is also skipped; neither needs an SSH target\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5170 recorded with the full intent; the Gateway replies 202\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-fleet-cli-converge\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209) for the selected Nodes\n  Note over SW: Freeze the Node list<br/>prepare\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - validates the full admitted workflow input and records the Nodes in order; no Gateway read\n  loop each Node, one at a time\n    Note over SW: Check the Node may publish<br/>fleet-release-node · gate\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the `fleet-release-node` child starts on Node 209; its `gate` step checks that every earlier Node succeeded; native cancellation stops the run\n    %%> - a skipped Node or a planning error records the outcome without touching the Node\n    SW->>N: Install the CLI release<br/>cli · node-cli-install\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209, with `@swamp/ssh` and the admitted host keys\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - reads `orbit --version`; a Node that runs this release, a newer one, or a commit build keeps it\n    %%> - otherwise downloads the release with `@swamp/cfgmgmt/fetch` and its `checksum`, checks the new file reports the release version, and switches the link with `@swamp/cfgmgmt/link`\n    %%> - a file that does not report a valid Orbit version is replaced by the admitted release\n    %%> - writes root's profile, 0600, with `@swamp/cfgmgmt/file`\n    Note over SW: Record the CLI outcome<br/>record\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the `record` step on Node 209 records `converged` or `unchanged` with the CLI version, or `cli.install_failed`\n    %%> Not included:\n    %%> - the Node agent; its `observer` step runs only for `fleet.rollout`, so this task needs no worker on Node 12\n  end\n  Note over SW: Report each Node<br/>finish\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - reads each Node's `record` output and returns one bounded result per Node\n  %%> - a native assertion fails the run after a failed Node; the Gateway captures the completed result even in a failed run\n  alt every Node converged\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event · per Node\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record each Node<br/>task done\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - task #5170 succeeds; `operation:show` returns each Node's outcome and CLI version\n  else a Node fails\n    N--xSW: install or check failed\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a download, checksum, version check, link or profile write fails on Node 12\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>Node 12 · cli.install_failed\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - Swamp visits no later Node; later Nodes stay `pending`\n    Note over GW: Fail the task<br/>Node 12 · task failed\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - the old CLI link stays when the switch did not happen; there is no rollback\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - fix the cause and call converge again; it is a new task and safe to rerun\n  end\n"
  },
  "fleet/reboot": {
   "kind": "fleet.reboot",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0253, drained Node reboots one at a time; docs/reference/gateway-recovery.md#fleet-reboot.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% kind: fleet.reboot\n  autonumber\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  Note over GW: Pick Nodes<br/>in risk order\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - every day at 03:06 Europe/Amsterdam, after the rollout admission; the window is 03:00–06:00\n  %%> - when an operator posts to `fleet/reboot`; that pass ignores the window\n  %%> - always while `ORBIT_FLEET_REBOOT` is on (the default)\n  %%> Picks:\n  %%> - every active Linux Node with a WireGuard address and a pinned host key, except task VMs; no Node is a task host, so none drains task guests\n  %%> - in the rollout's groups, then the control path (`vpn` hub, Gateway, `swamp.orbit` Node), then the `swamp` Node\n  %%> - a reboot hold is frozen as `defer: operator_hold`; a held Node is read but never drained or rebooted\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - no other pass, no fleet rollout and no Gateway release runs; they refuse each other\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-fleet-reboot\n  loop each Node, one at a time\n    SW->>N: Drain the Node<br/>drain phase\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - only the initial safe boot-state reads retry SSH exit 255: eight attempts, 10 s each, with Ansible backoff (0, 1, 3, 7, 15, 30, 30 s); at most 166 s before overhead\n    %%> - drain, stops and reboot use zero SSH retries; a disconnect after execution never replays the command\n    %%> - a held Node that cannot be read fails and halts the pass, as any other Node does\n    %%> - after a failed Node: `skipped`; outside the window: `deferred`; no `/var/run/reboot-required`: `not_due`\n    %%> - a due held Node records boot ID, kernel and due time, then `deferred` (`operator_hold`); without a due reboot it stays `not_due`\n    %%> - CI: stop each idle runner, frozen while read; a job still running after 15 min defers the Node\n    %%> - on the Gateway the scheduler stops and Horizon drains within 15 min\n    %%> - record `drain_started` before the first stop; rescue runs the existing Gateway and CI recovery only after that marker\n    SW->>N: Reboot and wait for SSH<br/>reboot phase\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the swamp Node instead schedules its own reboot after the run ends: `scheduled`\n    SW->>N: Check the new boot<br/>check phase\n    %%> Checks:\n    %%> - new boot ID, newest kernel, nothing due, no newly failed unit, agent, Gateway scheduler and Horizon, hub tunnel and source unit active\n    opt a CI host whose drain stopped runners\n      SW->>GW: Mint Actions write tokens<br/>github-token\n      %%> Why:\n      %%> - one `actions: write` token per Project repository, from the App's existing permission; no runner administration (ADR 0223)\n      SW->>SW: Re-run lost CI jobs<br/>rerun phase\n      %%> Picks:\n      %%> - failed jobs a stopped runner was still running at its freeze; never a cancelled run\n      %%> - `rerun-failed-jobs` once, only while the run is still at that attempt; the Node's result lists them\n    end\n    SW->>SW: Record phase failures<br/>Record the Node\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - always on the controller, including after UNREACHABLE bypasses Ansible rescue\n    %%> - private JSON callback reports supply `failed` / `fleet.node_unreachable` and bounded redacted SSH diagnostic text\n    %%> - a started phase without a terminal report or Node outcome is `failed` / `fleet.reboot_cut`; `not_reached` needs an earlier recorded halt\n  end\n  alt every Node rebooted, scheduled, not due, deferred or skipped\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>each Node's outcome\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - every Node result shows `reboot_hold` as admitted\n    %%> - a Node due for more than 3 days raises `reboot_overdue`\n  else a Node fails or has unknown outcome\n    SW--xGW: Pass failed<br/>outcomes · error_code\n    %%> Why stop:\n    %%> - without a Node report, the Gateway records `unknown` / `run_interrupted`, fails the pass and alerts once\n    %%> - missing records before a recorded halt stay unknown; only Nodes after that halt may be `not_reached`\n    %%> - after a recorded Node failure, later Nodes record `skipped` / `halted` without SSH; the next pass starts again there\n    Note over GW: Alert once<br/>reboot_halted\n  end\n  Note over GW: Confirm 209 boot<br/>from its uptime\n  %%> Retry:\n  %%> - 30 minutes after the pass without a new boot: `reboot_unconfirmed`\n"
  },
  "fleet/conn-server-rollout": {
   "kind": "fleet.conn_server_rollout",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% One Ansible phase, conn-server-rollout.yml, visits the admitted Conn Server Nodes one at a time, in order. docs/reference/development-containers.md#conn-server-rollout.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: none · POST /api/v1/fleet/conn-server/rollout\n  %% kind: fleet.conn_server_rollout\n  autonumber\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  Note over GW: Read the pin and<br/>the Conn Server Nodes\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - an operator posts `{\"dry_run\", \"scope\"}` to `POST /api/v1/fleet/conn-server/rollout`; both are required, so no default reaches the whole fleet\n  %%> - scope `nodes` also takes `node_ids`, one to four ids that each have a record, or a validation error; `node_ids` with another scope is refused\n  %%> - the release fleet pass admits the reviewed dry run and canary update only if one Node is the canary and its Tool desires that exact release; otherwise it leaves Conn alone\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - resolve the exact host release from the active file catalog or the Node's desired version; an override cannot change managed intent\n  %%> - exactly one `conn_server_nodes` record is the canary, or `conn_server.canary_unset`\n  %%> - a gated promotion requires a passing canary operation ID, newer than the latest canary check, which passed, and a checked dry run for every remaining Node\n  %%> Picks:\n  %%> - `canary`: one Node; `fleet`: enrolled, nonwithdrawn followers on the reviewed version, otherwise canary first; `nodes`: named records in request order\n  %%> - each record names platform, path, service, port, user and whether to notify before the restart; Nodes without one and container Conn Servers are out of scope\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>conn-server-rollout\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - version, dry run, artifacts and signing identity, notice, and pinned SSH targets; withdrawal names one withdrawn Linux service and needs no replacement asset\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - at most four Nodes, so the 13,620 s deadline covers each Node's worst case and its rollback, or `conn_server.too_many_nodes`\n  loop each Node, in the admitted order\n    SW->>N: Download and check<br/>conn-server-rollout\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - Mac: `launchctl print gui/<uid>/<label>` checks the configured LaunchAgent before staging, including in a dry run, or `conn_server.service_not_loaded`\n    %%> - checks reachability, then downloads the selected asset into staging and checks its SHA-256, or `conn_server.hash_mismatch`\n    %%> - Mac: `codesign --verify --strict --deep`, the selected release's `TeamIdentifier` and bundle id, or `conn_server.signature_invalid`, `team_mismatch`, `bundle_mismatch`\n    %%> - Linux: the unit's `ExecStart` is `<current>/conn-server` and the tarball holds `conn-server-<X>-linux-x64/conn-server`, or `conn_server.layout_mismatch`\n    %%> - Linux: `/health` on the unit's `--host`, or 127.0.0.1 when it has none or a wildcard, answers 200 with `server: conn-server` and a version, or `conn_server.health_unreachable`\n    %%> - Mac: a direct `/health` read on 127.0.0.1 with proxies and redirects disabled requires HTTP 200, `server: conn-server` and a nonblank version string, or `conn_server.health_unreachable`\n    alt Mac: staged `codesign -dr -` differs from the installed bundle\n      Note over N: Hold, install nothing<br/>held\n      %%> Why:\n      %%> - a new designated requirement resets the TCC grants; a hold stops a gated promotion or a canary update; earlier pins hold only the affected follower\n    else dry run\n      Note over N: Stop after the checks<br/>checked\n    else checks pass\n      opt the record asks for a notice\n        SW->>SW: Send the notice<br/>conn-server-notice\n        %%> What it does:\n        %%> - posts or runs `orbit.conn.server_rollout_notice` before anything changes; a failed notice fails the Node (`conn_server.notice_failed`)\n        %%> - logs neither the URL, the command nor their output\n      end\n      SW->>N: Back up and swap<br/>on the same path\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - Mac: a new backup folder (`conn_server.backup_exists` when it exists), the installed bundle into it, the staged bundle onto the same path, so TCC grants stay\n      %%> - Linux: a new `releases/<X>-<run>` folder, then `current` moves to it with one rename\n      SW->>N: Restart<br/>kickstart or systemctl\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - Mac: `launchctl kickstart -k gui/<uid>/<label>`, never a start from the SSH session; Linux: restarts the unit\n      %%> - never reads or prints a LaunchAgent plist; every command has a task timeout\n      loop each round, up to 180 s\n        opt Mac: a UserNotificationCenter prompt is pending\n          SW->>N: Answer the prompt<br/>System Events\n          %%> What it does:\n          %%> - reads the prompt's static texts and clicks Allow only on a Conn Server prompt for an expected permission\n          %%> - any other prompt fails the Node (`conn_server.unexpected_prompt`)\n        end\n        SW->>N: GET /health<br/>on 127.0.0.1 or the unit's --host\n        %%> What it does:\n        %%> - waits for `server: conn-server` and the pin's exact version\n        %%> - Mac: uses the same direct probe and response checks as the preflight read\n      end\n      alt /health names the version and the canary thread read succeeds\n        opt release canary\n          SW->>N: Read the known thread<br/>authenticated Conn CLI\n        end\n        Note over N: Remove the backup<br/>updated\n      else the swap, restart, prompt or health fails\n        SW->>N: Roll back<br/>restore and restart\n        %%> What it does:\n        %%> - Mac: sets the new bundle aside, puts the backup back on the path and kickstarts again; Linux: points `current` back and restarts\n        %%> - the Node fails with its cause, `conn_server.health_check_failed` when `/health` never names the version; `rolled_back` is true when health confirms the preflight version\n      end\n    end\n  end\n  Note over SW: Write each Node's result<br/>result\n  alt no failed Node or blocking hold\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Operation result lists each Node's outcome: `updated`, `checked` or `held`\n  else a Node fails or a hold stops the run\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>NODE · error_code\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - `error_code` is `conn_server.node_failed`, `conn_server.canary_held` for a held canary, or `conn_server.node_held` during promotion\n    %%> Why stop:\n    %%> - later Nodes are not visited and report `not_reached`, so one bad release reaches at most one Node\n  end\n"
  },
  "gateway/release-deploy": {
   "kind": "gateway.release",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: docs/reference/feature-flow.md section 6, one Swamp workflow per Gateway release, manual or automatic.\n  %% Container releases keep the former live stopped as old for rollback; migrations are expand-only. docs/reference/gateway-recovery.md#release-as-a-swamp-run.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: gateway:release:deploy\n  %% kind: gateway.release\n  %% behavior name: gateway-release-<step>-209 (fixed)\n  autonumber\n  %% lane TR icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane AC icon=workflow pill=child tone=green\n  %% lane GN icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant TR as CLI<br/>orbit · MCP\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant AC as Activate run<br/>child · new commit\n  participant GN as Gateway Node<br/>Node 14\n  alt Reload serving workflows\n    TR->>GW: gateway:release:deploy<br/>--reconcile · exact serving SHA\n    GW->>SW: Start reconciliation\n    SW->>GN: Check the serving release\n    SW->>SW: Reload serving workflows\n    SW->>GN: Verify the serving release\n    SW->>GN: Release the serving<br/>generation\n    SW-->>GW: Reconciled release record\n  else Deploy a release\n  TR->>GW: gateway:release:deploy<br/>full sha\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit gateway:release:deploy <full sha>`; the automatic tick admits the newest green commit the same way\n  Note over GW: Admit the release<br/>verify agent pin · web build\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the full commit; no other release queued or running\n  %%> - the agent pin passes its Sigstore check; CI published the commit's web build\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - a running release record; the pin, artifact, Horizon unit and SSH targets frozen as inputs\n  GW->>SW: Start release workflow<br/>shared or legacy selection\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - Container releases select production-release. The host release path selects operation-gateway-release.\n  %%> - At run start, the Gateway writes the scoped read token to the vault.\n  GW-->>TR: 202 Accepted<br/>running release record\n  opt Container release\n    Note over SW: Check release ownership\n  end\n  SW->>GN: Fetch the commit<br/>shared repository\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - fetches the commit with the read token when the shared repository lacks it; the token reaches git only in its environment\n  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for review:\n  %%> - the fetch is its own step, so that the build runs its script from the commit being released, not from the live release\n  SW->>GN: Build the release<br/>wake and update preparation\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - refuses a commit that does not descend from the current release on a container Gateway\n  %%> - on a container Gateway, checks the target commit's activation workflow and recovery script before waking it or loading its Swamp workflows\n  %%> - checks out the exact commit and installs dependencies in the preparation container; its workers and scheduler stay disabled\n  %%> - a retry after an incomplete build starts from a clean checkout and discards partial build output\n  %%> On failure:\n  %%> - the host PHP-FPM build checks disk space for the build and database copy\n  SW->>GN: Install the web build<br/>artifact · digest · rename\n  alt Container release\n    SW->>GN: Clone the prepared release<br/>seal · promote preparation\n    SW->>GN: Sleep preparation<br/>before migration\n  else Host PHP-FPM release\n    SW->>GN: Snapshot the database<br/>VACUUM INTO\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - keeps the newest two snapshots and any snapshot named by a pause\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - refuses before the copy when it would breach the disk floor\n  end\n  SW->>GN: Migrate<br/>artisan migrate --isolated=1\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the container candidate applies pending migrations to the shared production database\n  %%> - prints the end of the migration's output and its exit code; a failed exec would keep no output\n  Note over SW: Check the migration<br/>assert the exit code\n  %%> On failure:\n  %%> - a failed container migration leaves the current release serving\n  %%> - the record's message ends with the migration's output\n  opt Host PHP-FPM release\n    SW->>GN: Wait for active Operations<br/>before loading workflows\n  end\n    SW->>GN: Check the candidate<br/>FrankenPHP · health · commit\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - discard the candidate; live keeps serving and the Swamp load does not start\n    SW->>GN: Renew Swamp tokens<br/>Swamp Node and Gateway tokens\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - starts the Swamp Node's token renewal, renews the Gateway read and run tokens within ten days, and records the replaced names\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - the run fails with `gateway.release_swamp_token_expiring` while a live consumer's token still expires within three days\n    Note over SW: Load Swamp on Node 209<br/>operation-gateway-swamp-load\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the load child checks the target on the Gateway Node before updating the checkout, grants and loaded workflows\n    Note over SW: Hand over the release<br/>run the selected child\n    opt the Gateway serves through host PHP-FPM\n      AC->>GN: Drain Horizon<br/>systemctl stop · SIGTERM only\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - waits for the unit's whole control group to empty, up to the drain limit\n      %%> On failure:\n      %%> - the run fails with `gateway.release_horizon_unit_stale` for a unit without the stop policy, or `gateway.release_horizon_not_drained` when the unit did not stop in time\n    end\n    AC->>GN: Switch the release<br/>commit · publish upstream\n    par Public readiness\n      AC->>GN: Verify the Gateway<br/>health and serving SHA\n      %%> - reads `/up` and the status version with sudo: the pinned root CA sits in the private certificate folder, `root:caddy 0750`\n    and Container workers\n      AC->>GN: Start Horizon<br/>on the selected release\n    end\n    opt Container Gateway\n      AC->>GN: Quiesce the previous live<br/>drain HTTP and Horizon\n      AC->>GN: Wait for queued responses<br/>Ansible retries · bounded\n      AC->>GN: Retire the previous live<br/>stop it as old\n      AC->>GN: Recreate preparation<br/>clone · promote\n      AC->>GN: Record eligible versions<br/>file cleanup Tasks\n    end\n    opt Failed host PHP-FPM check\n      AC->>GN: Switch back<br/>restore host release\n    end\n    AC->>GN: Resume document cleanup\n    SW->>GN: Revoke replaced tokens<br/>after processes restart\n    %%> - a failure leaves the replaced tokens valid to their expiry; the release still succeeds\n    alt every step succeeded\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result\n      Note over GW: Record verified<br/>fleet rollout follows\n      %%> - the cleanup step files Tasks for earlier releases' versions once a later verified release has deleted the stopped old that held the earlier code\n      %%> Next:\n      %%> - the Gateway queues the existing fleet admission job; it follows each Node’s delivery and Node converge in order\n      %%> - when enabled, nested-fleet refresh follows the first transition to `current`; with fleet rollout disabled it follows verification\n    else a step before Migrate fails\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step\n      Note over GW: Mark failed<br/>nothing changed · rerun safe\n    else the host switched back\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step\n      Note over GW: Record switched back<br/>alert · next commit releases\n    else a container check fails\n      SW--xGW: Report failed phase\n      Note over GW: Roll back to old<br/>or deploy a fix\n    else a host migration or later step fails\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step\n      Note over GW: Pause automatic releases<br/>failed step · alert\n    end\n  SW->>GN: Release unused candidate<br/>after handover or failure\n  %%> Returns a candidate of this Operation that never served to the preparation slot. The selected release, old and a pending drain remain protected.\n  end\n"
  },
  "github/app-install": {
   "kind": "github.app_register",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: DECISIONS R1 (no Node, no run), root-of-trust-gateway-only (App key stays in the Gateway). docs/reference/github-app.md#register-and-install.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: github:app:install\n  %% kind: github.app_register\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant GH as GitHub<br/>App registration\n  CLI->>GW: github:app:install\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit github:app:install`\n  %%> - syntax: `[--name=NAME] [--owner=ORGANIZATION] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - no required input; the CLI prompts for an App name or defaults to `orbit`\n  %%> - without an owner, registration belongs to your personal account\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the browser reaches the Gateway's private HTTPS address, because GitHub redirects back to it\n  Note over GW: No App yet: start one<br/>registration · state · 1 h\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the Gateway has no GitHub App; otherwise the command only opens the install page\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - a registration with a random `state` value, which expires after one hour\n  GW-->>CLI: Registration page URL<br/>CLI opens it in the browser\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the URL of a Gateway page that posts the App's manifest to GitHub\n  CLI->>GH: Confirm the App<br/>manifest · name · permissions\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - GitHub shows the App name and its seven permissions; you confirm\n  %%> - a taken name makes GitHub ask for another one\n  GH-->>CLI: Redirect to the Gateway<br/>one-time code · state\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the browser carries the one-time code and the `state` value back to the Gateway\n  CLI->>GW: Open the callback<br/>/github/app/callback?code=…\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the browser opens the Gateway's callback with the code\n  Note over GW: Check the registration<br/>state matches · not expired\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the `state` value belongs to this registration, which has not expired or been used\n  %%> Refuses:\n  %%> - another state, or a replayed callback: `github.registration_invalid`\n  GW->>GH: Exchange the code<br/>POST app-manifests/conversions\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the Gateway itself exchanges the one-time code for the App ID, slug and private key\n  %%> Why no Swamp run:\n  %%> - it touches no Node (R1), and the App key is a root of trust: it never leaves the Gateway, not even through a vault\n  alt GitHub returns the App\n    GH-->>GW: App ID, slug, key\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the App's identity and its private key, once\n    Note over GW: Store the App<br/>key encrypted · Activity entry\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the App ID, slug and encrypted private key are stored; the code is consumed\n    %%> - no queue job, no task to follow; the answer comes in the same request\n    GW-->>CLI: App registered<br/>next: install it\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - a page that says the App is registered\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - run `github:app:install` again to install the App on an account and choose its repositories\n  else GitHub refuses\n    GH--xGW: refused or no reply\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the code expired or was used, or GitHub cannot be reached\n    Note over GW: Mark it failed<br/>github.registration_failed\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the registration is marked failed; no App is stored; `github:app:show` shows `failed`\n    %%> On GitHub:\n    %%> - an App registration that GitHub did create stays there; delete it on GitHub\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run `github:app:install` again; it starts a new registration\n    GW-->>CLI: Registration failed<br/>github.registration_failed\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the error at once; there is nothing to follow\n  end\n"
  },
  "github/app-sync": {
   "kind": "github.app_sync",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: DECISIONS R1 (no Node, no run), root-of-trust-gateway-only (App JWT stays in the Gateway). docs/reference/github-app.md.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: github:app:sync\n  %% kind: github.app_sync\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant GH as GitHub<br/>App installations\n  CLI->>GW: github:app:sync\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit github:app:sync`\n  %%> - syntax: `[--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - nothing; it refreshes the Gateway's one App\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - after you install the App on an account or change its repositories\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - `--idempotency-key`: the refresh answers at once and is safe to repeat\n  Note over GW: Check the App<br/>sign App JWT · 9 min\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Gateway\n  %%> - the Gateway has a registered App, else `github.app_missing`\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - signs a short App JWT with the App key; the key and the JWT never leave the Gateway\n  %%> Why no Swamp run:\n  %%> - it touches no Node (R1), and App-level authority stays in the Gateway\n  GW->>GH: List installations<br/>GET /app/installations\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - asks GitHub for every installation of the App, with the JWT\n  %%> - answers within the request; no queue job, no task to follow\n  alt GitHub answers\n    GH-->>GW: 2 installations\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - per installation: the account, `user` or `organization`, `all` or `selected` repositories, and whether it is suspended\n    Note over GW: Store the observation<br/>current · observed_at\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the installation list is stored with `installations_status: current` and the time\n    %%> - an Activity entry records the refresh\n    GW-->>CLI: App and installations<br/>same as github:app:show\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the App and one row per installation, at once\n  else GitHub fails\n    GH--xGW: refused or no reply\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - GitHub cannot be reached, or refuses the JWT\n    Note over GW: Keep the last list<br/>github.unavailable\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the last stored list stays, with its `observed_at`; it becomes `stale` after five minutes\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again\n    GW-->>CLI: github.unavailable<br/>last list · stale\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the error and the last known list, at once\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/analytics-disable": {
   "kind": "analytics.disable",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run; disable keeps the Plausible site. docs/reference/analytics.md#publish-a-tracking-host.\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: instance:analytics:disable\n  %% kind: analytics.disable\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Serving Node<br/>Instance or Router\n  Note over CLI: Confirm host removal<br/>default No\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - visits stop being counted as soon as the hosts are gone\n  CLI->>GW: instance:analytics:disable<br/>INSTANCE\n  Note over GW: Validate, find hosts\n  alt Instance has no hosts\n    GW-->>CLI: Skipped<br/>no hosts · no run\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the command succeeds and changes nothing\n  else hosts exist\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-analytics-disable-direct\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    opt host outside the TLD\n      Note over SW: Remove private DNS<br/>VPN Node over @swamp/ssh\n      %%> Why first:\n      %%> - the name stops resolving before its site leaves\n    end\n    SW->>N: Withdraw sites, certs<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file absent\n    %%> Why one step:\n    %%> - the site goes before its certificate, as `route:destroy` does; other sites stay\n    SW->>N: Check the hosts are gone<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file check\n    alt every step succeeds\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      Note over GW: Delete tracking Routes<br/>site binding deleted\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the Plausible site, its history and its goals stay; disable never calls Plausible\n    else a step fails\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      Note over GW: Keep the Routes<br/>marked failed\n      %%> On the Nodes:\n      %%> - sections already withdrawn stay withdrawn; there is no rollback\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same command again; it skips what is already gone\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/analytics-enable": {
   "kind": "analytics.enable",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run, certificate signing is the one step-to-Gateway call. docs/reference/analytics.md#publish-a-tracking-host.\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: instance:analytics:enable\n  %% kind: analytics.enable\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane P icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Serving Node<br/>Instance or Router\n  participant P as Plausible<br/>analytics role\n  CLI->>GW: instance:analytics:enable<br/>INSTANCE\n  opt --host\n    Note over GW: Use the named hosts<br/>up to ten, exact set\n  end\n  Note over GW: Validate, plan hosts<br/>tracking Routes provisioning\n  GW->>SW: Copy the Plausible login<br/>to the vault\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the run gets the vault key name, never the credentials; a missing login stops the command before any task starts\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-analytics-enable-direct\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n  SW->>P: Converge the site<br/>@nckrtl/analytics-site · site\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - creates the site when it is missing and updates it otherwise, never a duplicate; it sets the timezone and goals and logs out, also after a failure\n  opt tracking host served by a Router\n    SW->>N: Request the certificate<br/>CSR for the tracking host\n    SW->>GW: Sign the CSR<br/>operation:direct:certificate-sign\n    %%> Why a call to the Gateway:\n    %%> - certificate signing is the one allowed step-to-Gateway call; the Orbit CA stays in the Gateway\n    GW-->>SW: Signed certificate<br/>installed on the Router\n  end\n  SW->>N: Publish Caddy, reload<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · reload\n  %%> Why one step:\n  %%> - sites of hosts you left out go in the same validated reload\n  SW->>N: Probe the tracking host<br/>TLS · GET /js/script.js\n  opt host outside the TLD\n    Note over SW: Publish private DNS<br/>VPN Node over @swamp/ssh\n    %%> Why only outside the TLD:\n    %%> - private DNS answers the whole TLD, so a host inside it needs no record\n  end\n  alt every step succeeds\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    Note over GW: Tracking Routes active<br/>site binding saved\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - removed hosts' Routes are deleted\n  else a step fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    Note over GW: Mark the Routes failed<br/>task failed\n    %%> On the Nodes:\n    %%> - finished steps stay; a Plausible site already created stays and a rerun reuses it; there is no rollback\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again; it converges the same site and hosts\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/auto-deploy": {
   "kind": "instance.auto_deploy",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Production policy only. Development uses the independent Gateway precheck in container/deploy-default.mmd; this schedule and its persisted pause are never changed by that tick.\n  %% Target design: DECISIONS 3 (automatic deploy = Gateway-published native schedule, no POST back), R1-R2, ADR 0244, behavior names by target. Replaces instance.policy_event. docs/reference/deployments.md#automatic-deployments.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 3, 5 (no per-minute Node read: the schedule inputs carry the deployed commit, and the Gateway republishes them on each deploy).\n  %% Target design, production containers (ADR 0263, docs/reference/production-containers.md#deploy): the deployed commit is live's commit, and a fire's deploy is the hot deploy of container/deploy-production.mmd. A capacity refusal gets a retry time and never counts toward blocking a commit.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:auto-deploy:enable · Automatic deploys\n  %% kind: instance.auto_deploy\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as app-prod Node<br/>Node 12 over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: auto-deploy:enable 42<br/>orbit instance:auto-deploy:enable\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:auto-deploy:enable 42`\n  %%> - syntax: `<instance>`; `instance:auto-deploy:disable <instance>` turns it off\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - turns automatic deployment on for production Instance 42\n  %%> - also resumes an Instance that a manual deploy or rollback paused\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - deploys nothing now and changes nothing on the Node\n  %%> Typical use:\n  %%> - a production Instance follows `main`: merge a PR, CI turns green, the next fire deploys it\n  Note over GW: Check, record auto-deploy<br/>Instance 42 · on, unpaused\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12\n  %%> - Instance 42 is an active production Instance; before its first succeeded deployment the check reports `not_deployed`\n  %%> - when the Project has a `merge_check`, the GitHub App can read its check runs\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - auto-deploy recorded as on and unpaused\n  GW->>SW: Publish the schedule<br/>auto-deploy-42 · every minute\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - publishes one native Swamp schedule for Instance 42\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - branch, `merge_check`, blocked commits, retry times\n  %%> - the deployed commit: live's commit; the Gateway republishes the inputs after each deploy or rollback\n  %%> - vault key names for the read token; the Gateway keeps the token fresh in the vault\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the run has everything it needs; it never calls the Gateway\n  GW-->>CLI: Enabled<br/>auto_deploy: on\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the Instance with auto-deploy on, at once\n  %%> - no task: publishing the schedule is part of the command\n  loop every minute, second 30\n    Note over SW: tick\n    %%> Native policy producer:\n    %%> - the retained fleet policy selects work and produces the complete event receipt\n    SW->>SW: Find newest green commit<br/>ls-remote · merge_check passed\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - reads the branch head with `git ls-remote`\n    %%> - walks first-parent history to the newest commit whose `merge_check` passed\n    %%> - skips blocked commits and commits whose retry isn't due\n    %%> Not included:\n    %%> - never deploys a red or pending commit\n    %%> - compares with the deployed commit from the inputs; no SSH to the production Node each minute\n    alt a newer green commit\n      SW->>N: Deploy the pinned commit<br/>deploy-42 · hot … retire\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - the hot deploy of `instance:deploy`, pinned to the commit: hot builds and migrates it while live serves, then the Routes switch to it\n      %%> - behavior `deploy-42`: one lock per Instance, so a manual deploy and a fire never overlap\n      SW-->>GW: Fire ended<br/>result, commit, deployment\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the result (`started`, `failed`, `gate_failed`, …), the commit and the deployment outputs\n      Note over GW: Record the result<br/>history row · Activity · alert\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - a deployment history row with origin `automatic`\n      %%> - one Activity entry `auto_deploy.<result>` when the result changes\n      %%> - a release alert for a failed deploy, a stalled commit or a pause\n      %%> - the Gateway republishes the schedule with the new inputs\n      alt the deploy failed\n        Note over GW: Block or retry the commit<br/>retry 10, then 20 minutes\n        %%> Result:\n        %%> - a failure in a deploy step or later counts toward blocking the commit; an earlier failure gets a retry time\n      else Node lacked capacity\n        Note over GW: Set a retry time<br/>deployment.node_capacity\n        %%> Why it never blocks:\n        %%> - the cause is the Node, not the commit; live serves unchanged\n        %%> Alert:\n        %%> - a commit that waits 30 minutes raises the stalled-commit alert\n      end\n    else nothing to deploy\n      SW-->>GW: Fire ended<br/>up_to_date or gate_pending\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the Gateway updates the last result shown by `instance:show`\n      %%> - no history row, no Activity entry\n    end\n  end\n  Note over SW: failures\n  %%> Native end check:\n  %%> - the policy producer records zero failures on success\n  %%> - the Gateway captures its full fire_json in the same terminal history read, including events from a failed fire\n  %%> - no reporting shell forwards or prints the receipt\n  Note over GW,SW: Pause<br/>manual deploy or rollback\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - a manual `instance:deploy` or `instance:rollback` pauses auto-deploy\n  %%> - the Gateway removes the schedule's trigger; no fire runs\n  %%> Resume:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:auto-deploy:enable 42` publishes it again\n  %%> Disable:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:auto-deploy:disable 42` removes the schedule\n"
  },
  "instance/clone": {
   "kind": "instance.clone",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Gateway holds each Route-serving Node from admission to the terminal state; a second Route write on it waits (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task + full intent as inputs, R1-R4, single-swamp-node; DECISIONS 1 (token at admission, vault.get; signing is the one step→Gateway call), scope (Process/Schedule definitions, same-Node preview Route); database.clone merged in as a step; .env through the vault as in instance.create. docs/reference/instance-cloning.md.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 3, 17, 22 (no DNS step: the preview name is inside Node 14's TLD; cert install in the publish step as in route:create; cfgmgmt user/file/systemd, 0600 credential file for the fetch).\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Node work of the site child is one Ansible phase (see route:create).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:clone\n  %% kind: instance.clone\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane C icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant C as Node 12<br/>candidate · ssh\n  participant N as Node 14<br/>app-prod · ssh\n  CLI->>GW: instance:clone 42 14 shop\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:clone 42 14 shop --preview-name=shop.com`\n  %%> - syntax: `<candidate> <node> <name> --preview-name=NAME [--branch=BRANCH] [--sqlite-source-path=PATH] [--json]`\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - clones development Instance 42 on Node 12 into a new production Instance `shop` on Node 14\n  %%> Required input:\n  %%> - `--preview-name=NAME` is required: a terminal asks for it, and a noninteractive call without it is refused\n  %%> Options:\n  %%> - `--preview-name`: combined with Node 14's TLD, such as `shop.com.prod.orbit`\n  %%> - `--branch`: another branch; the default is the candidate's branch\n  %%> - `--sqlite-source-path`: seed the production SQLite file from this file on a checkout candidate\n  opt --branch\n    Note over GW: Select another branch<br/>override candidate branch\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - validates and fetches the supplied repository branch instead of inheriting the candidate branch\n  end\n  Note over GW: Validate, reserve 43<br/>render .env · task #5152\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - production clone is the sole new host-checkout exception until production containers ship; use an existing checkout or the Project’s active, running container default with a recorded seed\n  %%> - your Node has access grants to Node 12 and Node 14\n  %%> - a Project with `nested_fleet` on refuses with `operation.kind_unavailable` (503) before any record: only `instance:create` makes Instances of such a Project, as containers\n  %%> - Node 14 is active, has the `app-prod` role and a TLD\n  %%> - Instance 42 is active with a recorded source and no attached server database; a web Project's Node is not in an active Cluster\n  %%> - Project 7 has no production Instance on Node 14 yet, and the preview domain is free\n  %%> Plans:\n  %%> - the account `orbit-app-7` and its home `/home/orbit-app-7`\n  %%> - the Project's production Process and Schedule definitions\n  %%> - the preview Route for a web Project; no database is copied by default\n  %%> Renders .env:\n  %%> - from the stored values of Instance 42, without `DB_*` and WebSocket credentials\n  %%> - with the new database keys, such as `DB_DATABASE=/home/orbit-app-7/database.sqlite`\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - Instance 43 reserved; task #5152 recorded with the full intent\n  GW->>SW: Push token and .env<br/>vault: github-token, env-5152\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the repository is private, and only the Gateway holds the GitHub App key\n  %%> - `.env` and database passwords travel only through the vault\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - mints a read token for this one repository, valid 1 hour\n  %%> - pushes the token, the rendered `.env` and any new database password into Swamp's vault\n  Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - holds each Node that serves this Route's site (Caddy and PHP) from admission to the terminal state\n  %%> - a second Route write on one of those Nodes waits (task status `waiting`) and starts when the Node is free, oldest first\n  %%> Not held:\n  %%> - the DNS Node: its records are keyed, and the apply phase refuses a file that changed since the read\n  %%> Limit:\n  %%> - two Route writes on one Node run one after the other, so each waits for the other's whole run\n  %%> - a newer write waits behind an older waiting one that shares a Node, and a waiting write can be cancelled\n  %%> - when a waiting create gains its Nodes, the Gateway renders its Caddyfiles from the current site catalog before starting the run; the admitted resource and certificate identities stay fixed\n  %%> - a timed-out write keeps its Nodes until Swamp confirms that its run stopped, or for 15 minutes\n  %%> - task provision and cancel, instance:register and the Cluster Router writes hold the Nodes for their Route-publish step only, through the `route-hold` call\n  %%> - a task that waits starts from the Node's live files; a site block that matches none of its captured states still stops it with `owned_revision_conflict`\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-clone\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one run on Swamp serve (Node 209); every step runs there\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - both Nodes' addresses and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  %%> - the full intent; secrets only as vault key names\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5152\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>C: Check candidate source<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · git status\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over `@swamp/ssh` from 209, read-only\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - checks the branch and commit, a clean working tree and no submodules\n  %%> - checks the Git origin of an existing checkout; for a container default, reads as the workspace owner and verifies HEAD against the recorded seed commit\n  %%> - refuses an origin that contains credentials\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - copies no working files, dependencies or logs; the candidate keeps running\n  %%> Why on the Node:\n  %%> - the candidate's tree can change after admission, and the clone depends on it\n  SW->>N: Fetch the source<br/>file 0600 cred · git fetch\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - creates the account `orbit-app-7` and its home on Node 14 with `@swamp/cfgmgmt/user`\n  %%> - writes the vault token as a 0600 credential file with `@swamp/cfgmgmt/file`; git reads it, so the token never enters argv\n  %%> - fetches the committed source into the home's release layout and checks out the commit\n  %%> - leaves `current` absent until the first deploy\n  SW->>N: Write .env, prepare Vite+<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · vp install\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - creates the `orbit-env` group when the Node lacks it, and gives it traverse on the home with one `setfacl` argv, as Caddy has on a release\n  %%> - writes the rendered `.env` from `vault.get env-5152` with `@swamp/cfgmgmt/file`, mode `0640`, group `orbit-env`, so the Node agent can report its key names\n  %%> - installs and checks the pinned Vite+ runtime; a Project without one skips this\n  opt --sqlite-source-path\n    SW->>C: Snapshot the database<br/>SQLite export · dump\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - SQLite: makes a consistent copy of the chosen file on Node 12\n    %%> Why here:\n    %%> - this was the kind `database.clone`; it is now a step of the clone\n    SW->>N: Install the database<br/>home/database.sqlite · restore\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - SQLite: installs the copy as `/home/orbit-app-7/database.sqlite`\n  end\n  SW->>N: Add Processes, Schedules<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/systemd · forEach\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - installs the Project's production Process definitions, enabled but not started; the first deploy starts them\n  %%> - installs the per-minute Laravel scheduler as a timer on Node 14\n  %%> Other Schedules:\n  %%> - become native Swamp schedules; the Gateway publishes them after success\n  opt web Project needs a preview Route\n    SW->>N: Make a CSR on the Node<br/>openssl req · key stays on Node\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - makes a key and a certificate request for `shop.com.prod.orbit` on Node 14\n    SW->>GW: Sign the CSR<br/>operation:direct:certificate-sign\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the one allowed step→Gateway call; the Orbit CA key stays in the Gateway\n    GW-->>SW: Signed certificate<br/>Orbit CA key stays in Gateway\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the certificate for the preview domain\n    SW->>N: Publish preview Route<br/>child route-create-site · Ansible\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - installs the certificate and adds the Caddy site for `shop.com.prod.orbit` on Node 14\n    %%> - same-Node work: Caddy on Node 14 serves the Instance home\n    %%> - reloads Caddy; other sites keep serving\n    %%> No DNS step:\n    %%> - `shop.com.prod.orbit` is inside Node 14's TLD, and private DNS answers the whole TLD\n  end\n  SW->>N: Check the clone<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt check · curl\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - checks the checked-out commit and the `.env` file\n  %%> - probes the preview site over TLS\n  opt --sqlite-source-path\n    SW->>C: Remove the snapshot<br/>always runs · best effort\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - removes the snapshot and transfer files on Node 12, 209 and Node 14\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - after success and after failure\n    %%> Failure:\n    %%> - a failed cleanup leaves harmless files and does not fail the clone\n  end\n  Note over SW: Report the result<br/>commit · warnings · error code\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - prints the clone's public result: the checked-out commit and key-only warnings, or the error code\n  %%> Why a step of its own:\n  %%> - each chart step above is a child workflow, and a failed step keeps no output; the Gateway reads the result from this parent step\n  alt all steps pass\n    N-->>SW: commit, warnings\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the checked-out commit\n    %%> - key-only warnings, such as development service references in `.env`\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event · outputs\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record result<br/>Instance 43 active · no release\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Instance 43 active, with its preview Route, Processes and Schedules\n    %%> - with `--sqlite-source-path`, the SQLite copy is registered and attached under `DB`\n    %%> - task #5152 succeeds\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - review the warnings with `env:update`, add deploy steps, then `instance:deploy 43`\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or timeout\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a check, fetch, restore or Caddy step fails, or SSH fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed_step, error_code\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code\n    Note over GW: Keep Instance 43 reserved<br/>task failed · rerun reuses it\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Instance 43 stays reserved and shows the error; task #5152 fails\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - what Node 14 got stays; the candidate is unchanged\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - the same request reuses Instance 43 and repeats only what is missing\n    %%> - a changed candidate, Node, preview name, branch or SQLite path returns `instance.clone_retry_conflict`\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5152\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/container/create-production": {
   "kind": "instance.clone",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design, production containers (ADR 0263, docs/reference/production-containers.md#after-a-clone): instance:clone records a production Instance on an app-prod Node, with its storage volume and preview Route, and makes no container. Its first release container is the cold hot of the first deploy (container/deploy-production.mmd). A checkout production Instance reaches its first release container through container/migrate-production.mmd. When the charts move into place, this replaces the clone chart.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:clone\n  %% kind: instance.clone\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane C icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant C as Candidate<br/>Node · container\n  participant N as app-prod Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: instance:clone CAND NODE<br/>NAME --preview-name=NAME\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:clone CANDIDATE NODE NAME --preview-name=NAME`\n  %%> - syntax: `<candidate> <node> <name> --preview-name=NAME [--branch=BRANCH] [--sqlite-source-path=PATH] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - a development Instance, in a container or a checkout, as the candidate\n  Note over GW: Validate, reserve Instance<br/>render .env · TASK\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the Node is an active `app-prod` Node with the container setup and a TLD\n  %%> - the Project has no production Instance on the Node, and the preview domain is free\n  %%> Records:\n  %%> - the Instance, its environment, its Process and Schedule copies, its preview Route\n  %%> - the rendered `.env`, kept for the first deploy; with a SQLite source it names `/srv/shared/database.sqlite`\n  Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - holds each Node that serves the preview Route's site until the task ends, as every Route write does\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-clone\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - no GitHub token and no `.env`: the clone fetches no code; the first deploy does\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>TASK\n  SW->>C: Check candidate source<br/>Ansible · tags candidate\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - checks the repository, branch and commit and a clean tree, read-only\n  %%> Why on the Node:\n  %%> - the candidate's tree can change after admission, and the clone depends on it\n  SW->>N: Create storage volume<br/>Ansible · tags volume\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - creates the filesystem custom volume `instance-<id>-shared` on the Node's pool, with each shared path as a folder\n  %%> - writes the Instance's upstream file with a `503` answer, so a Route site can import it before the first release\n  opt --sqlite-source-path\n    SW->>C: Snapshot the database<br/>SQLite export · dump\n    SW->>N: Install into the volume<br/>shared/database.sqlite\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - installs the consistent copy as `database.sqlite` in the volume; the copy's transfer files go after success and failure\n  end\n  opt web Project needs a preview Route\n    SW->>N: Make a CSR on the Node<br/>openssl req · key stays on Node\n    SW->>GW: Sign the CSR<br/>operation:direct:certificate-sign\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the one allowed step→Gateway call; the Orbit CA key stays in the Gateway\n    GW-->>SW: Signed certificate\n    SW->>N: Publish preview Route<br/>child route-create-site · Ansible\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - installs the certificate and adds the Caddy site, which imports the Instance's upstream file, then reloads Caddy\n    %%> No DNS step:\n    %%> - the preview name is inside the Node's TLD\n  end\n  alt all steps pass\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    Note over GW: Record result<br/>Instance active · no release\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Instance is active with its volume and preview Route; no release container exists yet\n    %%> - its Schedules get their triggers when the first deploy records a live release\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - `instance:deploy`: its cold hot is the first release container\n  else a step fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step, error code\n    Note over GW: Keep Instance reserved<br/>rerun reuses it\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - the volume and site stay; the candidate is unchanged\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - the same request repeats only what is missing; a changed request returns `instance.clone_retry_conflict`\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/complete": {
   "kind": "instance.complete",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design (docs/reference/development-containers.md#complete-a-clone and #a-clone-create-is-two-operations): the second Operation of a clone create. It starts the Processes, fills the database, starts a nested fleet, publishes the Route when its create did not, and checks the clone; only its success makes the clone `active`.\n  %% Decided by the owner (2026-11-14): the create publishes the address when its Route Nodes are free at admission (container/create.mmd). The completion publishes only a Route that is not published: Nodes held at the create's admission, a publication that failed in the create, or a retry after a failed publication.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:complete\n  %% kind: instance.complete\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CL icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CL as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>Instance container\n  CL->>GW: instance:complete INSTANCE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:complete 42`\n  %%> - syntax: `instance:complete <instance> [--json]`\n  %%> Callers:\n  %%> - the command, to retry after a failed completion\n  %%> - the create's terminal projection, in the same transaction that ends the create\n  Note over GW: Check the clone<br/>take the Instance lock\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - a container clone, `source_resolved`, with its starting commit recorded\n  %%> - its T3 server is registered\n  %%> - no other Operation holds the Instance\n  %%> Captures:\n  %%> - the create's admitted database plan and Processes\n  %%> - a Route whose sites are not published: its publication from the current site catalog, and its serving Nodes held from admission to the end, as Route create does\n  %%> - a Route the create published: no publication and no Node hold\n  alt a check fails\n    %% path: refused\n    GW--xCL: Refused<br/>instance.complete_unavailable\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - nothing changes; no Operation starts\n    %%> - a held Instance lock refuses with `operation.target_busy`\n  else nothing is left on the Node\n    %% path: engine\n    Note over GW: End at once on the engine<br/>clone active, no Swamp run\n    %%> When: the create said `services: seed` (the seed holds the services part), and there is no database, Valkey user, Route publication or fleet start\n    %%> The engine runs it with no step; its projection makes the clone `active` as a succeeded run does\n  else the clone can be completed\n    %% path: main\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-complete\n    %%> What it sends:\n    %%> - the intent with the launcher program, and one vault copy: the release scripts beside the Process files, one line\n    GW-->>CL: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID\n    opt a database\n      SW->>N: Create the database<br/>database steps · SSH\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - the clone's own database on the default's server, then a copy of the default's data; the clone's own Valkey user beside it\n      %%> On failure:\n      %%> - `failed_step: database`\n    end\n    opt a nested-fleet clone whose fleet does not start in the background\n      SW->>N: Start the fleet<br/>clone-fleet · Ansible\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - runs only when the create's result has no `fleet_start: background`: a Swamp create, or a seed without the tunnel's boot unit\n      %%> - starts the nested containers and waits for Doctor and the three agents\n      %%> - runs under `ic-clone-fleet-<node>-<instance id % 64>`, one of 64 names per Node; copies started at once start their fleets side by side, because Swamp fails a step that waits 60 s for a name another run holds\n      %%> On failure:\n      %%> - `failed_step: fleet` with the fleet phase's code\n    end\n    SW->>N: Start, check services<br/>clone-services · SSH\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - one dispatch: stages the release scripts from the completion's one copy into this run's own private folder; the create's folder is not read\n    %%> - the services: PHP settings, file rights, Process files, web config, the Node store, then the units\n    %%> - file rights: root's workspace pass, the same program as a deployment's (swamp/ansible/instance_workspace_owner.py): the group gets back what the owner has, and `agent` gets each entry that `orbit` owns, a link itself and never its target (ADR 0268); a copy of an older seed gets its owner here\n    %%> - refuses when the checkout mark names another Instance\n    %%> - writes no tracked file: the user's work in the checkout stays as it is\n    %%> - then the check: the checkout mark, the agent's Git identity, PHP and every running unit\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - the stage or the services: `failed_step: workspace`\n    %%> - a dead unit: `failed_step: check`, `instance.container_process_inactive` with its `process_failure` receipt\n    opt a Route its create did not publish\n      SW->>N: Publish the Route<br/>operation-route-create\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - in its own job, beside the container steps from the run's start\n      %%> - only when the admission captured the Route: Nodes held at the create's admission, `route_publication: failed`, or a retry after a failed publication\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - the Route create children unchanged: CSR, sign, site, TLS probe\n      %%> On failure:\n      %%> - `failed_step: route`\n    end\n    alt the run succeeds\n      %% path: completed\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result\n      Note over GW: Record active<br/>one transaction\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the Route is `active`, then the Instance `active` with `first_setup_pending` false\n      %%> - for a task copy, in the same transaction, its reserved task runs and the task's completion-failure assistance request ends; any other request stays\n    else a step fails or the run is cancelled\n      %% path: failed\n      SW->>N: Withdraw the Route<br/>route-withdraw\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - only when this run's Route step ran; no half Route stays\n      %%> - an address the create published stays up, answering 502, until a retry or `instance:destroy`, which withdraws it; a retry that succeeds keeps it as it is\n      SW--xGW: Run failed or cancelled<br/>failed step, error_code\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the Instance stays `source_resolved` with `failed_step` and `error_code`; the container keeps running and T3 keeps its thread\n      %%> - `recovery.retry.command` is `instance:complete`; `instance:destroy` removes the clone\n      %%> - for a task copy, Orbit keeps the copy: its task stays `reserved` and asks for assistance with this retry\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - the same command runs the whole completion again, with the steps a create retry runs\n    end\n  end\n  loop until terminal\n    CL->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CL: Status and result\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/container/create": {
   "kind": "instance.create",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Container runtime (ADR 0250 step 3, docs/reference/development-containers.md#create): every new non-default development Instance is a ZFS clone of the Project's container default on its Node. A proof copy may clone another Node's current seed (docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-seed-on-each-ci-node). Create ends when the thread is usable: checkout, T3 and registration. Its terminal projection admits instance.complete for the database, nested fleet and Processes. Create publishes the address when the Route Nodes are free; otherwise completion publishes it. No build list applies. The kind and step names follow docs/cli/instance/create.mmd; container.clone marks a clone. The first default follows container/create-default.mmd.\n  %% On the Gateway engine (ADR 0271): with `instance.create` in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`, a container clone that the engine covers runs as the executor's `instance` family instead of the Swamp clone steps (docs/reference/gateway-engine.md#instancecreate): its steps are container/create-engine.mmd. Its address is left to its completion (`route_publication: deferred`, reason `engine_completion`).\n  %% Five Swamp dispatches for a ready clone (2026-11-19: the copy step waits for its seed itself, so no seed-wait step; 2026-11-15): the copy step stages the release scripts from the Gateway's stage copy, so a clone runs no `input` or `clone-input` step; `clone-register` builds the create's result, so a ready clone skips `finish`. Each Swamp behavior step costs about 0.9 s of dispatch, an assert none. The step names `clone-container`, `clone-t3` and `clone-register` stay, in this order: Conn's create card reads them.\n  %% Decided by the owner (2026-11-14) and the orchestrator (c3): the address is published during the create, beside the copy, in job `publication` (`clone-route-csr-<i>`, `clone-route`). The admission takes the Route Nodes only when it can and never waits for them: a Caddy-only publication shares them with removals and other such creates (at most eight on a Node), any other takes them alone; the Gateway frees them when `clone-route` ends, not at the create's end. Nodes held by another Route write leave the publication to the completion, as before.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:create\n  %% kind: instance.create\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane C icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  participant C as Container<br/>via incus exec\n  CLI->>GW: instance:create<br/>PROJECT NODE NAME\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:create PROJECT NODE NAME`\n  %%> - syntax: `<project> <node> <name> [--root=PATH] [--domain=HOST] [--branch=BRANCH] [--database-server=SLUG] [--wait-active]`\n  Note over GW: Validate, reserve Instance<br/>address · T3 port · .env\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has node access to the target Node, an active `app-dev` Node\n  %%> - no Node build list applies; nested_fleet still requires a Node that hosts nested fleets, else instance.nested_fleet_host_unavailable (409), before any record; the clone takes its default's Incus project\n  %%> - a checkout default refuses with instance.container_required (409); migrate it first; a missing default, wrong Node, inactive default or missing seed returns instance.default_missing; an asleep or stopped default is a valid source: the clone copies the seed and never wakes it\n  %%> - a proof copy may go to another Node that offers proof places when that Node's seed is the default's current seed (the same commit and time taken); it clones that seed, and the database refuses any other clone on another Node than its default\n  %%> - the T3 recording contribution is loaded, even while Conn is off, else `operation.kind_unavailable`; Conn and a ready model route are optional\n  %%> - the recipe's agent folder pins the program of the Project's `container_server`, else `project.container_server_unavailable` (409) before any record\n  %%> - before the admission transaction, the Gateway reads the Project's agent instructions, the `agent/` folder of its Documents (development-containers.md#agent-instructions): at most 32 files and 32 KiB of UTF-8 text (no control character but a tab or a line end) with plain skill names, else `projects.agent_instructions_too_large` or `projects.agent_instructions_invalid` (422) before any record; a store failure refuses with the store's own error\n  %%> - a database on a Database server clones on the default's server; another server refuses `instance.database_clone_unsupported`\n  %%> - a nested-fleet Project: the Node has the nested-fleet switch, else the placement rule refuses\n  %%> - a nested-fleet default whose runtime records an inner volume, while a deployment of it is open: `instance.default_seed_in_flight` (409) before any record, since the deployment retakes the two halves of the seed one after the other\n  %%> - a nested-fleet default whose runtime records an inner volume: the clone's runtime records its own `inner-<id>`, and `container.clone` names the default's volume; every other clone, a copy of the first nested-fleet default included, records none\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - admission records `model_route`: `ready: true`, or `ready: false` with `error_code` and `message`; it is returned in the create result, and human output prints one line when unavailable\n  %%> - the Instance is reserved with its runtime record: container name, bridge address, the next free T3 port after the Node's last one, wrapping from 3999 to 3800, the Project's `container_server`, and the default as clone source\n  %%> - `.env` is rendered from the Project's env template; it, the placeholder model key and a repository read token go to the vault\n  %%> - each attachment of the default marked shared is attached to the clone as it is (same connection, prefix and network, still shared) before `.env` renders, so `.env` holds its keys and the container intent its network path\n  %%> - the clone workspace reads one private JSON vault value with `.env`, the Process environment files and the names of the keys the clone owns, of the default's connection keys, of its credential keys and of the shared connections' keys, and the default's Route host with the clone's; SSH stdin takes the whole value\n  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent and `container.clone`\n  %%> - the Route record and the database records are reserved here; the create creates no database: the completion does\n  %%> - the Route publication is captured from the current site catalog: a Route the capture refuses (for example `cluster.router_required`) refuses the create before any record\n  %%> Route Nodes:\n  %%> - free (no publisher holds a serving Node and none waits for one; for a Caddy-only create fewer than eight shared writers, for any other no shared writer): the create takes them in this transaction and publishes the address; a Caddy-only publication (no PHP pool, DNS record, Ingress site or LAN rule) takes a shared hold, any other takes them alone\n  %%> - held: the create never waits; its intent carries no publication, and the completion publishes the address as before\n  %%> - the admission records why (`nodes_held`, `share_cap` or `waiter_first`) and logs one info line with it and the shared writers on each Node\n  %%> - the decision is one insert per Node into the hold tables in one transaction: Caddy-only creates admitted together all publish; of two other creates admitted together, exactly one takes the Nodes\n  %%> - a retained create retry records the Project's current development Processes in this admission transaction; changed definitions update their records, removed definitions remove theirs\n  %%> - each Process takes its desired state from the default's copy of the same definition: stopped on the default stays stopped (installed, not started, startable by `process:start`); failed, absent or running runs\n  Note over GW: Commit, push copies<br/>queue start · answer 202\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n  GW->>SW: Start run from queue<br/>operation-instance-create\n  opt a Route, its Nodes taken at admission\n    SW->>N: Publish the address<br/>clone-route-csr · clone-route\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - in its own job, `publication`, at the run's first level, beside the copy; no clone step waits for it\n    %%> - `clone-route-input` writes the run folder and the playbooks that the site child reads, beside the CSR: a clone runs no `input` step\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - per leaf, the key and CSR on the Node and the signature (`clone-route-csr-<i>`); then the Route create children (`clone-route`): the site in the Node's Caddy, the private DNS, the Ingress edge of a public Route and the TLS probe\n    %%> - the site puts the Route's own Caddy groups into what the Node holds, under the Node lock; every other block, its order and the global options stay as the Node holds them (docs/reference/routes.md#publish-a-development-site); its Node steps take one of 16 names per Node (`route-site-<node>-<route id % 16>`)\n    %%> - the site step skips the Route access and `APP_URL` (`prepared` names this Node): the copy's root disk does not exist yet, and `.env` comes with the checkout\n    %%> - Caddy proxies to the bridge address reserved at admission; until the completion starts the units, the address answers TLS and then 502\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - both steps allow their failure, so the run and the create still succeed; `result.route_publication` is `failed`, the Route records `instance.create_route_failed`, and the completion publishes it again\n    %%> - a merge that a Node refuses changes no file there and marks the run folder: `route_publication` is `deferred`, no Route warning, and the completion publishes the address\n    SW-->>GW: clone-route ended<br/>native progress event\n    Note over GW: Route active, free Nodes<br/>one transaction\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the run's progress event of `clone-route` succeeded; no step calls the Gateway\n    %%> In one transaction:\n    %%> - the Route turns `active` with its sites published, so the next Route writer's render keeps this site\n    %%> - the create's Route Nodes are freed, and waiting Route writes start oldest first\n    %%> Otherwise:\n    %%> - a failed or skipped `clone-route`, or a lost event, keeps the Nodes until the create's terminal state frees them\n    %%> - a waiting Route write that rendered its whole Caddyfile at its admission is refused at its start with `route.publication_stale`, because this site changed what its Nodes serve; a waiting create renders again and a removal composes from the live file\n    %%> - a Route that another write took meanwhile, such as a retiring one, stays as it is\n  end\n  opt a proof copy\n    SW->>N: Hold the CI place<br/>native clone-ci-hold\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - first in the clone job, before the copy exists; every other clone skips it\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - first checks that the `ci-runner` role's hold script is on the Node. When it is missing, the step calls nothing and prints a refusal; the copy does not start, and the create fails with `instance.ci_hold_unavailable`, naming the Node\n    %%> - runs the `ci-runner` role's hold script as root: `orbit-ci-runner-hold hold <copy> <places>`, with the Node's `ci_places`\n    %%> - records the copy's hold, then stops idle runner services on the Node down to the places left to runners; a busy runner is never stopped (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#proof-places-on-a-ci-node)\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - the copy does not start; the copy's destroy releases the hold\n  end\n    SW->>N: Clone default container<br/>native clone-container\n    %%> Stage, the step's first part:\n    %%> - SSH stdin is the Gateway's launch copy, one vault value on ONE line: `stage` (the clone script, its helpers and the container intent from the admitting Gateway's release) beside `private` (the read token's credential line and the model environment). Swamp turns a vault value with a newline into one regular expression, which a 32 KB line breaks (release 2080)\n    %%> - Swamp masks the launch copy only as a whole; the clone script's own redaction (each stdin line and value, credentials by shape) keeps a lone token or model key out of a failure detail\n    %%> - the launcher (the intent's `clone_launcher`) writes `stage` into a private folder for this run and gives the clone script only `private`; later steps run the staged launcher by path\n    %%> - scripts and intent never fill the SSH command; a copy that cannot be staged fails the step with `instance.clone_input_failed`\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the copy supplies the full root disk device (type, path, pool and size), whether the seed inherits it or stores it locally\n    %%> - `incus copy` of the default container's snapshot `seed-<commit>`, a ZFS clone; a stopped default needs no start\n    %%> Seed (docs/reference/development-containers.md#a-retake-keeps-the-seed):\n    %%> - reads the default's snapshots and copies the admitted seed when it is there; a normal copy reads them once\n    %%> - a retake at the admitted commit deletes `seed-<commit>` and renames its `next-` to that name a moment later: while the admitted seed is missing and its `next-` is there, the step reads the snapshots again every 2 s, for at most 120 s, the bound of a deployment's `seed` phase\n    %%> - the same 120 s bound covers a restart of the Node's Incus: a read before the copy that fails or stalls while the local Incus API does not answer waits for the API and reads once more; an answer of Incus is never retried. After either wait, a timed-out one too, one more read decides. The step's budget is 420 s, its SSH 430 s: the wait, then 300 s of work\n    %%> - when a deployment replaced it by a newer seed and no `next-` of the admitted seed is there, copies the newest `seed-` snapshot and records its commit for the later phases: a branch that the Node's mirror lacks starts at that commit, which the copied seed's repository holds\n    %%> - the admitted seed's `next-` still there at the bound (a retake that did not end within 120 s) fails the create with `instance.default_seed_moved` before any change, as before; a default without any `seed-` or `next-` snapshot refuses with `instance.default_seed_missing`\n    %%> Inner volume (a clone whose intent has `inner_volume` only; every other clone makes no volume call and copies the container as before):\n    %%> - the default's volume must hold its own snapshot `seed-<commit>`, the other half of the seed, else `instance.default_seed_incomplete` before any change; a deployment of the default takes the pair\n    %%> - before the container: `incus storage volume copy` of that snapshot to `inner-<id>`, a ZFS clone; Incus gives the copy a new btrfs fsid (about 4 s in the 2026-11-14 test); a volume of that name without its container, left by a failed copy, is deleted and copied again. The clone inherits `volmode=dev` from the pool's `custom` dataset, which the Node's host converge sets, so its device is made once (docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-inner-volume)\n    %%> - then `incus storage volume set` writes the intent's `inner_volume.size` on `inner-<id>`: a seed snapshot that a seed copy moved to a CI Node records `size: 0`, and the copy takes the snapshot's config; the ZFS volume keeps its size, so only the record changes\n    %%> - the container copy sets the disk `inner-pool` to `source=inner-<id>`, so the copy never attaches the default's volume\n    %%> - a failed container copy deletes the volume it copied, so a failed create leaves no volume without its container\n    %%> - the copy gets its own bridge address and ACL, and the proxy device `t3`: NAT from the Node's WireGuard address and the T3 port to T3's port 3773\n    %%> - the container's ACL lets the Node's Caddy reach port 8080, WireGuard peers reach port 3773, and the container reach its database server's port\n    %%> - UFW: a route rule admits WireGuard peers to T3, below a deny for the task VM range; a rule admits the container to its database server's port\n    %%> - before the first start, the stopped copy gets the Orbit root in its CA bundle when the seed's bundle lacks it, so Chromium trusts Orbit's sites; a copy without a bundle refuses\n    %%> - before the first start, the stopped copy gets the composed `NO_PROXY` drop-ins, the bytes `env:sync` writes\n    %%> - before the first start, the stopped copy gets T3's files and last `/etc/orbit/instance`, so its boot starts T3; they go in beside the boot files, which share no folder with them, and before the `NO_PROXY` drop-ins; a drop-in turns off T3's default dependencies, so T3 waits for nftables, the local file systems, the journal and sysctl, not for `sysinit.target`; a second drop-in sets `ExecStart` to the program of the runtime's recorded container server, `bin/t3` or `bin/conn-server`, which every later call in the container runs too\n    %%> - before the first start, the stopped copy gets `/etc/machine-info` with `PRETTY_HOSTNAME=<instance>.<project slug>`, so T3's first start shows that name; the copy's Incus templates leave the file alone\n    %%> - the clone script starts the copy without the `start` phase; its services completion runs the shared app-account swap before the Node store command, so an old-seed normal copy moves to `www-data` there; a nested-fleet copy keeps `orbit` and `work` (docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-app-users-name)\n    %%> - a nested-fleet copy starts only through the start guard: its config and devices exactly as the intent writes them, no `inner-pool` device without an inner volume in the intent, the copy's inner volume with exactly its keys, and a root volume without `zfs.delegate`\n    %%> - before the first start, a stopped copy with an empty on-disk idmap and a shifted source is unshifted on a btrfs pool, preserving owners, modes, ACLs and capabilities; another pool driver refuses with `instance.clone_idmap_unshift_unsupported`; idmapped-mount sources need no change; any normalization failure deletes the incomplete copy, fails with its code and leaves a retry to copy the seed again\n    %%> - right after the start, after the copy's own templates rewrote `/etc/hosts` and `/etc/hostname`: one exec writes `/etc/hostname`, sets the kernel hostname and reads `/etc/hosts`; the clone then writes the plan's private names block with the renderer `env:sync` uses and the `127.0.1.1` line of the machine name, each keeping the other's lines, only when the file changes; T3 has already started with the boot by then, and its label came from the `/etc/machine-info` boot file; a failure of any of it is `instance.clone_private_names_failed`\n    %%> Mail:\n    %%> - the same launch applies the exact SMTP path before the four root-owned mail units; no separate mail playbook or step\n    %%> - two IPv4 loopback sockets forward 1025 and 2525; foreign units or listeners skip setup without stopping their Process\n    %%> - the seed's mail socket links stay: the units are the Node's, so a clone's launch reports `unchanged` unless the Node's endpoint changed\n    %%> On mail failure:\n    %%> - keep a mail warning and continue workspace, T3 and registration; mail:update remains the repair command\n    %%> ACL:\n    %%> - reads the copy's own ACL by its name, never the list of every ACL on the Node; a missing ACL is created, a different one is set\n    %%> Branch (a branch the seed already holds needs no network and no mirror):\n    %%> - the branch is read and fetched on the Node beside the ID map step and the start: it needs nothing of the copy; the step waits for it before it ends\n    %%> - an explicit branch choice (`branch_selection`) runs no `ls-remote`: the admission read GitHub. A new branch whose admitted source commit is the seed commit fetches nothing; any other choice fetches its source into the Node's mirror with the read token\n    %%> - a create by name only reads GitHub with `ls-remote`: a branch on the origin is fetched into the Node's mirror; a branch the origin lacks fetches nothing\n    %%> - the Node's mirror holds the branch only when it was fetched; the token stays on the Node\n    %%> - a branch made on GitHub after the admission: the copy still starts at the admitted commit; a later push of it is refused as `tasks.not_fast_forward` when the GitHub branch has commits the copy lacks, and fast-forwards, losing no commit, when it does not\n    %%> Timing:\n    %%> - each clone phase prints the seconds of each of its parts; parts that run beside others (T3's files, the branch) keep their own seconds; the result carries them as `timings`, so every create shows where its time went\n    opt nested-fleet clone with a Club key\n      SW->>C: Deliver the Club key<br/>native clone-club-key\n      %%> When: right after the copy's first start; the copy's inner Swamp serve starts with the container and reads the key from `/run/orbit-club`\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - SSH stdin into the copy's /run (a tmpfs, never a snapshot); a new key restarts the nested Gateway's Swamp serve\n      %%> - the result's `nested_fleet_club_key` is what this step did; a clone that skips it says `missing`\n      %%> - the inner fleet starts later, in the completion ([instance:complete](docs/cli/instance/complete.mmd))\n      %%> On failure:\n      %%> - `instance.nested_club_key_not_tmpfs` or `instance.nested_club_key_delivery_failed`; the checkout and T3 do not run\n    end\n    SW->>C: Check out and wait for T3<br/>native clone-t3\n    %%> When: after the copy and the Club key; a failed copy or Club key skips it\n    %%> Why one step: the checkout, T3 and the sessions check were three steps on two levels, each with about 1 s of Swamp start; one SSH call runs them, the checkout beside T3\n    %%> Why beside: the T3 part never writes the workspace; T3 runs Git with `GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0`, so it takes no Git lock\n    %%> SSH stdin: the workspace's private JSON; the model environment is already in the copy, written by the copy step before the first start\n    %%> Checkout, beside the agent part and T3:\n    %%> - a branch in the Node's mirror goes in as a bundle; a branch the mirror lacks starts at the seed commit, which the seed's own repository holds, with no bundle; it is checked out in `/srv/workspace`; the group, setgid bits and default ACLs come with the clone\n    %%> - runs only the guest's `checkout` part: the bundle, the checkout mark, `.env`; PHP settings, Process files and units are the completion's `services` part\n    %%> - after the checkout it checks the branch and commit, then writes the root-owned mark `/var/lib/orbit/workspace-checkout` with the Instance id and commit; when the mark already names this Instance, a retry skips the fetch and checkout, so the branch never moves and the result's commit stays the mark's\n    %%> - writes `.env` for `www-data` with group `www-data`: the default's file from the seed, with the clone's own connection keys, the keys of the connections it shares with its default and `APP_URL`, without the default's other connection and Reverb keys or a copy of its credentials; `APP_KEY` stays the default's, because the cloned data is encrypted with it\n    %%> - `.env` names the clone's database before the completion creates it; nothing in the container reads it before the completion starts the units\n    %%> - a copied value that points at the default's Route host (its Route without a web root) points at the clone's Route host; the rest of the value stays\n    %%> - prints only the names of the copied, dropped, rewritten and shared keys; the result carries them as `environment`\n    %%> Task copy (`container.clone.clean_workspace`), at the first checkout, before the mark:\n    %%> - excludes `/.env`, clears index flags, then `git reset --hard` and, after it, `git clean -d -ff` without `-x` until a dry run finds nothing new, with the task check's ignore rules: the default's modified and untracked files go, what the start commit's ignore rules name stays\n    %%> - `git status --porcelain` must be empty, else the step fails\n    %%> - the result carries the counts and the first 20 names of the reset and removed paths as `workspace_cleaned`; a clone without the mark keeps the default's tree\n    %%> Agent part, then T3:\n    %%> - the agent part finds the model environment in place and writes the Claude Code and Codex settings that point at the model forwarder; a copy without the model environment fails the part\n    %%> - makes the agent folder `/home/agent/.config/orbit/agent` again from `clone.agent_instructions`, which only this part's intent carries, owned by root and readable by `agent`: `AGENTS.md` (the note of the Project type, then the Project's own text) and the Project's `.skills`; a path out of the folder, or a folder reached through a link, fails the part: `instance.create_agent_failed`\n    %%> - links `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` and `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` of `agent` to the folder's `AGENTS.md`, and `~/.claude/skills` and `~/.agents/skills` to its `.skills`, and writes `/etc/orbit/instance`, owned by root and readable by all; files the copy step wrote are found in place\n    %%> - the T3 part: a T3 that the copy's boot started is not started again (`timings.t3.at_boot` 1); a T3 that does not run is started here, and one that failed at boot is started once more after `reset-failed`, named `restart:t3`; T3 creates its identity at its first start\n    %%> - while T3 starts or runs, T3's own CLI issues the `Orbit Gateway` and `Orbit Swamp` sessions at once, as `agent`; the tokens go only to the run's private folder\n    %%> - after T3 answers, the T3 project `/srv/workspace` is added as `agent`; during T3's start that call fails\n    %%> - waits up to 30 s for `/.well-known/t3/environment`, else `instance.t3_unready`\n    %%> Sessions check, after both:\n    %%> - issues no session: it checks the two that the T3 part issued against the running T3, and the branch and the checkout mark\n    %%> - checks no Process unit: the units start in the completion, whose check refuses a dead one with `instance.container_process_inactive`\n    %%> - the checked reply replaces the issued sessions in the private `t3.json`; the step prints the checkout's lines and T3's receipt, with when T3 answered and when the sessions passed\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - before each part the step writes the part's name to its private `t3.json`; the checkout's name is written only when the checkout failed and the T3 part did not; `clone-session-copy` brings that file beside the run folder\n    %%> - the result's `failed_step` follows the part: `workspace` with `instance.create_workspace_failed`, `t3` with `instance.create_agent_failed` or `instance.t3_unready`, `register` with `instance.create_register_failed` for the sessions check\n    %%> - the Operation's failure detail is the part's own line, such as `instance.container_checkout_failed` or `instance.container_t3_failed`, or the timeout\n    SW->>N: Copy the checked sessions<br/>native clone-session-copy\n    %%> What it does: brings the checked reply from the Node's private folder to `<run folder>-t3.json` on 209; after a failure, the failed part's name. It is the clone job's last step\n    SW->>C: Register the T3 server<br/>native clone-register\n    %%> When: the first step of the create job, after the clone steps and the publication, beside the private folder's removal\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - see conn/environment-renew.mmd: the step checks the environment id and scopes, and the sessions go to the vault as sensitive fields\n    %%> - removes the copied `t3.json` on every path, before any check can refuse\n    %%> - builds the create's result from the same inputs as `finish` (one map), with only the sessions' vault keys; a ready result removes the run folder, so `finish` and the cleanup step skip\n    %%> - a refused check fails the step: `finish` then builds the failed result with `failed_step: register`\n  alt all steps pass in budget\n    C-->>SW: T3 answers\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - `clone-register` starts when the clone steps and the publication have both ended; at the measured times the publication ends first\n    %%> - its ready result is the run's result (the Gateway reads `clone-register` when `finish` skipped); `finish`, the stop and the cleanup step skip, and the run ends with the `accepted` assert, which costs no dispatch\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    Note over GW: Read baseline, record T3<br/>admit instance.complete\n    %%> Before the transaction:\n    %%> - reads the workspace baseline over SSH (`ContainerWorkspaceRead::baseline`, at most 4 s) and the T3 session from the vault; no lock waits on the network\n    %%> In one transaction:\n    %%> - the clone stays `source_resolved` with its `starting_commit`; the runtime is `running`; the Conn layer records the T3 server from the result\n    %%> - writes `workspace_baseline` and `workspace_baseline_at`, then turns the create `succeeded`, so the baseline is older than the create's end and than any pairing\n    %%> - after the create row is terminal, admits `instance.complete` with the Instance lock, so nothing takes the Instance in between; the result's `completion_operation_id` names it\n    %%> - the completion's vault copy and its Swamp start run after the commit\n    %%> - a completion that is refused all the same (the Route capture again, after a change to the fleet) is recorded as a failed `instance.complete` with that code, `failed_step: complete` and the recovery `instance:complete`; another error is recorded as `operation.admission_failed`; the create still succeeds and names it\n    %%> - a `route_publication` of `published` turns the Route `active` here too, when its progress event was lost; `failed` marks the Route `failed_step: native-create`, `instance.create_route_failed`\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the user can pair with T3 and work; the Processes, the database and a nested fleet follow in the completion\n    %%> - `route_publication`: `published` (the address answers TLS), `deferred` (the Route Nodes were held at admission, or a Node refused the merge of the site: the completion publishes) or `failed` (the completion publishes again); absent without a Route\n    %%> - `route_publication_reason` beside `deferred` only: `nodes_held`, `share_cap`, `waiter_first` (at admission) or `node_refused` (in `clone-route`); the API result carries it; Conn and the web views show nothing new, and the CLI prints it only inside the whole result (`--json`, `operation:show`)\n    %%> - \"fully set up\" is only the Instance's `status: active`, which a succeeded completion sets ([instance:complete](docs/cli/instance/complete.mmd))\n    %%> - the result's `processes_stopped` names the Processes the completion installs but leaves stopped, as on the default\n    %%> - the receipt includes when T3 answered and when its sessions passed\n    %%> On a failed baseline read:\n    %%> - the create fails with `instance.workspace_baseline_unavailable`, `failed_step: register`; the T3 server is not recorded and no completion starts\n    %%> - the container keeps running; a retry of the create runs again over it, and `instance:destroy` removes it\n  else Instance already created\n    Note over GW: Skipped — already created<br/>instance.exists\n  else a step fails or the budget ends\n    C--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or timeout\n    Note over SW: finish: the failed result<br/>stop-input: the stop's run folder\n    SW->>N: Attempt container stop<br/>incus stop\n    %%> What stays:\n    %%> - the reservation, runtime, and any container, workspace and owned database records stay until the owner acts; the create made no database\n    %%> - a published address stays `active` (502 until a retry or `instance:destroy`); a publication that started and did not succeed is withdrawn by `withdraw-route`, under the Nodes the create still holds\n    %%> - a refused retry removes none of them; stop is attempted; failed compensation leaves its step failed\n    %%> - shared service endpoint records and rules may remain even after destroy until endpoint lifecycle work is live\n    %%> - failed and cancelled creates, including cancellation before work starts, report retained_for_retry, the same create command with a new idempotency key, and forced destroy\n    %%> Cleanup:\n    %%> - remove the private clone folder after success or failure, beside `clone-register`, even if staging or the first launch failed\n    %%> - the cleanup step removes the run folder and the copied `t3.json` after a failure\n    %%> - a failed `stop-input` leaves the stop without its playbooks: the container keeps running until a retry replaces it or `instance:destroy` removes it\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step, error_code\n    Note over GW: Record the failure\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again; it resumes on the same container\n    %%> - a resume never moves a branch this Instance checked out: commits on it stay\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n  opt --wait-active\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show COMPLETION\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    end\n    CLI->>GW: instance:show INSTANCE\n    GW-->>CLI: status\n    %%> - exits 0 only when the Instance is `active`; any other end exits 1\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/create": {
   "kind": "instance.create",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): a withdrawal clears the Router answers of the Route from the private DNS catalog in the same step, not only its records.\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Gateway holds each Route-serving Node from admission to the terminal state; a second Route write on it waits (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).\n  %% Target design: new development Instances are containers. The container charts own their execution; the checkout phases below apply only to retries of existing checkout Instances.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten, rules 1-28 (rule 22: Ansible phases, 0600 credential file for the private fetch).\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Node work is two Ansible phases, `checkout` (clone, check out, write .env) and `setup`, behind one input step. Measured on Node 209: every placed Swamp step costs about 1.1 s of dispatch before it does any work, and the old flow ran 8 steps for the checkout and one per setup step.\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the run-folder cleanup runs beside the result step, not before it (a Swamp job runs steps by dependency depth, so they were one after the other in a create without setup or Route). Since 2026-11-14 a ready create's result step removes the run folder itself, and the cleanup runs only after a failure or a failed removal.\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Route's key, CSR and signature run at the start, beside the checkout, because they touch only the certificate folder and the Route child ran them in series after the setup steps (about 7 s of a 46 s create).\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Route runs beside the setup steps, not after the Processes. The checkout phase grants Caddy its access to the checkout and writes APP_URL (what setup creates inherits the default entries, so nothing races the setup), and the signature is made in the same child as the key, beside the checkout. A Laravel create spent 19 s of 42 s on the Route after the setup. The served root must exist in the checkout (`public` does).\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): a failed create withdraws the Route it published and nothing else. The chart said it removed everything the run made (the same steps as instance:destroy); docs/reference/instance-setup.md says a failed create retains its reserved Instance and owned checkout, and a removal there is irreversible. The Route is the part that serves traffic, so it goes; the Instance, checkout, Processes and database stay for `instance:destroy` or a fresh create.\n  %% Decided by the owner (2026-11-14): a container clone publishes its address during its create, beside the copy, when its Route Nodes are free at admission; the Gateway frees them when `clone-route` ends. The clone path is container/create.mmd; this checkout flow keeps `route-csr` and `route`.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:create\n  %% kind: instance.create\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: instance:create<br/>PROJECT NODE NAME\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:create PROJECT NODE NAME`\n  %%> Admission:\n  %%> - every new default is a container on an app-dev Node; there is no build list\n  %%> - every new non-default clones the container default; no default refuses with instance.default_missing, a checkout default refuses with instance.container_required (409) and must migrate first\n  %%> - new containers follow instance/container/create-default and instance/container/create; only an existing checkout may retry the host phases below\n  %%> - syntax: `<project> <node> <name> [--root=PATH] [--domain=HOST] [--branch=BRANCH] [--database-server=SLUG] [--wait-active]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Project, the Node, and the Instance name\n  %%> - a name equal to the Project's default branch creates the `default` Instance\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, reserve Instance<br/>render .env\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target Node\n  %%> - nested_fleet requires a Node that hosts nested fleets, else instance.nested_fleet_host_unavailable (409); an existing checkout retry for that Project refuses with instance.nested_fleet_container_required (409), before any record\n  %%> - the name is one DNS label of at most 63 characters; a web Route has labels of at most 63 characters and at most 64 characters in total while the Node CSR uses the domain as its common name (`route.domain_invalid`), checked before the Operation starts\n  %%> - the target is an active `app-dev` Node and not a task VM; a production Node is refused (`instance.candidate_required`)\n  %%> - a CI-only Node (`ci-runner`, CI places, no `app-dev`) is refused first, `instance.node_ci_only` (409), before any record: only a proof copy is made there (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#proof-copies-on-a-ci-node)\n  %%> - the checkout path is free, and no other change runs on the Instance; the Gateway holds one lock per Instance\n  %%> Allowed:\n  %%> - repeat the command for an Instance that failed or never finished: it resumes (see the failure branch)\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the Instance is reserved, with its Route when the Project serves web pages\n  %%> - `.env` is rendered here from the Project's env template: `APP_URL`, a new `APP_KEY` and the database keys\n  %%> - a read token for this one repository is minted, valid one hour\n  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent; nothing has happened on the Node yet\n  Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - holds each Node that serves this Route's site (Caddy and PHP) from admission to the terminal state\n  %%> - a second Route write on one of those Nodes waits (task status `waiting`) and starts when the Node is free, oldest first\n  %%> Not held:\n  %%> - the DNS Node: its records are keyed, and the apply phase refuses a file that changed since the read\n  %%> Limit:\n  %%> - two Route writes on one Node run one after the other, so each waits for the other's whole run\n  %%> - a newer write waits behind an older waiting one that shares a Node, and a waiting write can be cancelled\n  %%> - a timed-out write keeps its Nodes until Swamp confirms that its run stopped, or for 15 minutes\n  %%> - task provision and cancel, instance:register and the Cluster Router writes hold the Nodes for their Route-publish step only, through the `route-hold` call\n  %%> - a task that waits starts from the Node's live files; a site block that matches none of its captured states still stops it with `owned_revision_conflict`\n  Note over GW: Commit, push copies<br/>queue start · answer 202\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n  GW->>SW: Start run from queue<br/>operation-instance-create\n  %%> Why the vault:\n  %%> - the token and `.env` reach the run only through the vault; no step calls the Gateway back\n  %%> Budget:\n  %%> - 60 s, plus the Project's setup step timeouts, plus 30 s for the Processes, plus 240 s for the Route (180 s to publish it, 60 s to take it back if the create fails) and 180 s for a database copy; fixed at admission\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task ID, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>SW: Write the playbook input<br/>intent file · assets\n  %%> Why one input step:\n  %%> - the intent is too large for an environment variable, and the same file serves both phases\n  opt web-serving Project\n    SW->>N: Make, sign Route key<br/>child route-create-csr\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the key, the CSR and the signature share the checkout's depth, so a Swamp job does not wait for them; they touch only the certificate folder, and the Gateway signs with the one certificate call\n    %%> Why here:\n    %%> - the Route child below then takes the signed certificates, so its 5 s of key and signature steps are off the critical path\n  end\n  SW->>N: Clone, write .env<br/>Ansible · tags checkout\n  %%> Why one phase:\n  %%> - one placed step and one Ansible start cover every task of it\n  %%> Why the file:\n  %%> - git reads the token from a 0600 file, so the token never enters argv; the file is removed after the fetch, success or failure\n  %%> Branch:\n  %%> - the requested origin branch, else the existing local branch, else a new branch from the default branch\n  %%> Why 0640 orbit-env:\n  %%> - the Node agent can report the key names and never the values\n  %%> - `.env.testing` is written the same way, only for an owned MySQL or PostgreSQL database\n  %%> With a Route:\n  %%> - the same phase denies Caddy the checkout, grants it the served roots and sets `APP_URL` in the `.env`; the Route's own phase skips both on this Node\n  opt default Instance has a SQLite database\n    SW->>N: Copy the default database<br/>child instance-clone-database\n    %%> Why before setup:\n    %%> - the copy lands first, so the setup steps migrate a copy, not an empty file\n  end\n  opt database on a Database server\n    SW->>N: Create owner and databases<br/>child operation-database-create\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - `--database-server` is given, or the default Instance's database is MySQL on a server; that dump is then copied in\n  end\n  opt web-serving Project\n    SW->>N: Create the Route<br/>child operation-route-create\n    %%> What it adds:\n    %%> - the same steps as `route:create`, then a TLS probe of the site\n    %%> Why beside the setup steps:\n    %%> - the Route needs the signed certificate and the access the checkout phase gave, not the installed dependencies; the setup steps run at the same time\n    %%> Why it marks the Instance awake:\n    %%> - so the first request reaches the app without a wake\n  end\n  SW->>N: Run Project setup steps<br/>Ansible · tags setup\n  %%> As whom:\n  %%> - the steps run as the Node's configured user, one after another; the first failure stops the run\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - a Project without setup steps runs no setup phase\n  SW->>N: Create, start Processes<br/>child operation-process-create\n  %%> Why after setup:\n  %%> - Processes need the installed dependencies\n  alt all steps pass in budget\n    N-->>SW: Site answers<br/>Processes running\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record result\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Instance and its Route become active\n    %%> - a successful create means every setup step succeeded; an empty list counts as success\n  else Instance already created\n    Note over GW: Skipped — already created<br/>instance.exists\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Instance is active and its first setup finished, so no step runs\n    %%> - the skip reason is part of the successful result in human and machine output, not an error\n  else a step fails or the budget ends\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or timeout\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a command exits non-zero, a Process or the site does not come up, or the budget ends\n    %%> - the container step of a nested-fleet default refuses with `instance.nested_fleet_image_unavailable` before any Incus change while the Node records a failed nested-fleet image build; a later converge that builds the image clears the record\n    SW->>N: Withdraw the Route<br/>child route-withdraw\n    %%> Caddy publication:\n    %%> - one Node-local transaction under the Node lock: read, merge the captured states, compare every other site, scope and record byte for byte, keep the previous generation, test, then commit and reload\n    %%> - a change outside the Route refuses with `route.withdraw_foreign_change`, a failed test with its code, locks busy for 30 s with `caddy.publish_busy` or `dns.publish_busy`; every file stays as it was and the normal failure path runs\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - takes back what the run published, as the reverse of the publication that the Gateway captured at admission: the Caddy site, the PHP pool, the private DNS records and the certificate folders\n    %%> - the catalog's Router answers (`overrides`, a list per requesting Node) name a Route only while the captured fleet has a site for it, as the host records do, so the name of the withdrawn Route leaves with its records in this same step and no stale answer stays\n    %%> - it runs before the run reports the failure, and the failure it reports is the one that was read before it\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the setup, a Process or the Route fails, or any step fails after the Route's key was made; a failure before that leaves nothing to take back\n    %%> What stays:\n    %%> - the reserved Instance, its checkout, its Processes and its database, marked failed; the failed step and its output are in the task\n    %%> - `instance:destroy` removes them; the rest of the removal is not part of a failed create (see the note at the top)\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step, error_code\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code; a missing setup command is `instance.setup_step_unavailable`, any other setup failure `instance.setup_step_failed`\n    %%> - the original error code is kept, whether or not the Route was withdrawn\n    Note over GW: Record the failure\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the task fails with the failed step; the Route no longer exists on the Nodes, and the Instance stays marked failed\n    %%> If the withdrawal did not finish:\n    %%> - the task still reports the original failure, the Route may stay published, and the failed withdrawal step is in the run; `instance:destroy` withdraws it\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again; a changed `--root` or `--branch` is refused until then (`instance.placement_conflict`)\n    %%> - `instance:destroy` removes the failed Instance and anything left of its Route\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/deploy": {
   "kind": "instance.deploy",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task + full intent as inputs, single-swamp-node; DECISIONS 1 (token minted at admission, vault.get), 3 (automatic deploy = Gateway-published native schedule, no POST back), 4 / R1-R4 (process.stop shape); .env through the vault as in instance.create; rule 7 (reload over restart, restart_on_deploy only). docs/reference/deployments.md.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 21, 22 (automatic deploys only in instance.auto_deploy; cfgmgmt file/link/systemd, 0600 credential file for the fetch).\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: instance:deploy\n  %% kind: instance.deploy\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Instance Node<br/>Node 12 over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: instance:deploy 42\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:deploy 42`\n  %%> - syntax: `<instance> [--commit=SHA] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Instance; without `--commit`, the newest commit of its deployment branch\n  %%> - `--commit`: a full commit SHA that must be on the branch's first-parent chain\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  %%> - automatic deploys start the same deploy steps from a native schedule; see the `instance.auto_deploy` chart\n  alt AppProd container release\n    GW->>SW: Start production-release\n    Note over SW: Check release ownership\n    SW->>N: Build the Instance release<br/>deploy in preparation\n    SW->>N: Clone the prepared release\n    SW->>N: Sleep preparation\n    SW->>N: Check the Instance<br/>candidate\n    SW->>N: Hand over the release<br/>switch Routes and workers\n    SW->>N: Release unused candidate\n    SW->>N: Read the serving release\n    SW-->>GW: Verified commit and owner\n  else Development or host checkout\n  opt Development default in a container\n    Note over GW: Capture source facts<br/>project:update capture\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - reads the requested commit, or the default branch head once; freezes that SHA for the run\n    %%> - checks the Instance migration before reading; reads outside the write transaction; admission rechecks migration, source configuration and idempotency\n    %%> - capture or classification failure refuses admission; see /reference/deployments#container-source-facts\n    Note over GW: Mark removed Processes<br/>status removing\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - a Process whose development definition the Project removed is marked `removing` and leaves the run's units; a definition added again under its name replaces it\n  end\n  Note over GW: Validate, freeze intent<br/>Instance 42 locked · task #5150\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12\n  %%> - Instance 42 is an active Instance of Project 7 on an app-prod or app-dev Node\n  %%> - a pending default Route projection refuses `deployment.projection_pending`; successful container create or migration completes that projection with its seed\n  %%> - no other operation holds Instance 42; else `operation.target_busy`\n  %%> Freezes:\n  %%> - the branch and commit, the deploy steps, the shared paths and the release name\n  %%> - the Project's Process and Schedule definitions and the PHP runtime\n  %%> - the account `orbit-app-7` and its home `/home/orbit-app-7`\n  %%> Renders .env:\n  %%> - from the stored environment of Instance 42\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - deployment 88 recorded as running; task #5150 recorded with the full intent\n  %%> - a manual production deploy pauses automatic deployment of Instance 42\n  %%> - nothing has happened on the Node yet\n  GW->>SW: Push token and .env<br/>vault: github-token, env-5150\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the repository is private, and only the Gateway holds the GitHub App key\n  %%> - `.env` can hold secrets, which travel only through the vault\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - mints a read token for this one repository, valid 1 hour; confirmed public repositories remain readable anonymously\n  %%> - credential preparation refuses source.github_app_denied before writing the fetch credential, naming the App and owner/repository; install the App or add the repository\n  %%> - source.github_unavailable means GitHub could not supply a credential; it never becomes an empty credential\n  %%> - pushes the token and the rendered `.env` into Swamp's vault\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the run reads both with `vault.get`; no step calls the Gateway back\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-deploy-direct\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one run on Swamp serve (Node 209); every step runs there\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  %%> - the frozen intent; secrets only as vault key names\n  %%> Budget:\n  %%> - the sum of the deploy step timeouts plus 900 s\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5150\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task until it ends\n  opt Development checkout\n    SW->>N: Write the fetch credential<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Fetch the branch<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Remove fetch credential<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Check tracked changes<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Resolve the commit<br/>ssh exec argv\n    opt --commit\n      SW->>N: Count commits after it<br/>ssh exec argv\n      SW->>N: Read the branch ancestor<br/>ssh exec argv\n      Note over SW: Check it is on the branch<br/>native assert\n    end\n    SW->>N: Read the local branch<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Check the local branch<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: List untracked entries<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: List target directories<br/>ssh exec argv\n    Note over SW: Plan the cleanup<br/>typed data\n    SW->>N: Check the checkout lock<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Clean stale directories<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Check out the commit<br/>ssh exec argv\n  end\n  opt Development default in a container\n    Note over SW: Write the container intent<br/>ansible-input\n    SW->>N: Start the container<br/>Ansible · start · probe\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - one play runs the phases `start` and `probe`, with `checkout_probe`, before the seed hold and before any other change\n    %%> - `start`: starts a stopped default container and delivers what every start delivers; the Project's PHP link and settings wait for `php` in \"Prepare the container\"\n    %%> - `probe`, first, as `fetch` does: fetches the branch into the Node's mirror and refuses a pinned commit off its first-parent chain with `deployment.commit_not_on_branch`, even one the workspace's own history has behind HEAD\n    %%> - `probe`, then only reading the workspace, with the checkout's rules in their order: tracked changes refuse `deployment.checkout_dirty`; a workspace off the branch refuses `deployment.branch_diverged`; a pinned commit that HEAD reaches only through a merge's second parent refuses `deployment.commit_not_on_branch`; a pinned commit neither behind nor ahead of HEAD refuses `deployment.branch_diverged`\n    %%> - `probe`, a pinned commit on HEAD's first-parent chain behind HEAD: a deployment by hand refuses `deployment.commit_behind_checkout` with a detail that names both commits; one that a release or the automatic policy admitted (its admission's `origin`, intent `keep_newer`) prints `orbit-checkout=already_newer` and writes `outcome` `already_newer`, `kept_commit` (the real HEAD) and `target_commit`; every later step skips, so the run holds no seed, takes no seed, applies no PHP, pushes no bundle and restarts nothing\n    %%> - `probe`, any other case (HEAD itself, a newer commit, a commit the workspace lacks): the deployment goes on, with the live PHP untouched\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - a refusal or a failed start restores nothing: the workspace has not changed\n    SW->>GW: Hold the seed<br/>seed-hold call\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - only the proof Project's default, whose seed a seed copy sends; its container intent has `seed_hold`; every other default skips the step\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - after the start and the probe, for a deployment that goes on, asks the Gateway for the default's seed hold and waits while a seed copy holds it, for up to 1,800 s; the deployment then holds the seed to its end (docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-seed-hold, container/deploy-default.mmd)\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - the hold stays busy to the bound: `instance.seed_hold_timeout`; nothing changed and no seed is restored\n    SW->>N: Prepare the container<br/>Ansible · php · fetch\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - runs only for a deployment that goes on after the probe of \"Start the container\"\n    %%> - one play runs the phases `php` and `fetch`; a nested-fleet default runs `php`, `fleet_start`, `seed` and `fetch`, in this order\n    %%> - `php`: the Project's PHP link and settings, and a restart of the running units when the settings changed; after the seed hold, so a failed hold leaves the live PHP as it was\n    %%> - `start`: starts a stopped default container; see docs/cli/instance/container/deploy-default.mmd; a nested-fleet default's root disk grows to the intent's size first, online, and never shrinks; it waits only for FrankenPHP and the enabled Process units, which the container's boot starts\n    %%> - `start` of a sleeping container (frozen or stopped by a sleep): resumes it through the power helper's `restore`, which reads the plan's pages back while frozen, consumes the plan and closes swap on each cgroup of `limits.json` that still exists, or discards the plan and boots a stopped one, then removes the asleep marker once the `start` part passed (the container runs and its units are active; no web probe), so the Route serves the app without a wake; the result records `power_state` `running`; the end of the deployment counts as activity, `deployed_recently`\n    %%> - `start`, before the boot of a stopped container: the presented machine name: a stopped container gets `/etc/hostname`, `/etc/machine-info` and the `127.0.1.1` line of `/etc/hosts` through the file API before the boot, so the boot gives the kernel and T3 the name; a running or frozen container gets nothing, and nothing restarts\n    %%> - `fleet_start`, nested-fleet default only: the run's one fleet check; compares the inner fleet with its rules, starts what is stopped and brings up the tunnel with the hub's resolver for the fleet's TLDs, and changes no key; refuses `instance.container_fleet_drift` for a conflicting build rule before the seed, but admits an absent build rule because refresh will install it with the fleet stopped\n    %%> - `seed`, nested-fleet default only: retakes the seed of the default as it is, fleet included, so a failed refresh never restores a seed from before the fleet\n    %%> - `fetch`: fetches into the Node's mirror with the read token and pushes the branch into the container as a bundle that holds only what the workspace lacks; a pinned commit off the branch's first-parent chain refuses `deployment.commit_not_on_branch`\n    %%> Why one step:\n    %%> - none of these phases changes the workspace, so a failure in any of them restores nothing; they share their failure handling\n    %%> - each Ansible step costs about 2.5 s to start its play, so one step saves about 2.5 s per phase it holds\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - the code names the failed phase, such as `instance.container_fetch_failed`, or is the phase's own refusal\n    %%> - a sleeping container's `start`: `instance.power_helper_busy` when another power action on the Instance held the power helper's lock for 10 s, `instance.power_helper_failed` when the helper refused its input or crashed; either leaves the asleep marker and the container's power state as they were\n    SW->>N: Check out in the container<br/>Ansible · tags checkout\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - refuses tracked changes with `deployment.checkout_dirty` and a branch the commit does not fast-forward with `deployment.branch_diverged`\n    Note over SW: Read the steps to skip<br/>run folder\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - reads the steps the checkout found unchanged; only a deployment with a step that has `inputs` runs it\n    SW->>N: Run dev deploy steps<br/>incus exec · vault copy\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - each Project command as `agent`, the workspace's owner (ADR 0268), in the workspace, with the runtime folder's PATH\n    %%> - a step with `inputs` is skipped when its inputs at the commit and its command are as at its last passed run in this workspace\n    SW->>N: Finish the container<br/>Ansible · processes · seed · check\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - one play runs the phases `processes`, `php_index`, `seed` and `check`, in this order; a nested-fleet default runs `fleet_refresh` between `processes` and `php_index`\n    %%> - `processes`: writes changed `orbit-*` units and new environment files from the vault, removes former ones, and restarts the changed units; stops and removes the units and environment folders of the Processes the run does not own, such as those marked `removing`; enables and starts each running unit and waits for it; on failure the detail names the unit that is not active\n    %%> - `fleet_refresh`, nested-fleet default only: docs/cli/instance/container/fleet-refresh.mmd\n    %%> - `php_index`, a Laravel default on an active app-dev Node only (its intent has `php_index`): `agent` runs Phpactor's incremental `index:build` at the application's folder, niced and within 600 s; an unchanged tree starts no Phpactor; a failure is `instance.php_index_failed`, and the old seed stays (docs/reference/development-containers.md#php-symbol-index)\n    %%> - `seed`: stops the units, snapshots `seed-<commit>`, starts them again on the new commit and deletes older seeds; a nested-fleet default retakes it at the same commit, and refuses a Club key file on its disk\n    %%> - `check`: the branch is at the commit and the units are active\n    %%> - the handlers a phase notifies run before the next phase starts, as with separate plays\n    %%> Why one step:\n    %%> - every one of these phases runs after the checkout, so a failure in any of them restores the old seed of a nested-fleet default; they share their failure handling\n    %%> - each Ansible step costs about 2.5 s to start its play, so one step saves about 2.5 s per phase it holds\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - the code names the failed phase, such as `instance.container_fleet_refresh_failed`, or is the phase's own refusal\n    opt nested-fleet default failed after checkout\n      SW->>N: Restore the old seed<br/>Ansible · fleet_restore\n      SW->>N: Start on the old seed<br/>Ansible · tags start\n      SW->>N: Start the restored fleet<br/>Ansible · tags fleet_resume\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - puts the default back on its newest seed and starts it, inner fleet included; the failed phase's result stays the run's result\n      %%> - with signed power the restart signs a discard order of the Operation before it boots; its receipt joins the failed result when that result has none, also the copy a failed `fleet_resume` starts from, so the Gateway ends the run with the failure's own code instead of `executor.result_unknown` (ADR 0266, R2)\n      %%> - admits absent build rules in that restored fleet, as Prepare did, without writing a map; conflicting maps still refuse, and the next deployment installs missing rules\n    end\n    Note over SW: Read the container result<br/>result file\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the deployed commit, or the public code of the refusal\n    Note over SW: Remove the run folder<br/>always\n  end\n  opt Checkout production Operation admitted before the container workflow\n    SW->>N: Write release credential<br/>cfgmgmt/file apply\n    SW->>N: Prepare the release<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Remove release credential<br/>cfgmgmt/file apply\n    SW->>N: Read the release commit<br/>ssh exec argv\n    opt --commit\n      SW->>N: Read first-parent chain<br/>ssh exec argv\n      Note over SW: Check the branch commit<br/>native assert\n    end\n    SW->>N: Prepare shared paths<br/>ssh exec argv\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - seeds only empty persistent directories and links the admitted shared paths after the branch check\n    SW->>N: Link the release<br/>cfgmgmt/link apply\n    SW->>N: Read the key names<br/>ssh exec argv\n    Note over SW: Check for foreign keys<br/>native assert\n    SW->>N: Create the orbit-env group<br/>cfgmgmt/group apply\n    SW->>N: Write .env<br/>cfgmgmt/file apply\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the Instance account writes `.env` as `0640` in its home, so no root write follows a link it placed\n    SW->>N: Set the group orbit-env<br/>ssh exec argv\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - root runs `chgrp -h orbit-env` on the written files, which never follows a link, so the Node agent may read the key names\n  end\n  opt Deploy steps need Vite+\n    SW->>N: dev-vp-read-package<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: dev-vp-read-pnpm_lock<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: dev-vp-read-npm_lock<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: dev-vp-read-node_version<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: dev-vp-read-nvmrc<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: dev-vp-home<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: dev-vp-store<br/>ssh exec argv\n    Note over SW: dev-vp-links<br/>typed data\n    SW->>N: dev-vp-read-workspace<br/>ssh exec argv\n    Note over SW: dev-vp-requirements<br/>typed data\n    SW->>N: Read Vite manager home<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Read Vite manager CLI<br/>cfgmgmt/file check\n    SW->>N: dev-vp-manager<br/> run\n    Note over SW: dev-vp-manager-ready<br/>native assert\n    SW->>N: Prepare Vite cache<br/>cfgmgmt/directory apply\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - gives the production account its own cache; the Node manager still supplies the shared CLI\n    SW->>N: Publish public Vite CLI<br/>ssh exec argv\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - publishes only the managed standalone binary; compare skips an unchanged copy, and no private manager data is copied\n    SW->>N: dev-vp-cli<br/>ssh exec argv\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - reads the CLI version; the first invocation also bootstraps the app's private cache\n    Note over SW: dev-vp-cli-ready<br/>typed data\n    SW->>N: dev-vp-install<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: dev-vp-inventory<br/>ssh exec argv\n    Note over SW: dev-vp-verify<br/>typed data\n    Note over SW: dev-vp-result<br/>typed data\n  end\n  opt Development deploy steps\n    SW->>N: Run the deploy steps<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Read the checkout branch<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Read the checkout commit<br/>ssh exec argv\n    Note over SW: Check the checkout<br/>native assert\n  end\n  opt Activation of a previously admitted checkout deployment\n    SW->>N: Run before steps<br/>ssh exec argv\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - runs each frozen private command as the Project account in the new release; later required commands stop after a failure\n    SW->>N: Grant Caddy access<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Activate release<br/>cfgmgmt/link apply\n    SW->>N: Swap current<br/>ssh exec argv\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - atomically renames current.next over current; failure before this point keeps the earlier selection\n    SW->>N: Prepare PHP directories<br/>cfgmgmt/directory apply\n    SW->>N: Read local PHP tuning<br/>cfgmgmt/file check\n    SW->>N: Write PHP configuration<br/>cfgmgmt/file apply\n    SW->>N: Write default PHP tuning<br/>cfgmgmt/file apply\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the dedicated runtime has no local tuning file; existing operator settings stay intact\n    SW->>N: Test PHP configuration<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Start PHP runtime<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd apply\n    SW->>N: Refresh PHP cache<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Check PHP runtime<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd check\n    SW->>N: Install Processes<br/> run\n    SW->>N: Read Schedule group<br/>ssh exec argv\n    Note over SW: Render Schedules<br/>typed data\n    SW->>N: Write Schedule services<br/>cfgmgmt/file apply\n    SW->>N: Install Schedule timers<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd apply\n    SW->>N: Read Schedule timers<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd check\n    Note over SW: Check Schedule timers<br/>native assert\n    SW->>N: Restart Processes<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Run after steps<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Find releases in use<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Clean up releases<br/>ssh exec argv\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - removes only releases outside the retained three, after checking no process uses them; cleanup failure is a warning\n    SW->>N: Read current back<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Check Process state<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd check\n    SW->>N: Read container state<br/>ssh exec argv\n    SW->>N: Read deployed commit<br/>ssh exec argv\n    Note over SW: Check the deployment<br/>native assert\n  end\n  alt all required steps pass\n    N-->>SW: release, commit\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the selected release and its commit, and the restarted Processes\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event and reads the outputs; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record the deployment<br/>release, commit · task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - deployment 88 succeeds with its release and commit\n    %%> - a container default deletes the Processes marked `removing` and names them in the result's `processes_removed`; a failed run keeps them `removing`\n    %%> - a container default records each best-effort step's exit code in the result's `best_effort_steps`, never its output; a nonzero code does not fail the deployment\n    %%> - task #5150 succeeds\n  else a required step fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or timeout\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a required step exits with an error, SSH fails, or the budget runs out\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed_step, selected release\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its safe error code; private command output is suppressed\n    %%> - which release `current` selects now\n    Note over GW: Record failed deployment<br/>selection kept · task failed\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - deployment 88 is marked failed; task #5150 fails\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - a failure before activation keeps the old release selected\n    %%> - a failure after activation keeps the new release selected\n    %%> - Orbit undoes no step and no data change; the new release folder stays until a cleanup\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - deploy again after a fix; it is a new task with a new release\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5150\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status until it has ended\n    %%> - the CLI uses Reverb events when the `websocket` role is active\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/container/deploy-production": {
   "kind": "instance.deploy",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% command: instance:deploy\n  %% kind: instance.deploy\n  %% status: migrated\n  autonumber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP\n  participant GW as Gateway\n  participant SW as Swamp\n  participant N as AppProd Node<br/>Instance owner\n  CLI->>GW: instance:deploy<br/>optional pinned commit\n  GW->>GW: Hold the Instance<br/>record its admitted commit\n  Note over GW: First host migration<br/>pin the serving commit\n  GW->>SW: Start production-release<br/>private Operation input\n  SW->>N: Verify target and source<br/>check room for the release\n  SW->>N: Build in preparation<br/>apply deploy steps and seal\n  SW->>N: Clone the sealed build\n  SW->>N: Sleep preparation<br/>reclaim memory to swap\n  SW->>N: Check candidate readiness\n  SW->>N: Switch Instance Routes<br/>hand over Processes and Schedules\n  SW->>N: Drain and stop old live<br/>retain it stopped for rollback\n  SW->>N: Read live and old commits\n  SW-->>GW: Verified release receipt<br/>Node, Instance, commit and digest\n  GW-->>CLI: Record the result<br/>verified live and rollback commits\n"
  },
  "instance/destroy": {
   "kind": "instance.destroy",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): a withdrawal clears the Router answers of the Route from the private DNS catalog in the same step, not only its records.\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Gateway holds each Route-serving Node from admission until the Route is withdrawn (the terminal state when the withdrawal did not hold); a second Route write on it waits (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, R1-R4, single-swamp-node (@swamp/ssh from 209), DECISIONS 1 (token minted at admission, vault.get), Schedules native (Nick 2026-11-02). docs/reference/instance-removal.md.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 3, 8, 14, 17, 22 (Route first, DNS only outside the TLD, cfgmgmt absent applies, no script read, source capture, report or assert step).\n  %% Decided: Nick 2026-11-02, the Route is withdrawn first, before teardown; a failed teardown leaves the site down and a rerun completes.\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): an Instance without a Route skips the Route withdrawal and the DNS records (a bare destroy spent 33 s and 11 s there on nothing), and a destroyed name waits no DNS grace (it has no new target to move to; the 31 s wait was 27% of a 116 s destroy).\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): a destroy applies each published state once. Without the grace, the prepared, withdrawn and live Caddyfiles and the prepared and final DNS records are identical, so the Route mutation publishes each once (two of three Caddy publications and two of three DNS publications were repeats); the separate DNS step of the Instance, which repeated the mutation, is gone.\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Route withdrawal is one Ansible read, one composition and one Ansible apply per Node, the Nodes in parallel, in place of about 30 serial dispatches of the Route mutation (34 s of a 46 s destroy). The pool and Caddyfile composition is unchanged; it runs once against what the Node holds, and the captured states keep their revision checks. DNS records leave in the same phase as the Caddy site of another Node, not after it.\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-14): the withdrawal is one Node-local transaction under the Node lock (read, merge, byte compare of every other section, previous generation, test, commit), so removals on one Node share its hold and run side by side; on 14 November five removals started together waited 21, 37 and 51 s for each other's withdrawal. A publisher still holds its Nodes alone.\n  %% Container clones follow the forced cleanup branch; defaults remain unavailable.\n  %% On the Gateway engine (ADR 0271): with `instance.destroy` in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`, a forced clone removal that InstanceDestroyEngineFlow accepts runs as the executor's signed `instance.destroy` step instead of this run (docs/reference/gateway-engine.md#instancedestroy): its steps are container/destroy-engine.mmd.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:destroy\n  %% kind: instance.destroy\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: instance:destroy 42\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:destroy 42`\n  %%> - syntax: `<instance> [--yes] [--force] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Instance's id, as `instance:list` shows it\n  %%> Confirmation:\n  %%> - without `--yes`, the CLI asks first; the default is No\n  %%> - without a terminal, or with `--json`, `--yes` is required\n  %%> With `--force`:\n  %%> - also deletes dirty or unpublished source; never deletes Git data needed by another worktree\n  %%> - it never waives the source identity checks, and it is not consent\n  %%> - migration ownership still applies: a running migration or an unsettled reserved runtime refuses with `instance.lifecycle_busy`, before cleanup and again under the Instance admission lock\n  Note over CLI: Confirm removal\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - asks for default-No consent before sending the removal request\n  %%> - `--yes` supplies that consent without the prompt; noninteractive and JSON calls require it\n  opt --force\n    Note over GW: Skip pushed-source checks\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - skips token minting and the git credential and pushed-work steps below\n    %%> - permits dirty or unpublished source after source identity and worktree dependency checks\n    %%> - supplies no removal consent; the prompt or `--yes` is still required\n  end\n  opt host checkout Instance\n    GW->>N: Read worktrees<br/>Git · SSH\n    %%> Why first:\n    %%> - before Schedule removal or any Operation write; inspect the owner’s Git registry and recorded checkout paths, never trust source_layout\n    %%> On refusal:\n    %%> - HTTP 409 instance.worktree_dependents reports visible dependent Instances, a count of others, and unowned paths; --force never overrides it\n    %%> - the target’s unreadable Git refuses; an unrelated checkout is classified from bounded Git pointer reads, with named warnings when unknown\n    %%> - every unreadable candidate is unknown with a named warning; only target read failures and confirmed dependents refuse\n    %%> - admission only: queue wait and the whole run are outside the check; an admitted retry does not check again\n  end\n  Note over GW: Validate, capture the set<br/>Instance 42 · task #5132\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs Instance 42\n  %%> - Instance 42 is a development Instance; production removal is not charted here\n  %%> - no other lifecycle change and no unfinished transfer holds Instance 42\n  %%> - no Route is required; a sole pending or failed Route always runs withdrawal, including when sites_published=false; an absent publication is accepted\n  %%> - multiple owning Routes, a foreign Project, wrong targets or unsafe placement still refuse\n  %%> Publication validation:\n  %%> - prepare every participant connection and its SSH pin, Caddyfile, shared PHP configuration and private DNS state before any Schedule effect\n  %%> - the dry run uses the same pure preparation as admission, records no intent, changes no status, stages no copy and takes no Route hold\n  %%> Captures:\n  %%> - the source: path, owner, origin, branch, linked worktrees\n  %%> - what Instance 42 owns: Processes, Schedules, Route, SQLite files, server databases\n  %%> - the Project teardown steps\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - discard every prepared result; admission builds its intent fresh after Schedule removal\n  opt owned PostgreSQL database\n    GW->>N: Read replication slots<br/>Database server Node · SSH\n    %%> Why first:\n    %%> - a slot makes the drop fail after the fence closed the database; the removal would stop half way\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - on each Database server's Node, one read of pg_replication_slots for every database the drop plans, with the drop's admin client and the admin password as the first protected stdin line\n    %%> - at most 5 s per server, no retry\n    %%> On refusal:\n    %%> - HTTP 409 instance.database_replication_slot names the database, server and slot; --force never overrides it; Orbit never drops a slot\n    %%> - HTTP 503 instance.database_slots_unreadable when the read fails\n    %%> - before Schedule removal and any record: the Instance, Route and holds stay as they were\n  end\n  GW->>SW: Remove Schedule triggers<br/>workflow trigger removal\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - removes the native Swamp schedule trigger of each Schedule of Instance 42\n  %%> - there is no Node work here; Schedules install nothing on Nodes\n  %%> Why first:\n  %%> - no new fire starts against a checkout that is being removed\n  %%> Running fire:\n  %%> - a fire that already started runs to its end, within its own timeout, and is still recorded\n    %%> Fresh admission:\n    %%> - if a fact changes after the dry run, admission uses its current value and may refuse; Schedules may already be stopped\n    %%> - build all publication, participant, database, member, runtime, Route and root facts fresh in the admission transaction; keep no lock across Schedule calls\n  Note over GW: Record removal intent<br/>mark removing\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - run the same preparation again inside the existing admission transaction and record only this fresh result\n  %%> - record the Operation and mark the Instance removing and Route retiring only after Schedule removal succeeds\n  %%> On Schedule failure:\n  %%> - keep the Instance and Route active; record no destroy Operation, as before\n  opt private repository\n    GW->>SW: Push the token<br/>vault: github-token\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - a normal removal checks that the work is pushed, which needs a fetch from origin\n    %%> - only the Gateway holds the GitHub App key\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - mints a read token for this one repository, valid 1 hour, and pushes it into Swamp's vault\n    %%> Not needed:\n    %%> - with `--force`, or for a public repository\n  end\n  Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - holds each Node that serves this Route's site (Caddy and PHP) from admission to the terminal state\n  %%> - a second Route write on one of those Nodes waits (task status `waiting`) and starts when the Node is free, oldest first\n  %%> Not held:\n  %%> - the DNS Node: its records are keyed, and the apply phase refuses a file that changed since the read\n  %%> Limit:\n  %%> - two Route writes on one Node run one after the other, so each waits for the other's whole run\n  %%> - a newer write waits behind an older waiting one that shares a Node, and a waiting write can be cancelled\n  %%> - a timed-out write keeps its Nodes until Swamp confirms that its run stopped, or for 15 minutes\n  %%> - task provision and cancel, instance:register and the Cluster Router writes hold the Nodes for their Route-publish step only, through the `route-hold` call\n  %%> - a task that waits starts from the Node's live files; a site block that matches none of its captured states still stops it with `owned_revision_conflict`\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-destroy\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one run of `operation-instance-destroy` on Swamp serve (Node 209)\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  %%> - the captured set, the teardown steps, the Node's managed user\n  %%> - secrets only as vault key names\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5132\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  Note over SW: Prepare container removal\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - both source and container phases need the saved intent and playbook assets\n  %%> - container defaults refuse; clones require --force and skip source checks and Project teardown\n  SW->>N: Write the git credential<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · 0600\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - writes the vault token as a 0600 credential file, so the token never enters argv\n  SW->>N: Check work is pushed<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · git fetch\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - for each checkout, fetches origin with that credential file, then compares with `git status --porcelain` and `git branch -r --contains HEAD`\n  %%> Refuses:\n  %%> - dirty source\n  %%> - a `HEAD` that no origin branch or tag contains\n  %%> Result of a refusal:\n  %%> - the run stops here and nothing changes\n  %%> Why first:\n  %%> - a fact that can change after admission, and every later step depends on it\n  SW->>N: Remove the git credential<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file absent\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - always after the check, also when it refused\n  opt Instance has a Route\n  SW->>N: Withdraw the Route<br/>plan, one Node transaction\n  %%> Caddy publication:\n  %%> - one Node-local transaction under the Node lock: read, merge the captured states, compare every other site, scope and record byte for byte, keep the previous generation, test, then commit and reload\n  %%> - a change outside the Route refuses with `route.withdraw_foreign_change`, a failed test with its code, locks busy for 30 s with `caddy.publish_busy` or `dns.publish_busy`; every file stays as it was and the normal failure path runs\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - removes the Caddy site of Instance 42, its PHP pool and its certificate, then reloads Caddy; no restart\n  %%> - per Node, in parallel: Swamp checks the captured states and writes the merge plan; one Ansible apply runs the Node transaction, then removes the certificate folders\n  %%> - under the lock: reads the pools, the Caddyfile and the private DNS files, merges, keeps the previous generation in /var/lib/orbit/route-previous, tests PHP-FPM, Caddy and dnsmasq, then reloads PHP-FPM and Caddy and restarts the DNS backend; a failed reload puts the previous file back\n  %%> - for a Route domain outside the Cluster or Node TLD it also removes the private DNS records on Node 1 (private DNS answers the whole TLD, so a domain inside it has none), in this step and not in a step of its own\n  %%> - the catalog's Router answers (`overrides`, a list per requesting Node) name a Route only while the captured fleet has a site for it, as the host records do, so the name of the withdrawn Route leaves with its records in this same step and no stale answer stays\n  %%> If it does not hold:\n  %%> - every later step is skipped and the task fails with `route.withdrawal_failed`; the Instance and its source stay\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - Swamp runs the Ansible phases from 209 over SSH; the playbook ships with the run, and nothing is copied to the Node\n  %%> - a live file that matches none of the captured states stops the phase before it writes (`owned_revision_conflict`)\n  %%> - another removal on Node 12 may run beside it: each reads what the other wrote, under the lock\n  %%> Why first:\n  %%> - unpublish before removing, so no request reaches a half-removed Instance\n  %%> - accepted: a failed teardown leaves Instance 42 without its site until a rerun completes the removal\n  %%> No DNS grace:\n  %%> - the name is removed, not moved, so no client has a new target to switch to; the Route is withdrawn right after its DNS records go\n  %%> Keeps:\n  %%> - every other site, pool and certificate on Node 12\n  SW->>GW: Release the Route Nodes<br/>route-release · run ID\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - only after the withdrawal held; the later steps write no Caddy, PHP pool or DNS file, so the next Route write on Node 12 starts now, not at the end\n  %%> Records:\n  %%> - the removal's route_withdrawn_at: a retry skips the withdrawal and holds no Node\n  %%> If it is refused:\n  %%> - the run goes on; the task's end frees the Nodes, and its result says route_hold_released false with the HTTP status\n  end\n  opt checkout Instance\n  SW->>N: Stop, remove Processes<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/systemd absent\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - removes each Process of Instance 42 as a `forEach`: `@swamp/cfgmgmt/systemd` or `docker_container` with `ensure: absent`, as `process:destroy` does\n  %%> Why before teardown:\n  %%> - nothing still writes to the application while teardown runs\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - Processes of other Instances on Node 12\n  SW->>N: Run teardown steps<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · in order\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - runs the Project's teardown steps in order, as the Node's managed user, from the repository root, one `@swamp/ssh exec` each\n  %%> Limits:\n  %%> - each step has its own timeout\n  %%> Failure:\n  %%> - a failed step ends the run with its own exit code; the next `instance:destroy` runs the whole list again\n  opt SQLite files recorded\n    SW->>N: Remove SQLite files<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file absent\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - deletes the SQLite files that Instance 42 owns, by their recorded paths\n    %%> - runs beside the database step; neither needs the other\n  end\n  end\n  opt container clone\n    SW->>N: Stop the container<br/>Ansible · tags stop\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - stops the container with `incus stop --force`, so its workers and every other client in it close their database sessions before the drop\n    %%> - a stopped or missing container counts as stopped and changes nothing\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - a session on a planned database refuses the drop with `database.in_use` (Operation 25427, Instance 3472)\n  end\n  opt owned databases\n    SW->>N: Drop owned databases<br/>docker exec -i · SQL on stdin\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - drops each database and user that Instance 42 owns on a Database server, one `docker exec -i` per statement with the SQL on stdin\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the admin login comes from the server's own vault key, as the first stdin line for MySQL and Postgres, `AUTH` on stdin for Valkey\n    %%> Not included:\n    %%> - registered external databases; their records go with the Instance, nothing on their host changes\n    %%> Failure:\n    %%> - a session or dependency on a planned database refuses the drop before the first DROP; the server's end check then fails, and the removal names the child's code, `database.in_use`, not `instance.removal_incomplete`\n  end\n  opt checkout Instance\n  SW->>N: Remove hibernation files<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file absent\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the awake and cold markers and the Caddy access log outlive the Instance otherwise\n  SW->>N: Remove the source<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · worktree, rm\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the original source-removal command runs without a dependency check, using Git for a linked worktree and recursive removal as on main\n  %%> - the paths come from the Gateway's records; a rerun converges\n  %%> Runs as:\n  %%> - the Node's own user, not root\n  SW->>N: Check it is gone<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt check absent\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the `check` of the directory, file, systemd and `docker_container` behaviors with `ensure: absent`: checkout, units and containers are gone\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - Instance 42 only counts as removed when the Node shows it; this is the one end check\n  end\n  opt container clone\n    SW->>N: Delete the container<br/>Ansible · tags remove\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - removes the container, volume, snapshots, Processes, T3, proxy, owned ACL, firewall rules, mirror and, for a task copy, the Node scratch `/var/tmp/orbit-task-base-<id>` that a cancelled `task:sync` may leave; it holds no credential\n    %%> - requires successful Route withdrawal when present, and owned database removal; other Instances stay\n    %%> - the power helper's `discard`, as the Instance's lock unit: waits for a helper call still at work, then removes the plan, `limits.json`, the target descriptor and the Instance's fast wake request and outcome files, so a late worker finds nothing to act on\n    %%> - removes the executor's freeze state folder `/var/lib/orbit/executor/power/<instance id>` (plan, swap limits, receipt) with no signed order\n    Note over SW: Read container removal\n    %%> Failure:\n    %%> - a skipped deletion has no result and cannot report success\n    %%> - a failed deletion's result names the failed task with its stderr tail, which the Operation keeps\n    Note over SW: Clean container files\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - after every user of the run folder settles, also after a failure\n  end\n  alt all steps pass\n    N-->>SW: Instance 42 absent\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the check passed\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Delete Instance, records<br/>task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Instance 42 is deleted, with its Processes, Schedules and Route records\n    %%> - owned database records are deleted\n    %%> - a container clone also loses its execution copies and Conn records, even while Conn is disabled; its runtime row frees the address and T3 port\n    %%> - closed transfer history stays, without its Instance link\n    %%> - task #5132 succeeds\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or refusal\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the pushed check refuses, a teardown step fails, or something is still there\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the failing step ends the run with its own exit code; there is no report step\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its exit code, mapped to `error_code`\n    Note over GW: Keep Instance, mark failed<br/>rerun the same destroy\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Instance 42 and its records stay, marked removal failed\n    %%> - task #5132 fails\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - what was already removed stays removed; no rollback\n    %%> - the Route is already withdrawn, so the site stays down; a rerun of the same destroy completes the removal\n    %%> - the Schedule triggers stay removed\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run `instance:destroy 42` again; it is a new task and converges the same captured set\n    %%> - after a held withdrawal the retry skips it and waits for no Route write\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5132\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/container/destroy-engine": {
   "kind": "instance.destroy",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% A container clone's removal on the Gateway engine (ADR 0271, docs/reference/gateway-engine.md#instancedestroy, docs/reference/instance-removal.md): what #4211 shipped, InstanceDestroyEngineFlow with one step `remove` running the executor's `instance.destroy` (apps/agent/crates/instance/src/destroy.rs). Every other removal is instance/destroy.mmd on Swamp.\n  %% Built, proof pending: a clone that owns private Routes withdraws them on the engine first, with the primitives of route/engine/destroy.mmd.\n  %% status: built\n  %% command: instance:destroy\n  %% kind: instance.destroy\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane EN icon=workflow pill=engine tone=purple\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=executor tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant EN as Engine<br/>Gateway and NATS\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>executor\n  CLI->>GW: instance:destroy 42<br/>--yes --force\n  Note over GW: Validate, capture the set<br/>Instance 42 · task #5132\n  %%> Same admission as instance/destroy.mmd: consent, captures, Schedule trigger removal, then the removal intent in one transaction that marks Instance 42 removing\n  alt the engine admits the whole Operation\n    %%> Admission, one decision per Operation (EngineKinds::routes):\n    %%> - `instance.destroy` is in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`, and the Node reports `instance.destroy` in `$KV.NODES`, independently of `instance.create`\n    %%> - InstanceDestroyEngineFlow accepts the plan, all of it checked against the saved intent:\n    %%> - the target is an Instance with a positive ID, equal to the intent's `instance_id` and, checked on its own, to `container_destroy.instance_id`, the field that becomes the signed step's target; a plan that names 43 there refuses; the Operation's Node is positive and equals both the intent's and the saved plan's `node_id`\n    %%> - development, `--force`, not the default name; a positive clone source that is not the Instance itself\n    %%> - no `after_operation_id` (no wait for a create or completion) and no CI hold\n    %%> - exactly one member, and it is this Instance, with `run_teardown: false`; the plan's Routes are an empty list\n    %%> - a member `route_id` or `additional_route_ids` only for private Routes with no LAN rule or Ingress edge, whose whole after-state the admission rendered (`route_engine`: each Node's Caddyfile, changed shared pools and DNS files without the Routes, with the digests of the files they replace); a PHP version whose pool empties refuses; each changed pool carries its rendered-from state; any order over the signer's 4 KB limit refuses; every VPN Node with a DNS change reports `dns.guarded_records` and each serving Node `route.withdraw_site`\n    %%> - exactly one teardown entry, for this Instance, with no steps; no Processes, Schedules, database connections, database servers or SQLite files\n    %%> - the saved identity is the signed contract: container and ACL `instance-42`, project `orbit-instances` or `orbit-instances-nested`, pool `orbit-instances`, mirror `/var/lib/orbit/instance-repositories/42.git`, firewall prefix `orbit:instance-42-`, the asleep marker, inner volume `inner-42` when present\n    %%> - else `EngineUnsupported`, and the whole removal runs on Swamp\n    Note over GW: Bind remove, take the lock<br/>commit, publish the start\n    %%> The Gateway binds step `remove` (budget 600 s, resend 15 s) and the Instance's Operation lock in one transaction before it publishes the start\n    %%> Cancel still works during order preparation, deadline scheduling and the Node consumer's setup; just before the first publish the Gateway checks cancel and records the first order's signed expiry under the same lock\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5132\n    opt clone owns a Route\n      EN->>N: Withdraw the Route first<br/>route/engine/destroy steps\n      %%> `dns-node` (guarded `dns.publish_records`), then `withdraw-node` (`route.withdraw_site`) and `sibling-node` (a TLS probe of another Route on that Node), with no DNS grace (a destroyed name has no new target, as instance/destroy.mmd decides)\n      %%> Every step is receipt-bound like `remove`: a lost receipt resends the same step, the Node's journal answers its stored result, and the Instance stays fenced until a terminal receipt; a `blob.*` content failure comes before the journal, so it never ends a step\n      %%> Admitted only when every Route is served by the clone's own Node; the removal takes every Node whose whole files it replaces, its DNS Nodes too, alone from admission (an exclusive hold, not a shared withdrawal slot), so a second removal there waits; a removal that waited and was promoted, or that shares its Nodes, runs on Swamp, which merges from the live files\n      %%> A retry of a removal whose earlier run withdrew the Route (`route_withdrawn`) has none of these steps, as the Swamp run skips its withdraw\n      Note over EN: Record the withdrawal<br/>free the Route Nodes\n      %%> Gateway step `release`, as the Swamp run's release step: `route_withdrawn_at` on the removal, then every Route Node hold of the removal is freed and the waiting writes are woken; `route_hold_released` is true only when a hold row was freed; the Instance's own lock stays until the removal's receipt; a refused release never fails the removal, and its line (`route_hold_released`, `route_hold_release_status`) is in the result, also after a later container failure\n      %%> `remove` waits for it; a failed withdrawal fails the Operation as `route.withdrawal_failed` at \"Withdraw the Route\" (removal step `route_target_clear`) before the container is touched\n    end\n    EN->>N: Remove the clone<br/>instance.destroy · step remove\n    %%> Input: `project`, `name`, `pool`, `acl`, `inner_volume`; the executor checks it again (`instance.destroy_input_invalid`)\n    %%> Holds the same target lock as sleep and wake; checks that `instance-42` is a container on the admitted pool\n    %%> Force-stops it unless Incus reports Stopped (`instance.removal_stop_failed`), deletes it with `--force`; a failed delete restores the snapshots' missing datasets and deletes again\n    %%> Removes the inner volume and its owned unused `build-` and `spare-` volumes, then the ACL\n    %%> Read-back: the container, the ACL and every owned volume are gone, else `instance.removal_incomplete`\n    %%> Then the `orbit:instance-42-` ufw rules: an owned rule that shares its specification with a foreign rule refuses (`instance.removal_incomplete`); each is deleted by specification and their absence is read back\n    %%> Then the power helper's legacy `discard` (`instance.power_discard_failed`), then the mail folder, the mirror, `/var/tmp/orbit-task-base-42` and the hibernation asleep, cold, log and awake files; an absent one counts as removed\n    %%> Last, the executor discards its own freeze state for Instance 42 (`discard_destroyed`); a failure there is `instance.power_discard_failed`, never success\n    N-->>EN: One terminal receipt<br/>after the whole sequence\n    %%> Sent only after every part above held, or with the first failure's code; the executor appends its `done` record first. A refusal such as `instance.removal_dataset_taken` is kept as a result too\n    opt the receipt is lost\n      EN->>N: Resend the same step<br/>fresh nonce · same step ID\n      %%> The journal repeats the stored result with `repeat: true`; the deletion never runs twice\n      %%> A lost acknowledgement, cancel, expiry or order refusal never releases the Instance lock: none proves the Node did not act. An `expired` refusal keeps the fence and the scheduled retry\n      %%> Resends stop once one could expire after the first order's expiry minus 10 s plus 24 h of journal retention; then Instance 42 stays fenced until a matching terminal receipt or authoritative recovery; an absent record after compaction settles nothing\n      N-->>EN: Stored result<br/>repeat: true\n    end\n    %%> A receipt counts only when its Node, Operation step ID, action and target match\n    alt the removal held\n      EN->>GW: Project the removal<br/>applyEngine\n      %%> DevelopmentInstanceDestroyProjection::applyEngine records the same removal as a Swamp run: Instance 42 and its records are deleted, task #5132 succeeds\n    else a terminal failure\n      EN--xGW: Failed step<br/>error_code · redacted detail\n      Note over GW: Keep Instance, mark failed<br/>removal intent kept\n      %%> `error.detail` is a bounded, redacted tail of whole lines, or says the diagnostic was omitted\n      %%> Retry: the same command; the existing retry rules apply\n    end\n  else the engine does not admit it\n    Note over GW: Start the Swamp run<br/>operation-instance-destroy\n    %%> The kind is not enabled, the Node lacks `instance.destroy`, `$KV.NODES` is unreadable, or the flow throws `EngineUnsupported`: the whole removal runs instance/destroy.mmd; an Operation never splits across engines\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/container/work-read-engine": {
   "kind": "instance.destroy",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% The workspace unsaved-work read that Conn's Remove opens (GET /api/v1/conn/instances/{instance}/work, ContainerWorkspaceRead) as a signed executor read (ADR 0267, ADR 0271): the executor action `instance.work_read`, sent as a core NATS request to `orbit.node.<node>.reads` (docs/reference/executor.md#signed-reads). Built; runtime proof pending.\n  %% Documented exception: this is a synchronous read and no Operation, so it has no kind of its own. It carries `instance.destroy`, the Operation whose Remove it gates, so instance/destroy.mdx can place it beside instance/container/destroy-engine.mmd.\n  %% Answer contract unchanged (docs/reference/development-containers.md#workspace-work-state): state clean, dirty or unknown; unknown carries a reason from stopped, asleep, timeout, not_a_repository, unreachable.\n  %% status: built\n  %% command: instance:destroy\n  %% kind: instance.destroy\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane EN icon=workflow pill=engine tone=purple\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=executor tone=amber\n  %% lane C icon=server pill=copy tone=green\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant EN as Engine<br/>Gateway and NATS\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>executor\n  participant C as Container<br/>via executor\n  CLI->>GW: Remove clicked<br/>GET conn/instances/42/work\n  %%> Conn reads afresh on every Remove click; no answer is cached or reused\n  Note over GW: Resolve the runtime<br/>Node · container · baseline\n  %%> The recorded baseline, or the default's container for an Instance without one; the published commits (a task copy's publish)\n  alt the Node reports the read action\n    %%> The 4 s budget counts from the read's start: the capability lookup (at most 1 s), the content publication and every send end by it, each on a connection of its own whose connect, greeting, handshake and request all end by it (DeadlineRequest), and no reply is taken after it\n    %%> No Operation, no lock and no engine decision: the Gateway signs one read order and waits for its reply within the request's 4 s budget\n    GW->>EN: Signed read order<br/>machine bus · request\n    EN->>N: Read the workspace<br/>read-only · 2 s budget\n    %%> Target: Instance 42; the order changes nothing, so the executor writes no journal record, takes no target lock and stores no result; its nonce map still refuses the same order twice\n    %%> A recorded baseline and the published commits travel as content the order names by its hash (KV_CONTENT): a baseline may name 2,000 untracked files, beyond the order's 4 KB\n    alt Incus reports Running\n      N->>C: Git read in /srv/workspace<br/>as agent · no repository code\n      %%> The fixed reader of workspace-git-read.py: status, untracked set, stashes, commits beyond the baseline and the published commits; no hooks, no fsmonitor, no write to the index\n      %%> The SSH reader's exact Git calls, environment and overrides, so both readers answer alike, submodules and filters included. As over SSH, Git applies the workspace's own configuration: configured clean, smudge and process filters run as agent during the read exactly as they do on today's SSH path (pre-existing, not introduced here). Git itself writes no index, lock or ref (GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0, --no-optional-locks, no refresh). The Node stops each command at the deadline to the millisecond, and the read ends by the order's expiry less 0.5 s; a command still running at the deadline is killed with its whole process group\n      %%> A running copy is always read, also when an asleep marker is present: the marker never hides a dirty workspace\n      C-->>N: Status, stashes, commits\n      N-->>EN: clean or dirty<br/>counts · names · head\n    else not running, asleep marker present\n      N-->>EN: unknown · asleep\n      %%> As today: the marker only tells asleep from stopped for a copy that is not running; it is not woken to read it\n    else not running, no marker\n      N-->>EN: unknown · stopped\n    end\n    EN-->>GW: Reply\n    opt the reply is lost\n      GW->>EN: Read again<br/>fresh nonce · rest of the budget\n      %%> A read has no effect, so a second send is harmless. The first send waits 2.5 s (the Node's 2 s and 0.5 s for the reply); the one re-read gets what is left of the 4 s with a Node budget 0.5 s shorter. A connection that closed after the order went out whole is a lost reply too, and so is a content publication whose acknowledgement is missing; a publication the bus did not take whole or refused (-ERR) is `unreachable`. A reply that completes after the deadline is dropped. A reply still missing then is `unknown` `timeout`; an order the Node found expired, on arrival or before it ran, is `timeout` too; two sends that both failed to reach the bus are `unreachable`\n    end\n  else the Node lacks the action\n    Note over GW: Read over pinned SSH<br/>ContainerWorkspaceRead\n    %%> Today's path, with the same answer shape; nothing else differs\n  end\n  GW-->>CLI: Work state<br/>clean · dirty · unknown\n  %%> `unknown` only for a real read failure (timeout, not_a_repository, unreachable) or the stopped and asleep states above; never for a dirty or clean workspace\n  %%> The dialog's answer authorizes nothing: work can change before the user confirms\n  CLI->>GW: Remove confirmed<br/>POST conn/instances/42/destroy\n  %%> Body `{\"acknowledge_unsaved\": bool}` (DestroyConnInstanceAction)\n  alt acknowledge_unsaved false\n    Note over GW: Read the workspace again<br/>the same read, fresh\n    %%> ConnInstanceWorkState::assertRemovalSafe: an independent fresh read, by the same executor or SSH path as above, inside this request; still no Operation\n    alt the fresh read is clean\n      Note over GW: Admit the removal<br/>instance.destroy\n      %%> instance/container/destroy-engine.mmd, or instance/destroy.mmd on Swamp\n      GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    else dirty or unknown\n      GW--xCLI: 409 instance.unsaved_work<br/>details: the fresh read\n      %%> Nothing is recorded; Conn asks again, and only an acknowledged POST removes unsaved work\n    end\n  else acknowledge_unsaved true\n    Note over GW: Admit the removal<br/>no second read\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/logs": {
   "kind": "instance.logs",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:logs\n  %% kind: instance.logs\n  autonumber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP\n  participant GW as Gateway\n  participant A as Node observer\n  participant R as Reverb\n  CLI->>GW: instance:logs <instance>\n  alt bounded snapshot\n    Note over GW: Read bounded log\n    GW-->>CLI: Snapshot and request ID\n  else --follow (CLI or web viewer)\n    CLI->>GW: Open viewer lease\n    Note over GW: Authorize source<br/>Store inactive lease\n    GW-->>CLI: Channel and signature\n    CLI->>R: Subscribe to channel\n    CLI->>GW: Activate viewer lease\n    GW->>A: log-streams.changed\n    A->>GW: Fetch active sources\n    loop new lines while lease is active\n      A->>R: Read-only log observations\n      R->>GW: Log batch\n      Note over GW: Redact log lines<br/>Keep loss markers\n      GW->>R: Viewer log.lines frames\n      R-->>CLI: New lines\n      CLI->>GW: Renew every 20 s\n    end\n    Note over CLI: Reopen on reconnect<br/>Read snapshots if offline\n    CLI->>GW: Close viewer lease\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/mail-update": {
   "kind": "instance.mail_update",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Development mail: shared path, then the exact one-line rule.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:mail:update\n  %% kind: instance.mail_update\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>Instance container\n  CLI->>GW: instance:mail:update ID\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:mail:update INSTANCE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<instance-id> [--json]`\n  Note over GW: Admit mail update\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - a running development container on the Node with the main Mailpit; hold its Instance lock\n  %%> On failure:\n  %%> - unsupported placement, stopped runtime or busy lifecycle work starts no run\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-mail-update\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID\n  SW->>N: Apply development mail<br/>Ansible · tags mail\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - reuse the lifecycle mail path before changing the exact mailer line\n  alt path ready\n    %% path: main\n    N-->>SW: Path and mailer result<br/>status · ports · line numbers\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - only a changed file clears config and restarts running PHP and PHP workers\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result\n  else listener or unit conflict\n    %% path: conflict\n    N-->>SW: Mail setup skipped<br/>mail_port_in_use / unit conflict\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - `mail_port_in_use` or `mail_unit_conflict`; the file stays untouched\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>named skip\n  else phase failed\n    %% path: failed\n    N--xSW: Mail update failed\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - keep completed path changes; remove private temporary files; a repeat repairs the path\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error code\n  end\n  loop until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: Status and result\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/migrate": {
   "kind": "instance.migrate",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Container runtime (ADR 0250 step 8, docs/reference/development-containers.md#migrate-a-checkout-instance): one checkout Instance moves into a container, with its code. Its database stays where it is, and the container gets its own way to it. A linked worktree can move while its owner remains a host checkout; the owner moves after its last dependent leaves. The command exists only until the last checkout Instance has moved (docs/reference/old-paths.md). The container steps are the phases of docs/cli/instance/container/create-default.mmd and create.mmd.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:migrate\n  %% kind: instance.migrate\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane C icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  participant C as New container<br/>via incus exec\n  CLI->>GW: instance:migrate 42\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:migrate INSTANCE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<instance> [--force] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  Note over CLI: Confirm the downtime\n  %%> What it asks:\n  %%> - the Instance goes down during the move, and its old checkout moves to retention after it; `--force` answers yes\n  Note over GW: Validate, reserve runtime<br/>address · T3 port\n  %%> If it never starts:\n  %%> - cancellation or admission expiry frees the new reservation and releases deferred Schedule fires under the Instance lock; an uncertain start keeps its reservation\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - a previous migrate must be settled by the reconciler; otherwise instance.migrate_settlement_pending (409) names its Operation and hold limit; retry after settlement\n  %%> - the Instance is an active development checkout Instance on an app-dev Node, else instance.migrate_unsupported; production Instances refuse even on mixed-role Nodes; no build list applies\n  %%> - a Project with `nested_fleet` on refuses with `operation.kind_unavailable` (503) before any record: only `instance:create` makes Instances of such a Project, as containers; the runtime is not reserved\n  %%> - a non-default Instance needs its Project's default on the same Node and its own source identity; no default seed or snapshot is consumed\n  %%> - the Instance has only ordinary systemd or supported `vp-dev` Processes, no unfinished deployment or Process operation/runtime cleanup and no Route beside its own; a non-default Instance needs the T3 recording contribution loaded, even while Conn is off; Conn and a ready model route are optional\n  %%> - `vp-dev` keeps its identity, assigned port and desired state; unsupported preset versions, custom commands, external paths and recorded container port conflicts refuse before downtime, naming the Process and reason\n  %%> - a web-serving Project names its PHP version; a missing version refuses with `project.source_defaults_incomplete`, naming `orbit project:update PROJECT --source-access=github_app` to record it from source\n  %%> - a database on a Database server has an IPv4 address and a port, else `instance.database_clone_unsupported`\n  %%> - a non-default Instance: the recipe's agent folder pins the program of the Project's `container_server`, else `project.container_server_unavailable` (409) before any record\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - a non-default Instance records `model_route` at admission and returns it in the result: `ready: true`, or `ready: false` with `error_code` and `message`; human output prints one line when unavailable\n  %%> - the runtime reservation records its owning Operation; late receipts cannot change a newer owner\n  %%> - the whole-run budget is 5,700 seconds: 4,830 seconds of declared phase and child timeouts, including Node preparation, restoration and cleanup, rounded up to five minutes, plus 600 seconds for admission and settlement\n  %%> - maps only known host command locations, preserving opaque env/shell strings, and records each Process/from/to translation in the terminal receipt\n  %%> - the runtime record is reserved: the next bridge address and, for a non-default Instance, the next free T3 port after the Node's last one and the Project's `container_server`, which names the program of every call and the unit's `ExecStart`\n  %%> - the checkout stays the Instance's runtime until the Route has moved; the Route's Node is held for the run\n  %%> - the Instance takes no deployment and no wake while the run lasts\n  %%> - a non-default Instance's placeholder model key goes to the vault\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-migrate\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n  SW->>SW: Write source credential<br/>source-credential\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - resolves the same repository-scoped Contents read credential as deploy; a confirmed public repository uses anonymous HTTPS\n  %%> - source.github_app_denied names the App and repository; source.github_unavailable distinguishes a GitHub failure; no refused credential is written\n  %%> - gh_cli keeps the existing local-copy migration without this GitHub App preflight\n  SW->>N: Launch, probe container<br/>Ansible · tags container\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - makes one HTTPS read of the Project default branch with the deploy credential; the Instance branch stays local for copying and retirement, before any Node change or quiesce; its 30-second bound allows slow successful reads; source.read_timed_out says the source could not be reached in 30 s, source.head_unavailable names missing refs or network failures; Git authentication refusal names the App and repository with source.github_app_denied\n  %%> - before any Node change, checks the recorded branch and every planned retirement; a refusal leaves prior resources untouched\n  %%> - predicts symlinks against the copied layout before creating the container, using the same resolver as the final copied-tree check; it skips `.git` and installed package contents during link inspection; broken links or targets outside the checkout refuse with `instance.migrate_symlink_unsupported`, naming every path and target\n  %%> - permits only guaranteed generated Git metadata targets during prediction; source-only metadata such as `.git/local-only` refuses before downtime with the link path\n  %%> - retirement preflight refuses other worktrees of an owner repository, registered descendants, changed Git identity and submodule changes; local branches, stashes and staged or unstaged tracked edits are carried; every refusal keeps all data in the source or retained folder\n  %%> - refuses active clean/smudge filters and intent-to-add entries before effects with named recovery messages; Git work commands use private metadata views with controlled config, so no repository-defined program runs\n  %%> - checks service hosts selected by cache, session, queue, broadcasting, database or mail drivers (never by Redis client or endpoint declarations alone) in the primary application `.env`, each Process working-directory `.env` inside the checkout, and environment files named by its Processes or Schedules before any target or container change; attached services require an exact endpoint from the shared container access plan, and a private name of that plan (`reverb.orbit` of a private websocket app) only at its port, 8443; unsupported endpoints refuse with `instance.migrate_env_service_unreachable`, naming the file, key and host:port without credentials; unreadable syntax names only the file and line, and file read failures name only the path\n  %%> - selected SMTP on 127.0.0.1 or localhost, port 1025 or 2525, is satisfied by the same admitted main Mailpit endpoint used by the mail phase; absent mail explains the missing forwarder, and direct ::1 refuses because listeners are IPv4 only; both selector checks read the source before the copy and mailer rule\n  %%> - deletes a container that an earlier failed move left, then creates and starts the new container through the shared container phase; discard leaves shared bridge endpoint rules for host reconciliation\n  %%> - the new container gets its presented machine name in that phase: `/etc/hostname`, `PRETTY_HOSTNAME` in `/etc/machine-info` and the `127.0.1.1` line of `/etc/hosts`, and its kernel hostname at once\n  %%> - each attached target gets an exact TCP allow in both the bridge and NIC ACLs; both keep default reject. Endpoint adds reread on ETag conflict, at most five attempts with a one-second delay\n  %%> - probes every attached target from inside the container before downtime, else `instance.database_unreachable`\n  %%> - attached Node services wait up to three seconds per target, polling every 50 ms; `instance.node_service_unreachable` names the last reason, both unit states and elapsed time (see the Node service targets reference)\n  %%> - checks mapped Process executables inside the container, then repeats before quiesce; unavailable paths report `instance.migrate_process_binary_unavailable` naming every Process and path, leaving the checkout serving\n  %%> - fetches the branch from the checkout into the Node's mirror, including commits never pushed\n  SW->>C: Prepare Node runtimes<br/>Ansible · tags node-runtime\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - while the checkout still serves, mounts its source read-only in the new container, converts an inherited shared store to a private copy, and lets Vite+ resolve and install each Process's Node; the mount is removed on success and failure\n  %%> - this phase is bounded at 150 seconds; Vite+ resolution and verified download share 90 seconds; failure names the pin and reason with instance.node_runtime_prepare_failed and restores without downtime\n  SW->>N: Check and stop<br/>Ansible · tags quiesce\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - repeats the branch and retirement checks\n  %%> - checks every registered Node-timer Schedule and captures timer activation before stopping any writer; unsupported Schedules refuse with their name and reason\n  %%> - repeats source symlink and environment checks before stopping any writer\n  %%> - unattached loopback/private endpoints get a host TCP check: only explicit connection refusal warns with instance.migrate_env_service_unused; an answering or inconclusive endpoint keeps the refusal; selected host sockets refuse before TCP probing; public or mixed-address names keep the original handling; at most 20 unique endpoints share two seconds each per check\n  %%> - stops and disables host Schedule timers and Process units, then removes the awake marker\n  %%> - records that quiesce began, so a failure before it never restarts host units or rewrites the awake marker\n  SW->>C: Build the workspace<br/>Ansible · tags workspace\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - pushes the selected branch as the workspace base in a standalone repository in `/srv/workspace`; the files phase carries the rest of the local Git state\n  %%> - the container gets independent Git metadata, whether the source is an owner or a linked worktree\n  SW->>C: Copy the files<br/>Ansible · tags files\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - exports all local branch tips, the complete ordered stash reflog and index entries without changing source metadata; packs every reachable branch, stash-parent and staged object, including alternates\n  %%> - imports objects, installs branch refs atomically and rebuilds the stash reflog and staging state; copies no source hooks, config, alternates or worktree registrations\n  %%> - copies every file that differs from the commit, every untracked file and every ignored file, `.env` among them, plus missing directories including empty ignored trees; the result counts untracked files that Git does not ignore, expanding selected nested repositories to archived leaf entries, without paths or contents\n  %%> - untracked files stay in the migrated default until its next deployment, whose checkout removes those that Git does not ignore (development-containers.md#the-default-keeps-a-clean-tree); ignored files, `.env` among them, stay and enter its seed, as ignored build output already does\n  %%> - restores added directories' exact modes after the shared file-mode rule, including setgid and sticky bits; ownership remains www-data:www-data; restoration runs as www-data with the manifest on stdin, opens every component as a directory without following links, and refuses absolute paths or parent traversal\n  %%> - removes the tracked files that the checkout deleted, and rewrites the checkout's path in `.env` to `/srv/workspace`\n  %%> - removes as www-data each ignored file under a `cache` folder, outside `vendor` and `node_modules`, whose content names the old checkout path, such as PHPStan's and Rector's caches; Git trusts the workspace on its own call; a Git failure warns `instance.migrate_tool_cache_clear_failed` and the move goes on\n  %%> - in a PHP container with `artisan`, clears as www-data the Laravel caches built at the old path: config, route, event, compiled and view caches, and the cache store when its driver is `file`; a failed clear warns `instance.migrate_cache_clear_failed` and the move goes on\n  %%> - before path rewrites or application starts, compares branch refs, selected HEAD, exact stash reflog, index, porcelain status and hashes of every carried file, including untracked and ignored paths, on both sides; git fsck proves object completeness; a mismatch fails before cutover and restore discards the container\n  %%> - checks the exact copied tree inside the container, maps old-checkout targets into the workspace and rewrites absolute internal links there; broken or external links fail with the same code before any Process starts, relative links keep their targets, and source links never change\n  %%> - a non-default Instance gets the proxy device that publishes its T3 port\n  opt Node runs the main Mailpit\n    SW->>C: Apply development mail<br/>Ansible · tags mail\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - after copying the checkout's `.env`, before application Processes start\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - record `instance.mail_update_failed` for a failed attempt or missing/malformed receipt; application starts, Route cutover and retirement continue, with no rollback for mail; `instance:mail:update` repairs it later\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the shared path status and exact-line result, with line numbers only; conflicts skip the line rule\n  end\n  SW->>C: Start the container<br/>Processes · T3 · seed\n  %%> - first installs carried Schedule pairs disabled, after the workspace copy and before application Processes start; services run as www-data\n  %%> - container units use `network.target` and nonblocking starts; reads all moved desired-running Processes active with no pending job, waits 5 seconds once for the whole set, then requires each still active with the same positive MainPID and NRestarts; failure names the Process with `instance.migrate_processes_failed` and restores before cutover\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts FrankenPHP and the Instance's existing Processes as `www-data`, with the same ids and desired states; their environment files come through the vault\n  %%> - writes the shared FrankenPHP Caddyfile and unit; only the Node's bridge peer with `X-Forwarded-Proto: https` sets PHP's HTTPS scheme, port and resolved client IP; other requests keep native values\n  %%> - `vp-dev` uses the runtime folder’s `current/bin/vp`, workspace paths and its assigned port on `0.0.0.0`; only the Node can reach that port, and Caddy sends `/__orbit/vite` there\n  %%> - a non-default Instance gets `/etc/orbit/instance`, the agent settings with the drop-in that names its recorded container server, and that server, started after a systemd reload and registered as in create\n  %%> - a default Instance gets its first `seed-<commit>` snapshot\n  %%> - attached Node service proxies are installed and proven before quiesce, inside the container launch; their Processes run without root\n  SW->>N: Move the Route<br/>child route-mutation-forward\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - points the Instance's Route at FrankenPHP in the container, and withdraws the checkout's PHP-FPM pool\n  alt all steps pass\n    SW->>N: Remove the checkout<br/>Ansible · tags retire\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - a due timer fire waits as a recorded Schedule Operation until migration terminal projection reopens admission; its context is rebuilt for the final runtime, on success or rollback\n    %%> - every terminal transition settles deferred fires under the Instance lock; closed runtime admission explicitly fails them, including bound start failure and expiry; Schedule settlement preserves the migration result and late native recovery cannot replay a settled fire\n    %%> - activates carried container timers with Persistent=false and the recorded desired state; removes their host pairs at retirement\n    %%> - a Schedule activation or host-unit cleanup failure is advisory after cutover: record id, name, not_activated and cause, keep the container serving and succeed migration\n    %%> - removes the old host Process units and their environment folders\n    %%> - rechecks the source snapshot, canonical Git identity, registrations and retirement guards immediately before retaining it; any changed, new or deleted carried path refuses retirement and keeps every source file\n    %%> - atomically moves each old checkout under `<checkout>.orbit-retired-git/<sha256 of original path>`; the retained root is beside the migrating Instance's checkout\n    %%> - keeps every tracked, untracked and ignored file; for a linked worktree, copies the common objects/refs/reflogs and its index and private metadata into an independent .git, materializes alternates, and verifies recovery before removing only its identity-matched, now-missing owner registration\n    %%> - records `retained_git_path` and `retained_git_at` on the Instance; `instance:show` and the Instance API expose them\n    %%> - writes the awake marker; owner retirement refuses while any other worktree is registered (not the moving worktree, not a prunable, unlocked entry whose folder is gone); linked retirement never changes the owner store or prunes another registration\n    %%> If it fails:\n    %%> - the container stays the Instance; the run fails with `instance.migrate_retire_failed`, and the source or retained checkout stays on the Node; a pending linked-unregister identity receipt makes repeat retirement fail explicitly\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    Note over GW: Record runtime<br/>and Process definitions\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Instance's workspace is the container's; the runtime runs, a default's row holds its seed commit\n    %%> - the same transaction captures each direct moved Process as a Project development definition and links its existing row; known commands use runtime names, opaque guest argv stays exact, and a changed same-name incompatible definition refuses instead of overwriting\n    %%> - proven cutover captures definitions even if retirement failed; preflight failures and restored checkout outcomes capture nothing\n    %%> - a SQLite file moved with the files, so its connection record goes; stored `.env` values follow the new path\n    %%> - the Conn layer records a non-default Instance's T3 server from the result\n  else a step fails\n    SW->>N: Restore the checkout<br/>Route · container removed\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - puts the Route back on the checkout when the run had moved it\n    %%> - deletes the new container, its ACL, its firewall rules and its mirror\n    %%> - restores host Process units and the awake marker only when quiesce began; an unreachable target causes zero downtime\n    %%> - restores host Schedule timers to their captured activation states; their unit files stay unchanged before cutover\n    %%> - the checkout and database remain in place\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step, error_code\n    Note over GW: Project native end state\n    %%> - the reconciler settles native moved/restored/ready after expiry; shows return recorded state, and admission waits for settlement\n    %%> - an independent phase failure keeps its code; deadline abort stays failed/`operation.timed_out`, user abort stays cancelled\n    %%> - a native finish supplies step statuses and times, even when no events streamed\n    %%> - missing finish output gets 60 seconds from the native end, capped by the original deadline\n    %%> - verified end and rollback then release the runtime reservation with instance.migrate_result_unavailable; the result keeps native status and known failure facts\n    %%> - otherwise the deadline ends result recovery; the reservation and Instance runtime stay\n    %%> - retained reservations block another migrate; the result names missing evidence and owner recovery\n    %%> - no Orbit command repairs missing cutover or rollback proof\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again; it starts with a new container\n    %%> - a restore that failed keeps the reserved record and its address, and names the failed step\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/container/migrate-production": {
   "kind": "instance.migrate",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design, production containers (ADR 0263, docs/reference/production-containers.md#a-checkout-production-instance): instance:migrate moves a checkout production Instance into its first release container, a cold hot built at the commit that `current` selects. The checkout serves until the switch. Its native Schedules move to the live link when the run ends; the first deploy after the move copies the shared paths a last time and removes the home (container/deploy-production.mmd). The command exists only until the last checkout Instance has moved (docs/reference/old-paths.md).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:migrate\n  %% kind: instance.migrate\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane C icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as app-prod Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  participant C as Hot<br/>release container\n  CLI->>GW: instance:migrate INSTANCE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:migrate INSTANCE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<instance> [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - no downtime prompt: the checkout serves until one Caddy reload switches the Routes\n  Note over GW: Validate, freeze intent<br/>Instance locked · TASK\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - an active checkout production Instance on an `app-prod` Node with the container setup; no open deployment or Process operation\n  %%> Freezes:\n  %%> - the commit that `current` selects, the steps, the shared paths, the Process and Schedule definitions, the health path and the domain of each Route\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the Instance takes no deployment while the run lasts; its storage volume is recorded\n  GW->>SW: Push token and .env<br/>vault: token-TASK, env-TASK\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - hot fetches the commit and gets the rendered `.env`; both travel only through the vault\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>instance-migrate-production\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - one run; secrets only as vault key names\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>TASK\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task until it ends\n  SW->>N: Check capacity, launch hot<br/>Ansible · tags admit · hot\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the capacity check of a deploy, then a cold hot from the image with the storage volume, the memory limit when one is set, and the pinned runtime folder version\n  SW->>N: Copy shared paths<br/>Ansible · tags copy\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - copies each shared path of the home, and a SQLite database of the home, into the volume; the checkout still serves\n  %%> Why before the steps:\n  %%> - a step that makes keys only when they are missing finds the existing keys\n  SW->>C: Fetch, check out, .env<br/>Ansible · tags source\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the source step of a deploy, at the frozen commit\n  SW->>C: Run before steps<br/>incus exec · as www-data\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - each `before_activation` step; `migrate` finds the schema at this commit and changes nothing\n  SW->>C: Install units, snapshot<br/>Ansible · tags release\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - disabled units and timers, then the `release` snapshot, as in a deploy\n  SW->>N: Check hot is ready<br/>Ansible · tags ready\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the readiness of a deploy; on failure `deployment.not_ready`\n  SW->>N: Switch the Routes<br/>Ansible · tags switch\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - writes the Instance's upstream file and live link, points each Route's site at the upstream file instead of PHP-FPM and reloads Caddy once\n  %%> - requests in progress on PHP-FPM finish there\n  SW->>N: Hand over from the home<br/>Ansible · tags handover\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - disables and stops the host Node timers and Process units, with the bounded graceful stop, then the PHP-FPM pool\n  %%> - enables and starts the new live's timers and Processes, and turns `boot.autostart` on\n  SW->>C: Run after steps<br/>incus exec · as www-data\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - each `after_activation` step, in the new live\n  SW->>N: Copy changes, read back<br/>Ansible · tags copy · check\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - copies only files the volume lacks or holds in an older version, and deletes none: live already writes there\n  %%> - reads back the live link, the upstream file, the commit and the Process states\n  alt all steps pass\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    Note over GW: Record live release<br/>move Schedules to live link\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Instance runs in containers; its runtime row is live\n    %%> - the Gateway rewrites each native Schedule's workflow for the live link and sets its trigger again, as an Instance transfer does; a fire that started in the home finishes there\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - the first deploy after the move copies the shared paths a last time and removes the home\n  else a step fails before or at the switch\n    SW->>N: Remove hot<br/>always · checkout serves\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - deletes hot; the volume stays and the rerun copies into it again\n    %%> - the checkout, its Processes, timers and pool serve as before\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step, error code\n  else a step fails after the switch\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step, error code\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - live serves; the home stays unserved\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again; it repeats only the handover, the after steps and the copy\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/node-service-attach": {
   "kind": "instance.node_service_attach",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Record-only attachment: recorded Process intent, no Node execution.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:node-service:attach\n  %% kind: instance.node_service_attach\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  Note over CLI: Resolve command inputs\n  alt local input refused\n    CLI--xCLI: Invalid input or cancelled<br/>No attach request\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - instance.id_invalid refuses an invalid supplied or missing noninteractive Instance ID, or an empty Instance list after the registry GET; no mutation request is sent.\n    %%> - instance.node_service_process_required refuses a missing noninteractive Process ID, or an empty Node Process list after its registry GET; no mutation request is sent.\n    %%> - instance.node_service_host_key_required and instance.node_service_port_key_required refuse omitted key names only in JSON or noninteractive mode.\n    %%> - validation.failed refuses an invalid supplied Process ID or port, including an explicitly empty port.\n    %%> - input.cancelled ends a cancelled terminal selection or text prompt before mutation.\n    %%> - State stays unchanged. Invalid supplied IDs or port and missing noninteractive inputs send no request. Terminal cancellation or empty choices can follow registry reads.\n  else local input available\n    opt human terminal needs input\n      CLI->>GW: Read registry choices\n      GW-->>CLI: Registry choices\n      Note over CLI: Select IDs and enter keys\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - Omitted IDs use registry data lists; omitted environment keys use validated text prompts. Reads may refuse without an attachment change.\n    end\n    CLI->>GW: instance:node-service<br/>attach INSTANCE --process=PROCESS\n    %%> Command:\n    %%> - orbit instance:node-service:attach INSTANCE --process=PROCESS --host-env-key=KEY --port-env-key=KEY\n    %%> - syntax: instance:node-service:attach\n    Note over GW: Check request and access\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - peer.identity_unknown, node_access.required or http.404 refuses the caller or numeric Instance; authority is required for both the Instance Node and selected Process Node.\n    %%> - validation.failed refuses invalid body fields or environment keys.\n    alt request refused\n      GW--xCLI: Named refusal<br/>No attachment change\n    else request admitted\n      Note over GW: Lock and check Instance\n      alt busy or container runtime\n        GW--xCLI: Instance refused<br/>No attachment change\n        %%> On failure:\n        %%> - instance.lifecycle_busy refuses an inactive Instance, running migration or detach.\n        %%> - instance.node_service_runtime_unsupported refuses an existing container runtime.\n      else checkout available\n        Note over GW: Check standalone Process<br/>and TCP publication\n        %%> Why:\n        %%> - Admission uses recorded ownership, desired state and Docker publication; it contacts no Node.\n        alt Process or endpoint refused\n          GW--xCLI: Target refused<br/>No attachment change\n          %%> On failure:\n          %%> - instance.node_service_process_missing and instance.node_service_process_owned refuse an unknown, non-Node or service-owned Process; remote Docker Processes are eligible.\n          %%> - instance.node_service_protocol_unsupported and instance.node_service_process_stopped refuse non-TCP or desired-stopped targets.\n          %%> - instance.node_service_port_required and instance.node_service_port_unpublished refuse an omitted choice among several ports or an unpublished TCP host port.\n          %%> - instance.node_service_port_reserved, instance.host_unavailable and instance.node_service_listener_private refuse infrastructure ports, a missing Instance bridge, or an ineligible bind. A remote target needs exactly one TCP publication at its owner's private IPv4 WireGuard address.\n          %%> - instance.dev_band_reserved refuses a same-Node target in the development band, 5173-5199\n        else valid endpoint\n          alt identical attachment\n            GW-->>CLI: Already attached<br/>changed false, reason code\n            %%> When:\n            %%> - The Process, port and both keys match the existing record; its stable skip reason is instance.node_service_target_unchanged.\n          else environment key conflict\n            GW--xCLI: Key conflict<br/>No attachment change\n            %%> On failure:\n            %%> - instance.node_service_environment_conflict requires detaching the old record before a correction.\n          else unused keys\n            Note over GW: Record service Node<br/>and attachment\n            alt transaction fails\n              GW--xCLI: Transaction failed<br/>Attachment write rolls back\n            else transaction commits\n              GW-->>CLI: Target result<br/>changed true, request ID\n            end\n          end\n        end\n      end\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/node-service-detach": {
   "kind": "instance.node_service_detach",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Withdraw container access before deleting the attachment.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:node-service:detach\n  %% kind: instance.node_service_detach\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CALL icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  participant CALL as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>Node service phase\n  Note over CALL: Resolve input and consent\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - Omitted Instance and attachment IDs use registry choices. The attachment GET is scoped to the selected Instance and completes before consent.\n  alt local input refused\n    CALL--xCALL: Refused or cancelled<br/>No delete request\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - instance.id_invalid refuses an invalid supplied or missing noninteractive Instance ID, or an empty Instance list after the registry GET; no mutation request is sent.\n    %%> - instance.node_service_target_id_invalid refuses an invalid supplied attachment ID, an omitted attachment ID in JSON or noninteractive mode, or an empty attachment list after its registry GET; no mutation request is sent.\n    %%> - instance.node_service_confirmation_required refuses a JSON or noninteractive call without --yes.\n    %%> - input.cancelled ends a cancelled terminal selection or confirmation, or declined consent, before mutation.\n    %%> - State stays unchanged. Invalid supplied IDs, missing noninteractive IDs and missing noninteractive consent send no request. Terminal selection or confirmation cancellation can follow registry reads.\n  else inputs and consent ready\n    CALL->>GW: instance:node-service<br/>detach INSTANCE TARGET\n    %%> Command:\n    %%> - orbit instance:node-service:detach INSTANCE TARGET\n    %%> - syntax: instance:node-service:detach\n    Note over GW: Check request and access\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - peer.identity_unknown or node_access.required (403): the caller lacks authority.\n    %%> - http.404 (404): the Instance cannot be resolved.\n    %%> - validation.failed (422) or operation.idempotency_conflict (409): the request key is invalid or conflicts.\n    %%> - Every refusal leaves state unchanged and creates no new receipt.\n    alt request or access refused\n      GW--xCALL: Request refused<br/>No changes or new receipt\n    else request admitted\n      Note over GW: Lock the Instance<br/>Begin one transaction\n      alt lifecycle busy\n        GW--xCALL: Lifecycle busy<br/>No changes or new receipt\n        %%> On failure:\n        %%> - instance.lifecycle_busy (409): the Instance is not active, migration is running or a detach is already running.\n      else failed move retains runtime\n        GW--xCALL: Runtime cleanup required<br/>No changes or new receipt\n        %%> On failure:\n        %%> - instance.node_service_runtime_cleanup_required (409): retry instance:migrate to finish or restore the move before detaching.\n      else target missing or foreign\n        GW--xCALL: Attachment missing (404)<br/>No changes or new receipt\n        %%> On failure:\n        %%> - instance.node_service_target_missing: the attachment must belong to the selected Instance.\n      else another Operation owns target\n        GW--xCALL: Target busy<br/>No changes or new receipt\n        %%> On failure:\n        %%> - operation.target_busy (409): the Instance's Operation lock is held.\n      else target admitted\n        opt running container\n          GW->>SW: node-service (withdraw)\n          Note over SW: Withdraw owned path<br/>Keep shared ports\n          %%> On failure:\n          %%> - Keep the attachment; retry completes the withdrawal.\n          SW-->>GW: Withdrawal complete\n        end\n        Note over GW: Remove attachment<br/>Complete Operation\n        %%> When:\n        %%> - A checkout needs no Node work. A container keeps the record until withdrawal succeeds.\n        alt transaction fails\n          GW--xCALL: Transaction failed<br/>Both writes roll back\n          %%> On failure:\n          %%> - The attachment remains and no new receipt is persisted, including no failed receipt.\n        else transaction commits\n          GW-->>CALL: Completed Operation\n        end\n      end\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/register": {
   "kind": "instance.register",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): only the Route-publish step of this run holds the Route-serving Nodes, for its seconds, through the Gateway's `route-hold` call; the rest of the run is not held (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:register\n  %% kind: instance.register\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane A icon=server pill=observer tone=amber\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP\n  participant GW as Gateway\n  participant A as Node agent\n  participant SW as Swamp\n  participant N as Target Node\n  loop on change\n    A->>GW: Report checkout facts\n  end\n  CLI->>GW: instance:register<br/>--project=7\n  Note over GW: Check against the facts<br/>path · origin · layout\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - a new host registration refuses with instance.container_required (409) before reading checkout facts or recording anything; use instance:create\n  %%> - only a previously registered checkout can repeat the phases below; existing checkout operation and migration remain supported\n  %%> - a Project with `nested_fleet` on refuses with `operation.kind_unavailable` (503) before any record: only `instance:create` makes Instances of such a Project, as containers; the Gateway checks it before it reads the facts and again when it records the Instance\n  alt facts match\n    Note over GW: Record the Instance<br/>branch · commit · application\n    alt record-only\n      GW-->>CLI: 201 Instance active\n    else Node work needed\n      GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-register\n      GW-->>CLI: 202 Task accepted\n      SW->>N: Protect the .env<br/>@swamp/ssh exec\n      %%> Only when an existing .env is publicly readable.\n      opt --setup\n        SW->>N: Run Project setup steps<br/>@swamp/ssh exec\n        SW->>N: Create, start Processes<br/>child create · start\n      end\n      opt --domain\n        SW->>GW: Hold the Route Nodes<br/>operation:direct:route-hold\n        %%> What it does:\n        %%> - asks the Gateway for the hold of each Node that serves the Route's site, for the seconds of this step only, not the whole operation\n        %%> - the Gateway reads the Nodes from the admitted intent; the call carries the run ID\n        %%> - a Node that another Route write holds is waited for, up to 5 s per call and 2 minutes in all; then the step fails with `route_hold.busy` and the Route step does not run\n        %%> - the hold has a 5-minute lease and ends with the Operation, so a lost run cannot keep a Node\n        SW->>N: Create the Route<br/>child route-create\n        %%> A completed registration needs --setup to check its Processes before marking the runtime awake.\n        SW->>GW: Release the Route Nodes<br/>operation:direct:route-release\n        %%> What it does:\n        %%> - gives the Nodes back as soon as the Route step ends, whatever its result; the Route writes that wait for them then start\n        %%> - runs beside the next step, so it adds no time\n      end\n      alt every step passes\n        SW-->>GW: Run succeeded\n        Note over GW: Record the result<br/>Instance active · URL\n      else a step fails\n        SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step · error code\n        Note over GW: Keep the Instance reserved<br/>rerun repairs it\n      end\n    end\n  else facts do not match\n    GW--xCLI: Refuse registration\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/release-list": {
   "kind": "instance.release_list",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: Nick 2026-11-06, agent strip (b): a release list answers from the deployment history the Gateway records. No Node contact, no agent command (R1).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:release:list\n  %% kind: instance.release_list\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  CLI->>GW: instance:release:list 14\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:release:list <instance>`\n  %%> - syntax: `instance:release:list <instance> [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the numeric Instance ID from `instance:list`\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Node that serves the Instance\n  Note over GW: Read the history<br/>retained · selected\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - every deploy and rollback is a run whose terminal result the Gateway records, including the releases the deploy's cleanup removed\n  %%> - the retained releases are the ones the history keeps: the first release, `initial`, and after each successful deploy the new selection, the selection before it and the newest other release, three in all\n  %%> - the selected release is the one the last deploy or rollback activated\n  %%> - it starts no run and contacts no Node\n  alt production Instance\n    GW-->>CLI: 200 releases<br/>selected release\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the retained release names sorted by name, and the selected one\n    %%> - the list is what the history retains, not a scan of the Node: a release removed by hand is still listed, and one kept only because a running process uses it is not\n  else development default\n    GW-->>CLI: 200 empty list\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - a development default deploys in its checkout and has no releases\n  else any other Instance\n    GW--xCLI: 409<br/>deployment.identity_invalid\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the Instance is not an active production Instance with a release layout and a recorded user and home\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/rename": {
   "kind": "instance.rename",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: docs/cli/instance/rename.mdx, ADR 0244, R1 record-only; read-only rule (Nick 2026-11-02): the Gateway checks the Node agent's checkout facts, like instance:register.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rule 5 (its own example: rename records the branch the Node agent reported, with no run).\n  %% Decided: Nick 2026-11-02, this chart is the ideal record-only flow. Implemented in the observer pass (agent 0.7.0, 2026-11-03).\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: instance:rename\n  %% kind: instance.rename\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane A icon=server pill=observer tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant A as Node agent<br/>orbit-agent\n  loop continuously, on change\n    Note over A: Observe checkouts<br/>under the apps root\n    %%> What it observes:\n    %%> - each Git checkout under `<apps-root>/<project-slug>/<name>`, Instance 42's included\n    %%> - its top level, origin, symbolic `HEAD` and commit\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - event-driven, with inotify or FSEvents, on `.git/HEAD`, `refs/heads`, `packed-refs`, `config` and each worktree `HEAD`\n    %%> - a full resync every 10 minutes and at agent start\n    %%> Runs as:\n    %%> - read-only; the Node agent never changes a Node\n    A->>GW: Report observations<br/>with observed_at\n    %%> What it sends:\n    %%> - what changed since the last report, with the time it was observed\n    Note over GW: Store latest facts<br/>per Node, per checkout\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Gateway always has a recent picture of the checkouts on each app-dev Node\n  end\n  CLI->>GW: instance:rename 42\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:rename 42 --branch=t3code/login-redirect`\n  %%> - syntax: `instance:rename <instance> --branch=BRANCH [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - a local branch you already checked out, for example after `git branch -m t3code/login-redirect`\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - it does not rename the Instance, move its directory or change Git `HEAD`\n  %%> - `--domain` returns `operation.kind_unavailable`\n  Note over CLI: Require a rename field\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - with neither `--branch` nor `--domain`, returns `validation.failed`; no request is sent\n  opt --domain\n    GW--xCLI: Domain rename unavailable<br/>operation.kind_unavailable\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - refuses before changing any field, also when `--branch` is supplied\n  end\n  opt --branch\n    Note over GW: Check against the facts<br/>HEAD · identity · observed_at\n    %%> Requirements:\n    %%> - Instance 42 is an active development checkout or linked worktree on Node 12\n    %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12\n    %%> - the branch is a valid local branch name\n    %%> Checks in the agent's latest report:\n    %%> - symbolic `HEAD` of Instance 42's checkout names `t3code/login-redirect`\n    %%> - the checkout still matches its recorded top level and origin\n    %%> - the report is fresh enough to trust\n    %%> Not needed:\n    %%> - no Swamp run and no SSH: the fact is already recorded (R1)\n    alt HEAD names the branch\n      Note over GW: Record the branch<br/>selected_branch · override\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - `selected_branch` and `branch_override` of Instance 42 become `t3code/login-redirect`\n      %%> - recording the already recorded branch keeps its existing override\n      %%> - a dirty or unpublished branch can be recorded\n      GW-->>CLI: Branch recorded<br/>Instance 42 · t3code/login-red…\n      %%> What you get:\n      %%> - the final result at once; there is nothing to follow\n    else other branch, detached or stale\n      GW--xCLI: Refused or can't verify<br/>instance.branch_not_checked_out\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - symbolic `HEAD` names another branch or is detached\n      %%> - or the checkout no longer matches its recorded identity\n      %%> - or the latest report is too old: the refusal says it can't verify\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - Instance 42 keeps its recorded branch; nothing is recorded\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - check out the branch, wait for the next agent report, then run `instance:rename` again\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/restore": {
   "kind": "instance.restore",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: restore a host development Instance whose removal failed after its Route withdrawal; no fence settlement, no new publish path.\n  %% Tightened: refusals at admission; the run checks the databases, republishes through Route create, then clears this removal's journal rows.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:restore\n  %% kind: instance.restore\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Target Nodes<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: instance:restore 42\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:restore 42`\n  %%> - syntax: `instance:restore <instance> [--json]`\n  Note over GW: Check the failed removal\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - a host development Instance, not a container, in `removing`\n  %%> - its live removal is `failed`, and every removal Operation has ended; a timed-out run without a native end refuses\n  %%> - every removal run recorded each planned step that deletes data as skipped: source, Processes, teardown\n  %%> - the removal saved no Schedule and no SQLite file\n  %%> - its one Route is `retiring` with the same domain and targets\n  %%> - the Node agent reports the checkout: same top level, origin of the Project's repository\n  %%> - no other Operation holds the Instance or its Route\n  alt a check fails\n    %% path: refused\n    GW--xCLI: Refused<br/>instance.restore_*\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - nothing changes; no Operation starts\n  else the removal can be undone\n    %% path: main\n    GW->>SW: Start the restore run<br/>operation-instance-restore\n    %%> Captures:\n    %%> - the database names the removal planned, per server\n    %%> - the Route create plan for the same Route, targets and domain\n    %%> - holds the publication Nodes, as Route create does\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the CLI follows the instance.restore Operation\n    SW->>N: Check the databases<br/>admin client · read-only\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - each planned database exists and accepts connections\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - `instance.restore_database_closed` or `instance.restore_database_missing` names the databases; this command opens nothing\n    SW->>N: Republish the Route<br/>operation-route-create\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the Route create children unchanged: CSR, sign, site, TLS probe\n    %%> - writes the awake marker again\n    SW->>N: Clear the fence journal<br/>admin client · one transaction\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - checks again that every planned database is open\n    %%> - deletes only the journal rows whose OID and name match a planned database\n    alt the run succeeds\n      %% path: restored\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded\n      Note over GW: Record restored<br/>one transaction\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - Instance `active`, removal and members `abandoned`, Route `active` and published\n      %%> - the destroy Operation keeps its failure\n      GW-->>CLI: Instance restored\n    else a step fails\n      %% path: failed\n      SW--xGW: Run failed\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the Instance stays `removing`, the removal `failed`, the Route `retiring`\n      %%> - a site published before a later failure can serve until the next Caddy publication on that Node\n      %%> - run `instance:restore` again, or `instance:destroy`\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/rollback": {
   "kind": "instance.rollback",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task + full intent as inputs, single-swamp-node; DECISIONS 4 / R1-R4 (process.stop shape), round 2 (admit instead of refuse; php_input and runtime_definitions as inputs), 3 (rollback removes the auto-deploy schedule); rule 7 (reload over restart, restart_on_deploy only). docs/reference/deployments.md#roll-back.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 5, 16, 22 (no release read: the Gateway records retained releases and holds the Instance lock; cfgmgmt link/systemd).\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: instance:rollback\n  %% kind: instance.rollback\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as app-prod Node<br/>Node 12 over ssh\n  alt AppProd container rollback\n    CLI->>GW: Select the retained commit\n    GW->>SW: Start production-release\n    Note over SW: Check release ownership\n    SW->>N: Build the Instance release<br/>wake the retained release\n    SW->>N: Sleep preparation\n    SW->>N: Check the Instance<br/>candidate\n    SW->>N: Hand over the release<br/>switch Routes and workers\n    SW->>N: Release unused candidate\n    SW->>N: Read the serving release\n    SW-->>GW: Verified commit and owner\n  else Host checkout rollback\n  CLI->>GW: instance:rollback 42\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:rollback 42 --release=20261030T101500Z`\n  %%> - syntax: `<instance> --release=NAME [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Instance, and one retained release name from `instance:release:list 42`\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  %%> Required input:\n  %%> - `--release=NAME` is mandatory; omission returns `deployment.release_required` before admission\n  Note over GW: Check release, admit<br/>Instance 42 locked · task #5151\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12\n  %%> - Instance 42 is a production Instance; else `deployment_config.unavailable`\n  %%> - Instance 42 retains release `20261030T101500Z`; else `rollback.release_not_retained` and nothing changes\n  %%> - no started operation holds Instance 42; else `operation.target_busy`\n  %%> Wins over:\n  %%> - a deploy that no worker has started; it ends `cancelled` with `operation.superseded`\n  %%> Freezes:\n  %%> - `php_input`: the PHP runtime and pool of Instance 42\n  %%> - `runtime_definitions`: its Process and Schedule definitions\n  %%> - the account `orbit-app-7` and its home\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - deployment 89 recorded as a running rollback; task #5151 recorded with the full intent\n  %%> - automatic deployment pauses, and its schedule is removed\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>php_input, runtime_definitions\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one run of `operation-instance-rollback` on Swamp serve (Node 209); every step runs there\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  %%> - the release name, `php_input` and `runtime_definitions`\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - no GitHub token and no `.env`: a rollback fetches nothing and writes no environment\n  %%> Budget:\n  %%> - 900 s\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5151\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task until it ends\n  SW->>N: Activate release<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/link · current.next\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over `@swamp/ssh` from 209 with `@swamp/cfgmgmt/link`; nothing is copied to the Node\n  %%> Why no read first:\n  %%> - the Gateway's retained-release records come from deploy results, and Instance 42's lock keeps a deploy's cleanup from removing the release meanwhile\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - adds each served application's missing `.env` link in the release\n  %%> - writes `current.next`, owned by `orbit-app-7`, pointing at `releases/20261030T101500Z`\n  %%> When a link fails:\n  %%> - `current.next` is not written, and `current` keeps the old release\n  SW->>N: Swap current<br/>ssh exec · mv -fT\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - renames `current.next` over `current` with one atomic rename, so `current` always exists\n  %%> - the link keeps its owner\n  %%> Cancel:\n  %%> - a cancel before this step stops the rollback; after it, the rollback finishes\n  opt Instance serves PHP\n    SW->>N: Refresh PHP cache<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/systemd reload\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - converges the dedicated PHP-FPM pool from `php_input`\n    %%> - reloads it gracefully, so running requests finish; then resets OPcache and waits for it\n    %%> When a restart is needed:\n    %%> - only when the pool's runtime itself differs, such as the PHP version\n  end\n  SW->>N: Restart Processes<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/systemd · forEach\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - installs `runtime_definitions`, in the `process.create` and `schedule.create` shape\n  %%> - for each running Process with `restart_on_deploy`: reload when the Process allows it, else restart\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - Processes without `restart_on_deploy`, and stopped Processes, stay as they are\n  %%> - no deploy step, no Git fetch, no database change\n  SW->>N: Read current back<br/>ssh exec · readlink\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - reads the live `current` link on Node 12\n  Note over SW: Check the selection<br/>native assert\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - checks the link read back is `releases/20261030T101500Z`\n  %%> - each restarted Process was checked by its own restart\n  alt all steps pass\n    N-->>SW: release, commit\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the selected release, its commit, and the restarted Processes\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record the rollback<br/>block old commit · task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - deployment 89 succeeds with the release and its commit\n    %%> - the commit it moved away from is blocked for automatic deploys\n    %%> - automatic deployment stays paused until `instance:auto-deploy:enable 42`\n    %%> - task #5151 succeeds\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or timeout\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the release is gone, a check or reload fails, SSH fails, or 900 s run out\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed_step, selected release\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code\n    %%> - which release `current` selects now\n    Note over GW: Record failed rollback<br/>selection kept · task failed\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - deployment 89 is marked failed; task #5151 fails\n    %%> - the old commit is still blocked, and automatic deployment stays paused\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - a failure before activation keeps the old selection\n    %%> - a failure after it keeps the rollback release selected\n    %%> - Orbit restores no data; you own application recovery\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again after a fix; it is a new task and safe to repeat\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5151\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/container/rollback-production": {
   "kind": "instance.rollback",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% command: instance:rollback\n  %% kind: instance.rollback\n  %% status: migrated\n  autonumber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP\n  participant GW as Gateway\n  participant SW as Swamp\n  participant N as AppProd Node<br/>Instance owner\n  CLI->>GW: instance:rollback<br/>previous release\n  GW->>GW: Hold the Instance<br/>confirm its rollback commit\n  GW->>SW: Start production-release<br/>private Operation input\n  SW->>N: Confirm current live<br/>check retained commit\n  SW->>N: Start the stopped old<br/>apply current environment\n  SW->>N: Check candidate readiness\n  SW->>N: Switch Instance Routes<br/>hand over Processes and Schedules\n  SW->>N: Drain and stop old live<br/>retain it stopped for rollback\n  SW->>N: Read live and old commits\n  SW-->>GW: Verified release receipt<br/>Node, Instance, commit and digest\n  GW-->>CLI: Record the result<br/>verified live and rollback commits\n"
  },
  "instance/server-switch": {
   "kind": "instance.server_switch",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Switch an existing T3 container runtime to the Conn Server (ADR 0258, docs/reference/development-containers.md#container-server).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:server:switch\n  %% kind: instance.server_switch\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>Instance container\n  CLI->>GW: instance:server:switch ID\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:server:switch INSTANCE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<instance-id> [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  Note over GW: Validate & check idle\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - active container runtime with `container_server = t3` and `conn-server` pinned in the recipe\n  %%> - for a running container, confirm no agent turn, pending request or held reservation is active\n  %%> On failure:\n  %%> - serverless runtime (`instance.container_server_none`), already `conn` (`instance.container_server_already_conn`), active turn/task (`instance.container_server_busy`), unreadable T3 (`instance.t3_unreadable`) or lifecycle work (`instance.lifecycle_busy`) refuses with 409\n  alt container is stopped\n    %% path: deferred\n    Note over GW: Record conn & complete<br/>switch: deferred\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - `instance_runtimes.container_server` becomes `conn` at once; the next `instance:wake` writes `20-orbit-server.conf` before boot and updates `server_version`\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Succeeded Operation ID\n  else container is running\n    %% path: main\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-server-switch\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID\n    SW->>N: Switch container server<br/>Ansible · tags switch\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - re-check `t3.service` processes with `t3-service-processes.j2`; refuse `instance.container_server_busy` if any terminal or command runs\n    %%> - read `bin/conn-server --version`, push `20-orbit-server.conf`, reload systemd, restart `t3.service`, and wait up to 600 s (the import) until `/.well-known/t3/environment` reports the Conn Server version\n    %%> - Conn Server imports `statev2.sqlite` into `engine.sqlite` on first start while leaving `statev2.sqlite` unchanged\n    alt switched\n      %% path: switched\n      N-->>SW: Result<br/>switch · server_version\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result\n      Note over GW: Record conn & version<br/>runtime · ConnEnvironment\n    else busy or failed\n      %% path: failed\n      N--xSW: Switch failed<br/>error_code\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>t3, or conn once written\n    end\n  end\n  loop until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: Status and result\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/setup": {
   "kind": "instance.run_steps",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, R2-R4 (process.stop shape: 209, @swamp/ssh, full intent as inputs), DECISIONS instance.run_steps (commands as inputs, sudo -u intent account, terminal projection, no API reads). docs/reference/instance-setup.md.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 5, 14, 15, 22 (no script read, no checkout check, no report or assert step).\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: instance:setup\n  %% kind: instance.run_steps\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: instance:setup 42\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:setup 42`\n  %%> - syntax: `instance:setup <instance> [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the numeric Instance ID\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, record the task<br/>Instance 42 · task #5133\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, the Node of Instance 42\n  %%> - Instance 42 is an active development Instance on an `app-dev` Node\n  %%> - no other task holds Instance 42; the Gateway holds one lock per Instance and waits briefly for a busy one\n  %%> Records:\n  %%> - the Project's setup steps as they are now: names, commands, timeouts, and their digest\n  %%> - a later change to the list does not change this task; start a new one to use it\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5133 recorded with the full intent\n  %%> - nothing has happened on the Node yet\n  GW->>SW: Start run · 550 s<br/>operation-instance-setup-direct\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - `kind`: instance.run_steps\n  %%> - every step's name, command and timeout\n  %%> - the checkout path, the Instance account and the Node's Vite+ store (`VP_HOME`)\n  %%> - the seed: `ORBIT_SEED_PATH` and `ORBIT_SEED_COMMIT` of the `default` Instance on Node 12\n  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  %%> Budget:\n  %%> - 550 s for the whole list; the step timeouts add up to at most 540 s\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5133\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>N: Run the setup steps<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · in order\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209 with `@swamp/ssh`; nothing is copied to the Node\n  %%> - one `@swamp/ssh exec` per setup step: `sudo -u <account> timeout <s> env -C <checkout> bash -euc <command>`, the same runner `instance:create` uses\n  %%> - the checkout path comes from the Gateway's records; no step checks it first\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - runs each step's command in order with `bash -eu` from the repository root of the checkout, and stops at the first failure\n  %%> - with `ORBIT_INSTANCE_ID`, `ORBIT_INSTANCE_NAME`, `ORBIT_SEED_PATH` and `ORBIT_SEED_COMMIT` set\n  %%> Typical steps:\n  %%> - `composer install`\n  %%> - `vp install`\n  %%> - `php artisan migrate`\n  %%> Runs as:\n  %%> - the Instance account from the intent, with `sudo -u`; not root\n  %%> Limits:\n  %%> - `timeout` stops the step's whole process group at its own timeout, so no command keeps running after it\n  %%> Progress:\n  %%> - one item per setup step, in order; the first failure stops the run with that step's exit code\n  %%> Not kept:\n  %%> - command text and stdout; only the exit code and a redacted stderr tail\n  Note over SW: Check every step passed<br/>assert · end check\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - fails when any setup step failed; the Gateway reads the run's result from this step\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - each step is one forEach item, so the run needs one named step that holds the outcome\n  alt every step exits 0\n    N-->>SW: exit 0 for each step\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - each step's exit code; that is the end state, so no other check runs\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event and reads the run's outputs; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record result<br/>setup: done · task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Instance 42's setup is recorded as done, and an earlier failed step is cleared\n    %%> - task #5133 succeeds; an empty list also succeeds\n  else a step fails or times out\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or timeout\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a command exits with an error or runs past its timeout\n    %%> - exit 127 or 126: the command is missing or not executable\n    %%> Effect:\n    %%> - later steps do not run\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed_step, error_code\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - the failed step, its exit code and the redacted stderr tail\n    %%> - the Gateway maps the exit code to the outcome: 127 or 126 is `missing`, a timeout is `deadline`, others are `failed`\n    Note over GW: Keep Instance, mark failed<br/>task failed · rerun from start\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Instance 42 stays active; its setup is marked failed with the step name\n    %%> - task #5133 fails with `instance.setup_step_failed`, or `instance.setup_step_unavailable` for a missing command\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - what earlier steps did stays; there is no rollback and no teardown\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - fix the cause and run `instance:setup` again; it starts at the first step, so steps should converge\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5133\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/sleep": {
   "kind": "instance.sleep",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Container runtime (ADR 0250 step 8, ADR 0261, docs/reference/development-containers.md#sleep-and-wake, docs/reference/prepared-restore.md): freeze a development container, record the pages its processes had resident, move its memory to swap and leave its Route to wake it on the next visit. Defaults and nested-fleet Instances sleep under the same rule as clones (owner decision 2026-11-14). The hibernation sweep admits the same kind after an idle hour; the command admits it at once. Planned (ADR 0265, docs/reference/production-containers.md#sleep-and-wake): a production Instance's live release container sleeps by the same rule on its app-prod Node, and never takes the stop.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:sleep\n  %% kind: instance.sleep\n  %% ADR 0266: ordinary sleep and the idle sweep share one admitted Operation and one signed action, delivered only over the WebSocket.\n  %% Executor sleep: record and fsync non-max subtree swap limits, open them, freeze through Incus local API, scan every process in parallel (readable mappings, pagemap bits 63 OR 62, excluding bit 58 guard markers), atomically fsync the plan, reclaim until memory.current settles or 2 s, apply the 32 MiB anonymous policy, write the asleep marker. Recovery keeps an existing valid plan. Discard removes plan and limits after stop/fresh boot; destroy deletes the Rust state folder without an order (signed destroy: task 5891).\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · sweep\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>Instance container\n  Note over GW,N: Slice 4 proof: Rust sleep<br/>Incus freeze → scan → reclaim\n  %%> Executor proof path:\n  %%> - a durable executor.power_proof Operation signs sleep or discard; the observer only relays bytes\n  %%> - Rust records non-max swap limits and fsyncs; Incus API freeze; parallel whole-subtree capture includes every readable present-or-swapped page (bits 63 and 62), excluding bit 58 guard markers inside readable mappings\n  %%> - Rust publishes the marker before freeze, atomically fsyncs the plan, and gives reclaim 2 s to settle; the 32 MiB residual policy still applies\n  %%> - discard drops the Rust plan and limits after stop or fresh boot; destroy removes the Rust state folder as plain cleanup; no production flow switches in this slice\n  %%> - sleep and discard first wait up to 10 s for a stalled restore read of an earlier wake of the same target, else `instance.restore_readers_busy`\n  CLI->>GW: instance:sleep INSTANCE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:sleep INSTANCE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<instance-id> [--json]`\n  %%> Callers:\n  %%> - the command, at once\n  %%> - the idle sleep of the runtime hibernator, every 10 min, after it read no HTTP, wake or deployment activity for an hour and, for an Instance with a T3 server, no T3 activity and no running turn (docs/reference/development-containers.md#sleep-and-wake)\n  %%> - planned: the same sweep and command for a production Instance; they act on its live release container, which has no T3 server\n  opt the Instance has a T3 server\n    Note over GW: Last read of T3 threads<br/>Conn thread index\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - after the refusals and the replay of a repeated request, before the Instance lock; within 3 seconds\n    %%> - only while Conn and its index switch `ORBIT_CONN_THREAD_INDEX_POLL` are on; the sleep setting does not matter\n    %%> - a default has no T3 server: nothing to read\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - reads the container T3 server's threads once more, so the index keeps them while the Instance sleeps (docs/reference/conn.md#thread-index)\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - the index keeps its earlier rows; the sleep goes on\n  end\n  Note over GW: Admit the sleep\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - an active development container, a default and a nested-fleet Instance included; take the Instance lock\n  %%> - planned: a production Instance's live release container; hot and old are never admitted, and the sweep reports `not_live` for them; an open deploy or rollback keeps the Instance awake (`busy`)\n  %%> On failure:\n  %%> - no container, a non-development Instance, an active task or lifecycle work starts no run\n  %%> - planned: the reason `not_development` goes; a production Instance is admitted for its live only\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID\n  alt signed power enabled at admission\n    %% path: signed-sleep\n    GW->>N: Publish signed sleep<br/>executor-order · WebSocket\n    %%> - Check the loaded Caddy rule before any freeze and put up the marker before the app stops answering. Without a published Route there is no site to check.\n    %%> - The executor owns freeze, capture, swap and the final marker. If sleep fails after the freeze, it reports the incomplete sleep and keeps the state for the same engine to wake. It never stops a nested fleet.\n    %%> - The WebSocket is the only delivery; the Gateway publishes once, never relays the order over SSH, and starts no Swamp run.\n    alt a receipt arrives\n      Note over GW: Project sleep or failure<br/>one terminal Operation\n      %%> - A known refusal or failure does not record asleep. Repeated deliveries return the first result without freezing twice.\n      %%> - R1: a recorded `done` receipt is the sleep's outcome even when a later transport fails; the Operation succeeds and records asleep\n      %%> - R3: the freezing engine owns the freeze; a cancelled or failed later sleep does not hand it to the other engine\n      %%> - More than 32 MiB of anonymous RAM after reclaim takes the same stop rule as Python for a plain container. Report `sleep: stopped`, `prepared_restore: skipped`, reason `stopped` and the residual counters. The CLI says the next wake boots without restoring memory.\n    else no result by 6 s\n      %% path: journal\n      GW->>N: Read journal<br/>SSH · read-only · this order\n      %%> - covers a publish that threw, no `accepted` line and a lost result; none proves what the Node did, so the lock never goes on silence\n      %%> - the command prints the Node's clock and the journal lines for this Operation, action and target; it sends no order and cannot act\n      %%> - the executor records an order when it accepts it, before the target lock; an order that expired waiting ends `executor.order_expired` and never acts\n      %%> - a `seen` or `open` record without `done` reads again after 1 s; each read is its own job, so an unreachable Node delays no other Instance\n      alt a done record\n        Note over GW: Project it as the receipt<br/>delivery journal\n        %%> - R1: the record is the sleep's outcome, success or failure, completed by the same code as an on-time receipt, also after an interruption; the lock goes with it\n      else no record, order expired on the Node\n        %% path: not-accepted\n        Note over GW: Fail, free the lock<br/>executor.not_accepted\n        %%> - the Node never acted and never will; `executor.publish_failed` when the publish threw\n      else the Node cannot be read\n        %% path: result-unknown\n        Note over GW: Interrupted, keep lock<br/>executor.result_unknown\n        %%> - R2: the executor may have frozen the container, so the lock stays and cancel is refused; the Gateway reads the journal again every 10 s until a record or its absence settles it\n      end\n    end\n  else signed power disabled at admission\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-sleep\n  SW->>N: Sleep the container<br/>Ansible · tags prepared_sleep\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - one phase: first installs the power helper from this commit when it differs and probes it, so a failed install changes nothing a visitor sees\n  %%> - checks that Caddy's loaded site of the Instance has the asleep rule, else `instance.sleep_route_unready`; an Instance without a published Route has no site and nothing to check; the intent's `route` says which\n  %%> - writes the asleep marker, so each request from now on waits while the Instance wakes\n  %%> - runs `orbit-instance-power prepare` as the unit `orbit-instance-power-<id>`, the Instance's lock; a busy lock is retried 0.25 s apart, 40 times\n  %%> - prepare reads the state from Incus first; running: removes the earlier plan and `sleep.json`, writes `max` to `memory.swap.max` of every cgroup of the subtree that holds 0 and records them in `limits.json` before the pause; `incus pause`\n  %%> - prepare, frozen: lists `cgroup.procs` of the whole subtree, every process alike, and records each one's identity and the resident pages of its readable mappings; publishes the plan atomically; one `memory.reclaim`\n  %%> - then runs `orbit-instance-power residual` until anonymous and file memory are 0, 20 calls 0.1 s apart, through the same call and busy rule as `prepare`; each call reads `memory.stat` once and stores it in `sleep.json`; the last one is what stays in RAM\n  %%> - a frozen container stays as it is, keeps its plan for the next wake and reports the earlier sleep's result from `sleep.json`; a stopped one stays stopped, its old plan is discarded, `not_applicable`\n  %%> - a busy lock is only systemd's `UnitExists` refusal; a helper crash fails the phase with `instance.power_helper_failed`, never retried\n  %%> - a nested-fleet Instance gets no plan: `skipped`, `placement_unproven`\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - one phase: each Ansible start costs about 4 s\n  %%> - the marker comes before the freeze: a request during the freeze goes to the activation check, not to a frozen app\n  %%> - the plan is taken frozen, so it is exact; the reclaim follows it, so nothing stays in RAM by design\n  %%> - with only the outer cgroup open, 1,531 MiB of a nested default stayed in RAM: a cgroup with 0 keeps its pages\n  %%> - a cgroup swaps only as far as its ancestors allow, and Incus writes 0 unless `limits.memory.swap` is a byte size: a nested-fleet container has it equal to its memory, so its own cgroup never caps the swap of its fleet's Instances\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - no HTTP request, no command in the container, no app file read, no route or process-name choice\n  %%> On failure:\n  %%> - a capture that is unsupported, too large (docs/reference/prepared-restore.md#bounds) or too slow leaves no plan; the sleep goes on, `skipped` with its reason\n  %%> - a failed swap write or pause closes each limit it opened and removes the marker, `instance.container_freeze_failed`; the container serves as before\n  opt more than 32 MiB anonymous stays in RAM, same phase\n    alt a nested-fleet Instance\n      Note over N: Resume, never stop<br/>drop plan, restore, close swap\n      %%> Why:\n      %%> - a stop loses the Club key in /run and the tunnel `wg-nested`; only a deployment or a start gives them back\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the container runs again, swap closes on each cgroup the freeze opened, the marker goes; the run fails `instance.container_freeze_failed`\n      %%> - the Instance serves as before; free swap and run the command again\n    else a production live, planned\n      Note over N: Resume, never stop<br/>drop plan, restore, close swap\n      %%> Why:\n      %%> - a stopped live would cold-boot its Processes and timers at the next visit (ADR 0265)\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - live runs again, swap closes on each cgroup the freeze opened, the marker goes; the run fails `instance.container_freeze_failed`\n      %%> - the sweep keeps live awake for one idle window with `swap_full`\n    else any other Instance\n      Note over N: Stop the container<br/>drop plan, restore, incus stop\n      %%> Why:\n      %%> - swap is full; a frozen container would hold RAM, which a sleep exists to free\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - `sleep: stopped`; `discard --plan-only` dropped the plan before the resume, so no prefetch ran while swap is full; `skipped`, `stopped`; the next wake boots it behind the same marker\n      %%> On failure:\n      %%> - the resumed container runs: swap closes again, the marker goes, `instance.container_freeze_failed`\n    end\n  end\n  alt the run passes\n    %% path: main\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result\n    Note over GW: Record asleep\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the runtime's `power_state` is `stopped` for a frozen and a stopped container; the result says `sleep: frozen` or `sleep: stopped`, and `swap_subtree`, the number of inner cgroups the freeze opened\n    %%> - `prepared_restore`: `ready` with the plan's processes, bytes and `prepare_seconds`, or `unsupported`, `skipped` or `not_applicable` with a reason; `residual_anon_bytes` and `residual_file_bytes` from the last read\n    %%> - `t3_turn` is `idle` when the idle sleep read T3 first, `no_server` for an Instance without a T3 server, whoever started the sleep, and `not_checked` for the command\n    %%> - the Conn layer shows its T3 server asleep; the hourly renewal skips it\n    %%> - planned, a production live: Caddy holds each request that meets the marker while the wake runs (wake.mmd); live's swap opened for the sleep, and a running live never swaps\n  else a step fails\n    %% path: failed\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - `power_state` stays as it was. A marker left on a running container is harmless: the next request wakes the Instance, which removes it\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - the same command again\n  end\n  end\n  loop until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: Status and result\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/transfer": {
   "kind": "instance.transfer",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Gateway holds each Route-serving Node from admission to the terminal state; a second Route write on it waits (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task + full intent as inputs, R1-R4, single-swamp-node; DECISIONS scope (SQLite only when chosen, Schedules move, cert from destination CSR in-run), 1 (signing is the one step→Gateway call), DNS hold retired (PR #2443); tightened by flow-tighten 2026-11-02 (rules 3, 6, 8, 13); .env and Processes as in instance.create. docs/reference/instance-transfer.md.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 5, 17, 22 (destination preflight from recorded state; cfgmgmt file/systemd, Route publish as in route:create).\n  %% Decided: Nick 2026-11-02, .env.testing only for an owned MySQL/PostgreSQL database, written by the same file step.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% Interim: the run signs inside the Route child, and SQLite copies stay refused. The Swamp steps below are the real workflow steps.\n  %% command: instance:transfer\n  %% kind: instance.transfer\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane S icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane D icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant S as Node 12<br/>source · over ssh\n  participant D as Node 15<br/>destination · ssh\n  CLI->>GW: instance:transfer 42 15\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:transfer 42 15`\n  %%> - syntax: `<instance> <node> [--name=NAME] [--sqlite-source-path=PATH] [--force] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - moves development Instance 42 from Node 12 to Node 15; the Instance id and Project stay\n  %%> Confirmation:\n  %%> - without `--force`, the CLI asks for consent that names the source, destination and downtime; the default is No\n  %%> - without a terminal, or with `--json`, `--force` is required\n  %%> Options:\n  %%> - `--name`: another Instance name on Node 15\n  %%> - `--sqlite-source-path`: currently refused with `operation.kind_unavailable`; no database file moves\n  Note over CLI: Confirm the downtime\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - asks for default-No consent naming the source, destination and old-placement deletion\n  opt new transfer of a host checkout\n    GW->>S: Read worktrees<br/>Git · admission only\n    %%> On refusal:\n    %%> - target read failures and confirmed dependents refuse before any transfer record or Process change\n    %%> - unreadable candidates warn; an admitted retry never checks again\n    %%> Limit:\n    %%> - worktrees created after admission, during the queue wait or run, are not caught\n  end\n  opt --name\n  Note over GW: Set destination name\n    %%> What it changes:\n    %%> - uses the supplied name for the destination path and generated domain instead of keeping the Instance name\n  end\n  opt --sqlite-source-path\n    GW--xCLI: SQLite copy unavailable<br/>operation.kind_unavailable\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - refuses before reservation or any run; the steps below do not run\n  end\n  Note over GW: Preflight, render .env<br/>Instance 42 · task #5133\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has access grants to Node 12 and Node 15\n  %%> - both Nodes have the `app-dev` role and belong to active Clusters\n  %%> - Project 7 may be placed on Node 15, and the destination path and planned ports are free, from Node 15's recorded Instances and Processes\n  %%> - Instance 42 is an active development Instance with one active Route, and no removal runs\n  %%> Plans:\n  %%> - the Processes from Project 7's definitions for `development`, with Node 15's ports\n  %%> - the Route on Node 15, and the Schedules' new target\n  %%> Renders .env:\n  %%> - from the stored environment of Instance 42, with Node 15 values such as `APP_URL`\n  %%> - `.env.testing` only when Instance 42 owns a database on a MySQL or PostgreSQL server: `.env` with `APP_ENV=testing` and the test database credentials\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - Instance 42 marked transferring; task #5133 recorded with the full intent\n  GW->>SW: Pause Schedule triggers<br/>workflow trigger removal\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - removes the native Swamp schedule trigger of each Schedule of Instance 42\n  %%> - there is no Node work here; Schedules install nothing on Nodes\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - no fire runs against Node 12 during the move\n  GW->>SW: Push .env to the vault<br/>vault: env-5133\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - `.env` can hold secrets, which travel only through the vault\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the run reads it with `vault.get`; no step asks the Gateway for it\n  Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - holds each Node that serves this Route's site (Caddy and PHP) from admission to the terminal state\n  %%> - a second Route write on one of those Nodes waits (task status `waiting`) and starts when the Node is free, oldest first\n  %%> Not held:\n  %%> - the DNS Node: its records are keyed, and the apply phase refuses a file that changed since the read\n  %%> Limit:\n  %%> - two Route writes on one Node run one after the other, so each waits for the other's whole run\n  %%> - a newer write waits behind an older waiting one that shares a Node, and a waiting write can be cancelled\n  %%> - a timed-out write keeps its Nodes until Swamp confirms that its run stopped, or for 15 minutes\n  %%> - task provision and cancel, instance:register and the Cluster Router writes hold the Nodes for their Route-publish step only, through the `route-hold` call\n  %%> - a task that waits starts from the Node's live files; a site block that matches none of its captured states still stops it with `owned_revision_conflict`\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-transfer\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one run of `operation-instance-transfer` on Swamp serve (Node 209)\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - both Nodes' addresses and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  %%> - paths, Process plan, Route plan, SQLite path when chosen\n  %%> - secrets only as vault key names\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5133\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>S: Stop source Processes<br/>process child · forEach\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - stops the Processes of Instance 42 on Node 12; downtime starts here\n  %%> Keeps:\n  %%> - the Process units, for a restore if the move fails\n  SW->>S: Archive the checkout<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · tar\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - reads the Git directories, then packs the Git history, branch, origin, index, unstaged edits and untracked files\n  %%> - refuses a shared common repository or alternates (`instance.transfer_source_shared`, `instance.transfer_source_invalid`)\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - `.env`; the Gateway renders a new one for Node 15\n  SW->>S: Copy the archive to 209<br/>@swamp/ssh copy · from\n  SW->>D: Copy it to the destination<br/>@swamp/ssh copy · to\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - through private scratch space on 209; archive bytes never enter results\n  SW->>D: Unpack the checkout<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · tar\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - extracts into the destination path, refusing an existing path (`instance.transfer_destination_exists`)\n  SW->>D: Make it standalone<br/>git config core.bare\n  SW->>D: Drop the worktree config<br/>git config --unset-all\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - a linked worktree becomes an independent checkout on Node 15\n  SW->>D: Create the orbit-env group<br/>cfgmgmt/group apply\n  SW->>D: Write .env<br/>cfgmgmt/file · 0640 orbit-env\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - writes the `.env` the Gateway rendered from Instance 42's stored configuration for Node 15, from `vault.get`, as root: owned by the Node user with group `orbit-env`, mode 0640\n  %%> Requires:\n  %%> - stored configuration; admission refuses an Instance without it (`env.configuration_missing`)\n  SW->>D: Run Project setup steps<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · in order\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - runs the Project's setup steps as in `instance:create`, so dependencies match Node 15; the first failure restores the source\n  SW->>D: Prepare Route workloads<br/>route workload child\n  SW->>D: Create, start Processes<br/>process child · forEach\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - creates the Instance's Processes on Node 15 and starts them; stopped ones stay stopped\n  SW->>D: Publish the Route<br/>route-mutation-forward\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - signs the CSR through the Gateway primitive, installs the certificate and site on Node 15, and reloads Caddy\n  %%> Cutover:\n  %%> - a ready result makes Node 15 authoritative\n  SW->>D: Check the destination<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · curl\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - one `curl` with the Route's domain as SNI: the site answers; a Project without a Route skips it\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the end check before the source is retired; a failure keeps the source for repair\n  alt destination published\n    SW->>S: Remove source Processes<br/>process child · forEach\n    SW->>S: Retire the source<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · rm -rf\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - deletes the checkout and prunes a linked worktree's entry; the common repository stays\n    %%> If it fails:\n    %%> - Node 15 stays authoritative; the Gateway keeps the placement and marks the task failed\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader reads the run's steps and the Route child's ready result\n    Note over GW: Record Node 15<br/>Instance, Processes, Route\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Instance 42, its Processes and its Routes are recorded on Node 15, in one change\n  else a step fails before cutover\n    SW->>S: Restore the Route<br/>route-mutation-restore\n    SW->>S: Restore the source<br/>process child · forEach\n    SW->>D: Remove new Processes<br/>process child · forEach\n    SW->>D: Discard the destination<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · rm -rf\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - removes the unpacked checkout, and only after the restore succeeded\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    Note over GW: Keep Node 12, mark failed<br/>rerun is safe\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Instance 42 stays on Node 12; a run that only restored also fails (`instance.transfer_failed`)\n  end\n  SW->>S: Remove the source archive<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · rm\n  SW->>D: Remove the copied archive<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · rm\n  Note over SW: Remove the 209 archive<br/>rm · always\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5133\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/unregister": {
   "kind": "instance.unregister",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: Nick 2026-11-16, the inverse of instance:register; Orbit forgets a host checkout Instance and the folder stays. The destroy chart's steps that run without a source effect; ADR 0244 task, R1-R4. docs/reference/instance-removal.md#unregister-a-host-checkout.\n  %% Decided: Nick 2026-11-16, an Instance that owns a server database refuses; there is no keep-database flag, so no database is left without an owner.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:unregister\n  %% kind: instance.unregister\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: instance:unregister 42\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:unregister 42`\n  %%> - syntax: `<instance> [--yes] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Instance's id, as `instance:list` shows it\n  Note over CLI: Confirm unregister\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - asks for default-No consent, naming the Instance and the effect: Orbit forgets it; the checkout folder and its files stay\n  %%> - `--yes` supplies that consent; noninteractive and JSON calls require it\n  Note over GW: Validate, capture the set<br/>Instance 42 · task TASK\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the Node that runs Instance 42\n  %%> - Instance 42 is an active host checkout development Instance\n  %%> - no other lifecycle Operation, transfer or migration holds it\n  %%> Captures:\n  %%> - what Instance 42 owns: Processes, Schedules, Route, database records\n  %%> - the Route publication to withdraw, as destroy captures it\n  %%> Not sent to the run:\n  %%> - the source and SQLite paths stay in the task's recorded input only; no teardown step or server database is captured: nothing on disk goes\n  alt a check fails\n    %% path: refused\n    GW--xCLI: Refused<br/>instance.unregister_*\n    %%> Refuses:\n    %%> - a container Instance: `instance.unregister_container`; use instance:destroy\n    %%> - an Instance that owns a server database: `instance.unregister_owned_database`\n    %%> - a production Instance: `operation.kind_unavailable`\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - before any change: no Schedule trigger is removed and no task is recorded\n  else the Instance can go\n    %% path: main\n    GW->>SW: Remove Schedule triggers<br/>workflow trigger removal\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - removes the native Swamp schedule trigger of each Schedule of Instance 42, as destroy does\n    %%> Why first:\n    %%> - no new fire starts against an Instance that Orbit is forgetting\n    Note over GW: Record removal intent<br/>mark removing\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - records the task and a removal marked as an unregister, from fresh admission facts\n    %%> - marks Instance 42 removing and its Route retiring\n    %%> - holds each Node that serves the Route, as destroy does\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-unregister\n    %%> Inputs:\n    %%> - the Node's address and SSH host keys, the Route withdrawal, the Processes and the hibernation paths\n    %%> - no source path, teardown step, database, git credential or token\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task TASK\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n    opt Instance has a Route\n      %% path: route\n      SW->>N: Withdraw the Route<br/>route-withdraw · forward\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - the destroy withdrawal: removes the Caddy site, PHP pool, certificate and private DNS records of Instance 42\n      %%> If it does not hold:\n      %%> - every later step is skipped and the task fails with `route.withdrawal_failed`\n      SW->>GW: Release the Route Nodes<br/>route-release · run ID\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - only after the withdrawal held, as destroy does; a retry then skips the withdrawal\n    end\n    SW->>N: Stop, remove Processes<br/>operation-process-destroy\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - stops each Process of Instance 42 and removes its unit, through the child that `process:destroy` uses\n    SW->>N: Remove hibernation files<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file absent\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the awake and cold markers and the Caddy access log outlive the Instance otherwise\n    SW->>N: Check units gone<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · test ! -e\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - checks that each removed unit file and hibernation file is absent\n    %%> Not checked:\n    %%> - the checkout: it stays, with its Git data and `.env`\n    alt all steps pass\n      %% path: unregistered\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n      %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n      Note over GW: Delete Instance, records<br/>task succeeded\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - deletes Instance 42 with its Route, Processes, Schedules, auto-deploy, environment and database records, as destroy does\n      %%> - the checkout folder and every file in it stay on the Node\n    else a step fails\n      %% path: failed\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      %%> The Gateway reads:\n      %%> - which step failed, mapped to `error_code`\n      Note over GW: Keep Instance, mark failed<br/>rerun the same unregister\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - Instance 42 and its records stay, marked removal failed; no rollback\n      %%> - run `instance:unregister 42` again; it converges and skips a held withdrawal\n    end\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/wake": {
   "kind": "instance.wake",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Container runtime (ADR 0250 step 8, ADR 0261, ADR 0266 slice 6, docs/reference/development-containers.md#sleep-and-wake, docs/reference/prepared-restore.md): read a sleeping development container's recorded pages back while it is still frozen, then resume it, or start one that was stopped. Defaults and nested-fleet Instances wake under the same rule as clones (owner decision 2026-11-14); a stopped nested-fleet Instance is never booted here. A request to its Route admits the same kind through the runtime activation endpoint of docs/reference/app-dev-runtime-hibernation.md#wake. Planned (ADR 0265, docs/reference/production-containers.md#sleep-and-wake): a production Instance's live release container wakes by the same rule, and its visitor is held as a development visitor is.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:wake\n  %% kind: instance.wake\n  %% ADR 0266: signed power applies to ordinary wakes; the WebSocket is its only delivery, and only a stopped container continues in Swamp.\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CL icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CL as Caller<br/>orbit · Caddy\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>Instance container\n  Note over GW,N: Signed Rust wake<br/>pool restore → Incus unfreeze\n  %%> Executor proof path:\n  %%> - a durable executor.power_proof Operation signs `wake`; the observer only relays bytes; the reply is `accepted`, then one result line\n  %%> - before any memory syscall: boot id, cgroup inode and Incus incarnation against the plan, then each process's start time through a pidfd; any mismatch drops the plan and the wake goes on without a restore (`partial`, `instance.restore_plan_stale`)\n  %%> - restore through the Node's one restore pool (`/etc/orbit/executor/restore-threads`, 32, floor 16), one target's items finished before the next starts: `process_madvise(MADV_WILLNEED)` then `process_vm_readv` of each recorded page that is not resident, within 2 s; at the bound the wake skips unstarted items and waits 250 ms for running ones, then reports `partial`, `instance.restore_deadline`; a read still stuck in its syscall then is stalled: `partial`, `instance.restore_stalled`, and the wake goes on, because a late read only copies into the executor's buffer; a later sleep or discard of the target waits up to 10 s for that reader, else `instance.restore_readers_busy`; a short read skips its failed page and continues with the rest of the item, counting unreadable pages; an unreadable pagemap or a failed page reports `partial`, `instance.restore_read_failed`; a plan that breaks a bound is `instance.restore_plan_invalid`\n  %%> - unfreeze through the Incus local API with its 5 s bounds, then wait up to 5 s for the kernel thaw; a kernel freeze that stays, or one Incus lost before the wake, fails `instance.container_frozen_unmanaged` with no cgroup write\n  %%> - close exactly the recorded swap limits, with no disk sync; one readiness request to the web port until an answer below 500, within 1.5 s, else `instance.web_unready` and the marker stays; then remove the asleep marker\n  %%> - a stopped container fails `instance.container_stopped` and drops its plan: a boot is the run's work; a running one gets only the steps after the unfreeze (`done`), so crash recovery and a repeat are safe\n  CL->>GW: instance:wake INSTANCE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:wake INSTANCE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<instance-id> [--json]`\n  %%> Callers:\n  %%> - the command\n  %%> - Caddy on the Instance's Node, when a request finds the asleep marker: `GET runtime-activations/app-instance/ID`\n  %%> - a durable Conn launch that opens the T3 server; planned: the task flow before a turn on an attached Instance\n  %%> - planned: a due native cron Schedule of a production Instance whose live sleeps; the fire waits for the wake, and this wake does not count as activity\n  %%> - not needed by a copy: a clone or task copy of an asleep default copies its seed snapshot\n  %%> - a deployment of a sleeping Instance resumes or starts it in its own run, and removes the marker there\n  %%> - planned: a production deploy does not wake live; its switch removes the marker (container/deploy-production.mmd)\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the caller is the Instance's Node or has node access to it\n  Note over GW: Start or join the wake<br/>one per Instance\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - an active development container, a default and a nested-fleet Instance included\n  %%> - planned: a production Instance's live release container; the wake is admitted beside an open deploy or rollback, which changes live only after its switch; the switch and the wake take the Instance's power lock\n  %%> - a nested-fleet Instance takes the wake only when its last sleep froze it and no wake since found it stopped; otherwise a nested-fleet clone takes its start (instance/start.mmd), and a nested-fleet default refuses: only its deployment starts it\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - an open wake of the Instance is joined: a second caller gets the same Operation; a visit that joins is held on it too\n  %%> - Caddy's visit is held while the wake runs, up to 30 s (`ORBIT_VISIT_WAKE_WAIT_SECONDS`): then `307` to the same URI, which the browser repeats to the awake app, or a plain `503` text with no page, script or reload (Nick 2026-11-19; supersedes the 2026-11-15 rule \"wake spinner shows at once; never hold the request\")\n  %%> - a join to a Conn launch's wake records shared use while locking the wake Operation. If launch cleanup already cancelled the queued wake, a new request admits a new wake\n  %%> - after a failed wake, the next visit admits a new wake; only an interrupted wake under its fence (R2) keeps answering its failure\n  %%> On failure:\n  %%> - no container, a non-development Instance, a nested-fleet Instance that cannot take the wake or its start, or other lifecycle work admits no wake\n  alt signed wake admitted\n    %% path: executor\n    GW->>N: Sign and publish wake<br/>executor-order · WebSocket\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - record or join instance.wake under the Instance lock before signing; requests and the background pass share one publication receipt for the same Operation\n    %%> - CLI, API and Conn queue the signed wake and follow the Operation; their wait is not limited by Caddy's request budget\n    %%> - keep the admitted wake queued with zero attempts until the publisher atomically claims it; Conn records cancellation of an unshared queued executor wake in the launch cancel transaction, before a publisher can claim it\n    %%> - sign and publish outside the database transaction under the non-blocking per-Operation publication lock, then record the publication receipt\n    %%> - the background pass takes committed completion handoffs, including old queued handoffs whose jobs did not finish; it projects recorded results before publishing any missing order for the same Operation\n    %%> - record Operation notices locally; delivering them never holds the visitor request\n    %%> - the observer passes the order unread to the executor; the path needs no SSH, Python or marker/times command\n    %%> - the WebSocket is the only delivery; the Gateway publishes once and never relays the order over SSH\n    opt Caddy, for a visit\n      GW->>GW: Hold the visit<br/>ExecutorResultWait · 30 s\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - check the listener's subsecond receipt while time remains, because another waiter may have consumed its Valkey copy\n      %%> - keep the observer's reply order; a bare replay refusal is not a result, and a late result cannot authorize the original request\n      alt done or partial\n        GW-->>CL: 307 to the same URI<br/>same method and body\n        %%> Result:\n        %%> - Caddy returns the 307 unchanged; the browser repeats the request and the awake app answers it. A 200 would end as an empty page on production's published routes, whose activation and app sit in separate `handle` blocks\n        %%> - the Operation succeeds and records the runtime running\n      else failed, refused or no result in 30 s\n        %% path: executor-failed\n        GW-->>CL: 503 plain text<br/>wake failed or still waking\n        %%> Result:\n        %%> - a failed or refused receipt, or an ended Operation, answers at once with its code; a reload admits a new wake\n      end\n    end\n    alt a receipt arrives\n      Note over GW: Project the receipt<br/>end the Operation\n      %%> - R1: the matching receipt decides; done or partial succeeds, refused or failed fails with the receipt's code; a transport failure never overrides a recorded receipt\n      %%> - R4: a visit answers a failed wake with a plain `503` and its code; under an interrupted wake's held lock every visit gets the same failure\n      %%> - the worker sends the Operation's notices without waiting for the sweep\n      %%> - the job after 6 s finishes a known result if the immediate job did not run\n      %%> - a background worker runs the lifecycle hook after success without holding a database writer, and records it once per Operation\n      opt the receipt is failed with instance.container_stopped\n        GW->>SW: Continue Operation<br/>operation-instance-wake\n        SW->>GW: Sign fresh order<br/>bound Operation\n        SW->>N: Relay fresh order<br/>orbit-executor relay · stdin\n        %%> - the run's phase on the Node is the relay's local caller; the journal returns the first result or acts once. A confirmed stopped container takes its existing boot and readiness phase; an unmanaged stopped nested default never boots here.\n        %%> - After that boot, the boot's own readiness result decides, never the earlier `container_stopped` line (R1)\n        %%> - R3: the engine that froze the container wakes it; a later cancelled or failed sleep and the current settings change nothing\n        SW-->>GW: Return result<br/>terminal projection\n      end\n    else no result by 6 s\n      %% path: journal\n      GW->>N: Read journal<br/>SSH · read-only · this order\n      %%> - covers a publish that threw, no `accepted` line and a lost result; none proves what the Node did, so the lock never goes on silence\n      %%> - the command prints the Node's clock and the journal lines for this Operation, action and target; it sends no order and cannot act\n      %%> - the executor records an order when it accepts it, before the target lock; an order that expired waiting ends `executor.order_expired` and never acts\n      %%> - a `seen` or `open` record without `done` reads again after 1 s; each read is its own job, so an unreachable Node delays no other Instance\n      alt a done record\n        Note over GW: Project it as the receipt<br/>delivery journal\n        %%> - R1: the record is the wake's outcome, success or failure, completed by the same code as an on-time receipt, also after an interruption (a stopped container still goes to the boot); the lock goes with it\n      else no record, order expired on the Node\n        %% path: not-accepted\n        Note over GW: Fail, free the lock<br/>executor.not_accepted\n        %%> - the Node never acted and never will; `executor.publish_failed` when the publish threw\n        %%> - a held visit sees the failed Operation and gets the plain `503`; a reload admits a new wake\n      else the Node cannot be read\n        %% path: result-unknown\n        Note over GW: Interrupted, keep lock<br/>executor.result_unknown\n        %%> - R2: the executor may have acted, so the lock stays and cancel is refused; the Gateway reads the journal again every 10 s until a record or its absence settles it\n        %%> - no Swamp run starts\n      end\n    end\n  else Python wake admitted\n  opt Caddy, for a development visit\n    Note over GW: Hold the visit<br/>up to 30 s\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Gateway admits or joins the wake, runs the Node's resume below within the visit, and waits for the Operation to end; `307` to the same URI, otherwise a plain `503`\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - a container that Python froze wakes in 14–19 s until its next executor sleep; 30 s covers it without a second state\n  end\n  opt the last sleep froze it, not a nested fleet\n    %% path: resume\n    GW->>N: Write the wake request<br/>agent · hibernation.marker\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the Operation is recorded without its run; the Node calls run after its commit\n    %%> - a command caller and Caddy's held visit both wait for these calls\n    %%> - the frozen agent's `hibernation.marker` writes `/dev/shm/orbit/hibernation/app-instance-<id>-op-<opid>.wake`; only root writes in that folder; the name carries the Operation, so no earlier wake's outcome can answer this one\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the Node has the worker template `/etc/systemd/system/orbit-instance-wake@.service`, which only the role's convergence installs; the first read asks for it, and a Node without it skips this path for 10 minutes\n    %%> - not for a nested-fleet Instance: its prepared fast path waits for a nested-fleet proof\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - measured on a frozen clone: the run's start-up and playbook took 8-13 s of the 9-19 s a visitor waited\n    %%> - the agent stays frozen (ADR 0253): two of its existing commands carry the request and the answer\n    Note over N: Restore, then resume<br/>orbit-instance-wake@ID-OP\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - a path unit's dispatcher renames the request to `.taken` and starts the worker for this Instance and Operation; a slow worker never holds up the next request\n    %%> - the worker acts only on a request under 1 s old, with the asleep marker and a valid target descriptor; it runs `orbit-instance-power restore` as the unit `orbit-instance-power-<id>`, the Instance's lock\n    %%> - restore, still frozen: checks boot id, incarnation, cgroup and every process's identity before any memory call, then `process_madvise` and `process_vm_readv` over the recorded pages in parallel, within 2 s; then one `incus resume`, and swap closes on every cgroup of `limits.json`\n    %%> - then one request from the Node to the web port for an answer below 500, removes the marker, writes one `.ready-complete`, `-partial`, `-skipped` or `-timed-out` and then `.done` in `/var/lib/orbit/hibernation/outcomes`\n    %%> - last, the library cache and CA bundle checks the run also makes\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - frozen processes cannot change their mappings, so the plan stays exact while it is read\n    %%> - the pages come back in large parallel reads, not one fault at a time after the resume\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - a changed or missing plan: no memory call; an ordinary resume, `.ready-skipped`\n    %%> - a declined request, a busy lock, a failed resume or check: `.failed`, the marker stays\n    GW->>N: Read the outcome<br/>agent · hibernation.times\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - reads the request, `.taken`, the outcome files and the marker every 25 ms, for at most 6 s\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - `.done` with one `.ready-*`: the Operation succeeds here with `wake: resumed`, `trigger_seconds`, `confirm_seconds`, `restore_mode`, `ready_at` from the ready file's time (`ready_at_resolution_seconds` 1) and `prepared_restore.status`; `power_state` is `running`; Conn's renewal below runs as after a run\n    %%> - after the projection, the Gateway removes this Operation's request and outcome files off the visitor's path\n    %%> - Caddy's held visit gets 200 and the app's own page\n    %%> - an API caller gets 202 with the ended Operation; a command caller who joins waits up to the bound; a visit that joins is held until the wake ends\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - `.failed`, an agent call that failed or the bound: the Gateway removes the request, and the same Operation starts the run below; the run takes the same lock, so it waits for a worker still at work and goes on from the state the Node reached\n    %%> - a Gateway worker that dies here: a delayed start runs the same Operation's run 15 s later\n    %%> - a cancel during the resume keeps the Operation cancelled, and a late outcome never overwrites it\n  end\n  opt Caddy, for a production visit, planned\n    GW-->>CL: Admitted, at once<br/>Caddy holds · bound 10 s\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the Gateway admits or joins the wake and answers at once, as in development; no Gateway worker waits for the wake\n    %%> - Caddy on the Node holds the visitor's request: it tries the live upstream again until the asleep marker is gone, within 10 s, and then proxies the request to live\n    %%> - a person, an API client or a webhook sees only a slower first answer; targets about 1.5 s with the prepared restore, 2.4 s without\n    %%> - the wake reads the live link after the power lock and acts only on that container; after a switch it finds no marker, `wake: already_running`\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - a page instead of the app's answer breaks an API client and a webhook (ADR 0265)\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - after 10 s, Caddy answers `503` with `Retry-After: 5` and a plain page that names the Instance; the wake goes on\n    %%> - a failure is stored as in development; while it is stored, the Gateway says so and Caddy answers `503` with `Retry-After: 30` and the same page at once\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - the first request 30 s or more after the failure starts one new wake by itself: at most one retry each 30 s\n    %%> - `instance:wake` retries at once\n    %%> - when the fast wake fell back to the run below, the hold goes on until the run ends or the bound\n  end\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-wake\n  GW-->>CL: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID\n  SW->>N: Wake the container<br/>Ansible · tags start\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - Frozen: `orbit-instance-power restore` as the Instance's lock unit, a busy lock (systemd's `UnitExists` refusal) retried 0.25 s apart, 40 times, else `instance.power_helper_busy`; a helper crash fails with `instance.power_helper_failed`: with a valid plan it reads the recorded pages back within 2 s while still frozen; then one `incus resume`, and `memory.swap.max` back to 0 on each inner cgroup of `limits.json` that still exists, and on the container's own cgroup back to its `limits.memory.swap` bytes, or 0 without a size (Incus gives 0 without a size; a nested-fleet container keeps its swap) (a short-lived unit's cgroup may be gone; that never fails the wake); FrankenPHP gets 10 tries 1 s apart from the Node to its address; nothing boots and nothing converges, `wake: resumed`\n  %%> - a missing, stale or invalid plan makes no memory call; the resume is ordinary\n  %%> - a run that follows the fast path finds the state the Node reached: a container that already runs is not resumed again, and the run reads this Operation's outcome files for their facts\n  %%> - a nested-fleet Instance resumes with its inner fleet, `wg-nested` and the Club key in /run: a freeze keeps them, so the wake starts and checks no fleet\n  %%> - Frozen and FrankenPHP silent: `incus stop --force`, then the boot below, `wake: restarted_after_resume`; a nested-fleet Instance is never stopped: the wake fails `instance.web_unready`\n  %%> - Stopped (after a host reboot or a sleep that fell back to a stop): the helper discards the plan, then the boot below, `wake: started`; a nested-fleet Instance is never booted here: see the branch below\n  %%> - Running (after a reboot with `boot.autostart`): no start and no resume, `wake: already_running`\n  %%> - a Frozen container without the marker is resumed; the result says `marker_found: false`\n  %%> - before the boot, the presented machine name: a stopped container gets `/etc/hostname`, `/etc/machine-info` and the `127.0.1.1` line of `/etc/hosts` through the file API before the boot, so the boot gives the kernel and T3 the name; a running or frozen container gets nothing, and nothing restarts\n  %%> - boot: `incus start`; nftables, T3, FrankenPHP and the enabled Process units start with the container\n  %%> - an older container's app account moves to the stock `www-data` (uid and gid 33) as soon as it runs, before any other command in it, by root's one swap file `/usr/local/libexec/orbit-app-user`; a moved container only reads, and a nested-fleet container keeps `orbit` and `work` for its inner fleet, with `www-data` in `work`\n  %%> - waits until each desired-running unit is active, then until FrankenPHP answers, else `instance.web_unready`\n  %%> - a 5xx answer gets up to 5 s to clear; an app that answers 5xx by itself is then served as it is\n  %%> - removes the asleep marker, so Caddy sends the next request to the app\n  %%> - then rebuilds the container's library cache with `ldconfig -X` when it lacks a soname of the runtime folder (a converge cannot exec in a frozen container); a failed check fails nothing\n  %%> - then runs `update-ca-certificates` when the CA bundle lacks the container's Orbit root (the converge writes the root's file, but a frozen container refuses the exec); a container without the file skips it\n  %%> - then waits up to 30 s for T3's environment endpoint in an Instance with a T3 server, else `instance.t3_unready`; a default has none and ends with the app\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - one playbook: a second Ansible start costs about 4 s on the visitor's path\n  %%> - the visitor waits for the app, not for T3; T3 gates only the Operation's end\n  %%> - unit enablement holds each Process's desired state, so a stopped Process stays stopped\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - the wake's state handling runs only in a start whose intent names `marker`; a deployment's start and a nested-fleet clone's start name `asleep_marker` instead, which only removes the marker once the `start` part passed, with no web probe\n  alt the run passes\n    %% path: main\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result\n    Note over GW: Record awake\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the runtime's `power_state` is `running`; the Conn layer shows the T3 server awake\n    %%> - the result names the path, `marker_found`, and a resume's `resume_seconds` and `web_probe_seconds`\n    %%> - `restore_mode` (`prepared` or `ordinary`), `prepared_restore` with its status and reason, and `ready_at`, when the marker went (`ready_at_resolution_seconds` 0.001); Operation list and show return them\n    %%> - a held visit gets 200 and the app answers its original path\n    opt the Gateway session ends within 7 days or has ended\n      Note over GW: Admit the renewal<br/>conn.environment_renew\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - after the wake's transaction; it never fails the wake\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - see conn/environment-renew.mmd; the run needs no old session, so a session that ended during the sleep renews too\n    end\n    opt a held session is due, or the server owes a revoke or cleanup\n      Note over GW: Renew held sessions<br/>owed revoke next\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - after the wake's transaction, while the Gateway session has not ended; it never fails the wake\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - see conn/session-hold.mmd: one renewal per held session that ends within seven days\n      %%> - and the server's next owed held revoke (conn/pairing-revoke.mmd), which waited while the Instance slept, then its owed cleanup (conn/gateway-sessions-revoke.mmd)\n      %%> - what the busy server cannot take yet starts at the end of what holds it; what fails, the five-minute `conn:owed-work:advance` tick admits again\n    end\n    opt the server is a Conn Server from 0.5.0\n      Note over GW: Extend dated sessions<br/>clients/extend\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - after the held sessions, while the Gateway session has not ended; `GET /health` names `conn-server` 0.5.0 or later\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - `POST /api/auth/clients/extend` with `sessionTtl: \"until-revoked\"` for the Gateway session and each held session that ends within a year, with their own bearers\n      %%> - never for a peer-link session: its holder keeps the date it stored, so an existing dated link must be linked again\n      %%> - stores each new end; a 409 keeps a T3-carried session dated, and the hourly renewal keeps renewing it\n      %%> - a 404 means the session is gone: a held one is settled and held anew at the Node's next read, and the Gateway session is recorded as ended, so its renewal issues a new one\n      %%> - a 400 `invalid_session_ttl` is logged as an error; the session stays dated\n    end\n  else T3 does not answer\n    %% path: t3-unready\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>instance.t3_unready\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Operation fails; the marker is gone and the Route serves the app\n    %%> - the runtime's `power_state` is `running`, because the container runs\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - the same command; a start of a running container changes nothing\n  else a nested-fleet container is stopped\n    %% path: nested-stopped\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>instance.nested_fleet_stopped\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - a host reboot or a stop ended the freeze; a boot without the Club key and `wg-nested` gives a fleet that cannot pass Doctor\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - nothing boots; the marker stays and `power_state` stays `stopped`\n    %%> - the sleep no longer counts as frozen: the next wake of a clone takes its start, which removes the marker; a default starts only by its deployment\n  else a step fails before the app answers\n    %% path: failed\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step, error_code\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the asleep marker stays; a held visit gets a plain `503`: the wake failed, with its code\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - a reload of the page or the same command; a start of a running container changes nothing\n    %%> - planned, a production live: the first visit 30 s or more after the failure starts one new wake by itself; until then Caddy answers `503` with `Retry-After: 30`\n  end\n  end\n  loop until terminal\n    CL->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CL: Status and result\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/start": {
   "kind": "instance.start",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Container runtime (docs/reference/development-containers.md#a-stopped-nested-fleet-clone-starts): `instance:wake` of a clone of a nested-fleet default starts the stopped copy and its inner fleet. A nested-fleet clone sleeps frozen and its wake resumes it (instance/wake.mmd); this start is for a copy that a host reboot, an OOM kill or a stop left stopped.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:wake\n  %% kind: instance.start\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CL icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CL as Caller<br/>orbit · task flow\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>Instance container\n  CL->>GW: instance:wake INSTANCE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit instance:wake INSTANCE`\n  %%> Callers:\n  %%> - the command, after a host reboot, an OOM kill or a stop by hand left the copy stopped\n  %%> - a Route visit to such a copy that slept: the runtime activation endpoint calls the same wake admission\n  %%> - planned: the task flow before it uses a task's copy\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the caller is the Instance's Node or has node access to it\n  Note over GW: Start or join the start<br/>one per Instance\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - an active development clone of a nested-fleet default, on a Node with the nested-fleet switch on, whose last sleep did not freeze it or whose wake since found it stopped\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the wake admits `instance.start` instead of `instance.wake`; an Idempotency-Key keys the start\n  %%> - an open start of the Instance is joined; any other Operation on the Instance refuses it with `operation.target_busy`\n  %%> - a join to a Conn launch's start records shared use while locking the start Operation. If launch cleanup already cancelled the queued start, a new request admits a new start\n  %%> On failure:\n  %%> - a stopped nested-fleet default: `instance.sleep_unsupported`; it starts only through its deployment\n  %%> - other lifecycle work: `instance.lifecycle_busy`; no run starts\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-start\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the run's one read of the Club key; without one the start fails before its run with `instance.nested_club_key_missing`\n  GW-->>CL: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID\n  SW->>N: Start the container<br/>Ansible · tags start\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - a nested-fleet default's root disk grows to the intent's size first; it never shrinks\n  %%> - the start guard: a nested-fleet container first gets the owned keys `boot.autostart=false` and `limits.memory.swap` equal to its memory (Incus gives no swap without a size), written over an absent or a different value; then the guard refuses any other key or device that differs, and checks the egress gates of every bridge NIC\n  %%> - the presented machine name: a stopped container gets `/etc/hostname`, `/etc/machine-info` and the `127.0.1.1` line of `/etc/hosts` through the file API before the boot, so the boot gives the kernel and T3 the name; a running or frozen container gets nothing, and nothing restarts\n  %%> - `incus start`, then the Club key into the container's /run (tmpfs) on stdin, the same script as the create's `clone-club-key`\n  %%> - waits until each desired-running unit is active; T3 starts with the container, and the start does not wait for it\n  %%> - a container frozen by a sleep resumes through the power helper's `restore`, under the Instance's lock: it reads the plan's pages back while still frozen, unfreezes once and closes swap on each cgroup of `limits.json`; a stopped container's plan is discarded before the boot; a start that left the container running removes the asleep marker\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - no seed or snapshot holds the key, so every start delivers it before the fleet checks\n  %%> On failure:\n  %%> - `instance.power_helper_busy` when another power action on the Instance held the power helper's lock for 10 s, `instance.power_helper_failed` when the helper refused its input or crashed; either leaves the asleep marker and the container's power state as they were\n  SW->>N: Start the inner fleet<br/>Ansible · fleet check, resume\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the create's `clone-fleet`: the fleet rules, the three nested containers, the tunnel `wg-nested` with the hub's resolver for `~nested-dev` and `~nested-prod`\n  %%> - writes the copy's proxy bypass for units and login shells: the drop-ins and the manager environment\n  %%> - restarts T3 once, only when the write changed something and this start booted the container (`last_used_at`)\n  %%> - waits for Doctor over the tunnel and the three agents\n  %%> - records its own seconds as `fleet_seconds`\n  alt the run passes\n    %% path: main\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result\n    Note over GW: Record running\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the runtime's `power_state` is `running`\n    %%> - `nested_fleet_club_key` says what the start did with the key; `fleet_seconds` the fleet's time\n  else a step fails\n    %% path: failed\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step, error_code\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - a fleet failure names why in the failure's detail, redacted\n    %%> - the container may run with a fleet that is not ready; `power_state` stays as it was\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - the same command; each step leaves what already runs as it is\n  end\n  loop until terminal\n    CL->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CL: Status and result\n  end\n"
  },
  "instance/container/start-engine": {
   "kind": "instance.start",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% A stopped nested-fleet clone's start on the Gateway engine: the steps of instance/start.mmd (workflow operation-instance-start) as executor primitives of the `instance` and `power` families, reusing those of instance.create. Design for ADR 0267 and ADR 0271; no engine flow exists yet. Step names describe effects; the implementation settles action names unless an existing action is named.\n  %% The wake admits `instance.start` (instance/wake.mmd); this chart is placed beside instance/start.mmd on instance/wake.mdx.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: instance:wake\n  %% kind: instance.start\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane EN icon=workflow pill=engine tone=purple\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=executor tone=amber\n  %% lane C icon=server pill=copy tone=green\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant EN as Engine<br/>Gateway and NATS\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>executor\n  participant C as Container<br/>via executor\n  CLI->>GW: instance:wake INSTANCE\n  Note over GW: Start or join the start<br/>one per Instance\n  %%> Same admission as instance/start.mmd: an open start is joined; other work refuses (`operation.target_busy`, `instance.lifecycle_busy`); a nested-fleet default refuses (`instance.sleep_unsupported`)\n  %%> The Club key is read once here; without one the start fails before any step (`instance.nested_club_key_missing`)\n  alt the engine admits the whole Operation\n    %%> Admission, one decision per Operation (EngineKinds::routes):\n    %%> - `instance.start` is in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`\n    %%> - every Node a step touches reports its action in `$KV.NODES`: `discard`, `instance.start`, `instance.club_key` and `instance.fleet_ready` (all exist), and the new container-state read, unit wait and marker removal actions\n    %%> - `instance.start`'s decoder refuses unknown fields, so the new inputs below need evidence first: the Node lists a feature for them in its reported `features` (as `instance.t3_pairing`), else the whole start stays on Swamp; it never fails after earlier steps acted\n    %%> - the flow accepts the plan shape; a part it does not cover throws `EngineUnsupported` before any order\n    %%> - eligible plan: a stopped clone whose seed holds Orbit's tunnel boot unit (`fleet_tunnel` recorded at create); a frozen copy (its wake resumes it) and a seed without the tunnel unit (Swamp's clone-fleet) throw `EngineUnsupported`\n    Note over GW: Bind steps, take the lock<br/>commit, publish the start\n    %%> - the step rows and the Operation lock commit in one transaction before the start event; an Operation never moves between engines after this\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    EN->>N: Read the container state<br/>Incus status · read-only\n    N-->>EN: Stopped or Running\n    opt Incus reports Stopped\n      EN->>N: Discard the freeze plan<br/>power discard\n      %%> A stopped copy's prepared plan and limits record go before the boot, under the Instance's target lock, as the Swamp start does\n      %%> Skipped when the read finds the copy Running: `discard` refuses a running target, so a retry Operation after the boot never sends it\n    end\n    EN->>C: Start the copy<br/>instance.start · guard · names\n    %%> Reuses `instance.start`: NIC guards and egress gates, the allowed config of a nested-fleet copy, ID maps, boot, hostname and `/etc/hosts`; an already running copy changes nothing\n    %%> New inputs, behind the feature above: the start guard's owned keys `boot.autostart=false` and `limits.memory.swap` written over an absent or different value; the presented machine name; and, for a copy still Stopped, the recorded server's drop-in `/etc/systemd/system/t3.service.d/20-orbit-server.conf` before the boot, so t3.service starts the recorded server\n    C-->>EN: Running<br/>applied\n    EN->>C: Deliver the Club key<br/>instance.club_key · tmpfs\n    %%> Into the copy's `/run`, never a seed or snapshot; answers changed, changed_serve_pending or unchanged\n    EN->>C: Wait for the boot units<br/>FrankenPHP · boot Processes\n    %%> As the Swamp start: reads the Process units that start at boot (installed and enabled) and FrankenPHP, then waits for each to be active with an empty `Job=`; a bounded read from the Node, failing `instance.container_start_failed` at the bound\n    C-->>EN: Units active<br/>names · seconds\n    EN->>N: Remove the asleep marker<br/>after the units answer\n    %%> Right after the boot units are ready, before the fleet check, as the outer start phase does (instance-container-phase.yml `Remove the asleep marker after the start`): Caddy sends the next request to the running app; an absent marker counts as removed\n    %%> A later fleet failure fails the Operation but never puts the app back behind the sleep gate; a failure before this step keeps the marker\n    EN->>C: Wait for the fleet<br/>instance.fleet_ready · start\n    %%> Readiness, as the Swamp start's fleet check: inner Incus, stopped nested containers started, the tunnel unit active, Doctor healthy over the tunnel, every agent online\n    %%> `seed_without_tunnel` fails the step; the engine never hands the rest to Swamp\n    C-->>EN: fleet ready<br/>started · fleet_seconds\n    opt the receipt is lost\n      EN->>C: Resend the same step<br/>fresh nonce · same step ID\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - the step passes its resend deadline with no receipt; two sends, one execution\n      %%> - the Operation keeps its lock, so Instance 42 stays fenced; no other Operation and no Swamp run acts on it meanwhile\n      %%> On the Node:\n      %%> - the journal holds `done` for this Operation step, action and target: the executor answers the stored result with `repeat: true` and does not act again\n      %%> - an order still `open` replays under the target lock first; an order that expired before the lock did nothing (`executor.order_expired`), and the step is sent again\n      C-->>EN: Stored result<br/>repeat: true\n    end\n    alt every step verified\n      EN->>GW: Record running<br/>power_state · club key\n      %%> - the kind's own projection records the result, the same rows a Swamp run records\n    else a step fails\n      EN--xGW: Failed step<br/>error_code · redacted detail\n      %%> - a deterministic refusal is a terminal receipt too; the journal keeps it and a resend repeats it\n      Note over GW: Record the failure<br/>power_state unchanged\n      %%> - the container may run with a fleet that is not ready; retry: the same command, and each step leaves what already runs as it is\n    end\n  else the engine does not admit it\n    Note over GW: Start the Swamp run<br/>operation-instance-start\n    %%> - the kind is not enabled, a Node lacks the action, `$KV.NODES` is unreadable, or the flow throws `EngineUnsupported`: the whole Operation runs operation-instance-start (instance/start.mmd)\n    %%> - no step of it runs on the engine; an Operation never splits across engines\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "metrics/credentials": {
   "kind": "metrics.credentials_reset",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R2-R4 (secrets Gateway → vault → step), env_import vault-overwrite decision, single-swamp-node. docs/reference/metrics.md#credentials.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 16, 20, 22 (the pending-login read becomes the guard of the change step; password file via cfgmgmt file).\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: metrics:credentials\n  %% kind: metrics.credentials_reset\n  %% Rule 23: 2026-11-03 required-only main path; --reset is required for this kind.\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Node 12<br/>runs Grafana\n  CLI->>GW: metrics:credentials<br/>--reset\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit metrics:credentials --reset`\n  %%> - syntax: `[--reset] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - `--reset`: required for this kind; without it, the command is only a Gateway read of the stored credential, with no task\n  %%> - `--json`, optional: the CLI returns the task and does not read the credential afterwards\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - gives Grafana's `admin` user a new random password\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Check, keep a pending one<br/>task #5175 · vault key\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - exactly one active Gateway Node, and your Node has an access grant to it\n  %%> - the `metrics` role on Node 12 is active\n  %%> - no other reset or Metrics role change runs; the Gateway holds one Metrics lock\n  %%> Secrets:\n  %%> - creates a random pending password, or reuses the one a failed reset left; both are stored encrypted\n  %%> - pushes the active and the pending password to the Swamp vault as key `metrics-credentials-operation-5175`; the inputs hold only that name (R3)\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5175 recorded with the full intent\n  %%> - the active password still works; nothing has changed in Grafana yet\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-metrics-credentials\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - Grafana's address on Node 12, and the vault key name\n  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5175\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>N: Change the password<br/>guard · PUT /api/user/password\n  %%> Guard:\n  %%> - skips the change when the pending password already logs in as `admin`; an earlier reset may have changed it already\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - logs in with the active password and sets the pending one through Grafana's API\n  %%> - writes the pending password to the role's protected password file with `@swamp/cfgmgmt/file`, so the role's health check uses it\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - official `@swamp/ssh` from 209; the passwords reach the step with `vault.get` and go through stdin\n  %%> Gentle:\n  %%> - no container restart; Grafana keeps serving\n  SW->>N: Confirm the new login<br/>Grafana GET /api/user\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - logs in with the pending password and expects success\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the reset only counts when Grafana accepts the new password\n  alt Grafana accepts it\n    N-->>SW: login ok\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the check passed; no password in the output\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Promote the pending one<br/>active password · task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the pending password becomes the active one\n    %%> - the vault key is deleted with the native `vault.delete` frame\n    %%> - task #5175 succeeds\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: login refused or exit ≠ 0\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - Grafana doesn't answer, refuses the active password, or refuses the new one\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code\n    Note over GW: Keep both passwords<br/>task failed · rerun reuses it\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the active password stays stored, and the pending one is kept\n    %%> - task #5175 fails\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run `orbit metrics:credentials --reset` again; it reuses the pending password and checks it before any change\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5175\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n  CLI->>GW: metrics:credentials<br/>read the active one\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - after success, the CLI reads the stored credential\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - human output only; with `--json`, the CLI returns the task and does not read\n  GW-->>CLI: URL, user, password<br/>Cache-Control: no-store\n  %%> Returns:\n  %%> - the URL `https://metrics.orbit`, the user `admin` and the active password\n  %%> - the read runs nothing on a Node\n"
  },
  "metrics/disable": {
   "kind": "metrics.disable",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4, metrics.exporter_disable decisions (order, full reverse), single-swamp-node. docs/reference/metrics.md#disable-and-purge.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 17, 22 (cfgmgmt absent applies named; their checks are the end check).\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: metrics:disable\n  %% kind: metrics.disable\n  %% Rule 23: 2026-11-03 required-only main path.\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane G icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Node 12<br/>Prometheus·Grafana\n  participant G as Gateway Node<br/>Caddy for Grafana\n  CLI->>GW: metrics:disable<br/>the one Metrics Node\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit metrics:disable`\n  %%> - syntax: `[--force] [--purge-data] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - no Node: the role has one holder, here Node 12\n  %%> - `--purge-data`, optional: also deletes the Prometheus and Grafana volumes and the stored Grafana passwords (the `opt --purge-data` blocks); by default they stay\n  %%> - `--force`, optional: skips the confirmation\n  %%> - `--json`, optional: return the final task as JSON\n  %%> Confirmation:\n  %%> - in a terminal, the CLI shows what goes and asks; the default is No\n  %%> - `--force` is required without a terminal, with `--json`, and with `--purge-data` (`metrics.force_required`)\n  Note over GW: Check, mark removing<br/>role: removing · task #5174\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - exactly one active Gateway Node, and your Node has an access grant to it\n  %%> - a `metrics` role exists on Node 12\n  %%> - no other Metrics change runs; the Gateway holds one Metrics lock\n  %%> Builds:\n  %%> - the list of exporter Nodes, the Caddy site, the private DNS name and the firewall rules to remove\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the role marked removing, and task #5174 recorded with the full intent\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-metrics-disable\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - what to remove, and with `--purge-data` the two exact volume names\n  %%> - Node 12's and the Gateway Node's addresses and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5174\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>G: Withdraw metrics.orbit<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file absent\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - removes the `metrics.orbit` site from the Gateway Node's Caddyfile, validates it, and reloads Caddy gracefully\n  %%> - removes the `metrics.orbit` private DNS name through the shared DNS step on the `vpn` Node\n  %%> - removes the site's certificate and key\n  %%> Why first:\n  %%> - nobody reaches Grafana while it goes away\n  SW->>N: Remove the containers<br/>docker_container absent\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - stops and removes `orbit-metrics-prometheus` and `orbit-metrics-grafana`, and their unit files\n  %%> - Prometheus stops collecting before any exporter goes\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - official `@swamp/cfgmgmt` behaviors (systemd, docker_container, file) over SSH from 209\n  Note over SW: Remove the fleet exporters<br/>same steps as exporter:disable\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - for each exporter Node: stops and removes the exporters and the `prometheus-node-exporter` package, one Node at a time\n  %%> - the same steps that `metrics:exporter:disable` runs for one Node\n  %%> Kept:\n  %%> - each Node's exporter preference, so a later enable selects the same Nodes\n  Note over SW: Remove the fleet allows<br/>same allows as exporter:disable\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - for each exporter Node: removes the firewall allows for Node 12, one Node at a time\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - the `metrics-exporter-allows` child that `metrics:exporter:disable` runs\n  SW->>N: Remove the Grafana rules<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall absent\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - removes the allow for the Gateway Node\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - official `@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall` over SSH from 209\n  SW->>N: Remove the configuration<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/directory absent\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - deletes the configuration directory and the Grafana password file\n  %%> Kept:\n  %%> - Docker and the installed packages\n  opt --purge-data\n    SW->>N: Delete the volumes<br/>docker volume rm · 2 volumes\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - deletes exactly the Prometheus and Grafana volumes, so all metrics history and dashboards go\n    %%> Without --purge-data:\n    %%> - both volumes stay, so a later enable continues with the old data\n  end\n  SW->>N: Check it is gone<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · one command\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - one command fails unless no `orbit-metrics-` container and no `/etc/orbit/metrics` remain\n  %%> - a cfgmgmt `check` reports drift without failing, so it cannot be the end check\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the role only counts as removed when Node 12 shows it\n  alt every step passes\n    N-->>SW: Metrics absent\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the check passed\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Delete the assignment<br/>role gone · task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the `metrics` assignment is deleted, and the exporters are recorded as removed\n    %%> - the stored Grafana password stays\n    %%> - task #5174 succeeds\n    opt --purge-data\n      Note over GW: Delete the passwords<br/>active · pending\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the active and pending Grafana passwords are deleted\n    end\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or still present\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a Caddy, container, exporter, firewall, removal or check step fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code\n    Note over GW: Keep the assignment<br/>failed · resume the failed step\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the assignment and the Grafana passwords stay; the role is marked failed with the step's error code\n    %%> - task #5174 fails\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - parts removed before the failure stay removed; there is no rollback\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - Swamp resumes from the failed step, or run the same command again; removal steps skip what is already gone\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5174\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "metrics/enable": {
   "kind": "metrics.enable",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4 + 1 (certificate signing is the one step call), metrics.exporter_enable decisions (reload, don't restart), single-swamp-node. docs/reference/metrics.md.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 6, 9, 22 (configuration and Grafana password in one cfgmgmt file step; health read folded into the start; the end check is metrics.orbit; the Grafana allow uses @swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall).\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: metrics:enable\n  %% kind: metrics.enable\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane G icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Node 12<br/>Prometheus·Grafana\n  participant G as Gateway Node<br/>Caddy for Grafana\n  CLI->>GW: metrics:enable 12<br/>Node id or name\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit metrics:enable 12`\n  %%> - syntax: `[node] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Node that runs Prometheus and Grafana; in a terminal the CLI lists the active Nodes and asks\n  %%> - with `--json` or without a terminal, the Node is required (`metrics.node_required`)\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - gives Node 12 the `metrics` role, and shows its dashboards at `https://metrics.orbit`\n  Note over GW: Check, claim the role<br/>role: provisioning · task #5173\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - exactly one active Gateway Node, and your Node has an access grant to it\n  %%> - Node 12 is an active, managed Linux Node\n  %%> - no Node has the `metrics` role yet, not even a failed one (`node.role_conflict`)\n  %%> - no other Metrics change runs; the Gateway holds one Metrics lock\n  %%> Builds:\n  %%> - the Prometheus configuration with every selected exporter Node as a target\n  %%> - Grafana's datasource and 3 dashboards, and both container specs with pinned images\n  %%> - the Grafana firewall rules, the Caddy site on the Gateway Node, and the private DNS name\n  %%> Secrets:\n  %%> - creates the Grafana `admin` password once, stored encrypted; a later enable reuses it\n  %%> - pushes it to the Swamp vault as key `metrics-role-operation-5173`; the inputs hold only that name\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the `metrics` assignment on Node 12 recorded with status provisioning\n  %%> - task #5173 recorded with the full intent\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-metrics-enable\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - the configuration files, container specs, exporter list and publication\n  %%> - the vault key name of the Grafana password\n  %%> - Node 12's and the Gateway Node's addresses and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5173\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>N: Install Docker<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/apt · systemd\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - installs `docker.io` when no healthy Docker is present, and keeps it running\n  %%> - waits for a busy apt lock within the run's time budget\n  SW->>N: Write the configuration<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · promtool\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - writes the Prometheus configuration as a candidate and validates it with `promtool` first\n  %%> - publishes it and the Grafana provisioning files in `/etc/orbit/metrics`\n  %%> - writes the Grafana password from `vault.get`, mode 0400, in the same step\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - official `@swamp/cfgmgmt/directory` and `@swamp/cfgmgmt/file` behaviors over SSH from 209\n  %%> Why one step:\n  %%> - the password file is part of the Grafana configuration; it never appears in inputs, outputs or logs (R3)\n  SW->>N: Start the containers<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/docker_container\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - pulls the pinned images and starts `orbit-metrics-prometheus` and `orbit-metrics-grafana`\n  %%> - keeps the named data volumes, so a re-enable continues with the old data\n  %%> Gentle:\n  %%> - a container whose spec is unchanged keeps running; only a changed configuration is applied, with a Prometheus reload\n  %%> - a container is recreated only when its spec or image changes\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - official `@swamp/cfgmgmt` behaviors (docker_image, systemd, docker_container) over SSH from 209\n  %%> Read-back:\n  %%> - one bounded `timeout 60` wait for Prometheus `/-/ready`, and one for a Grafana login with the stored `admin` password\n  %%> - curl reads the password file on Node 12 and hides it from the process list; it never appears in inputs, outputs or logs\n  Note over SW: Add the fleet exporters<br/>same steps as exporter:enable\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - for each selected Node: the exporters and their WireGuard listeners, one Node at a time\n  %%> - the same steps that `metrics:exporter:enable` runs for one Node\n  %%> Which Nodes:\n  %%> - every eligible Node with a role that takes part in Metrics, or an `enabled` preference, and Node 12 itself\n  Note over SW: Allow the fleet scrapes<br/>same allows as exporter:enable\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - for each selected Node: the firewall allows for Node 12 on the exporter ports, one Node at a time\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - the `metrics-exporter-allows` child that `metrics:exporter:enable` runs\n  SW->>GW: Sign metrics.orbit cert<br/>operation:direct:certificate-sign\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - a step creates the key on the Gateway Node and reads only the CSR\n  %%> - the Gateway signs it with the Orbit CA; this is the one call a step may make to the Gateway\n  GW-->>SW: Signed certificate<br/>leaf + chain\n  %%> What happens next:\n  %%> - Swamp writes the certificate beside its key on the Gateway Node\n  SW->>N: Allow the Gateway in<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - allows the Gateway Node to reach Grafana on port 3000\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - the official `@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall` behavior over SSH from 209\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - no deny for other peers yet; that needs a rule above the managed WireGuard trust, which `@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall` cannot place\n  SW->>G: Publish metrics.orbit<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · reload\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - adds the `metrics.orbit` site to the Gateway Node's Caddyfile, validates it, and reloads Caddy gracefully\n  %%> - Caddy asks the Gateway to authorize each request, then proxies over WireGuard to Grafana\n  %%> - publishes `metrics.orbit` to the Gateway's WireGuard address through the shared DNS step on the `vpn` Node\n  %%> Why a record:\n  %%> - `metrics.orbit` names one service on the Gateway Node; no Cluster or Node TLD answer covers it\n  %%> End check:\n  %%> - one bounded `timeout 60` wait on the Gateway Node until `https://metrics.orbit` answers with its Orbit certificate\n  alt every step passes\n    G-->>SW: metrics.orbit answers\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - Caddy serves the site with the new certificate\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway for a result\n    Note over GW: Record result<br/>role: active · task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the `metrics` role on Node 12 becomes active\n    %%> - each fleet exporter is recorded as active\n    %%> - task #5173 succeeds\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or not ready\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a package, config validation, container, health check, exporter, certificate, firewall or Caddy step fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code\n    Note over GW: Mark the role failed<br/>keep assignment · resume step\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the assignment and the Grafana password stay; the role is marked failed with the step's error code\n    %%> - task #5173 fails\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - finished steps stay, so a publication failure keeps the running containers; there is no rollback\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - Swamp resumes from the failed step; finished steps don't run again\n    %%> - a new enable is refused while the failed assignment exists; converge it with `orbit node:role:add 12 metrics --converge`\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5173\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "metrics/exporter-disable": {
   "kind": "metrics.exporter_disable",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: inverse of metrics.exporter_enable. ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4, official @swamp/cfgmgmt behaviors. Prometheus stops scraping first, then the exporters go.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rule 22 (allows removed with cfgmgmt firewall).\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: metrics:exporter:disable\n  %% kind: metrics.exporter_disable\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane M icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Node 12<br/>exporters removed\n  participant M as Metrics Node<br/>Prometheus\n  CLI->>GW: metrics:exporter:disable<br/>12 · Node id\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit metrics:exporter:disable 12`\n  %%> - syntax: `<node>`\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - stops collecting metrics from Node 12 and removes its exporters\n  %%> - metrics already collected stay in Prometheus until they expire (7 days)\n  Note over GW: Check, record<br/>preference · task #5141\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12\n  %%> - Node 12 is not the Metrics Node itself\n  %%> - no other exporter change runs for this Metrics Node; the Gateway holds one lock\n  %%> Builds:\n  %%> - the complete new Prometheus configuration, without Node 12\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - Node 12's exporter preference recorded as `disabled`\n  %%> - task #5141 recorded with the full intent\n  %%> - nothing has happened on any Node yet\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-metrics-exporter-disable\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - Node 12's and the Metrics Node's addresses and SSH host keys\n  %%> - the complete Prometheus configuration without Node 12\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5141\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>M: Publish the config<br/>validate, reload Prometheus\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - validates the configuration without Node 12 with `promtool`\n  %%> - copies it over the live file in place (Prometheus bind-mounts the file) and sends Prometheus SIGHUP, so it reloads while running\n  %%> - no restart, so collection from every other Node continues without a gap; Grafana keeps running\n  %%> When a restart is needed:\n  %%> - only when the Prometheus container itself changes, which is a role update, not an exporter change\n  %%> Why first:\n  %%> - Prometheus stops scraping Node 12 before its exporters go, so no scrape errors show up in between\n  SW->>N: Stop, remove exporters<br/>node-exporter · cAdvisor\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - stops and disables both units\n  %%> - removes the drop-in, the cAdvisor binary and the `prometheus-node-exporter` package\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - official `@swamp/cfgmgmt` behaviors (systemd, file, apt) over SSH from 209\n  SW->>N: Remove the allows<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall absent\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - removes the firewall allows for the Metrics Node on ports 9100 and 9102, with `ensure: absent`\n  Note over SW: Prove the exporter state<br/>end check · assert\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - Publish the config already proved that Prometheus no longer lists Node 12's exporter address\n  %%> - requires the exporter removal to have succeeded; it is the operation's end check\n  %%> - with no Metrics Node it passes at once, because only the preference is recorded\n  alt all steps pass\n    N-->>SW: exporters gone\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - both units are gone and the ports are closed\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record result<br/>exporter off · task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Node 12's exporter recorded as off\n    %%> - task #5141 succeeds\n  else a step fails\n    M--xSW: config bad or step fails\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the configuration doesn't validate, Prometheus isn't ready, or a removal step fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code\n    Note over GW: Keep the preference<br/>failed · resume the failed step\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the preference stays `disabled`; the exporter is marked failed\n    %%> - task #5141 fails\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - Swamp resumes from the failed step; finished steps don't run again\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5141\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "metrics/exporter-enable": {
   "kind": "metrics.exporter_enable",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4 (process.stop shape), official @swamp/cfgmgmt behaviors, single-swamp-node. docs/reference/metrics.md.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 18, 22 (allows via cfgmgmt firewall, beside the install).\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: metrics:exporter:enable\n  %% kind: metrics.exporter_enable\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane M icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Node 12<br/>gets the exporters\n  participant M as Metrics Node<br/>Prometheus\n  CLI->>GW: metrics:exporter:enable 12<br/>Node id\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit metrics:exporter:enable 12`\n  %%> - syntax: `<node>`\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - makes Prometheus on the Metrics Node collect CPU, memory, disk and container metrics from Node 12\n  %%> - shows up in Grafana at `https://metrics.orbit`\n  Note over GW: Check eligible, record<br/>preference · task #5140\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12\n  %%> - a Node has the `metrics` role\n  %%> - Node 12 is an active, managed Linux Node with a WireGuard address; never a task VM\n  %%> - no other exporter change runs for this Metrics Node; the Gateway holds one lock\n  %%> Builds:\n  %%> - the complete new Prometheus configuration, with Node 12 added as a target\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - Node 12's exporter preference recorded as `enabled`\n  %%> - task #5140 recorded with the full intent\n  %%> - nothing has happened on any Node yet\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-metrics-exporter-enable\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - Node 12's and the Metrics Node's addresses and SSH host keys\n  %%> - the exporter unit settings and the complete Prometheus configuration\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5140\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>N: Install the exporters<br/>node-exporter · cAdvisor\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - installs `prometheus-node-exporter` (machine metrics) and cAdvisor (container metrics)\n  %%> - writes a drop-in so both listen only on Node 12's WireGuard address, ports 9100 and 9102\n  %%> - starts both, and restarts them if they stop\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - official `@swamp/cfgmgmt` behaviors (apt, fetch, file, systemd) over SSH from 209\n  SW->>N: Allow the Metrics Node<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - adds firewall allows on Node 12 for the Metrics Node's address only, ports 9100 and 9102\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - `@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall`: a managed rule goes below the operator rules, so appending is right and no insert position is needed\n  %%> - runs beside the install step; neither needs the other\n  SW->>N: Check the endpoints<br/>both answer on WireGuard\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - checks that both exporters answer on their ports, retrying for up to 30 seconds while a restarted exporter starts (cAdvisor takes about 5 seconds)\n  SW->>M: Publish the config<br/>validate, reload Prometheus\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - validates the new Prometheus configuration with `promtool` before using it\n  %%> - copies it over the live file in place (Prometheus bind-mounts the file) and sends Prometheus SIGHUP, so it reloads while running\n  %%> - no restart, so collection from every other Node continues without a gap; Grafana keeps running\n  %%> When a restart is needed:\n  %%> - only when the Prometheus container itself changes, which is a role update, not an exporter change\n  %%> - checks once that Prometheus loaded the new configuration\n  Note over SW: Prove the exporter state<br/>end check · assert\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - checks that Prometheus lists Node 12's exporter address as an active target after the reload (inside Publish the config)\n  %%> - is the operation's end check; with no Metrics Node it passes at once, because only the preference is recorded\n  alt all steps pass\n    M-->>SW: Prometheus ready\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - Prometheus is running with Node 12 as a target\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record result<br/>exporter active · task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Node 12's exporter recorded as active\n    %%> - task #5140 succeeds\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: unit or check fails\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - an install, unit, firewall step or endpoint check fails, or the configuration doesn't validate\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code\n    Note over GW: Keep the preference<br/>failed · resume the failed step\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the preference stays `enabled`; the exporter is marked failed\n    %%> - task #5140 fails\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - Swamp resumes from the failed step; finished steps don't run again\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5140\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "metrics/status": {
   "kind": "metrics.status",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: Nick 2026-11-06, agent strip (c): the status reads Prometheus on the Metrics role for which scrape targets are up. No agent. Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review: one `up` query shows Prometheus and Grafana healthy and each exporter active or inactive; the old `drift` actual is gone, because the Node configuration check ran in the agent.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: metrics:status\n  %% kind: metrics.status\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane M icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant M as Metrics Node<br/>Prometheus\n  CLI->>GW: metrics:status\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit metrics:status`\n  %%> - syntax: `metrics:status [--json]`\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Read the records<br/>role · exporters\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - reads the Metrics assignment, which Nodes have an exporter, and the stored degradation of each\n  %%> - without a Metrics role, the answer is `enabled: false` and nothing else is read\n  GW->>M: Ask which targets are up<br/>one query through Grafana\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - one instant query, `up` for the jobs `orbit-node-exporter` and `orbit-cadvisor`\n  %%> - Grafana's datasource proxy re-serves Prometheus's API, with the stored Grafana credential, on the Metrics Node's WireGuard address; the Gateway already has a firewall path to it\n  %%> - it starts no run and contacts no Node but the Metrics Node\n  alt the query answers\n    M-->>GW: up per target\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - one series per scrape target: the address and port, and 1 or 0\n    %%> Both:\n    %%> - Prometheus and Grafana are `healthy`: Grafana answered and relayed Prometheus's answer\n    %%> - an exporter is `active` when its Node's node-exporter and cAdvisor targets are both 1, and `inactive` when a target is 0 or missing\n    %%> - an exporter with a stored degradation, for example a Node the last convergence skipped, is `unknown`, whatever the query says\n  else it fails or times out\n    M--xGW: no answer\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - up to 5 s per call, a wrong credential, or either service down\n    %%> Both:\n    %%> - Prometheus and Grafana are `unhealthy`, and every exporter is `unknown`\n  end\n  GW-->>CLI: 200 status\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - whether Metrics is enabled, the Grafana URL, the assignment, the health of both services, and one row per exporter with its desired and actual state\n  %%> - a selected exporter that is not `active` makes `reconcile_status` degraded\n  %%> - a failed read is part of the answer, not an error\n"
  },
  "node/add": {
   "kind": "node.add",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0243/0244 task, one Swamp run, agent-observer-swamp-acts, root-of-trust-gateway-only, Ansible phases for idempotent Node state (rule 22). docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#add-a-node.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: node:add\n  %% kind: node.add\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane HUB icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as New Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  participant HUB as WireGuard hub<br/>vpn Node\n  CLI->>GW: node:add NAME HOST<br/>--host-key-fingerprint=SHA256\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit node:add NAME HOST --host-key-fingerprint=SHA256`\n  %%> - syntax: `<name> [host] [--host-key-fingerprint=SHA256] [--ssh-port=PORT] [--user=USER] [--orbit-user=USER] [--platform=linux|macos] [--architecture=ARCH] [--tld=TLD] [--role=ROLE]… [--cluster=ID] [--wireguard-ip=IP] [--lan-ip=IP] [--dns-server=IP] [--setting=PATH:VALUE] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Node name, its public SSH host, and the approved SSH host key fingerprint; a new Node, or an existing Node with no pinned host key, needs all three\n  %%> - every other option is recorded in the intent; only `--role` and `--platform=macos` add steps\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - adds the machine to the fleet as a Node\n  %%> - for a Node that already exists, the same command converges it again with its new TLD, roles or settings\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, record the Node\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the name is free, or names a Node that owns no Instances (`node.has_instances`)\n  %%> - a new Node, or an existing Node with no pinned host key, has a host and a host key fingerprint (`node.ssh_host_fingerprint_required`); for an existing Node the fingerprint equals the recorded one (`node.ssh_host_key_mismatch`)\n  %%> - Linux accepts only `x86_64`; `app-dev` needs a TLD, unless its Cluster has one\n  %%> - macOS names one existing account in `--user` and `--orbit-user`, an existing WireGuard address, and no role\n  %%> - no other lifecycle change runs for the name; the Gateway holds one lock per Node name\n  %%> - Node convergence, role addition or convergence, enrollment and agent rekey share one Node admission lock (`operation.target_busy`); this run holds it through all frozen role work without child Operation admission\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - a new Node is recorded as `provisioning`; an existing Node keeps its record until the result\n  %%> - the WireGuard address and the hub peer entry are chosen\n  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent; nothing has happened on the machine yet\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-add\n  %%> Secrets:\n  %%> - the bootstrap SSH identity and the new Node agent secret travel only through the vault, under one key name per Node; the run inputs hold key names, never values\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - the host, port, bootstrap user, managed user and approved fingerprint\n  %%> - packages, pins, Node agent configuration and the frozen role resources\n  %%> - the hub's address and SSH host keys, and the new peer entry\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task ID, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>N: Read the host key<br/>SSH · first contact\n  %%> Why first:\n  %%> - first contact ignores the worker's known hosts, so nothing on the machine changes until the host key matches the approved fingerprint\n  SW->>SW: Require approved host key<br/>native assert\n  SW->>N: Read the platform<br/>SSH argv\n  SW->>N: Read the bootstrap account<br/>SSH argv\n  %%> Linux machines:\n  %%> - the account tells where its authorized keys live; macOS skips this step\n  SW->>SW: Bind the Node identity<br/>native\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - binds the observed host key into the frozen role, CLI and DNS plans\n  SW->>SW: Write the intent<br/>ansible-input\n  %%> Linux machines:\n  %%> - writes the admitted intent to a private run folder on the Swamp Node and ships the playbook; macOS runs no Ansible phase\n  SW->>SW: Write the private input<br/>0600 files from the vault\n  %%> Secrets:\n  %%> - the Node agent secret goes to a private file in the run folder; the cleanup step removes them, also after a failure\n  SW->>SW: Write the playbook input<br/>inventory · pinned host keys\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - builds the inventory and `known_hosts` from the intent and the observed host key; nothing is scanned and host key checking stays on\n  SW->>N: Set up the machine<br/>Ansible phase: baseline\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - pins the Node for later operations and appends the Swamp control public key to the bootstrap account when it is absent\n  %%> - base packages (read through the package cache of the task VM for a Node of its nested fleet, else from the archive; `apt_proxy` in the intent), the managed account, a sudo rule that is validated before it replaces the old one, so no staged file stays, and the Orbit SSH keys\n  %%> - the public recovery SSH rule, then the firewall\n  %%> - the kernel policy that `node:converge` also applies: SCTP blocked, non-local bind for Docker publications\n  SW->>N: Set up the tunnel<br/>Ansible phase: tunnel\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - creates the WireGuard key and requires its public key to be unique, before any tunnel change: two Nodes with the same public key cannot both be peers, and an existing hub must keep its peer-facing key\n  %%> - on the hub's private network (both recorded `lan_ip` in one IPv4 /24), proves before any tunnel change that the kernel routes the hub's LAN address directly from the Node's `lan_ip` and that an SSH host key there has the hub's recorded fingerprint; a failed proof fails the phase, its assertion reads `node.hub_lan_unreachable` or `node.hub_lan_identity_mismatch`, and Orbit never falls back to the public endpoint\n  %%> - a Node whose live tunnel would move to another hub endpoint is refused before any tunnel change (`node.hub_switch_by_converge`); `node:converge` moves it with a rollback\n  %%> - writes the tunnel files, starts the tunnel, sets the tunnel address and trusts the WireGuard members\n  %%> - on the hub itself, writes and starts the source unit, checks that marked WireGuard packets to public peers leave from the public endpoint address, and opens SSH on its LAN address to its /24\n  %%> Secrets:\n  %%> - the private key stays in a key file on the Node; only the public key leaves it\n  SW->>N: Read WireGuard public key<br/>SSH argv\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - hands the public key to the hub phase and to the Gateway\n  opt Linux peer joins the VPN hub\n    SW->>HUB: Add the peer<br/>Ansible phase: hub\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - writes the peer entry, applies the hub peers with the hub's port and mark, and checks that the hub key did not change\n    %%> - the hub loads every peer file it has when it applies, not the peers of the admitted snapshot, so a Node that joined after admission keeps its live peer\n    %%> - the hub peer stays after a failure; it is harmless and a rerun reuses it\n    %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-07): the hub's `orbit.conf` (written here, or by the `tunnel` phase when the Node is the hub) is the file the hub converge writes: no peer inline, and the `PostUp` loader of `orbit-peers.d`. Every peer the hub knows also gets its own file, so a peer that has only its file (the operator of a task VM's nested fleet) survives a restart of the hub\n  end\n  SW->>N: Install the Node agent<br/>Ansible phase: agent\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - installs the pinned `orbit-agent` release, its configuration and the observer secret, and starts it\n  %%> - the unit starts the agent after `wg-quick@orbit`, which brings up the tunnel, so the agent's first request to the Gateway address has a route\n  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):\n  %%> - measured on a booted task VM: the agent started 0.8 s before the tunnel was up, its first requests then hung for the 10 s connect timeout, and the Node was online 29 s after the VM started instead of about 19 s\n  SW->>N: Verify managed access<br/>SSH argv\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - logs in as the managed user over the tunnel; on Linux it runs the command with passwordless sudo\n  opt eligible CLI plan\n    SW->>N: Publish the Orbit CLI<br/>native child workflow\n    %%> Best effort:\n    %%> - a failure here does not fail the add\n  end\n  opt --role\n    SW->>N: Converge each role<br/>native child workflow\n    %%> Root boundary:\n    %%> - roles are frozen child-workflow inputs in this run, with no child tasks\n    %%> Phase digests:\n    %%> - each role run compares its phase digests with no record, so every role phase runs; it records none (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#skip-a-phase-whose-input-did-not-change)\n    %%> The first role:\n    %%> - closes public recovery SSH, so the Node is reachable over WireGuard only afterwards\n    %%> - the `vpn` role also enables forwarding on the hub\n  end\n  opt DNS needs publication\n    SW->>HUB: Configure DNS backend<br/>Ansible phase: dns\n    SW->>HUB: Install DNS listener<br/>native child workflow\n    SW->>N: Publish private DNS<br/>native child workflow\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a new VPN hub needs its DNS listener first, or private DNS records need publication\n  end\n  opt --platform=macos\n    SW->>N: Inspect the Mac tunnel<br/>SSH argv\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - macOS runs the host key check, the platform read, the identity bind, the managed access check, this step and the native check\n    %%> - it has no Ansible phase, no sudo and no handshake read, so the input steps and the cleanup are skipped\n  end\n  SW->>N: Check the Node state<br/>Ansible phase: check\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the agent and its binary run, the observer secret is in place, and the tunnel handshakes with the hub; the hub itself is exempt\n  %%> - the phase waits up to 20 s for the first handshake\n  SW->>SW: Check the Node<br/>native assert\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the managed login reaches the machine that the first login reached\n  %%> - on macOS, the tunnel address is up\n  %%> - it is the one end check the Gateway reads\n  SW->>SW: Remove the playbook input<br/>cleanup\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - on Linux, always, also after a failure: the run folder holds the private files\n  alt every step passes\n    N-->>SW: arch · host key · WG key\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the architecture, the host key and the WireGuard public key\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event and reads the run's outputs; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record the Node active\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Node becomes active, with its architecture, SSH host key pins and WireGuard key\n    %%> - each converged role becomes active; storage settings are stored\n    %%> - the task succeeds\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - Metrics exporters start only with `metrics:exporter:enable`\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or mismatch\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the host key or architecture differs, the bootstrap or a role fails, the hub refuses its configuration, or the check fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    Note over GW: Mark the Node failed\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - a new Node stays, marked failed at that step, so you can see it\n    %%> - an existing Node keeps its earlier record and stays active\n    %%> - a role created by this request that did not finish stays marked failed, and the rerun converges it\n    %%> - the task fails\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - finished steps stay; there is no rollback\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same `node:add` again after fixing the cause; every step skips work that is already done\n    %%> Removal:\n    %%> - `node:remove` removes a failed Node\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "node/converge": {
   "kind": "node.converge",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: fleet maintenance redesign (core.yaml read by Ansible, selected packages held). docs/cli/node/converge.mdx. Phases: input, agent, software, roles, tools, network, check; network applies hub first, then the Node and waits for the handshake.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: node:converge\n  %% kind: node.converge\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane H icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  participant H as VPN hub<br/>WireGuard peers\n  CLI->>GW: node:converge NODE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit node:converge NODE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<node> [--base | --roles=a,b] [--full] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  Note over GW: Validate, reserve targets\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - access follows the [shared Node authority rule](/cli/node) (`NodeAccessAuthorizer`)\n  %%> - admission uses recorded Node, role, Tool and network state; no live preflight\n  %%> - Node convergence, role addition or convergence, enrollment and agent rekey share one Node admission lock; enrollment's role work stays inside its parent run\n  alt unknown Node\n    %% path: unknown-node\n    GW--xCLI: Refused<br/>http.404\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - no Operation, run or Node change\n  else caller refused\n    %% path: caller-refused\n    GW--xCLI: Refused<br/>node_access.required\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - no Operation, run or Node change\n  else unsupported Node\n    %% path: unsupported-node\n    GW--xCLI: Refused<br/>node.converge_unsupported\n    %%> Requirements:\n    %%> - the target must be an active, managed Linux Node\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - no Operation, run or Node change\n  else selected role not assigned\n    %% path: unassigned-role\n    GW--xCLI: Refused<br/>validation.failed\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - no Operation, run, role assignment or Node change\n  else target or hub busy\n    %% path: target-busy\n    GW--xCLI: Refused<br/>operation.target_busy\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - no new Operation or run; the existing holder keeps its reservation\n  else admitted\n    %% path: admitted\n    opt --base\n      %% path: base-scope\n      Note over GW: Select base scope\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - narrows the admitted intent, without adding or removing assignments\n    end\n    opt --roles=a,b\n      %% path: selected-roles\n      Note over GW: Select recorded roles\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - narrows the admitted intent to the selected assignments\n    end\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-converge\n    %%> Inputs:\n    %%> - the phase digests the Node records; admission clears the record, and `--full` or a recorded failed agent pin sends none; a scoped run holds a record that a full converge wrote for its result (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#skip-a-phase-whose-input-did-not-change)\n    %%> - target, scope, recorded roles, Tools, network and vault key names; playbooks read release pins\n    %%> - the Gateway includes a CLI plan only for a Node with a recorded `ci-runner` assignment that is not being removed; scope flags do not change that decision\n    %%> - reserve the Node and its recorded hub until the Operation settles; two converges of different peers of the hub pass each other, whatever their scope (each writes the same recorded peer set to the hub); beside a converge of the hub Node itself, only two base converges pass (a base intent has no network)\n    %%> - automatic release role work waits for Instance and task Operations that participate on its target Node\n    %%> - the seven Ansible plays lock a behavior per Node (`node-converge-converge-<phase>-<node_id>`), so converges of different Nodes run side by side\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - execution continues asynchronously under the admitted Operation\n    SW->>SW: Prepare the input<br/>phase: input\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - writes private inventory, vars and known_hosts from schema-validated inputs\n    %%> - stages the shipped Ansible files and Tool helper in the private run folder shared by the Swamp Node workers\n    %%> - after it writes the input files for the phases, computes each phase digest from what the phases read: those files with the Operation ID masked, the Ansible bundle and the Tool helper with its Tool files; lists the phases whose digest equals the record\n    opt digest equals the record\n      %% path: phase-unchanged\n      Note over SW: Skip an unchanged phase\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - the phase does not run and does not read the Node; the result shows it `unchanged`, with no changes and no time\n      %%> - drift is not an input: the check phase still reads the Node, and `--full` runs every phase\n    end\n    SW->>N: Install Orbit's layer<br/>Ansible phase: agent\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - applies the agent, Orbit-owned files and units, and the kernel policy (SCTP blocked, non-local bind); restarts only changed services\n    %%> - writes the units the Node's roles own (the Swamp worker template, the websocket unit) and reloads systemd; it never starts or restarts them, and never writes the Swamp serve unit\n    %%> - with a CLI plan: requires and installs its pinned release and root profile\n    %%> - without a CLI plan: needs no CLI release or probe; removes the managed `/usr/local/bin/orbit` link, regular `orbit-0.N.0` binaries and root profile at `/root/.orbit/config.json` in this phase, under the Node update lock; preserves unrelated executables, backups and directories\n    SW->>N: Install pinned software<br/>Ansible phase: software\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - reads core.yaml and holds its selected packages at their pins\n    SW->>N: Configure the roles<br/>Ansible phase: roles\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - repairs selected role configuration without changing an Instance or role assignment\n    %%> - on a Node that offers proof places, other than the proof default's Node: first reads root's Incus client certificate on the default's Node, as `node:role:converge` does, and writes the seed listener's trust; on any other Node with `ci-runner` it removes the trust and stops the listener; it never opens the listener or changes its firewall rules (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#the-seed-listener)\n    %%> - on an app-dev Node: writes each Instance container's presented machine name (`/etc/hostname`, `PRETTY_HOSTNAME` in `/etc/machine-info`, the `127.0.1.1` line of `/etc/hosts`) through the file API, running, stopped or frozen; nothing restarts, so the kernel hostname follows at the container's next start and T3's label at T3's next start\n    SW->>N: Apply recorded Tools<br/>Ansible phase: tools\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - runs the Tool helper once on the Swamp Node, before it judges a Tool\n    %%> On a helper that cannot run:\n    %%> - fail the phase and mark no Tool failed\n    %%> On a Tool failure:\n    %%> - continue with the other Tools and mark only that Tool failed\n    alt recorded network in scope\n      %% path: recorded-network\n      SW->>H: Apply hub network<br/>Ansible phase: network\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - applies the recorded hub side before the peer; matching configuration is unchanged\n      %%> - prunes only the peer files of Nodes that are gone or being removed, decided when the prune runs: it keeps every Node of the snapshot and every file above the snapshot's newest Node id, so a task VM that joins while the run waits keeps its peer\n      %%> - removes the task VM hub filter that the removed `task_vm.hub_converge` left: stops and disables `orbit-task-vms-hub.service`, deletes its unit file and `/etc/orbit/task-vms/hub.nft`, and deletes the nft table `inet orbit_task_vms`; deletes every UFW rule whose comment starts with `orbit:task-vm-` by its spec from `ufw show added`, never by number, and no other rule, so two peer converges that run it together cannot delete a live rule; a hub without the filter or those rules changes nothing\n      %%> - on the hub: carries the mark in `wg syncconf`, opens SSH on its LAN address to its /24, adds the routed rule `orbit:vpn-peer-forwarding` (in and out on `orbit`, from the VPN subnet to the VPN subnet; UFW skips the same rule added by hand), writes the source unit (`orbit-hub-source.service`), restarts it on change or drift, and checks that a marked packet to a public address leaves from the public endpoint address; an IPv4 endpoint the hub does not hold fails the phase with the assertion `vpn.endpoint_not_local`\n      SW->>N: Apply peer, wait<br/>Ansible phase: network\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - when the rendered hub endpoint is the hub's LAN address, first proves that the kernel routes it directly from the Node's `lan_ip` and that an SSH host key there has the hub's recorded fingerprint; a failed proof fails the phase before any WireGuard change, with the assertion `node.hub_lan_unreachable` or `node.hub_lan_identity_mismatch`, with no fallback to the public endpoint\n      %%> - a changed hub endpoint, read from the previous `orbit.peers`, moves in one detached job on the Node: it takes WireGuard packets only from the new endpoint's address while it runs (assertion `node.hub_switch_unpinned` when it cannot), installs the staged files, ends the old session and accepts only a handshake from after the move; without one, it puts the previous files back and loads them, and the phase fails with the assertion `node.hub_switch_no_handshake`; when `wg` does not load the new files, it puts the previous ones back at once, and the assertion is `node.hub_switch_load_failed`\n      %%> - applies the recorded Node side and waits for the tunnel handshake\n      %%> - both arrows are one phase; the peer arrow is only for a peer target\n      %%> - a target hub is applied once; it requires every recorded peer on its interface (assertion `vpn.hub_peer_missing`) and waits only for the peers that had a handshake in the 120 seconds before the change, each for a handshake in the last 180 seconds, so an offline peer does not fail it\n    else no recorded network in scope\n      %% path: no-recorded-network\n      Note over SW: Network: no peer change<br/>Ansible phase: network\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the phase has no recorded configuration or handshake to apply\n    end\n    SW->>N: Check the Node<br/>Ansible phase: check\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - checks the selected software, units and role configuration, plus the recorded tunnel; on a target hub, every recorded peer on its interface, with no handshake wait\n    %%> - checks the pinned CLI only when the Gateway supplied a CLI plan; otherwise checks that the managed CLI files are absent\n    %%> - the phases run in order, idempotently; an empty scope item needs no mutation; input and check always run\n    opt phases completed or failed\n      %% path: cleanup\n      SW->>SW: Remove private run files\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - declared cleanup waits for Node work to stop and removes only this run's files\n      %%> - preserve the original failure and expose incomplete cleanup; interruption proves no cleanup\n    end\n    alt end check passes\n      %% path: succeeded\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the shared reader marks the Operation succeeded and records each Tool's result\n      %%> - with `ci-runner` in scope, records the seed listener in `nodes.seed_sender_id`: it keeps the sender it names when the intent names the same sender, and clears it otherwise (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#the-seed-listener)\n      %%> - with `ci-runner` in scope, records the hold script in `nodes.ci_hold_digest`: it keeps the digest when it is the digest of the script in the intent, and clears it otherwise; it keeps `nodes.ci_nested_hosting` only when the intent carries the nested branch (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#proof-places-on-a-ci-node)\n      %%> - the result lists each Instance container that got no Chromium trust store, with its reason\n      %%> - a full-scope run records each phase digest on the Node, whether the phase ran or was unchanged; a scoped run keeps the full-converge record it held at admission without the phases it changed, and without `network` when its network phase ran with a hub\n      %%> - a repeat is another Operation, not an admission skip; its unchanged phases skip; with `--full`, matching tasks report zero changes\n    else a phase fails\n      %% path: phase-failed\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code · failed step\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - stop at the failed phase and mark the Operation failed; later phases do not run\n      %%> - with `ci-runner` in scope, records the seed listener as on success when the roles phase passed, and clears `nodes.seed_sender_id` when it did not\n      %%> - with `ci-runner` in scope, records the hold script as on success when the roles phase passed, and clears `nodes.ci_hold_digest` and `nodes.ci_nested_hosting` when it did not\n      %%> - the result names the earliest fatal task among hosts whose statistics fail: each host contributes its last failed or unreachable record; ties select hub; names are bounded and may be withheld\n      %%> - censored-only evidence from a failed host reports a hidden task; missing, malformed or task-free reports leave `failure` absent; private callback output stays private\n      %%> - unreachable targets fail here, not at a live admission check\n      %%> On the Node:\n      %%> - completed and partial changes remain; no rollback and no Instance or assignment change\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - the Node keeps no phase digest, so a new attempt starts at input and reapplies every phase safely\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "node/list": {
   "kind": "node.list",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: recorded-state reads under the shared Node authority rule (/cli/node#authority). The request uses Gateway records and agent facts only; no Node or external-system contact.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: node:list\n  %% kind: node.list\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  CLI->>GW: node:list\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit node:list`\n  %%> - syntax: `orbit node:list [options]`\n  Note over GW: Check collection access<br/>shared Node authority\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the [shared Node authority rule](/cli/node#authority)\n  alt caller refused\n    %% path: access-refused\n    GW--xCLI: Access required<br/>node_access.required\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - exit 1; no records change\n  else caller admitted\n    %% path: authorized\n    Note over GW: Read visible Nodes<br/>name, then ID\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - selects only the caller's visible records under the shared authority rule\n    %%> - starts no Operation or Swamp run\n    alt no Nodes\n      %% path: empty\n      GW-->>CLI: No nodes.\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - exit 0; the Node list is empty\n    else Nodes recorded\n      %% path: recorded\n      Note over GW: Project recorded state<br/>shared Node and role projection\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - includes every assigned role with its recorded state and failure fields\n      %%> - uses the same role projection as node:show and node:role:list\n      %%> - includes the Cluster's name alongside its ID and the stored reboot and image refresh holds\n      opt missing agent data\n        %% path: agent-missing\n        Note over GW: Keep missing data visible<br/>not seen or no agent\n        %%> Result:\n        %%> - an agent that has never reported is not_seen; a Node with no observing agent has agent null\n      end\n      opt stale Node heartbeats\n        %% path: agent-offline\n        Note over GW: Keep offline Nodes<br/>last recorded values\n        %%> Result:\n        %%> - with a healthy Gateway view and stale Node heartbeats, the agent is offline; since is the last seen time\n        %%> - no refresh or reachability check runs\n      end\n      opt missing or stale view\n        %% path: agent-unknown\n        Note over GW: Mark agent state unknown\n        %%> Result:\n        %%> - unknown is not offline; the remaining recorded fields stay visible\n      end\n      GW-->>CLI: Nodes\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - exit 0, including when agent data is missing, stale or unknown\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "node/metrics": {
   "kind": "node.metrics",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: a synchronous read of the Node agent's metrics snapshot from Gateway records. Node family read rules and docs/reference/node-agent.md#liveness.\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: node:metrics\n  %% kind: node.metrics\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  opt Node omitted in an interactive terminal\n    CLI->>GW: List registered Nodes\n    GW-->>CLI: IDs and names\n    Note over CLI: Search and choose a Node<br/>Empty or cancel stops\n  end\n  CLI->>GW: node:metrics NODE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit node:metrics [NODE]`\n  %%> - syntax: `[node] [--json]`\n  Note over GW: Resolve and authorize\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - apply the shared Node authority rule (`NodeAccessAuthorizer`), linked from `docs/cli/node/metrics.mdx` to the Node family page\n  alt unknown Node\n    %% path: unknown-node\n    GW--xCLI: 404 Not Found<br/>http.404\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - no Node record matches the ID\n  else refused caller\n    %% path: refused-caller\n    GW--xCLI: 403 Forbidden<br/>node_access.required\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the shared Node authority rule refuses the caller\n  else authorized Node\n    %% path: authorized\n    Note over GW: Read recorded snapshot\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - reads only the latest metrics snapshot reported by this Node's agent and the recorded agent liveness\n    %%> - contacts no Node or other system, waits for no report and starts no run\n    alt no snapshot yet\n      %% path: no-snapshot\n      GW--xCLI: 422 Unprocessable<br/>node.metrics_unavailable\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - no metrics snapshot has been recorded for this Node\n    else stale snapshot\n      %% path: stale-snapshot\n      GW-->>CLI: 200 OK<br/>snapshot · possibly outdated\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - returns the retained snapshot, its observation time and agent state; `possibly_outdated` is true when the agent state is not `online`\n      %%> - offline wording matches `node:show`: `offline since <time>`; `not_seen` and `unknown` keep their shared meanings\n    else current snapshot\n      %% path: current-snapshot\n      GW-->>CLI: 200 OK<br/>snapshot · observation time\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - returns the snapshot with Node name and ID, observation time, per-mount observation times and stale flags, and agent state; `possibly_outdated` is false\n    end\n  end\n  Note over GW: Leave state unchanged\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - success and refusal change no records or Node state\n  %%> - a repeat reads the latest recorded snapshot again; it is not a skipped action\n"
  },
  "node/remove": {
   "kind": "node.remove",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run, Ansible phases for idempotent Node state (rule 22). docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#remove-a-node.\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: node:remove\n  %% kind: node.remove\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane P icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  participant P as Fleet Nodes<br/>hub · DNS\n  CLI->>GW: node:remove NODE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit node:remove NODE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<node> [--force] [--offline] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Node ID\n  %%> - `--force` and `--offline` are consent; the Gateway requires `--force` for an offline removal or a Node with roles\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, mark removing\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target Node, and the target is not the caller\n  %%> - the Gateway, VPN and control Nodes are protected\n  %%> - no Instances, Database servers, Schedules, Routes or user firewall rules remain; an online removal also has no Processes and no role dependents\n  %%> - a task VM host is drained and its task VM settings cleared\n  %%> - no other removal runs for the Node (`node.removal_busy`)\n  %%> Builds:\n  %%> - the pinned SSH targets, the publication changes for the fleet, and the exact managed paths\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the Node is recorded as `removing`, and a task is recorded with the full intent\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-remove\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task ID, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>N: Reach the Node<br/>SSH argv\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - probes the Node with its approved identity\n  %%> - an unreachable Node without `--offline` fails the run here (`node.removal_incomplete`); with `--offline` only its registry membership and fleet publications are removed\n  SW->>N: Shed the Node's roles<br/>native child workflow\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - removes each mutable role and withdraws its publications on other Nodes; protected roles stay with their owning lifecycle\n  %%> - an unreachable Node's own services are not touched\n  opt Linux Node reachable\n    SW->>N: Remove Orbit from the Node<br/>Ansible phase: remove\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - restores public SSH, removes the services, files, configuration, containers and firewall rules Orbit created, and the agent and worker footprint\n    %%> - withdraws residual catalog-owned task-host DHCP/DNS input rules through the shared Ansible phase before removing the management keys\n    %%> Retained:\n    %%> - packages, users, user data, package managers and unrelated SSH keys\n    SW->>N: Check the removal<br/>Ansible phase: check\n    %%> Why before the keys:\n    %%> - the check needs SSH, and a failed run must stay reachable for the rerun\n    SW->>N: Revoke management keys<br/>Ansible phase: keys\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - removes only the admitted Orbit management keys; the control key goes last, and nothing connects to the Node afterwards\n  end\n  opt macOS or iPadOS peer reachable\n    SW->>N: Remove the peer's keys<br/>SSH argv\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - revokes the admitted keys; the account, tools and host tunnel stay\n  end\n  SW->>P: Remove the hub peer<br/>native child workflow\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review: deletes this Node's own peer file on the hub, then loads the files that remain with `wg syncconf`, so the live peer goes with the file\n  %%> - it runs for every removed Node, also one whose key the Gateway never recorded, because the Node's own enrollment run may have written the file\n  %%> - it writes no other peer and keeps none: the intent is a snapshot from admission, and a removal admitted at the same time as another must not put the other's peer back (two removals five seconds apart left a removed VM live on the hub for five minutes)\n  SW->>P: Withdraw Router ingress<br/>SSH argv\n  SW->>P: Withdraw Metrics<br/>cfgmgmt file · SSH argv\n  SW->>P: Withdraw private DNS<br/>native child workflow\n  opt routing domains change\n    SW->>P: Refresh peer DNS<br/>native child workflow\n  end\n  %%> Why after the Node:\n  %%> - the hub peer is withdrawn after the Node-side cleanup, so the cleanup still has a path\n  SW->>SW: Confirm the removal<br/>native assert\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - checks the fleet no longer lists the Node; it contacts no machine\n  %%> Run files:\n  %%> - the run folder with the intent, inventory and any secrets is removed after success or failure\n  alt every step passes\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Delete the Node record\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Node record and its owned records are deleted, and the removal is broadcast\n    %%> - before the delete, extensions keep what must outlive the Node: Conn withdraws the Node's held sessions and hands their owed revokes to the Gateway Node, which revokes each by pairing_id\n    %%> - the result lists `roles_shed` and `retained_on_node`; for an unreachable machine it also names the Orbit keys and the worker unit that stay on it\n  else a step fails, is cancelled or the budget ends\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or timeout\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    Note over GW: Keep the Node, mark failed\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Node stays with status `failed` and the failed step `remove:operation`, so you can see it; its claimed roles are marked failed too\n    %%> - a cancelled or interrupted run ends the same way; later retirement steps do not run\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - finished steps stay; there is no rollback\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; every step skips work that is already done\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "node/role-add": {
   "kind": "node.role_add",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0243/0244 task, one Swamp run, Ansible phases for idempotent Node state (rule 22). docs/reference/node-provisioning.md.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: node:role:add\n  %% kind: node.role_add\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane M icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>gets the role\n  participant M as Fleet Nodes<br/>role participants\n  %% path: main\n  Note over CLI: Resolve command inputs\n  alt local input refused\n    CLI--xCLI: Invalid input<br/>No admission request\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - node.id_invalid refuses a non-positive Node ID or a missing noninteractive Node.\n    %%> - node_role.role_required refuses an omitted noninteractive role.\n    %%> - node_role.storage_process_required refuses invalid, missing noninteractive or unavailable Analytics storage selectors.\n    %%> - validation.failed refuses Analytics-only options for another role.\n  else local input available\n  CLI->>GW: node:role:add NODE ROLE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit node:role:add NODE ROLE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<node> <role> [--converge] [--postgres-process=ID] [--clickhouse-process=ID] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  Note over GW: Check recorded state\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - apply the [Node family's shared authority rule](/cli/node#authority)\n  %%> - use recorded Node eligibility, role compatibility, singleton ownership and Cluster membership; admission contacts no Node\n  %%> - `database` needs a recorded WireGuard address; Analytics needs its two valid storage Processes\n  alt unknown Node\n    %% path: node-not-found\n    GW--xCLI: http.404\n  else caller refused\n    %% path: access-refused\n    GW--xCLI: Caller refused\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - peer.identity_unknown or node_access.required; no assignment or Operation changes.\n  else request key conflicts\n    GW--xCLI: Request key conflict\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - operation.idempotency_conflict refuses a key with another target or input, before admission.\n  else database address missing\n    %% path: database-address-missing\n    GW--xCLI: Database address missing<br/>database.wireguard_ip_missing\n  else Node not eligible\n    %% path: node-ineligible\n    GW--xCLI: operation.kind_unavailable\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the Node is not active Linux or lacks recorded SSH management\n  else role not admissible\n    %% path: role-refused\n    GW--xCLI: validation.failed\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the role is unknown or protected, conflicts with an assigned role, has another singleton holder, or violates the ingress Cluster rule\n    %%> - an existing assignment needs explicit convergence, except an active ingress assignment\n    %%> - Analytics storage options are refused for another role\n  else Process preparation refused\n    %% path: analytics-storage-refused\n    GW--xCLI: Process refused<br/>No committed admission change\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - analytics.settings_missing, analytics.postgres_process_missing or analytics.clickhouse_process_missing refuses missing settings or records.\n    %%> - analytics.process_not_node, analytics.process_not_docker or analytics.process_not_database_node refuses the owner, runtime or placement.\n    %%> - analytics.postgres_unsupported, analytics.clickhouse_unsupported or analytics.version_invalid refuses the image or version.\n    %%> - analytics.storage_port_not_wireguard_bound, analytics.storage_credentials_missing or analytics.storage_port_missing refuses storage publication or credentials.\n    %%> - process.wireguard_ip_missing or process.name_taken refuses runtime preparation.\n    %%> - process.node_service_ownership_conflict names every dependent Instance before owner changes. Admission failures roll back assignment and Operation writes.\n  else storage path refused\n    %% path: storage-path-refused\n    GW--xCLI: Storage path refused\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - node.settings_path_invalid, node.settings_path_protected or node.settings_path_managed refuses an invalid, protected or managed-checkout storage path before changes.\n  else Node held\n    %% path: target-busy\n    GW--xCLI: operation.target_busy\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - No Operation or assignment change is committed.\n  else convergence requested\n    %% path: converge-requested\n    opt --converge\n      %% path: converge-mode\n      alt assignment not eligible\n        %% path: converge-refused\n        GW--xCLI: validation.failed\n        %%> When:\n        %%> - the assignment is absent, fresh provisioning, removing, or failed outside a `converge:` step\n      else convergence admitted\n        %% path: converge\n        Note over GW: Claim convergence\n        %%> Requirements:\n        %%> - accept an active role, a `converge:` failure, or provisioning at least 11 minutes old with no Operation holding the Node\n        %%> Why:\n        %%> - Freeze intent while the Node lock prevents competing role changes.\n        GW->>SW: Start convergence run<br/>node.role_converge\n        %%> Next:\n        %%> - execute the [node.role_converge chart](https://github.com/hardimpactdev/orbit/blob/main/docs/cli/node/role-converge.mmd), not the add-only phases below\n        GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID\n        alt convergence succeeds\n          %% path: converge-succeeded\n          SW-->>GW: Convergence succeeded<br/>terminal event\n          %%> When:\n          %%> - Clear earlier failure details only after the terminal result proves convergence.\n        else convergence fails\n          %% path: converge-failed\n          SW--xGW: Convergence failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n          %%> On failure:\n          %%> - The terminal reader records failure; an already-active assignment stays active.\n        end\n        loop every 2 s until terminal\n          CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n          GW-->>CLI: status + result\n        end\n      end\n    end\n  else addition admitted\n    %% path: add\n    Note over GW: Claim the role and Node\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - Freeze public intent and vault references under the Node lock before dispatch.\n    %%> Digests:\n    %%> - the run carries the Node's phase digest record and compares by the one rule (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#skip-a-phase-whose-input-did-not-change); a new role changes the input, so a first add runs every phase; a repeated add of an `active` `ingress` role skips its unchanged phases as a convergence does\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-role-add\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID\n    SW->>N: Apply prerequisites<br/>Ansible phase: prerequisites\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - An unsupported Ubuntu release refuses before host changes.\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - Bootstrap Swamp's pinned Ansible and collections before its remaining phases.\n    %% path: tools\n    SW->>N: Read Tool Manager accounts<br/>Ansible phase: tools-read\n    SW->>SW: Compose tools resources\n    SW->>SW: Write tool variables\n    SW->>N: Set up Tool Managers<br/>Ansible phase: tools\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - Rerun manager commands only when their admitted pin or lock changes.\n    SW->>N: Apply the role<br/>Ansible phase: node\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - For Swamp, repair an existing compile cache before service startup and checks.\n    %%> On an Instance fleet Node (`nested-app-dev` under the nested Gateway's fleet marker, with `app-dev`):\n    %%> - the egress proxy takes the fleet's egress proxy, from `HTTPS_PROXY` in the nested Gateway's `/etc/environment`, as its only parent and never goes direct; its table admits exactly that address and port\n    %%> - a nested Gateway that reads no such proxy refuses `instance.fleet_egress_unavailable` (409) when it renders the intent, before any change\n    %%> On a CI-only Node (`ci-runner`, CI places, no `app-dev`):\n    %%> - the same phase runs the Instance container branch of `app-dev` from the same `instances` intent, with its nested part: the projects `orbit-instances` and `orbit-instances-nested`, the bridge, the ACLs, the egress proxy, the shared folders, both images; it checks the CI Node's subordinate-ID demand first\n    %%> - the intent leaves out the own-site rules, the development port redirect and the wake of frozen containers: the Node runs no Caddy\n    %%> - the runner side (`orbit-ci`, `orbitci0`) stays as it is; a bridge range that overlaps it refuses `instance.host_unavailable` before any change\n    %%> On a Node that offers proof places (`ci-runner`, CI places, with or without `app-dev`), other than the proof default's Node:\n    %%> - the same phase trusts root's Incus client certificate of the default's Node for `orbit-instances-nested` only (`orbit-proof-seed`); the firewall phase inserts two UFW rules in front of the WireGuard member trust (allow port 8444 from that Node, deny it from any other), and only then sets the Incus listener on the WireGuard address, port 8444 (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#the-seed-listener)\n    %%> - on any other Node with `ci-runner` it removes that trust, that listener and both rules\n    opt new Analytics assignment\n      %% path: analytics\n      SW->>M: Open Analytics storage<br/>Ansible phase: storage-firewall\n      SW->>M: Apply Analytics runtime<br/>Ansible phase: analytics\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - Initialize storage only for a new Analytics assignment.\n      SW->>M: Wait for Analytics<br/>Ansible phase: readiness\n      %%> On failure:\n      %%> - A later publication failure retains and records a completed Analytics runtime.\n    end\n    opt swamp role\n      %% path: swamp\n      SW->>N: Install Swamp<br/>Ansible phase: swamp\n      %%> Why:\n      %%> - Publish beside the running binary and drain workers one at a time so active work can finish.\n      %%> - Keep the selected and previous binary plus mapped versions, install hourly journal, apt and Git maintenance, and clear downloaded bundles only at a drained worker start.\n    end\n    opt role needs certificates\n      %% path: certificates\n      SW->>GW: Sign role certificates<br/>native child workflow\n    end\n    SW->>M: Read role publications<br/>Ansible phase: publication-read\n    SW->>SW: Compose publications\n    SW->>SW: Write publication vars\n    opt role changes PHP pools\n      %% path: php-pools\n      SW->>M: Publish PHP pools<br/>Ansible phase: php\n    end\n    SW->>M: Publish role sites<br/>Ansible phase: publication\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - Invalid Caddy configuration refuses the reload.\n    SW->>M: Open the firewall<br/>Ansible phase: firewall\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - Place specific allows before isolation denies and broader peer trust; preserve unrelated rules.\n    opt role changes DNS\n      %% path: dns\n      SW->>M: Publish private DNS<br/>Ansible phase: dns\n      %%> Why:\n      %%> - publish only after the site serves and its firewall is open\n    end\n    opt role joins Metrics\n      %% path: metrics\n      SW->>N: Add the exporters<br/>Ansible phase: exporters\n      SW->>M: Publish Prometheus<br/>Ansible phase: prometheus\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - Publish membership only after the target's exporters are ready.\n    end\n    SW->>M: Check the role<br/>Ansible phase: check\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - Require end-state evidence before marking a new assignment active.\n    alt every phase passes\n      %% path: succeeded\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - Clear earlier failure details only after the terminal result proves the role is ready.\n      %%> Digests:\n      %%> - record this run's phase digests on the Node; the first `--converge` after the add has another kind, so it runs every phase, and its repeat can skip\n      opt Node offers proof places\n        Note over GW: Admit the seed copy<br/>instance.seed_copy\n        %%> When:\n        %%> - the Node offers proof places (`ci-runner`, CI places, with or without `app-dev`), is not the proof default's Node, and its recorded seed is not the default's current seed, and no seed copy is open; an open copy admits it when it ends\n        %%> Why:\n        %%> - a new CI Node gets its seed now, not at the next release (docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-seed-on-each-ci-node)\n        %%> Result:\n        %%> - the role is active either way; until the copy passes, the Node offers no proof places\n      end\n    else a phase fails\n      %% path: failed\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      %%> On failure:\n      %%> - a first add stays recorded as failed at `converge:STEP`; an already-active ingress assignment stays active\n      %%> - retain the failed step and error code on the Operation and the failed assignment\n      %%> - finished host changes remain, with no rollback; recover with `node:role:add NODE ROLE --converge`\n    end\n    opt Analytics init containers\n      %% path: analytics-cleanup\n      SW->>M: Remove init containers<br/>Ansible phase: cleanup\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - condition: the OS check passed and the Analytics phases completed or were skipped; best-effort cleanup on the admitted runtime Node\n      %%> - a run that fails at the OS check changed nothing on the Node, so this cleanup does not run\n    end\n    SW->>SW: Remove the run folder<br/>condition: completed\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - Wait for role phases and init-container cleanup, then remove private intent and credential files on either result.\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    end\n  end\n  end\n"
  },
  "node/role-converge": {
   "kind": "node.role_converge",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0243/0244 task, one Swamp run, Ansible phases for idempotent Node state (rule 22). docs/reference/node-provisioning.md.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: node:role:add\n  %% kind: node.role_converge\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane M icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>has the role\n  participant M as Metrics Node<br/>Prometheus\n  CLI->>GW: node:role:add NODE ROLE<br/>--converge [--full]\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit node:role:add NODE ROLE --converge`\n  %%> - syntax: `<node> <role> --converge [--full] [--json]`\n  %%> - `--full`: compare with no digest record, so every phase runs\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Node and a role it already has; without `--converge` the same call is refused (`validation.failed`), except an `active` `ingress` role, which runs `node.role_add`\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - applies the role's packages, files, units and publications again and repairs drift\n  %%> - never changes an Instance, a Process, a Schedule or the assignment itself\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, claim the Node\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the same Node checks as for an add, and the role is `active`, `failed` at a `converge:` step, or `provisioning` for more than 11 minutes with no Operation holding the Node's role lock\n  %%> - Node convergence, role addition or convergence, enrollment and agent rekey share one Node admission lock (`operation.target_busy`)\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - an `active` role keeps its status; a `failed` or stale role is marked `provisioning` for the run\n  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent and the Node's phase digest record, read before the admission clears it (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#skip-a-phase-whose-input-did-not-change)\n  %%> - with `--full`, or while the Node records a failed agent pin, the task holds no record\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-role-add\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task ID, at once\n  Note over SW: Write input<br/>compare phase digests\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - after it writes every file the phases read, it computes each phase digest by the one rule of node:converge and compares it with the recorded digest\n  %%> - `prerequisites`, `tools`, `node` and `firewall` skip when their digest is equal: they do not connect to the Node\n  %%> - every other step and the end check always run, so the check still reads drift; Publish role sites always runs, because it can change other Nodes\n  SW->>N: Require supported Ubuntu<br/>SSH argv · native assert\n  opt swamp role\n    SW->>N: Install Ansible<br/>native bootstrap\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the Swamp role provides the worker's Ansible; it installs the pinned version and collections first\n  end\n  SW->>N: Apply prerequisites<br/>Ansible phase: prerequisites\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the role's repositories and packages, at the pins of the release\n  %%> - a Node of a task VM's nested fleet reads its apt packages through the VM's package cache (`apt_proxy` in the intent, else the archive)\n  opt role needs Tool Managers\n    SW->>N: Set up Tool Managers<br/>native child workflow\n  end\n  SW->>N: Apply the role<br/>Ansible phase: node\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the role's files, units, ports and storage directory owner and mode; protected paths are refused before any host work\n  %%> On an Instance fleet Node (`nested-app-dev` under the nested Gateway's fleet marker, with `app-dev`):\n  %%> - the egress proxy takes the fleet's egress proxy, from `HTTPS_PROXY` in the nested Gateway's `/etc/environment`, as its only parent and never goes direct; its table admits exactly that address and port\n  %%> - a nested Gateway that reads no such proxy refuses `instance.fleet_egress_unavailable` (409) when it renders the intent, before any change\n  %%> On a CI-only Node (`ci-runner`, CI places, no `app-dev`):\n  %%> - the same phase runs the Instance container branch of `app-dev` from the same `instances` intent, with its nested part: the projects `orbit-instances` and `orbit-instances-nested`, the bridge, the ACLs, the egress proxy, the shared folders, both images; it checks the CI Node's subordinate-ID demand first\n  %%> - the intent leaves out the own-site rules, the development port redirect and the wake of frozen containers: the Node runs no Caddy\n  %%> - the runner side (`orbit-ci`, `orbitci0`) stays as it is; a bridge range that overlaps it refuses `instance.host_unavailable` before any change\n  %%> On a Node that offers proof places (`ci-runner`, CI places, with or without `app-dev`), other than the proof default's Node:\n  %%> - the same phase trusts root's Incus client certificate of the default's Node for `orbit-instances-nested` only (`orbit-proof-seed`); the firewall phase inserts two UFW rules in front of the WireGuard member trust (allow port 8444 from that Node, deny it from any other), and only then sets the Incus listener on the WireGuard address, port 8444 (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#the-seed-listener)\n  %%> - on any other Node with `ci-runner` it removes that trust, that listener and both rules\n  opt Analytics uses local storage\n    SW->>N: Open Analytics storage<br/>Ansible phase: storage-firewall\n    %%> Not included:\n    %%> - a converge creates no Process and runs no database migration\n  end\n  opt swamp role\n    SW->>N: Install Swamp<br/>native steps\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - installs the binary beside the running one, links it, then applies the identity, the serve unit and the worker units\n    %%> - retain the selected and previous binary plus process-mapped versions; install hourly native journal, apt and Git maintenance\n    %%> - a worker removes downloaded bundles only at its next start, after its previous process drained; machine identity stays\n    SW->>N: Enable renewal<br/>systemd timer\n    %%> Order:\n    %%> - the Ansible node phase installs the renewal program and rendered units first\n    %%> - start the timer after all three permanent workers are started; renew the oldest due worker only while its two peers accept dispatches\n    %%> - active dispatches finish during each drain; two workers keep accepting work\n    %%> - all three workers stay running; only one worker renews per round, after its two peers are accepting\n    %%> - a failed or inactive worker can reset its failure limit and start while its two peers serve\n    %%> Why not in place:\n    %%> - replacing the running binary breaks every dispatch, so the running serve and workers keep working until they restart\n  end\n  opt role needs certificates\n    SW->>GW: Sign role certificates<br/>native child workflow\n    %%> Why this calls the Gateway:\n    %%> - certificate signing is the one call a step makes to the Gateway\n  end\n  opt role changes PHP pools\n    SW->>N: Publish PHP pools<br/>native delta publication\n  end\n  SW->>N: Publish role sites<br/>Ansible phase: publication\n  SW->>N: Open the firewall<br/>Ansible phase: firewall\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - adds the role's rules, each specific allow before its isolation deny and the broader peer trust rule; unrelated rules stay\n  opt role changes DNS\n    SW->>N: Publish private DNS<br/>native delta publication\n    %%> Why last:\n    %%> - a name is reachable only after the site serves and the firewall is open\n  end\n  opt role joins Metrics\n    SW->>N: Add the exporters<br/>Ansible phase: exporters\n    SW->>M: Publish Prometheus<br/>Ansible phase: prometheus\n  end\n  SW->>N: Check the role<br/>Ansible phase: check\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the one end check: the role's services, ports, sites and rules are in place\n  alt every step passes\n    N-->>SW: role services ready\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record the role active<br/>and the phase digests\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the assignment is `active` and an earlier failed step is cleared; a second convergence skips its unchanged phases\n    %%> - the Node's digest record is the digests of this run; a failed or interrupted run leaves no record\n    opt Node offers proof places\n      Note over GW: Admit the seed copy<br/>instance.seed_copy\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - the Node offers proof places (`ci-runner`, CI places, with or without `app-dev`), is not the proof default's Node, and its recorded seed is not the default's current seed, and no seed copy is open; an open copy admits it when it ends\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the role stays active either way; until the copy passes, the Node offers no proof places (docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-seed-on-each-ci-node)\n    end\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or no match\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    Note over GW: Keep the role as it was\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - an `active` role stays `active`, so a role such as `gateway` is never taken out of service by a failed run\n    %%> - a role that was `failed` or stale is marked `failed` again, with the new step's code\n    %%> - running services keep their last valid configuration, and Caddy never reloads one that fails validation\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - finished host changes stay; there is no rollback\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again; the failed step and error code stay on the Operation and `doctor` shows the drift\n  end\n  opt run completed or failed\n    SW->>SW: Remove the run folder<br/>private run files\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the intent, vault values and role configuration do not outlive the run, whatever its result\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "node/role-list": {
   "kind": "node.role_list",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: node:role:list reads recorded Gateway state only. No request-time Node or external-system contact.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: node:role:list\n  %% kind: node.role_list\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>caller\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>recorded state\n  CLI->>GW: node:role:list NODE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit node:role:list NODE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<node> [--json]`\n  Note over GW: Resolve and authorize NODE\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - Node access follows the [shared Node authority rule](/cli/node#authority).\n  alt unknown Node\n    %% path: node-not-found\n    GW--xCLI: Refused<br/>http.404\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - no assignments are returned\n  else caller refused by shared authority rule\n    %% path: access-refused\n    GW--xCLI: Refused<br/>node_access.required\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - no assignments are returned\n  else known Node and authorized caller\n    %% path: main\n    Note over GW: Read role assignments<br/>shared Node role projection\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - includes every assigned role and its recorded lifecycle state, with the same role, status, failed_step and error_code values as node:list and node:show\n    %%> - reads records only; starts no Operation and contacts no Node, Grafana or other system\n    alt no role assignments recorded\n      %% path: no-roles\n      GW-->>CLI: Empty role list\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - an empty assignment list is a successful result, not missing agent data\n    else Node unreachable or agent facts missing or stale\n      %% path: recorded-only\n      GW-->>CLI: Recorded role assignments\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - assignments do not depend on agent liveness or fact freshness; no refresh or wait is needed\n    else assignments recorded\n      %% path: roles\n      GW-->>CLI: Role assignments<br/>state and failure details\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - includes active, provisioning, failed and removing assignments\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "node/role-relocate": {
   "kind": "node.role_relocate",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0243/0244 task, one Swamp run, Ansible phases for idempotent Node state (rule 22), swamp refused at admission. docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#native-role-relocation.\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: node:role:relocate\n  %% kind: node.role_relocate\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane S icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>gets the role\n  participant S as Source Node<br/>gives up the role\n  CLI->>GW: node:role:relocate<br/>NODE ROLE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit node:role:relocate NODE ROLE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<node> <role> [--from=NODE] [--force] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the target Node and the role: `gateway`, `websocket`, `metrics` or `ingress`\n  %%> - `--from` names the source; without it, the current holder is the source (for `ingress`, the Ingress of the target's Cluster)\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  opt role is swamp\n    GW--xCLI: 422 swamp role held<br/>node.role_relocate_swamp_held\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - serve on the Swamp Node runs every Swamp run, so a run cannot move it; nothing is recorded\n  end\n  Note over GW: Validate, move the role\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the Gateway Node\n  %%> - the target is active, has recorded SSH management and holds no conflicting role (`node.role_conflict`); `ingress` never goes to the Gateway Node\n  %%> - for `ingress`, the target is a member of the Cluster whose Ingress moves; the move never changes membership\n  %%> - for `ingress`, the target is not the Router and runs no target of a public Route (`validation.failed`, reason `target_is_router` or `target_serves_public_route`): its public site would replace the private hop before Let's Encrypt issues\n  %%> - for `ingress`, when the source runs a target of a public Route, the Router is the source too (`validation.failed`, reason `source_serves_public_route`): a separate Router would trust only the Orbit certificate while the source still answers with Let's Encrypt\n  %%> - the role is relocatable, assigned and `active` (or `failed` or `provisioning` in a retry), and `--from` owns the move\n  %%> - consent: `--force`, or the CLI prompt, asked only after these checks pass\n  %%> - one add, converge, remove or relocate Operation per target Node (`operation.target_busy`)\n  %%> - for `ingress`, each live public Process Route as at its activation: the Process Node's recorded firewall admits the target (`route.upstream_firewall_mismatch`) and the Process publishes one listener on the address the target selects (`route.upstream_unresolved`), which is the listener the Route recorded (`route.upstream_listener_changed`)\n  %%> - for `ingress`, the Route publication hold on every Node of the Cluster is free (`route.publication_held`); the move keeps it until it ends, so a Route write started meanwhile waits and no captured Caddyfile goes stale\n  %%> Builds:\n  %%> - for `websocket`, the Reverb `APP_KEY` copied only when the target lacks one; for `metrics`, the role credentials the target lacks, without overwriting any; for `gateway`, the access grants to the `vpn` and `metrics` Nodes\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the assignment moves to the target and stays `active`, so the role is never out of service\n  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-role-relocate\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task ID, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  opt role is metrics\n    SW->>N: Apply the Metrics role<br/>child operation-metrics-enable\n  end\n  opt role is ingress\n    SW->>N: Probe the Process backends<br/>ssh exec: curl --head\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the activation probe from the target: an HTTP HEAD request to each public Process listener; any HTTP answer passes, a refused or timed-out connection fails the move\n    SW->>N: Apply the Ingress role<br/>child operation-node-role-add\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the `node:role:add --converge` intent without the Metrics fleet, private DNS or the source's Caddyfile: Caddy and the Orbit root, the target's Caddyfile with every public site (Let's Encrypt, `tls force_automate`), a separate Router's Caddyfile, and the Ingress rules while a public Route is live\n    %%> - the target's sites reach the Router over HTTPS with the system bundle, which holds the Orbit root and the public roots, so the hop works while the source still answers with its Let's Encrypt certificate\n    %%> - public Routes stay public; the source keeps serving until its removal\n  end\n  opt role is websocket\n    SW->>N: Build the Reverb release<br/>Ansible phase: release\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - clones and checks out the Reverb ref as the Node's user, installs its packages, writes its private `.env` and unit, and switches the release link\n    %%> - the target needs Git, PHP and Composer\n    SW->>N: Start Reverb<br/>Ansible phase: service\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - enables and restarts the unit, then checks that Reverb answers on its port\n  end\n  opt role is gateway\n    SW->>N: Add the DNS route<br/>Ansible phase: dns-route\n    %%> Best effort:\n    %%> - a failed route on the target reports `follow_up` and does not stop the move\n  end\n  opt role needs a certificate\n    SW->>N: Publish the certificate<br/>child role-certificate-publish\n  end\n  opt role is websocket or gateway\n    SW->>N: Open the role ports<br/>Ansible phase: firewall\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - opens the role's ports on the target and closes the retired ones\n  end\n  SW->>N: Publish the Caddy sites<br/>child operation-route-caddy\n  %%> Order:\n  %%> - the target serves before anything leaves the source; the source's sites are not published again\n  opt role is ingress\n    SW->>N: Probe the Router hop<br/>ssh exec: curl --head https\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - for each public app Route, the target asks the Router address its site uses, over HTTPS with the system bundle; any HTTP answer passes, a refused connection or a bad certificate fails the move\n    SW->>N: Check public certs<br/>ssh exec: curl https\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the target asks itself for every public name over HTTPS and verifies the certificate with the system roots\n    %%> Bounds:\n    %%> - curl's own retry: 20 tries, 15 s apart, at most 300 s; Let's Encrypt issues only once public DNS names the target\n    %%> - an answer of 500 or more after the last try fails the move (`Check the certificate answers`)\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - private DNS switches and the source withdraws only after the target serves every public name\n  end\n  SW->>N: Publish the role DNS<br/>child route-dns-publish\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - writes each DNS Node's records and catalog, the source's too when it is one; for `ingress`, names only the Ingress serves now answer with the target\n  %%> - also withdraws a live answer from the snapshot's `retired` list, such as the Router LAN answer earlier releases gave a public Process name\n  opt websocket or ingress moved\n    SW->>SW: Let DNS answers expire<br/>child route-dns-grace\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - clients that still hold the old answer keep working until it expires\n  end\n  SW->>S: Remove from the source<br/>child node-role-remove\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - removes the role's runtime and publications from the source and forgets its settings there; for `gateway` it does not stop Caddy or PHP-FPM, so clients on the old address keep working\n  %%> - for `metrics` it keeps the fleet exporters and the `metrics.orbit` name and certificate, which the moved role uses\n  %%> - for `ingress` it rebuilds the source's Caddyfile without the public sites and closes `orbit:ingress-http` and `orbit:ingress-https`; public DNS is the operator's, pointed at the target when the run starts; the grace covers private DNS only\n  SW->>N: Check the role<br/>Ansible phase: check\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the one end check: Reverb answers, the ports, sites and DNS are in place on the target\n  alt every step passes\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record the role active\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the role is `active` on the target, and the task succeeds\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - relocation does not reconcile Metrics; `follow_up` names the `node:role:add METRICS-NODE metrics --converge` command when a Metrics role exists and `websocket`, `gateway` or `ingress` moved\n  else a step fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    Note over GW: Keep the role active\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the role already belongs to the target and stays `active`; a demoted `gateway` role would lock the Gateway out of its own access checks\n    %%> - the task fails with the step's own code, and the failed step stays on the Operation: `converge:STEP`, or `remove:Remove from the source` when the source removal failed\n    %%> - for `ingress`, a failed probe, child, Caddy or certificate step, before `Publish the role DNS` ran, gives the role back to the source: the assignment names the source again and stays `active`, and the result has `source_kept: true`; a `--from` retry never does, because an earlier run may have switched private DNS\n    %%> - for `ingress`, a failed source removal keeps the target `active`, so it keeps its public sites; the result has `source_cleanup_failed: true`\n    %%> Who serves:\n    %%> - after a target step failure the source still serves, because DNS moves after the target steps and the source keeps its sites; after a source removal failure the target serves\n    %%> On the Nodes:\n    %%> - finished steps stay; there is no rollback\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - fix the cause and run the same command with `--from`; a cancelled or interrupted run leaves the claim `provisioning`, and the retry takes it over\n  end\n  opt run completed or failed\n    SW->>SW: Remove the run folder<br/>private run files\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "node/role-remove": {
   "kind": "node.role_remove",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run, Ansible phases for idempotent Node state (rule 22). docs/reference/node-provisioning.md.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: node:role:remove\n  %% kind: node.role_remove\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane P icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  participant P as Fleet Nodes<br/>hub · DNS\n  CLI->>GW: node:role:remove<br/>NODE ROLE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit node:role:remove NODE ROLE`\n  %%> - syntax: `<node> <role> [--force] [--purge-data] [--offline] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Node and the role to remove\n  %%> - `--force` is consent; without a terminal, `--purge-data` and `--offline` need it\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target Node\n  %%> - the target is an active, managed Linux Node, and the role is not `vpn` or `swamp`\n  %%> - `router` only as a stale assignment, under the Cluster's Router lock: not the active Router, `failed` (or `provisioning`), no replacement step on any Router row of the Cluster, and no open Router Operation of the Cluster; else `node_role.router_protected`\n  %%> - the role is `active` or `failed`; a `provisioning` or `removing` role is refused until its claim is stale (`node_role.claim_busy`), except a stale `router` above\n  %%> - while the stale `router` removal runs, `cluster:router:set` and `cluster:router:unset` of its Cluster are refused (`cluster.router_transition_conflict`)\n  %%> - `app-dev` and `app-prod` have no Route targets, `ingress` no public Routes, and `analytics` no tracking hosts\n  %%> - one add, converge or remove Operation per Node (`operation.target_busy`), on both paths below; a second removal after the first finished finds no such role (`http.404`)\n  %%> - until the `--offline` flow below is built, `--offline` is refused on both paths (`operation.kind_unavailable`)\n  %%> Then:\n  %%> - the Gateway reads the recorded state of the assignment and picks one of the two paths below\n  %%> - on both paths the admission clears the Node's phase digest record, so the next converge or role Operation runs every phase (docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#skip-a-phase-whose-input-did-not-change)\n  alt the role never touched the Node\n    Note over GW: Delete the role\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - every run for the assignment, from its first add on, failed at `Read the OS release` or `Require supported Ubuntu`\n    %%> - one run that ended any other way keeps the assignment on the withdrawal below for good\n    %%> - so does an assignment from `node:add`, a relocation or Metrics, or from before this record\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the assignment and its settings are deleted; no run starts and nothing else is checked on the Node\n    %%> - `--purge-data` is accepted and has no effect\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID, already succeeded\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the task ID, at once; the task has ended `succeeded`\n  else the role may have changed the Node\n    Note over GW: Mark removing\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the assignment is recorded as `removing`, and a task is recorded with the full intent\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>node-role-remove\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the task ID, at once\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n    SW->>N: Reach the Node<br/>SSH argv\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - probes the Node with its approved identity\n    %%> - an unreachable Node without `--offline` fails the run here (`node.removal_incomplete`); a reachable Node gets ordinary cleanup even with `--offline`\n    opt CI role is being removed\n      SW->>N: Require drained CI<br/>SSH argv · native assert\n      %%> Why:\n      %%> - the run fails here, `node.removal_incomplete`, while CI guests still run on the Node, and the rerun after they are drained succeeds\n    end\n    opt the last role leaves\n      SW->>N: Keep public SSH access<br/>SSH argv\n      %%> Why first:\n      %%> - removing the last role restores public recovery SSH before anything else is torn down, so the Node stays reachable\n    end\n    SW->>P: Withdraw from the fleet<br/>PHP · sites · DNS · Metrics\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - withdraws the role's PHP pools, Caddy sites, private DNS records, Metrics exporter entries and role certificates on the other Nodes\n    %%> - an `--offline` removal of an unreachable Node does only the work on other Nodes\n    opt Node reachable\n      SW->>N: Remove the role<br/>Ansible phase: remove\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - on app-dev, withdraws its managed tmpfiles rule so later boots do not recreate the wake marker directory\n      %%> - stops the role's services and removes its files, configuration, containers, firewall rules and certificates\n      %%> - for a stale `router`: the Router LAN rules and the Router certificate folders of the Cluster's Routes; Caddy is rebuilt and reloaded, never stopped\n      %%> Retained:\n      %%> - role data, CI resources, release data and container volumes, unless `--purge-data`\n      opt --purge-data\n        SW->>N: Remove role data<br/>Ansible phase: purge\n        %%> What it does:\n        %%> - for `metrics`, the Prometheus and Grafana volumes and the stored credentials; for `websocket`, the Reverb checkout; for `ci-runner`, the Incus project, network and ACL\n      end\n      SW->>N: Check the removal<br/>Ansible phase: check\n      %%> Why:\n      %%> - the one end check: the removed files, directories and containers are gone\n    end\n    alt every step passes\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n      %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n      Note over GW: Delete the role\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the assignment is deleted\n    else a step fails\n      N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or mismatch\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      Note over GW: Mark the role failed\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the assignment stays, `failed` at `remove:STEP`, with the step's own error code\n      %%> - a failed public SSH restore leaves the Node reachable, and the rerun restores it\n      %%> On the Node:\n      %%> - finished steps stay; there is no rollback\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same command again: each removal step is safe to repeat over what a failed convergence left\n    end\n    opt run completed or failed\n      SW->>SW: Remove the run folder<br/>private run files\n    end\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "node/show": {
   "kind": "node.show",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: node:show reads recorded Gateway state only (docs/cli/node/show.mdx).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: orbit node:show NODE\n  %% kind: node.show\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>recorded state\n  CLI->>GW: node:show NODE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - orbit node:show NODE\n  %%> - syntax: orbit node:show <node>\n  alt Node not found\n    %% path: unknown-node\n    GW--xCLI: Unknown Node<br/>http.404\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - no records change and no Operation starts\n  else Node exists\n    %% path: node-found\n    Note over GW: Check Node authority\n    %%> Requirements:\n    %%> - apply the shared NodeAccessAuthorizer rule linked from /cli/node\n    alt Caller refused\n      %% path: refused-caller\n      GW--xCLI: Access refused<br/>node_access.required\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - no Node detail is disclosed and no Operation starts\n    else Caller allowed\n      %% path: allowed\n      Note over GW: Read recorded detail\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - include the stored reboot and image refresh holds; read Node records, named relations and recorded agent state; contact no Node, Grafana or other external system\n      %%> - use the shared role projection of node:list, node:show and node:role:list\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - no records change and no Operation starts\n      opt Recorded values absent\n        %% path: missing-values\n        Note over GW: Keep missing values\n        %%> Result:\n        %%> - return the available detail without fetching missing facts\n        %%> - missing human display values use the CLI standard's empty-value rendering\n      end\n      alt No observing agent\n        %% path: no-agent\n        GW-->>CLI: Recorded Node<br/>agent: null\n        %%> Result:\n        %%> - the Node is not eligible for an observing agent\n      else Agent view unavailable\n        %% path: agent-unknown\n        GW-->>CLI: Recorded Node<br/>agent.state: unknown\n        %%> Result:\n        %%> - the Gateway's recorded subscriber health cannot establish liveness\n      else Agent never seen\n        %% path: agent-not-seen\n        GW-->>CLI: Recorded Node<br/>agent.state: not_seen\n        %%> Result:\n        %%> - there is no recorded last event\n      else Agent data stale\n        %% path: agent-offline\n        GW-->>CLI: Recorded Node<br/>agent.state: offline\n        %%> Result:\n        %%> - since identifies the last recorded event; stale data is not a refusal\n      else Agent data fresh\n        %% path: agent-online\n        GW-->>CLI: Recorded Node<br/>agent.state: online\n        %%> Result:\n        %%> - a recorded event arrived within the freshness window\n      end\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "node/update": {
   "kind": "node.update",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, apps.path is Node work, one node:update. docs/reference/node-settings.md.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: node:update\n  %% kind: node.update\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  %% path: main\n  CLI->>GW: node:update NODE<br/>--apps-path=PATH\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit node:update NODE --apps-path=PATH`\n  %%> - syntax: `<node> [--name=NAME] [--platform=PLATFORM] [--lan-ip=IP] [--reboot-hold=BOOL] [--primary-agent=BOOL] [--apps-path=PATH] [--ci-places=N] [--ci-proof-only|--no-ci-proof-only] [--no-ci] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Node, and at least one setting to change\n  %%> - an apps-folder change needs Node work; other changes are records\n  %%> - an empty value unsets a setting: `--lan-ip=` clears the LAN address, `--apps-path=` unsets the folder, `--no-ci` clears the CI places\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  %%> - the API-only boolean `nested_fleet` records the Node hosting switch; absence keeps its value, including a combined asynchronous apps update; the next `app-dev` converge prepares nested-fleet hosting and builds the nested-fleet image while it is on (node-settings reference)\n  Note over GW: Validate, lock the Node\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target Node\n  %%> - only a Node with an active `ci-runner` role takes CI places; `ci.places` from 0 to 64 is shared by runner jobs and proof copies, and `ci.proof_only` keeps them for proof copies; an omitted member keeps its value\n  %%> - every value passes the checks that the Node settings reference lists; a failed check changes nothing\n  %%> - the removed members `task_vms` and `image_refresh_hold` are unknown fields: the Gateway refuses them like any other unknown field\n  %%> - a LAN address is IPv4 and no other Node of the same network has it (`cluster.lan_ip_conflict`): every Node on the hub's private network, else the Node's Cluster, or every Node outside a Cluster\n  %%> - no other lifecycle change runs for the target; the Gateway holds the Node's name lock, and the new name's lock for a rename\n  %%> Decides:\n  %%> - whether the apps folder changes and needs Node work, so a run starts\n  alt CI places without the role\n    %% path: ci-role-missing\n    GW--xCLI: No active ci-runner role<br/>node.settings_ci_runner_required\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the request sets CI places on a Node whose `ci-runner` role is absent or not active\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - HTTP 422; nothing changes and no run starts\n    %%> - clearing the CI places with `--no-ci` is allowed on any Node\n  else settings invalid\n    %% path: settings-refused\n    GW--xCLI: Settings refused<br/>node.settings_invalid\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a field fails its check, or the body has an unknown member, such as the removed `task_vms` or `image_refresh_hold`\n    %%> - a first CI setting has no `places`\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - nothing changes and no run starts\n  else apps folder changes\n    %% path: node-work\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-update\n    %%> Inputs:\n    %%> - the new apps folder, and the managed user and group that must own it\n    %%> - the target's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n    %%> Not stored yet:\n    %%> - the settings stay unchanged until the run succeeds; a new name, platform, LAN address, reboot hold or CI places in the same request are saved at once\n    %%> Unsetting on a Node with an active `app-dev` role:\n    %%> - prepares the default folder, `<managed-user-home>/apps`\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the task ID, at once\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n    SW->>N: Create the apps folder<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/directory apply\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - only when an apps-folder change needs preparation: creates the folder and any missing folders above it\n    %%> - gives it the managed user and group and mode `0755`\n    %%> Not included:\n    %%> - existing Instances don't move; new development Instances use the new folder\n    SW->>N: Check owner and mode<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · stat\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - it is the one end check: a real folder of the managed user and group, mode 0755\n    alt folder is ready\n      %% path: folder-ready\n      N-->>SW: owner, group, mode\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n      %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n      Note over GW: Save the settings\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the apps folder is set\n      %%> - the task succeeds\n    else a step fails\n      %% path: run-failed\n      N--xSW: wrong owner or exit ≠ 0\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - the folder can't be created, a file is in the way, or the check doesn't match\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      Note over GW: Keep the old settings\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the settings stay unchanged and the task fails\n      %%> On the Node:\n      %%> - folders created before the failure stay; an empty folder is harmless and there is no rollback\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it is a new task\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    end\n  else only records change\n    %% path: records\n    Note over GW: Save the records\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - saves the name, platform, LAN address, reboot hold or CI places at once when no apps preparation is needed; no Node is contacted\n    %%> - `--reboot-hold=true` or `false` saves a boolean column; every Node read shows it; the next fleet reboot admission freezes it\n    %%> - a new LAN address takes effect where Orbit next renders it; a Node on the hub's private network moves to the hub's LAN endpoint at its next `node:converge`\n    %%> Skipped:\n    %%> - when every value equals the stored one, nothing is written and the result reports `Skipped — already set (node.unchanged)`\n    %%> - the skip reason is part of the successful result in human and machine output, not an error\n    GW-->>CLI: 200 OK<br/>the Node\n  end\n"
  },
  "node/wireguard-key": {
   "kind": "node.wireguard_key",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run, hub peers as one file per Node (orbit-peers.d), native steps because the work is under five steps (rule 22). docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#wireguard-hub.\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: node:wireguard-key\n  %% kind: node.wireguard_key\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane H icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant H as WireGuard hub<br/>vpn Node\n  CLI->>GW: node:wireguard-key<br/>NODE PUBLIC-KEY\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit node:wireguard-key NODE PUBLIC-KEY`\n  %%> - syntax: `<node> <public-key> [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Node and its WireGuard public key, as canonical base64 of 32 bytes\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, store the key\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target Node\n  %%> - the target does not hold the `gateway` role (`node.wireguard_key_gateway`)\n  %%> - the target is unmanaged: it lacks a WireGuard address or a pinned SSH host fingerprint, or both; with both it is managed (`node.wireguard_key_managed`)\n  %%> - the target has a WireGuard address (`node.wireguard_ip_required`)\n  %%> - the key is valid and belongs to no other Node and not to the hub (`node.wireguard_key_conflict`); the check and the assignment are atomic across concurrent assignments\n  %%> - no other lifecycle change runs for the Node's name (`node.provisioning_busy`)\n  %%> - no other `node:wireguard-key`, `node:converge`, add or remove runs on the Node or holds the hub (`operation.target_busy`), so an older run cannot write an old key back\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the key is stored, the change is broadcast, and a task is recorded with the full intent; the target is never contacted\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-wireguard-key\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task ID, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>H: Write the peer file<br/>cfgmgmt file\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - writes this peer's file in the hub's peer folder with the new key; the other peers' files stay\n  SW->>H: Apply the peer<br/>SSH argv · wg set\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - removes the previous key from the live hub and adds the new one, so the old key is not left listed\n  %%> - sending the same key again repeats these steps, so a retry repairs a failed one\n  SW->>H: Check the hub<br/>native assert\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the one end check: the live hub lists the peer with the new key and its allowed address, and not the previous key\n  alt hub lists the peer\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record result\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the task succeeds\n  else a step fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    Note over GW: Keep the stored key\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the stored key stays and the task fails with the failed step\n    %%> On the hub:\n    %%> - after a failed write the old file stays; after a failed apply the new file is on disk and the live hub still has the old key; there is no rollback\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again after fixing the hub; a `node:converge` of the hub publishes the stored key too\n  end\n  opt run completed or failed\n    SW->>SW: Remove the run folder<br/>private run files\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "process/create": {
   "kind": "process.create",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS 4 + R1-R4 (process.stop shape: 209, @swamp/ssh, full intent as inputs). instance.create reuses this workflow for development Processes.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 1-22 (cfgmgmt file/systemd/docker_*, no shipped scripts, no report step).\n  %% Decided: Nick 2026-11-02, a Docker Process runs as one plain docker run through @swamp/ssh exec; pull with cfgmgmt docker_image, check with the docker_container check. \"Remove the retired unit\" belongs to process:update (rule 21).\n  %% Rule 23: 2026-11-03 required-only main path.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: process:create\n  %% kind: process.create\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: process:create queue<br/>--node=7 --command=…\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit process:create queue --node=7 --command=/usr/bin/php --command=artisan --command=queue:work`\n  %%> - syntax: `process:create <name> (--instance=ID-or-domain | --node=ID-or-name | --project=ID --for=production) [options]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Process name, its owner (an Instance, a Node, or a Project definition) and its command\n  %%> - without options: a `systemd` Process, restart policy `never`, installed and left stopped\n  %%> Optional:\n  %%> - `--start` starts it after it installs; see the `opt --start` blocks\n  %%> - `--preset=vp-dev` (with `--instance`) adds the Vite steps\n  %%> - `--runtime=docker` with `--image` runs it as a container instead of a unit; `--environment` (Docker only) adds the environment file; `--port` and `--volume` (Docker only) change only the container definition\n  %%> - `--working-directory`, `--user`, `--restart`, `--keep-alive`, `--no-restart-on-deploy` change only the rendered definition\n  %%> - `--idempotency-key` reuses a task start; `--json` returns JSON\n  %%> Not handled here:\n  %%> - a Project definition with `--for=production` is recorded only; it starts no run (R1)\n  %%> - development container definitions store supported command names and resolve them when rendering; capture adopts only a compatible unlinked same-name Process, keeping its id and desired state\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, record the task<br/>Process 418: pending · #5130\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the owner's Node\n  %%> - the name is free for this owner\n  %%> - the `--user` account exists on the Node, from the Node agent's recorded state\n  %%> - a Docker `--port` in 5173-5199 binds to an address other than every address or the Instance bridge address (`instance.dev_band_reserved`)\n  %%> - a production release directory is checked against the recorded releases, not on the Node\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - Process 418 recorded with its definition, status pending\n  %%> - the unit or container rendered; secret environment values pushed to the vault key `process-create-operation-5130`\n  %%> - task #5130 recorded with the full intent\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-process-create\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209); every step runs there\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - `kind`: process.create\n  %%> - the rendered unit, or the container definition without its environment values\n  %%> - the desired state and required mounts, from recorded state\n  %%> - vault key names for the environment, never the values\n  %%> - the Node's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  %%> Checked by the input schema:\n  %%> - no step reads or re-checks the intent\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5130\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  opt certificate account home is not recorded\n    SW->>N: Check the account<br/>ssh exec · getent\n  end\n  Note over SW: Prepare the definition<br/>public rendering only\n  %%> Resolves the observed home in certificate paths and shapes Docker argv; no Node effects.\n  opt --start\n    SW->>N: Check required mounts<br/>ssh exec · stat\n    %%> systemd only: each recorded required mount, or `/` when none is recorded.\n  end\n  opt --preset=vp-dev\n    SW->>N: Publish the dev route<br/>native Vite workflow\n    %%> Publishes the Vite environment before its unit starts.\n  end\n  opt Gateway scheduler or Horizon runs in a release container\n    SW->>N: Apply the Gateway Process<br/>store and apply the unit\n  end\n  SW->>N: Apply the unit<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd.apply\n  %%> Publishes unitFile, reloads on content change, and applies the admitted boot and active state: stopped by default.\n  opt previous activation is pending\n    SW->>N: Reload systemd<br/>ssh exec · daemon-reload\n    %%> Covers a prior publish that failed before daemon-reload.\n  end\n  SW->>N: Stop the unit<br/>ssh exec · stop\n  %%> The desired state is stopped by default; cancels activating units too, which pinned apply does not stop.\n  %%> With `--start` this step is skipped.\n  opt --start\n    opt changed unit is already running\n      SW->>N: Restart if changed<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd.restart\n      %%> Pinned apply leaves an already active process running after unitFile changes.\n    end\n  end\n  SW->>N: Check the unit<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd.check\n  opt --runtime=docker\n    SW->>N: Pull the image<br/>cfgmgmt/docker_image\n    %%> The Docker steps replace the systemd steps: the unit steps and the required-mount check are skipped.\n    opt --environment\n      SW->>N: Write the environment<br/>cfgmgmt/file · 0600\n      %%> Docker only: the env-file for the container; vault.get content, root owner; no secret in public run data.\n    end\n    SW->>N: Run the container<br/>ssh exec · docker run -d\n    %%> One argv command, env-file, workdir, image and exact command arguments.\n    SW->>N: Stop the container<br/>ssh exec · docker stop\n    %%> The detached run starts it; the desired state is stopped by default, so this step stops it.\n    %%> With `--start` this step is skipped and the container keeps running.\n    SW->>N: Check the container<br/>cfgmgmt/docker_container\n  end\n  opt --start\n    opt --preset=vp-dev\n      SW->>N: Check the listener<br/>ssh exec · ss\n      SW->>N: Mark it awake<br/>cfgmgmt/file\n    end\n  end\n  SW->>N: Verify it runs<br/>native assert\n  %%> Requires the admitted desired state (stopped, or active with `--start`), the boot policy, and any Vite listener.\n  alt desired state reached\n    N-->>SW: inactive (stopped)\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the Process has its desired state: stopped\n    %%> - with `--start`: active (running)\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event and reads the end check's output; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record result<br/>Process 418: active\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Process 418 becomes active with its desired state\n    %%> - task #5130 succeeds\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - SSH fails, a required mount is missing, or the state does not match\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the failing step ends the run with its own exit code; there is no report step\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its exit code, mapped to `error_code`\n    Note over GW: Mark the Process failed<br/>task failed · rerun converges\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Process 418 is marked failed with the error code\n    %%> - work done on the Node stays; nothing was copied, so nothing needs cleanup\n    %%> - a new `process:update` or create converges: each behavior skips state that is already right\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5130\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "process/destroy": {
   "kind": "process.destroy",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS 4 + R1-R4 (process.stop shape: 209, @swamp/ssh, full intent as inputs).\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 1-22 (cfgmgmt absent applies, mirror of process:create, no shipped scripts, no report step).\n  %% Rule 23: 2026-11-03 required-only main path.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: process:destroy\n  %% kind: process.destroy\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: process:destroy 417\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit process:destroy 417`\n  %%> - syntax: `process:destroy <process> [--project=ID] [--instance=ID] [--yes] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the numeric Process ID; the CLI asks for confirmation first\n  %%> - `--instance` names the Instance of a container Process; the Gateway refuses a container Process without it or of another Instance\n  %%> Optional:\n  %%> - `--yes` skips the confirmation; `--idempotency-key` reuses a task start; `--json` returns JSON; none adds a step\n  %%> Not handled here:\n  %%> - `--project` removes a Project definition only; it starts no run (R1)\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Check Process dependencies\n  alt attached Node service\n    GW--xCLI: Process required (409)<br/>No admission or runtime change\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - process.required_by_instance_node_service names every dependent Instance before recording an Operation; the Process, runtime and attachments remain.\n  else no attachment dependency\n  Note over GW: Validate, record the task<br/>Process 417: removing · #5130\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Process's Node\n  %%> - no Route points to the Process, else `process.has_routes`\n  %%> - the Process is not required by a Database server\n  %%> - a container Process: `--instance` names its own Instance, else `process.instance_mismatch`\n  %%> - a container Process that a current development definition owns refuses `operation.kind_unavailable`; the message names the definition destroy and the default's deploy\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - Process 417 marked removing; the record stays until the run succeeds\n  %%> - task #5130 recorded with the full intent\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-process-destroy\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209); every step runs there\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - `kind`: process.destroy\n  %%> - the unit or container name and runtime, and for Vite the Instance environment path\n  %%> - the Node's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  %%> Checked by the input schema:\n  %%> - no step reads or re-checks the intent\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5130\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  alt systemd\n    SW->>N: Disable and stop the unit<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd.apply\n    SW->>N: Stop the unit<br/>ssh exec · stop\n    SW->>N: Remove the unit<br/>cfgmgmt/file absent\n    SW->>N: Reload systemd<br/>ssh exec · daemon-reload\n    SW->>N: Check unit removal<br/>cfgmgmt/file.check\n    SW->>N: Check boot removal<br/>cfgmgmt/file.check\n    SW->>N: Read removed unit state<br/>ssh exec · systemctl show\n    SW->>N: Clear failed unit cache<br/>ssh exec · reset-failed\n    %%> Only the admitted unit is reset, after its file and boot link are absent, LoadState is not-found, and no PID, cgroup or pending job remains.\n    %%> A failed state alone never permits cleanup. Other remaining runtime or boot state fails the end check.\n    SW->>N: Check the unit<br/>ssh exec · systemctl show\n    %%> Reads the state again after cache cleanup; a running or loaded unit, boot policy, PID, cgroup or pending job refuses completion.\n  else Docker\n    SW->>N: Remove the container<br/>cfgmgmt/docker_container\n    SW->>N: Check container removal<br/>cfgmgmt/docker_container\n  else Instance container\n    SW->>N: Act in the container<br/>Ansible · tags process\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the child run `operation-process-container` acts through `incus exec` on the Node, on this Process's unit and folder only\n    %%> - disables and stops `orbit-process-417-<name>.service`, removes its unit file and `/etc/orbit/processes/417`, reloads systemd, and checks that the unit is not found\n    %%> - a stopped container fails the run with `process.target_inactive`; the record stays\n  end\n  opt Node Process\n    SW->>N: Remove the environment<br/>cfgmgmt/directory absent\n  end\n  opt Vite dev Process\n    SW->>N: Remove the dev environment<br/>cfgmgmt/file absent\n  end\n  SW->>N: Verify it is gone<br/>native assert\n  %%> Asserts absent unit/container and runtime and boot state; data volumes and the source environment file remain.\n  alt all removed\n    N-->>SW: unit gone\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - nothing of Process 417 is left on the Node\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event and reads the end check's output; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Delete the record<br/>Process 417 removed\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Process record is deleted\n    %%> - task #5130 succeeds\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - SSH fails, or something of Process 417 is still there after the removal\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the failing step ends the run with its own exit code; there is no report step\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its exit code, mapped to `error_code`\n    Note over GW: Keep the record, failed<br/>task failed · repeat destroy\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Process 417 keeps its record, marked with the error code\n    %%> - repeat `process:destroy` to converge\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5130\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n  end\n"
  },
  "process/logs": {
   "kind": "process.logs",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: process:logs\n  %% kind: process.logs\n  autonumber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP\n  participant GW as Gateway\n  participant A as Node observer\n  participant N as Node SSH\n  participant R as Reverb\n  CLI->>GW: process:logs <process>\n  alt bounded snapshot\n    Note over GW: Read bounded log\n    GW-->>CLI: Snapshot and request ID\n  else Instance container\n    GW->>N: Read container journal<br/>one bounded SSH call\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the Gateway builds fixed argv from the resolved Instance and Process; only the bounded lines count comes from the caller\n    %%> - the existing Node SSH account reads the journal with host-equivalent output bounds; the Gateway redacts it and records no Operation or workflow\n    GW-->>CLI: Snapshot and request ID\n  else --follow (CLI or web viewer)\n    CLI->>GW: Open viewer lease\n    Note over GW: Authorize source<br/>Store inactive lease\n    GW-->>CLI: Channel and signature\n    CLI->>R: Subscribe to channel\n    CLI->>GW: Activate viewer lease\n    GW->>A: log-streams.changed\n    A->>GW: Fetch active sources\n    loop new lines while lease is active\n      A->>R: Read-only log observations\n      R->>GW: Log batch\n      Note over GW: Redact log lines<br/>Keep loss markers\n      GW->>R: Viewer log.lines frames\n      R-->>CLI: New lines\n      CLI->>GW: Renew every 20 s\n    end\n    Note over CLI: Reopen on reconnect<br/>Read snapshots if offline\n    CLI->>GW: Close viewer lease\n  end\n"
  },
  "process/restart": {
   "kind": "process.restart",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS 4 + R1-R4 (process.stop shape: 209, @swamp/ssh, full intent as inputs).\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 1-22 (one cfgmgmt systemd apply, its check as the end check, no shipped scripts; rule 21: only this command's branches).\n  %% Rule 23: 2026-11-03 required-only main path.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: process:restart\n  %% kind: process.restart\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: process:restart 417\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit process:restart 417`\n  %%> - syntax: `process:restart <process> [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the numeric Process ID from `process:list`\n  %%> Optional:\n  %%> - `--idempotency-key` reuses a task start; `--json` returns JSON; neither adds a step\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, record the task<br/>Process 417: provisioning · #5130\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Node that runs Process 417\n  %%> - Process 417 exists and is not being removed; a container Process whose definition the Project removed refuses `process.removing` until the default's next deployment removes it\n  %%> - a production Process's release is checked against the recorded releases, not on the Node\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5130 recorded with the full intent: unit or container name, runtime, on-demand flag, Vite plan\n  %%> - Process 417 marked provisioning; its desired state does not change yet\n  %%> - nothing has happened on the Node yet\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-process-control\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209); every step runs there\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - `kind`: process.restart\n  %%> - the Process unit or container name, runtime and on-demand flag\n  %%> - the Node's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  %%> Checked by the input schema:\n  %%> - no step reads or re-checks the intent\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5130\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  opt Vite dev Process\n    SW->>N: Publish the dev route<br/>native Vite workflow\n  end\n  alt systemd\n    SW->>N: Set boot policy<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd.apply\n    SW->>N: Restart the Process<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd.restart\n    SW->>N: Check the unit<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd.check\n  else Docker\n    SW->>N: Restart the container<br/>ssh exec · docker restart\n    SW->>N: Check the container<br/>cfgmgmt/docker_container\n  else Instance container\n    SW->>N: Act in the container<br/>Ansible · tags process\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the Process is an `orbit` unit in its Instance container; the child run `operation-process-container` changes it through `incus exec` on the Node and checks its state\n    %%> - systemd starts use nonblocking jobs; readiness reads have 5-second bounds, with 10 retries at 1-second intervals\n    %%> - a stopped container fails the change; its next deployment starts it\n  end\n  opt Vite dev Process\n    SW->>N: Check the listener<br/>ssh exec · ss\n    SW->>N: Mark it awake<br/>cfgmgmt/file\n  end\n  SW->>N: Verify the state<br/>native assert\n  %%> The pinned checks report compliance; native assert fails the run on drift.\n  alt Process active\n    N-->>SW: active\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the Process is active\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event and reads the end check's output; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record result<br/>Process: active · task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Process 417 desired state becomes running; its status becomes active\n    %%> - task #5130 succeeds with the observed state\n  else restart or verify fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - SSH fails, the unit is missing, or the state does not match\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the failing step ends the run with its own exit code; there is no report step\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its exit code, mapped to `error_code`\n    Note over GW: Mark the Process failed<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Process 417 is marked failed with the error code; its desired state does not change\n    %%> - task #5130 fails\n    %%> - there is no rollback and nothing to clean up; run the same command again after fixing the cause\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5130\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "process/engine/restart": {
   "kind": "process.restart",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% process.restart on the Gateway engine: the `process` family's `process.read_instance` and `process.restart`, a restart that runs at most once per Operation even when a receipt is lost (ADR 0267, ADR 0271; ProcessControlEngineFlow). Proved on a nested fleet by swamp/proof/process/restart.cases.yaml.\n  %% A restart is not a desired state. The step carries evidence of the run it replaces (systemd's InvocationID, a random ID per run, or Docker's container ID and StartedAt) and records an attempt for its order before it calls the runtime. A changed value proves a restart happened; an unchanged value after an interrupted call proves nothing, so the step never restarts on that replay and reports the outcome unresolved.\n  %% status: built\n  %% command: process:restart\n  %% kind: process.restart\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane EN icon=workflow pill=engine tone=purple\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=executor tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant EN as Engine<br/>Gateway and NATS\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>executor\n  CLI->>GW: process:restart 417\n  Note over GW: Validate, record the task<br/>Process 417: provisioning\n  %%> Same admission as process/restart.mmd\n  opt drain-waiting, Vite or release plan\n    Note over GW: Refuse the engine<br/>EngineUnsupported\n    %%> - a restart stops first, so the drain-waiting Processes of process/engine/stop.mmd refuse here too, whatever their placement: a host Horizon unit (`GatewayHorizon::runs`, long-drain stop directives) and Gateway release-container Processes\n    %%> - dev-server Processes (`vp-dev`, `vite`, Vite plans), Processes of an Instance outside app-dev (release layouts), and Instance-container Processes, as for process/engine/start.mmd\n    %%> - Processes on a Node not placed in development (app-prod, both app roles or neither, no Node row) and `production_control` intents, from the Gateway's own Node roles, as for process/engine/start.mmd\n    %%> - decided at admission, before any order; the whole Operation takes the Swamp branch below\n  end\n  alt the engine admits the whole Operation\n    %%> Admission, one decision per Operation (EngineKinds::routes):\n    %%> - `process.restart` is in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`\n    %%> - every Node a step touches reports its action in `$KV.NODES`: `process.read_instance` and `process.restart` (agent 0.33.0); a Node that reports only `process.ensure_state` takes the Swamp branch\n    %%> - the flow accepts the plan shape; a part it does not cover throws `EngineUnsupported` before any order\n    Note over GW: Bind steps, take the lock<br/>commit, publish the start\n    %%> - the step rows and the Operation lock commit in one transaction before the start event; an Operation never moves between engines after this\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    EN->>N: Read the running instance<br/>InvocationID · StartedAt\n    %%> Step `read` (`process.read_instance`): read-only; the unit must be loaded; its `InvocationID`, empty while stopped (Docker: `<ID>@<State.StartedAt>` of the exact name). No boot ID: an InvocationID is random per run, so it never repeats across boots\n    %%> Its receipt output `{\"instance\": …}` is the step's evidence; the restart step's input is built from that recorded output on every send, so a resend carries the same pinned value\n    N-->>EN: Evidence<br/>output: instance\n    EN->>N: Restart if unchanged<br/>compare, journal, restart\n    %%> Step `restart` (`process.restart`), under the Process's target lock: first applies the admitted boot policy when it differs, as Swamp's `Set boot policy` does on every run (disabled for an on-demand unit); then, after the boot policy, reads the evidence again, so a run that changed meanwhile is left alone; only when it equals the pinned evidence, and no attempt of this order is recorded, does it write and sync the order's attempt file (`/var/lib/orbit/executor/process-restart/<operation>-<step>`, beside the journal; the process family owns it, so the shared executor journal gains no record type), and then run `systemctl restart` (Docker: `docker container restart`)\n    %%> Evidence changed before any attempt (the unit restarted by itself since the read, or the Node rebooted): no restart; the receipt says `restarted: already` with the new evidence\n    N-->>EN: New evidence<br/>active · new InvocationID\n    %%> Verification read-back: loaded, active and the boot policy as admitted (a container: running with its admitted image), else `process.state_mismatch`; and an InvocationID (or ID and StartedAt) different from the pinned one, else `process.restart_unverified`\n    %%> Identity and readiness are separate: after the decision the step reads the Process up to 30 times, 1 s apart, while the pinned run is still in place or the new run is still changing state (`activating`, `reloading`, `deactivating`; Docker `restarting`, `created`); an empty InvocationID is no run yet, not a new run; it never restarts again while it waits\n    %%> The attempt file stays after the result, so the order stays spent across a crash or a reboot before `done` is durable, whatever the result was; files older than two days are pruned only after the executor's startup recovery has finished every open order, so an attempt stays while its order is recoverable, whatever the outage\n    %%> The receipt's output is a JSON object with the members `instance` (the new run) and `restarted` (`now`, `already` or `recovered`); the executor writes them in key order and the Gateway accepts any member order\n    opt the receipt is lost\n      EN->>N: Resend the same step<br/>fresh nonce · same step ID\n      %%> `done` in the journal: the stored result with `repeat: true`; nothing runs again\n      %%> An attempt file without `done` (the executor died, or the Node rebooted, after the attempt), on a resend or on the executor's startup replay of the `open` order: it never restarts again; it waits up to 30 s for a verified new run, then reports `recovered`, else fails `process.restart_unresolved` (a stopped on-demand unit after a reboot keeps its empty InvocationID, so it fails unresolved and is not restarted again)\n      %%> No record of the step: the first send never ran (an expired order left `executor.order_expired`), so the compare and restart run as above\n      %%> Resends stop at the journal's replay window (`Engine::withinReplayWindow`): past it the Gateway sends nothing more, and Process 417 stays fenced by the Operation lock, awaiting a matching terminal receipt\n      N-->>EN: Stored or recovered<br/>never a second restart\n    end\n    alt every step verified\n      EN->>GW: Record Process active<br/>status · restarted\n      %%> - the kind's own projection records the result, the same rows a Swamp run records\n    else a step fails\n      EN--xGW: Failed step<br/>error_code · redacted detail\n      %%> - a deterministic refusal is a terminal receipt too; the journal keeps it and a resend repeats it\n      Note over GW: Mark the Process failed<br/>desired state unchanged\n      %%> - a failed `read` changed nothing; a failed `restart` left the unit as systemd reports it, and the retry is a new Operation with a fresh read\n      %%> - the Operation records `operation.native_failed` with the Swamp step the phase stands for, from the step's own code: a failed `read`, and a restart's `process.boot_failed`, are `Set boot policy` (a container's read: `Restart the container`); a restart's `process.apply_failed` (its read before the restart or the restart itself), `process.unit_missing`, `process.container_missing` and `process.restart_unresolved` are `Restart the Process` / `Restart the container`; `process.read_failed` (a read after the restart) is `Check the unit` / `Check the container`; `process.state_mismatch` and `process.restart_unverified` are `Verify the state`\n    end\n  else the engine does not admit it\n    Note over GW: Start the Swamp run<br/>operation-process-control\n    %%> - the kind is not enabled, a Node lacks the action, `$KV.NODES` is unreadable, or the flow throws `EngineUnsupported`: the whole Operation runs operation-process-control (process/restart.mmd)\n    %%> - no step of it runs on the engine; an Operation never splits across engines\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "process/start": {
   "kind": "process.start",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS 4 + R1-R4 (process.stop shape: 209, @swamp/ssh, full intent as inputs).\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 1-22 (one cfgmgmt systemd apply, its check as the end check, no shipped scripts; rule 21: only this command's branches).\n  %% Rule 23: 2026-11-03 required-only main path.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: process:start\n  %% kind: process.start\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: process:start 417\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit process:start 417`\n  %%> - syntax: `process:start <process> [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the numeric Process ID from `process:list`\n  %%> Optional:\n  %%> - `--idempotency-key` reuses a task start; `--json` returns JSON; neither adds a step\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, record the task<br/>Process 417: provisioning · #5130\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Node that runs Process 417\n  %%> - Process 417 exists and is not being removed; a container Process whose definition the Project removed refuses `process.removing` until the default's next deployment removes it\n  %%> - a production Process's release is checked against the recorded releases, not on the Node\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5130 recorded with the full intent: unit or container name, runtime, on-demand flag, Vite plan\n  %%> - Process 417 marked provisioning; its desired state does not change yet\n  %%> - nothing has happened on the Node yet\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-process-control\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209); every step runs there\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - `kind`: process.start\n  %%> - the Process unit or container name, runtime and on-demand flag\n  %%> - the Node's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  %%> Checked by the input schema:\n  %%> - no step reads or re-checks the intent\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5130\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  opt Vite dev Process\n    SW->>N: Publish the dev route<br/>native Vite workflow\n  end\n  alt systemd\n    SW->>N: Start the Process<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd.apply\n    SW->>N: Check the unit<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd.check\n  else Docker\n    SW->>N: Start the container<br/>ssh exec · docker start\n    SW->>N: Check the container<br/>cfgmgmt/docker_container\n  else Instance container\n    SW->>N: Act in the container<br/>Ansible · tags process\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the Process is an `orbit` unit in its Instance container; the child run `operation-process-container` changes it through `incus exec` on the Node and checks its state\n    %%> - systemd starts use nonblocking jobs; readiness reads have 5-second bounds, with 10 retries at 1-second intervals\n    %%> - a stopped container fails the change; its next deployment starts it\n  end\n  opt Vite dev Process\n    SW->>N: Check the listener<br/>ssh exec · ss\n    SW->>N: Mark it awake<br/>cfgmgmt/file\n  end\n  SW->>N: Verify the state<br/>native assert\n  %%> The pinned checks report compliance; native assert fails the run on drift.\n  alt Process active\n    N-->>SW: active\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the Process is active\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event and reads the end check's output; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record result<br/>Process: active · task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Process 417 desired state becomes running; its status becomes active\n    %%> - task #5130 succeeds with the observed state\n  else start or verify fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - SSH fails, the unit is missing, or the state does not match\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the failing step ends the run with its own exit code; there is no report step\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its exit code, mapped to `error_code`\n    Note over GW: Mark the Process failed<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Process 417 is marked failed with the error code; its desired state does not change\n    %%> - task #5130 fails\n    %%> - there is no rollback and nothing to clean up; run the same command again after fixing the cause\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5130\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "process/engine/start": {
   "kind": "process.start",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% process.start on the Gateway engine: one `process` family on the Process's Node, one desired-state primitive, `process.ensure_state`, for a host systemd unit and a host Docker container (ADR 0267, ADR 0271; ProcessControlEngineFlow). Runtime proof on a nested fleet is pending.\n  %% Covered: host systemd units and host Docker containers. A unit in an Instance container stays on Swamp: Swamp first moves the container's app account with a script, which no fixed argv reproduces, so the engine refuses it at admission (EngineUnsupported).\n  %% status: built\n  %% command: process:start\n  %% kind: process.start\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane EN icon=workflow pill=engine tone=purple\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=executor tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant EN as Engine<br/>Gateway and NATS\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>executor\n  CLI->>GW: process:start 417\n  Note over GW: Validate, record the task<br/>Process 417: provisioning\n  %%> Same admission as process/start.mmd: access to the Process's Node, a Process not being removed; the intent carries unit or container name, runtime, on-demand flag and Instance container\n  opt drain-waiting, dev-server, release or container plan\n    Note over GW: Refuse the engine<br/>EngineUnsupported\n    %%> - the same drain-waiting Processes as process/engine/stop.mmd, so one Process never splits its lifecycle across engines: a host systemd Process that runs Horizon (`GatewayHorizon::runs` with no Instance, whose unit carries the long-drain stop directives of SystemdProcessRenderer) and a Gateway release-container Process (Horizon, the scheduler)\n    %%> - a Vite dev Process (`vite_plan`: dev route, listener, awake marker) and a production release layout\n    %%> - decided from the Process and its placement, for both verbs, never from start-only intent fields: a `vp-dev` or `vite` Process, a Process of an Instance not placed on app-dev, any intent with a `container` (an Instance container, whose app-account move is a script, or a Gateway release container), and a Node's host systemd unit that runs Horizon (the renderer's own drain condition: `GatewayHorizon::runs` on a Node Process; a Process named like a Gateway one, an Instance's Horizon unit and a Docker Horizon Process carry no long-drain directives and stay eligible)\n    %%> - decided from the Gateway's own Node roles, never from the agent version or the Node's report: a Process on a Node not placed in development (an app-prod Node, both app roles or neither, no Node row) and an intent with `production_control` stay on Swamp\n    %%> - decided at admission, before any order: the engine admits nothing, and the whole Operation takes the Swamp branch below\n  end\n  alt the engine admits the whole Operation\n    %%> Admission, one decision per Operation (EngineKinds::routes):\n    %%> - `process.start` is in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`\n    %%> - every Node a step touches reports its action in `$KV.NODES`: the `process` family's desired-state action (one name for start and stop)\n    %%> - the flow accepts the plan shape; a part it does not cover throws `EngineUnsupported` before any order\n    %%> - eligible plans: a host systemd unit or a host Docker container of a Node Process or an app-dev Instance Process\n    Note over GW: Bind steps, take the lock<br/>commit, publish the start\n    %%> - the step rows and the Operation lock `process:<id>` commit in one transaction before the start event; an Operation never moves between engines after this\n    %%> - binding refuses `operation.target_busy` while another open Operation, on either engine, acts on the Process; while the lock is held, admission of any other Process verb refuses `operation.target_busy`\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    alt host systemd unit\n      EN->>N: Converge the unit<br/>running · boot policy\n      %%> Desired state: the unit is running and enabled at boot unless on-demand, as Swamp's `Set boot policy` / `Stop the Process` applies it; the admitted boot policy is an input of the step; an already-running unit only gets its boot policy\n      %%> Target: the Process (`process-<id>`), so two orders for one Process run one after the other\n    else Docker container\n      EN->>N: Converge the container<br/>docker start · running\n      %%> `docker container start` only when the state differs; an already-running container changes nothing\n    end\n    alt host systemd unit\n      N-->>EN: Observed state<br/>loaded · active · boot policy\n      %%> Read-back: LoadState=loaded, ActiveState=active, and UnitFileState=enabled for a persistent Process, disabled for an on-demand one (the admitted `on_demand`)\n    else Docker container\n      N-->>EN: Observed state<br/>State.Status running\n      %%> Read-back: `docker container inspect` of the exact saved name answers that name, State.Status `running` and the admitted image\n    end\n    %%> A read that systemd answers without one of LoadState, ActiveState, SubState or UnitFileState never counts as that value: the step fails\n    %%> The step fails with a code for its phase: the change (`process.input_invalid`, `process.unit_missing`, `process.container_missing`, `process.apply_failed`), a stop's plain `systemctl stop` after it (`process.unit_stop_failed`), the read-back read (`process.read_failed`), or a read that answered another state (`process.state_mismatch`); success is projected only from this read-back\n    %%> The Operation records what Swamp's failed native check records: `operation.native_failed`, with the Swamp step the phase stands for as its failed step: `Start the Process` / `Stop the Process` or `Start the container` / `Stop the container` for the change, `Stop the unit`, `Check the unit` or `Check the container` for the read, `Verify the state` for the assert; a cancel records no error code, as a cancelled Swamp run does; the step's own code stays on the engine step and in the failure's detail\n    %%> `applied` is 0 when nothing changed, so a repeat after a crash reports the same end state\n    opt the receipt is lost\n      EN->>N: Resend the same step<br/>fresh nonce · same step ID\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - the step passes its resend deadline with no receipt; two sends, one execution\n      %%> - the Operation keeps its lock, so Process 417 stays fenced; no other Operation and no Swamp run acts on it meanwhile\n      %%> On the Node:\n      %%> - the journal holds `done` for this Operation step, action and target: the executor answers the stored result with `repeat: true` and does not act again\n      %%> - an order still `open` replays under the target lock first; an order that expired before the lock did nothing (`executor.order_expired`), and the step is sent again\n      N-->>EN: Stored result<br/>repeat: true\n    end\n    alt every step verified\n      EN->>GW: Record Process running<br/>desired state · status\n      %%> - the kind's own projection records the result, the same rows a Swamp run records\n    else a step fails\n      EN--xGW: Failed step<br/>error_code · redacted detail\n      %%> - a deterministic refusal is a terminal receipt too; the journal keeps it and a resend repeats it\n      Note over GW: Mark the Process failed<br/>desired state unchanged\n      %%> - no rollback and nothing to clean up; the same command converges again after the cause is fixed\n    end\n  else the engine does not admit it\n    Note over GW: Start the Swamp run<br/>operation-process-start\n    %%> - the kind is not enabled, a Node lacks the action, `$KV.NODES` is unreadable, or the flow throws `EngineUnsupported`: the whole Operation runs operation-process-start (process/start.mmd)\n    %%> - no step of it runs on the engine; an Operation never splits across engines\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "process/stop": {
   "kind": "process.stop",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS 4 + R1-R4 (process.stop shape: 209, @swamp/ssh, full intent as inputs).\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 1-22 (one cfgmgmt systemd apply, its check as the end check, no shipped scripts; rule 21: only this command's branches).\n  %% Rule 23: 2026-11-03 required-only main path.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: process:stop\n  %% kind: process.stop\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: process:stop 417\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit process:stop 417`\n  %%> - syntax: `process:stop <process> [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the numeric Process ID from `process:list`\n  %%> Optional:\n  %%> - `--idempotency-key` reuses a task start; `--json` returns JSON; neither adds a step\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, record the task<br/>Process 417: provisioning · #5130\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Node that runs Process 417\n  %%> - Process 417 exists and is not being removed; a container Process whose definition the Project removed refuses `process.removing` until the default's next deployment removes it\n  %%> - a production Process's release is checked against the recorded releases, not on the Node\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5130 recorded with the full intent: unit or container name, runtime, on-demand flag, Vite plan\n  %%> - Process 417 marked provisioning; its desired state does not change yet\n  %%> - nothing has happened on the Node yet\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-process-control\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209); every step runs there\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - `kind`: process.stop\n  %%> - the Process unit or container name, runtime and on-demand flag\n  %%> - the Node's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  %%> Checked by the input schema:\n  %%> - no step reads or re-checks the intent\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5130\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  alt systemd\n    SW->>N: Stop the Process<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd.apply\n    SW->>N: Stop the unit<br/>ssh exec · stop\n    %%> Also cancels an activating unit, which pinned apply does not stop.\n    SW->>N: Check the unit<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd.check\n  else Docker\n    SW->>N: Stop the container<br/>ssh exec · docker stop\n    SW->>N: Check the container<br/>cfgmgmt/docker_container\n  else Instance container\n    SW->>N: Act in the container<br/>Ansible · tags process\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the Process is an `orbit` unit in its Instance container; the child run `operation-process-container` changes it through `incus exec` on the Node and checks its state\n    %%> - a stopped container fails the change; its next deployment starts it\n  end\n  SW->>N: Verify the state<br/>native assert\n  %%> The pinned checks report compliance; native assert fails the run on drift.\n  alt Process inactive\n    N-->>SW: inactive\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the Process is inactive\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event and reads the end check's output; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record result<br/>Process: active · task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Process 417 desired state becomes stopped; its status becomes active\n    %%> - task #5130 succeeds with the observed state\n  else stop or verify fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - SSH fails, the unit is missing, or the state does not match\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the failing step ends the run with its own exit code; there is no report step\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its exit code, mapped to `error_code`\n    Note over GW: Mark the Process failed<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Process 417 is marked failed with the error code; its desired state does not change\n    %%> - task #5130 fails\n    %%> - there is no rollback and nothing to clean up; run the same command again after fixing the cause\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5130\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "process/engine/stop": {
   "kind": "process.stop",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% process.stop on the Gateway engine: one `process` family on the Process's Node, one desired-state primitive, `process.ensure_state`, for a host systemd unit and a host Docker container (ADR 0267, ADR 0271; ProcessControlEngineFlow). Runtime proof on a nested fleet is pending.\n  %% Covered: host systemd units and host Docker containers. A unit in an Instance container stays on Swamp: Swamp first moves the container's app account with a script, which no fixed argv reproduces, so the engine refuses it at admission (EngineUnsupported).\n  %% status: built\n  %% command: process:stop\n  %% kind: process.stop\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane EN icon=workflow pill=engine tone=purple\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=executor tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant EN as Engine<br/>Gateway and NATS\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>executor\n  CLI->>GW: process:stop 417\n  Note over GW: Validate, record the task<br/>Process 417: provisioning\n  %%> Same admission as process/stop.mmd: access to the Process's Node, a Process not being removed; the intent carries unit or container name, runtime, on-demand flag and Instance container\n  opt drain-waiting, dev-server, release or container plan\n    Note over GW: Refuse the engine<br/>EngineUnsupported\n    %%> - a stop that waits for a drain, whatever its placement: a host systemd Process that runs Horizon (`GatewayHorizon::runs` with no Instance, whose unit carries the long-drain stop directives of SystemdProcessRenderer) and a Gateway release-container Process (Horizon, the scheduler)\n    %%> - a Vite dev Process (`vite_plan`: dev route, listener, awake marker) and a production release layout\n    %%> - decided from the Process and its placement, for both verbs, never from start-only intent fields: a `vp-dev` or `vite` Process, a Process of an Instance not placed on app-dev, any intent with a `container` (an Instance container, whose app-account move is a script, or a Gateway release container), and a Node's host systemd unit that runs Horizon (the renderer's own drain condition: `GatewayHorizon::runs` on a Node Process; a Process named like a Gateway one, an Instance's Horizon unit and a Docker Horizon Process carry no long-drain directives and stay eligible)\n    %%> - decided from the Gateway's own Node roles, never from the agent version or the Node's report: a Process on a Node not placed in development (an app-prod Node, both app roles or neither, no Node row) and an intent with `production_control` stay on Swamp\n    %%> - decided at admission, before any order: the engine admits nothing, and the whole Operation takes the Swamp branch below\n  end\n  alt the engine admits the whole Operation\n    %%> Admission, one decision per Operation (EngineKinds::routes):\n    %%> - `process.stop` is in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`\n    %%> - every Node a step touches reports its action in `$KV.NODES`: the `process` family's desired-state action (one name for start and stop)\n    %%> - the flow accepts the plan shape; a part it does not cover throws `EngineUnsupported` before any order\n    %%> - eligible plans: a host systemd unit or a host Docker container of a Node Process or an app-dev Instance Process\n    Note over GW: Bind steps, take the lock<br/>commit, publish the start\n    %%> - the step rows and the Operation lock `process:<id>` commit in one transaction before the start event; an Operation never moves between engines after this\n    %%> - binding refuses `operation.target_busy` while another open Operation, on either engine, acts on the Process; while the lock is held, admission of any other Process verb refuses `operation.target_busy`\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    alt host systemd unit\n      EN->>N: Converge the unit<br/>stopped · boot policy\n      %%> Desired state: the unit is stopped and disabled at boot, as Swamp's `Set boot policy` / `Stop the Process` applies it; the admitted boot policy is an input of the step; an already-stopped unit only gets its boot policy\n      %%> Target: the Process (`process-<id>`), so two orders for one Process run one after the other\n    else Docker container\n      EN->>N: Converge the container<br/>docker stop · stopped\n      %%> `docker container stop` only when the state differs; an already-stopped container changes nothing\n    end\n    alt host systemd unit\n      N-->>EN: Observed state<br/>loaded · inactive · disabled\n      %%> Read-back: LoadState=loaded, ActiveState=inactive exactly (`failed` or any other state fails the step), UnitFileState=disabled\n    else Docker container\n      N-->>EN: Observed state<br/>State.Status exited\n      %%> Read-back: `docker container inspect` of the exact saved name answers that name, State.Status `exited` (`created` or any other status fails the step) and the admitted image\n    end\n    %%> A read that systemd answers without one of LoadState, ActiveState, SubState or UnitFileState never counts as that value: the step fails\n    %%> The step fails with a code for its phase: the change (`process.input_invalid`, `process.unit_missing`, `process.container_missing`, `process.apply_failed`), a stop's plain `systemctl stop` after it (`process.unit_stop_failed`), the read-back read (`process.read_failed`), or a read that answered another state (`process.state_mismatch`); success is projected only from this read-back\n    %%> The Operation records what Swamp's failed native check records: `operation.native_failed`, with the Swamp step the phase stands for as its failed step: `Start the Process` / `Stop the Process` or `Start the container` / `Stop the container` for the change, `Stop the unit`, `Check the unit` or `Check the container` for the read, `Verify the state` for the assert; a cancel records no error code, as a cancelled Swamp run does; the step's own code stays on the engine step and in the failure's detail\n    %%> `applied` is 0 when nothing changed, so a repeat after a crash reports the same end state\n    opt the receipt is lost\n      EN->>N: Resend the same step<br/>fresh nonce · same step ID\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - the step passes its resend deadline with no receipt; two sends, one execution\n      %%> - the Operation keeps its lock, so Process 417 stays fenced; no other Operation and no Swamp run acts on it meanwhile\n      %%> On the Node:\n      %%> - the journal holds `done` for this Operation step, action and target: the executor answers the stored result with `repeat: true` and does not act again\n      %%> - an order still `open` replays under the target lock first; an order that expired before the lock did nothing (`executor.order_expired`), and the step is sent again\n      N-->>EN: Stored result<br/>repeat: true\n    end\n    alt every step verified\n      EN->>GW: Record Process stopped<br/>desired state · status\n      %%> - the kind's own projection records the result, the same rows a Swamp run records\n    else a step fails\n      EN--xGW: Failed step<br/>error_code · redacted detail\n      %%> - a deterministic refusal is a terminal receipt too; the journal keeps it and a resend repeats it\n      Note over GW: Mark the Process failed<br/>desired state unchanged\n      %%> - no rollback and nothing to clean up; the same command converges again after the cause is fixed\n    end\n  else the engine does not admit it\n    Note over GW: Start the Swamp run<br/>operation-process-stop\n    %%> - the kind is not enabled, a Node lacks the action, `$KV.NODES` is unreadable, or the flow throws `EngineUnsupported`: the whole Operation runs operation-process-stop (process/stop.mmd)\n    %%> - no step of it runs on the engine; an Operation never splits across engines\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "process/update": {
   "kind": "process.update",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS 4 + R1-R4 (process.stop shape: 209, @swamp/ssh, full intent as inputs). Update shares operation-process-create.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 1-22 (cfgmgmt file/systemd/docker_*, no shipped scripts, no report step).\n  %% Decided: Nick 2026-11-02, a Docker Process runs as one plain docker run through @swamp/ssh exec; pull with cfgmgmt docker_image, check with the docker_container check.\n  %% Rule 23: 2026-11-03 required-only main path.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: process:update\n  %% kind: process.update\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  alt Project definition update\n    CLI->>GW: Update definition<br/>only supplied fields\n    Note over GW: Merge and validate<br/>refuse unsupported markers\n    %%> The CLI preserves omitted fields in interactive and noninteractive modes; it reads no definition and sends no marker.\n    %%> Explicit clear options send null scalars or empty collections. Supplied environment entries replace the map, removing unsent keys.\n    %%> Changing runtime replaces the runtime specification; it requires the new runtime's command and fields.\n    %%> Null clears scalar overrides; empty maps and lists clear collections. Preset command and metadata remain canonical.\n    %%> Exact environment markers retain stored values per key; markers in other strings refuse with their field path.\n    GW-->>CLI: Updated or refused<br/>validation result\n    %%> A later default development container deploy applies the definition to its linked Processes; new clones inherit it.\n  else Installed Process update\n  CLI->>GW: process:update 418\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit process:update 418`\n  %%> - syntax: `process:update <process> [--runtime=systemd|docker] [--command=ARG ...] [--image=IMAGE] [options]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the numeric Process ID; without options it re-applies the recorded definition\n  %%> Optional:\n  %%> - `--runtime`, `--command`, `--image`, `--working-directory`, `--environment`, `--port`, `--volume`, `--restart`, `--keep-alive`, `--no-restart-on-deploy`, `--user` change only the fields they name; omitted fields keep their recorded value\n  %%> - these flags change the rendered definition, not the steps; the branches below follow recorded state, such as a retired runtime after `--runtime`\n  %%> - `--idempotency-key` reuses a task start; `--json` returns JSON\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, record the task<br/>keep old spec · #5130\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Process's Node\n  %%> - a Valkey Database server follows the [image-update rule and validation order](/reference/database-servers#update-a-valkey-servers-image)\n  %%> - a Route that points to this Process keeps the same listener, else `process.route_upstream_changed`\n  %%> - a Docker port in 5173-5199 binds to an address other than every address or the Instance bridge address (`instance.dev_band_reserved`)\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the new unit or container rendered; secret environment values pushed to the vault key `process-update-operation-5130`\n  %%> - the stored definition keeps the old spec until the run succeeds\n  %%> - development container definitions store supported command names and resolve them when rendering; capture adopts only a compatible unlinked same-name Process, keeping its id and desired state\n  %%> - task #5130 recorded with the full intent\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-process-create\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209); every step runs there\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - `kind`: process.update\n  %%> - the rendered unit, or the container definition without its environment values\n  %%> - the desired state, required mounts, and a retired name when the runtime or name changed, all from recorded state\n  %%> - vault key names for the environment, never the values\n  %%> - the Node's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  %%> Checked by the input schema:\n  %%> - no step reads or re-checks the intent\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5130\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  opt certificate account home is not recorded\n    SW->>N: Check the account<br/>ssh exec · getent\n  end\n  Note over SW: Prepare the definition<br/>public rendering only\n  %%> Resolves the observed home in certificate paths and shapes Docker argv; no Node effects.\n  opt required mounts set and running\n    SW->>N: Check required mounts<br/>ssh exec · stat\n  end\n  opt runtime or name changed\n    SW->>N: Remove the retired unit<br/>native destroy workflow\n  end\n  opt environment values set\n    SW->>N: Write the environment<br/>cfgmgmt/file · 0600\n    %%> vault.get content, root owner; no secret in public run data.\n  end\n  opt Vite dev Process\n    SW->>N: Publish the dev route<br/>native Vite workflow\n    %%> Publishes the Vite environment before its unit starts.\n  end\n  alt systemd\n  opt Gateway scheduler or Horizon runs in a release container\n    SW->>N: Apply the Gateway Process<br/>store and apply the unit\n  end\n    SW->>N: Apply the unit<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd.apply\n    %%> Publishes unitFile, reloads on content change, and applies the admitted boot and active state.\n    opt previous activation is pending\n      SW->>N: Reload systemd<br/>ssh exec · daemon-reload\n      %%> Covers a prior publish that failed before daemon-reload.\n    end\n    opt desired state is stopped\n      SW->>N: Stop the unit<br/>ssh exec · stop\n      %%> Cancels activating units too; pinned apply stops only active units.\n    end\n    opt changed unit is already running\n      SW->>N: Restart if changed<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd.restart\n      %%> Pinned apply leaves an already active process running after unitFile changes.\n    end\n    SW->>N: Check the unit<br/>cfgmgmt/systemd.check\n  else Docker\n    SW->>N: Pull the image<br/>cfgmgmt/docker_image\n    SW->>N: Remove the old container<br/>ssh exec · docker rm -f\n    SW->>N: Replace the container<br/>ssh exec · docker run -d\n    %%> One argv command, env-file, workdir, image and exact command arguments.\n    opt desired state is stopped\n      SW->>N: Stop the container<br/>ssh exec · docker stop\n      %%> The detached run starts it; this step converges a stopped creation or update.\n    end\n    SW->>N: Check the container<br/>cfgmgmt/docker_container\n  end\n  opt Vite dev Process, running\n    SW->>N: Check the listener<br/>ssh exec · ss\n    SW->>N: Mark it awake<br/>cfgmgmt/file\n  end\n  SW->>N: Verify it runs<br/>native assert\n  %%> Requires the admitted active/stopped state and boot policy, and any Vite listener.\n  alt Process running\n    N-->>SW: active (running)\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the Process has its desired state\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event and reads the end check's output; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Store the new spec<br/>Process 418: active\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the stored definition becomes the new spec\n    %%> - task #5130 succeeds\n  else a step fails\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - SSH fails, a required mount is missing, or the state does not match\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the failing step ends the run with its own exit code; there is no report step\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its exit code, mapped to `error_code`\n    Note over GW: Keep the old spec<br/>task failed · rerun converges\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Process 418 keeps its old definition and is marked failed\n    %%> - work done on the Node stays; nothing was copied, so nothing needs cleanup\n    %%> - run the update again to converge: each behavior skips state that is already right\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5130\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n  end\n"
  },
  "process/upgrade": {
   "kind": "process.upgrade",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0273 U3 (process.restart shape: 209, @swamp/ssh, full intent as inputs).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: process:upgrade\n  %% kind: process.upgrade\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane GH icon=globe pill=releases tone=gray\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant GH as GitHub<br/>release API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: process:upgrade 103 8.0.80\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit process:upgrade 103 8.0.80`\n  %%> - syntax: `process:upgrade <process> <version> [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the numeric Process ID and the upstream version; a leading `v` is dropped\n  Note over GW: Check the Process<br/>artifact · desired running · lock\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the Process has a release artifact, else `process.artifact_missing`\n  %%> - its desired state is running, else `process.not_running`\n  %%> - no other Operation holds it, else `operation.target_busy`\n  GW->>GH: Resolve the release<br/>tag v8.0.80 or 8.0.80\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - finds exactly one asset that matches the pattern and takes its SHA-256 from the checksum asset or the asset digest\n  %%> On failure:\n  %%> - `process.version_unknown` (422); nothing changes\n  GH-->>GW: asset URL + SHA-256\n  alt version already recorded\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task succeeded · unchanged\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the task succeeds at once with outcome `unchanged`; no run starts\n  else new version\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-process-upgrade\n    %%> Inputs:\n    %%> - the unit name, folder, binary, version, asset URL, SHA-256 and version arguments\n    %%> - the Node's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5140\n    SW->>N: Read the installed release\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - `current` must be a relative link to `releases/<version>`; that version is `before`\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - `process.artifact_layout_invalid`, before any change\n    SW->>N: Download the release\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - downloads into a staging folder under `releases/` and checks the SHA-256\n    SW->>N: Unpack the release\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - renames the staging folder to `releases/<VERSION>`; a checked existing copy is reused\n    SW->>N: Check the binary\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - with version arguments, requires the version in the new binary's output\n    %%> On failure before the switch:\n    %%> - `process.upgrade_failed`; the running release is untouched\n    SW->>N: Switch current\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - renames a new relative link over `current` in one step\n    SW->>N: Restart the Process\n    SW->>N: Verify it runs\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the unit is active, and still active with the same restart count 10 seconds later\n    alt it runs\n      SW->>N: Keep the previous release\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - keeps `current` and `before`, removes the other release folders\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>before, after, pruned\n      Note over GW: Record the version<br/>task succeeded · upgraded\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the artifact records `version`, `previous_version` and `sha256`\n    else switch, restart or verify fails\n      SW->>N: Switch back<br/>restart · verify\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - points `current` at `before` again, restarts the unit and verifies it\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>process.upgrade_rolled_back\n      Note over GW: Keep the old version<br/>task failed · rolled_back\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - nothing is pruned; when the switch back fails too, the code is `process.rollback_failed` and the Process shows failed\n    end\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5140\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "project/maintenance-inspect": {
   "kind": "project.detect",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task (driver workflow), ADR 0239 orbit.dependencies.v1, Recall swamp-owns-dependencies + deps-one-group-per-project, R1-R3.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 3, 9, 14, 15, 18 (no input read, no attempt records, head check folded into the read, no report or fail step).\n  %% Decided: Nick 2026-11-02, project:maintenance:detect becomes project:maintenance:inspect: per-Project opt-in, default branch only, blob-SHA short-circuit, changed files read through the GitHub App with no clone, daily registry and advisory check, no begin or finish notes, no orbit-gateway calls. The kind keeps its name until the code follows.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: project:maintenance:inspect\n  %% kind: project.detect\n  %% Rule 23: 2026-11-03 required-only main path.\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  %% lane REG icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant GH as GitHub<br/>tree · blobs\n  participant REG as Registries<br/>Packagist · npm\n  CLI->>GW: maintenance:inspect 4<br/>Project id\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit project:maintenance:inspect 4`\n  %%> - syntax: `project:maintenance:inspect <project> [--open] [--idempotency-key=KEY]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Project, required\n  %%> - `--open`, optional: also open the update task group for the findings (the `opt --open` block); by default, inspect shows the findings and creates nothing\n  %%> - `--idempotency-key`, optional: reuse a key to retry the same start\n  %%> - `--json`, optional: return the final task as JSON\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  %%> - the hourly `maintenance-projects` Schedule admits the same kind with `--open`, for each opted-in Project\n  Note over GW: Validate, mint read token<br/>task #5150 · vault key\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Gateway\n  %%> - maintenance is on for Project 4 (`project:maintenance:enable`); it is off by default, so customer repositories stay untouched\n  %%> - Project 4 reads its repository through the GitHub App and has a default branch; only that branch is inspected\n  %%> - no other `project.detect`, `project.routine` or `project.observe` task runs for Project 4\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - mints a GitHub App token with `contents: read` for this repository only\n  %%> - puts it in the `orbit-control-inputs` vault as `project-dependency-read-5150` (R3)\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5150 recorded with the full intent; no Node is a target\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>project-maintenance-inspect\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - the repository and its default branch\n  %%> - the blob SHAs of the last inspection and the stored package list\n  %%> - whether the registry check is due: once a day, or at once with `--open`\n  %%> - the vault key name of the token, never the token\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5150\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the caller follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>GH: Read the manifest SHAs<br/>head commit · Git tree\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - one `command/shell` step with `curl` on Node 209; the token from `vault.get` is only in its environment\n  %%> - reads the head commit of the default branch, then the Git tree of exactly that commit, into this run's directory\n  %%> Why no clone:\n  %%> - two small requests per opted-in Project each hour are cheap; a clone is not\n  Note over SW: Select the changed sources<br/>policy paths · blob SHAs\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - picks the blob SHAs of `composer.json`, `composer.lock`, `package.json`, workspace `package.json` files and the JavaScript lockfile of each policy path\n  %%> - compares them with the last inspection\n  %%> Short-circuit:\n  %%> - SHAs equal to the last inspection: the next steps are skipped by their `guard`, and the run directory is removed\n  opt a manifest SHA changed\n    SW->>GH: Read the changed files<br/>blobs by SHA · no clone\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - one `command/shell` step with `curl`; reads the files of the changed sources only, by blob SHA, so they match the tree just read\n    Note over SW: List the packages<br/>lockfiles · run directory\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - lists the installed packages and their versions from the lockfiles; other sources keep their stored packages\n    %%> - removes the run directory\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the new package list, in the step's output\n  end\n  opt check due or a package list changed\n    SW->>REG: Check latest, advisories<br/>Packagist · npm · advisory DB\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - compares the package list with the latest Packagist and npm versions and with the advisory database\n    %%> Cache:\n    %%> - answers are cached per package across Projects, so a package that many Projects use is asked once a day\n    %%> Why daily:\n    %%> - new releases and advisories appear without any change in the repository\n  end\n  Note over SW: Find the updates<br/>policy · one package group\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - applies release-age policy, version constraints and the Project's exceptions\n  %%> - at most one package group: every eligible major and advisory fix together\n  %%> - skipped with the registry check when no file changed and no check was due\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the update targets, the blocked findings and the group request, in the step's output\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - no `orbit-gateway` behavior and no Gateway call\n  %%> - no begin or finish note: Swamp records each run, and the Gateway reads the terminal result\n  alt run succeeds\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event · findings\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Store the inspection<br/>SHAs · packages · findings\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the blob SHAs, the package list and the findings stored for Project 4\n    %%> - nothing is created; the result shows the findings\n    %%> - task #5150 succeeds\n    opt --open\n      Note over GW: Open the update group<br/>task group #812 · tasks.provision\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - the findings hold eligible updates\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - task group #812 with one package group, for one pull request\n      %%> - the Gateway admits `tasks.provision` for it, which starts its task copy; no maintenance run follows, because the Pi task loop is removed\n      %%> Not admitted:\n      %%> - a group while Project 4 has an open maintenance task group or unfinished work, from the Gateway's own records\n    end\n  else a step fails\n    GH--xSW: token refused or error\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the token is refused, a read fails, or a registry does not answer\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code, for example `project.scan_failed`\n    Note over GW: Keep the last inspection<br/>task failed · rerun is safe\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - task #5150 fails; the stored SHAs and package list stay, and no task group is created\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the command again with a new key, or wait for the next hourly Schedule fire\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5150\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the counts of targets and blocked findings and any opened group ID once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "project/pr-merge": {
   "kind": "project.pr_merge",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review: a pull request that no Task group watches is merged by the same Swamp step as a task pull request. The Gateway admits and names the vault key, the step mints its token when it runs, and Swamp calls GitHub. No review is read: the authorized caller is the reviewer, and the green merge check on the exact head is the gate. Two further gates: the PR targets the default branch of the Project, and it comes from a branch of the repository itself. A fork can define its own workflow that reports a passing merge check, so a fork PR is refused until Nick decides how to trust it.\n  %% Target design: stage 4 of the command development pipeline (docs/reference/pipeline/4-pr-and-ci.mmd, \"Merge the PR\"), for a PR that is not in a Task group. The pattern of task:pr-merge.\n  %% Task group modes (ADR 0257): a PR that a task copy's publish opened is never merged here, also after its group ended. docs/reference/tasks.md#no-merge.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: none · API / MCP (project-pr-merge)\n  %% kind: project.pr_merge\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as API / MCP<br/>typed API request\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant GH as GitHub<br/>repository · PRs\n  CLI->>GW: Merge PR N of PROJECT<br/>head SHA\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `POST /api/v1/projects/PROJECT/pr-merge` with `pull_request`, `head_sha` and an `Idempotency-Key`\n  %%> - syntax: the MCP tool `project-pr-merge` with `project`, `pull_request`, `head_sha`\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - a Node with an access grant to the Gateway; there is no `orbit` command\n  %%> Refused at once:\n  %%> - a Project without review and merge: `project.review_and_merge_disabled`\n  %%> - a PR that a task copy's publish opened, whether its group is open or ended, matched by the repository the publish pushed to, so a copy of another Project on the same repository counts: `tasks.copy_pr_no_merge` (409), before any GitHub read; a person merges it on GitHub\n  GW->>GH: Read the PR and its checks<br/>read-only App token\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - reads the PR and the check runs of the head with read-only App tokens (`pull_requests: read`, `checks: read`)\n  GH-->>GW: PR · head · merge check\n  Note over GW: Check the gate<br/>open · head · CI green · key name\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the Project has review and merge enabled and a GitHub App repository\n  %%> - the PR is open, no longer a draft, and its head is exactly `head_sha`\n  %%> - the PR targets the default branch of the Project and comes from a branch of the repository itself, not from a fork\n  %%> - the Project's merge check (`Required checks`) passed on that head\n  %%> - the PR's head branch is not `task-<id>` of a group on the same repository that is or was a task copy group, else `tasks.copy_pr_no_merge`; it marks a copy publish that ended before it recorded the number\n  %%> - no group of another Project on the same repository published the PR or owns its `task-<id>` branch, else `project.pr_of_other_project` (409)\n  %%> - no open Task group watches the PR (a task PR merges through `task:pr-merge`; a group that ended no longer holds its PR) and no other merge of the Project runs\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - names the vault key `pr-merge-write-<uuid>` for the write token; it mints no token\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - a token lives one hour, so the merge step mints its own token when it runs\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-pr-merge\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209); no Node is a target\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - the repository, PR number and expected head\n  %%> - the vault key name of the token, never the token\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>operation recorded\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the operation number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the caller follows the operation with `operation:show`\n  SW->>GW: Mint the token<br/>operation:direct:github-token\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the first step posts the run id to the Gateway; the Gateway checks the Operation, the run and the repository, mints a token with only `contents: write` and `pull_requests: write`, and writes it to the vault key that admission named\n  SW->>GH: Squash on expected head\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - one squash merge straight into the base branch, with the admitted `sha`; a moved head refuses without merging\n  %%> - no merge queue and no GitHub auto-merge\n  %%> Refuses:\n  %%> - a conflict with the base branch; the author rebases, which reruns CI and the proof, and the caller asks again\n  SW->>GH: Clean up the token<br/>task-github-cleanup\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - runs the shared cleanup child: it revokes the token, best effort because the token expires within one hour, and removes the private GitHub CLI configuration directory of this run\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - always, on success and failure\n  Note over SW: Confirm the merge\n  alt merged\n    GH-->>SW: PR merged · sha\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event · merge sha\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record the merge<br/>operation succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the operation succeeds with the merge SHA; CI then runs on the new base commit, and nothing is deployed here\n  else refused or failed\n    GH--xSW: head moved or conflict\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code, such as `tasks_pr.merge_refused`\n    Note over GW: Mark the operation failed<br/>PR open · author fixes\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - a new request for the same head is a new operation with a new token; the gate is read again\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show OPERATION\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the operation's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the operation has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "proof/pool-drain": {
   "kind": "proof.pool.drain",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: proof:pool:drain\n  %% kind: proof.pool.drain\n  autonumber\n  participant CLI as Operator<br/>CLI · MCP\n  participant GW as Gateway\n  participant SW as Swamp\n  participant CP as CI Nodes<br/>warm copies\n  CLI->>GW: Drain the warm pool\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - A maintenance window that needs every copy of the proof Project's default gone runs this one command.\n  GW->>GW: Record the drain\n  %%> The drain is a Gateway record. It lasts until proof:pool:resume, across Gateway restarts. While it holds, no refill starts a warm copy. The drain is recorded under the lock of the proof default, which a refill takes again before it starts a copy: a refill whose reads ran during the drain starts none, and copies that a refill started before the drain are cancelled next.\n  GW->>GW: Cancel each idle warm copy\n  %%> Idle: no proof has taken it. Each cancel destroys the copy through its task group, as for any proof copy, with discard. A warm copy whose create still runs is cancelled the same way: the group's cancel waits for the create to end.\n  %%> Running proofs:\n  %%> - A proof's copies, also those taken from the pool, are not touched. Each is destroyed at its run's end, as always.\n  GW-->>CLI: Drained<br/>warm copies being destroyed\n  %%> The answer names the warm copies whose destroy started and the proof copies that still run. The operator waits for both lists to end: proof:pool:drain again shows them.\n  SW->>CP: Destroy each warm copy<br/>release its hold\n  %%> The copy's destroy drops its hold on the Node's places. The destroy of the last copy on a Node starts the runners that the holds stopped.\n"
  },
  "proof/pool-resume": {
   "kind": "proof.pool.resume",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: proof:pool:resume\n  %% kind: proof.pool.resume\n  autonumber\n  participant CLI as Operator<br/>CLI · MCP\n  participant GW as Gateway\n  participant SW as Swamp\n  participant CP as CI Nodes<br/>warm copies\n  CLI->>GW: Resume the warm pool\n  GW->>GW: Clear the drain\n  %%> A pool that is not drained stays as it is: the command changes nothing.\n  GW->>GW: Refill the warm pool\n  %%> Start warm copies until the pool holds proof.warm_copies, on Nodes with a free place, the Nodes in turns. A refill never waits for a place: a Node without one gets its warm copy at a later refill.\n  GW-->>CLI: Resumed<br/>warm copies being made\n  SW->>CP: Create each warm copy<br/>from the Node's seed\n  %%> The same instance.create as any proof copy: it holds its place and stops idle runners down to the Node's limit.\n"
  },
  "proof/proof": {
   "kind": "proof.verify",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: `orbit proof COMMAND`, the first step of stage 3 of the command development pipeline. The author proves one command in the task copy they work in, with the nested fleet that copy already has and no new copy. The Gateway records the intent and the verdicts; Orbit's own Swamp reaches the copy through its Node and runs the runner in it as `agent`, and resets the copy's inner fleet to `base` before every batch. `proof:run` is the CI path.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: proof\n  %% kind: proof.verify\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane CP icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as Operator<br/>orbit proof\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant CP as Task copy<br/>agent in the copy\n  CLI->>GW: Prove the command<br/>proof COMMAND --group\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the route needs Gateway access, which no copy holds; a Node with Gateway access names the group\n  %%> - the cases come from the copy's own checkout, so they run against the code the author has now, committed or not\n  Note over GW: Admit the proof<br/>copy · group · inputs\n  %%> What it records:\n  %%> - the copy's Incus project and container, its Node as the SSH target, the copy's inner fleet and the cases file; no secret, and no step calls back\n  %%> Refuses:\n  %%> - a request that names no group: `proof.group_required`, before any read; task VMs are removed, so no calling Node stands for a group\n  %%> - a copy whose last completion failed: `tasks.copy_completion_failed`, with its Operation, failed step, error code and the retry `orbit instance:complete ID`\n  %%> - an Instance that is not a clone that keeps its nested fleet, such as a Project's default: `proof.copy_fleet_missing`; the reset never runs on it\n  %%> - a group without a copy, a copy that is not active or not running, a Project without a nested fleet, a copy turn that may still run, or another operation on the group\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>operation ID\n  GW->>SW: Start the run<br/>proof-fleet-verify\n  SW->>CP: Select the cases<br/>runner plan --lines\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - every runner call goes Node SSH, `incus exec` into the copy, `runuser -u agent`, as a copy turn does; a missing or invalid cases file fails the run here, before the fleet changes\n  loop each batch, in the order of swamp/proof/batches.yaml\n    SW->>CP: Reset the inner fleet<br/>restore base · start · ready\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the author's fleet may have moved since the copy started, and cases undo nothing, so every batch, the first included, starts from `base`; a copy can prove again\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the fleet reset of the copy's container, from its Node: stop the three nested containers, restore `base` of each, start them, migrate the nested Gateway forward, wait for a healthy Doctor\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - before each batch, never after the last: the fleet keeps what the cases left for the author to read, and the next proof resets first\n    %%> Fails:\n    %%> - a failed reset stops the run: the cases of this batch and of every later batch are `unrun` with cause `infrastructure`\n    SW->>CP: Run the batch<br/>setup · cases\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the batch start state, then each case with its own resources and no undo\n    %%> - CLI callers use the checkout device saved in the copy fleet’s `base`; nested commands run as `orbit` with its groups\n    %%> - each noninteractive node:converge call in a copy may wait 600 seconds; that wait is separate from the case budget for other work\n    %%> - a converge CLI timeout fails that case and stops admission of later cases in the batch; they are unrun with cause infrastructure, because the inner Operation may still hold its locks\n    %%> - the reset duration is evidence in the batch result, never a verdict\n  end\n  Note over SW: Join the verdicts<br/>report step\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - one line a case, in plan order; a case of a batch that did not run is `unrun` with cause `infrastructure`\n  alt every case passes\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>verdict per case\n    Note over GW: Record the result once<br/>PASS\n    GW-->>CLI: PASS per case<br/>timings\n  else a case failed or did not run\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>verdict per case\n    Note over GW: Record the result once<br/>FAIL\n    GW--xCLI: Failed check per case<br/>exit nonzero\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - `proof.cases_failed` or `proof.run_failed`; the receipt of each case stays in the copy's evidence directory\n  end\n"
  },
  "proof/run": {
   "kind": "proof.run",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: proof:run\n  %% kind: proof.run\n  autonumber\n  participant CLI as CI job / CLI\n  participant GW as Gateway\n  participant GH as GitHub\n  participant Q as Proof queue<br/>FIFO\n  participant SW as Swamp\n  participant CP as CI Nodes<br/>copies · runners\n  CLI->>GW: Record the proof\n  %%> Record commit, authenticated caller and options, then queue admission. The proof Project is a nested-fleet Project whose tasks run as copies of its default Instance. Operator and CI proofs use the same CI places.\n  GW-->>CLI: Follow one proof Operation\n  GW->>GH: Select commands<br/>check trust\n  %%> Read the trusted commit, selection list and cases. A fork needs approval of this head and run-proofs. No selected command succeeds without a copy. Trust passes before any copy exists: a copy holds the nested Club key, so untrusted code never reaches one.\n  %%> Retry:\n  %%> - A GitHub read that fails with a transport error or a 5xx answer is read once more after 2 s, once in the first admission attempt (a later attempt keeps that attempt's selection and reads GitHub no more). A rate limit, 401, 403, 404 or 422 is not read again.\n  %%> On failure:\n  %%> - The refusal names the read, the HTTP status or the transport error class, and the reset of a rate limit: proof.github_unavailable (run again), proof.github_rate_limited (run after the reset), proof.github_refused (an operator checks the GitHub App), proof.github_not_found (do not retry).\n  opt --base supplied\n    GW->>GH: Verify the ancestor base<br/>list every intervening commit\n    %%> The base is exclusive and the tested commit is inclusive. Refuse a missing or non-ancestor base. An equal base selects nothing. Read each commit's changes against its first parent and its own selection list; never use the net diff of the range.\n  end\n  CLI->>CLI: Expand base and head<br/>with the runner\n  %%> The runner validates and expands fixtures, list vars, variants and batch setup. Compare its canonical per-case definitions by id. Command code changes select the whole file. Runner, selection code or selection list changes disable narrowing for every selected command. Tooling alone selects no commands. Unknown fixture keys and any planning doubt select the whole file.\n  CLI->>GH: Publish the selection<br/>for this base and head\n  %%> CI consumes this same result and uploads proof-selection.json before proof admission. Include every considered file, even when it selects zero cases.\n  GW->>GH: Read the runner selection<br/>for this base and head\n  %%> For a range, read each intervening commit's exact first-parent comparison. Accept only the exact version, head, merge base and cases file. Missing, expired, malformed or mismatched results select the whole file. The Gateway does not compare YAML definitions.\n  opt --base supplied\n    GW->>GW: Union selections<br/>on the tested tree\n    %%> A whole-file selection includes its current cases. Otherwise union selected ids and keep only ids still present at the tested commit. List removed files and ids as uncovered, gone at tested SHA. Trust remains on the newest exact SHA. Case receipts and execution are unchanged.\n  end\n  GW->>GW: Record case ids<br/>and reasons\n  %%> Select added and changed cases and their variants, and users whose expanded dependencies changed. Deleted cases select nothing. Report changed, added, uses changed fixture, uses changed var, batch setup changed, command code changed or proof tooling changed. A verified zero-case selection succeeds without a copy.\n  %%> A selected cases file with `cases: []`, by --all, by its command's code or by a range, selects nothing and is listed as uncovered (none: no case yet).\n  GW->>GW: Plan selected cases<br/>in batches\n  %%> Expand selected variant ids only. Sum measured case seconds from prior reports; unseen cases use the measured median, or one second with no history.\n  GW->>Q: Join by proof Operation id\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - Earlier open parents without runs block both operator and CI proofs, including during selection. Ended or cancelled parents and proofs with nothing to prove leave the queue. An older parent's own 7200-second admission allowance bounds its wait.\n  Q->>GW: Admit the front proof\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - The Swamp Node shares one CPU core, so only one proof works at a time. Under the admission transaction, check the place before creating groups or children; record runs and the execution start time together.\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - An open parent with runs or an unresolved child holds the place. Stale children and their parent's run list cannot hold it alone. Parent cancellation alone cannot free it.\n  %%> - A held start stays protected while its parent is open within its 7200-second allowance from creation. The watchdog cancels unreleased children without a native run when their parent ended or is missing, using normal group cleanup.\n  %%> - Other parents retry every 20 seconds. While a proof holds the place, show \"Waiting for proof Operation <id> to finish.\" as the step and capacity reason. A waiting parent reads `waiting` and has no start time; at admission it reads `running` with its start time, and the step clears. It holds no place.\n  %%> On failure:\n  %%> - A child that nothing ends stops holding the place after its own deadline plus the existing 60-minute stale-hold bound. Without a deadline, use its last activity plus the longest shard budget and that bound. A timed-out lock holder uses its finish time, or last activity when absent, plus the bound.\n  %%> - If that cancellation is lost, a held child of an ended or missing parent stops holding the place 60 minutes after its last activity. Admission past a stale child warns after commit and gateway:status reports gateway.proof_place_stale_child. Admission never changes the stale child.\n  %%> - Waiting more than 7200 seconds from creation fails proof.capacity_timeout, for both operator and CI proofs. No free CI place uses the same allowance.\n  GW->>GW: Count free CI places<br/>on every CI Node\n  %%> Which Nodes:\n  %%> - Every Node that offers proof places, records the hold script of this release (ProofCopyHosts::takesCopies) and holds the default's current seed. The default's own Node holds it.\n  %%> Places on a Node:\n  %%> - Its ci_places minus its busy runners minus the proof copies already on it (any proof's, and the warm copies of the pool; task copies take no place). No more than its MemAvailable holds whole copy peaks (proof.copy_memory). One rule, in ProofCopyHosts.\n  %%> - Plus the Node's warm copies that this proof may take: each is idle, active and from the Node's current seed. A warm copy already holds its place and its memory.\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - Read the runner state and MemAvailable of each Node from Prometheus before the admission transaction. A Node without a fresh read offers no place.\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - With no free place on any Node, the proof waits with the step \"Wait for a free CI place\" and tries again every 20 seconds.\n  %%> - The first attempt records the selected cases and the batch plan on the proof. A later attempt keeps them: it does not check the commit, read GitHub or select again.\n  GW->>GW: Balance batches<br/>by measured time\n  %%> One shard per free place, at most one per part. The read-only cases (`read_only: true`) of a batch with no setup form their own batch. The weight of a part is its measured case seconds, plus 180 s for its reset and setup when it is not read-only. Longest weight first; assign each part to the least loaded copy. Record expected cases and all shard operations atomically. Each shard records its run budget: 600 s for the shard, 180 s for each part that is not read-only (at least one) and the measured case seconds, times 1.5, rounded up to five minutes, at most two hours.\n  %%> Split:\n  %%> - A batch is one part until a split pays. While a place is free, take the heaviest part that is not read-only and has two or more cases, and split it by case into two parts: its cases in file order, cut where the two case-second sums are closest. Keep the split only when it lowers the heaviest copy's weight; then try again. A case never splits; its stages stay together.\n  %%> - Each part of a split batch is a batch run of its own on its own copy: its own reset, the batch's setup, then only its cases. A read-only batch never splits: it has no reset to pay for.\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - Each reset and setup costs as much as many short cases. A weight of case seconds alone puts many short batches on one copy, and that copy becomes the tail of the proof.\n  %%> - One converge or role-add batch alone took longer than the proof's 5 minutes. Its cases change the fleet but each uses its own resources, so they need the same starting state, not the same copy. A part pays 180 s for its reset; a split pays only when it shortens the longest copy.\n  GW->>GW: Take warm copies<br/>start the rest\n  %%> In the admission transaction, each shard takes one place on a Node. On each Node the shards take its warm copies first: the warm copy's task group becomes the shard's group, and the run child is recorded on it. Each other shard's task group starts an `instance.create` of a copy there, the same start as a task copy.\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - A warm copy's create has ended, so its shard skips the copy create, a large part of the fixed time before the first case.\n  %%> - The shards do not choose which of them take warm copies. So a selection fits 5 minutes from the push only when every shard it plans gets one: proof.warm_copies must be at least the shard count of the selections that must fit.\n  %%> Which warm copy:\n  %%> - Idle (no run child), active and from the Node's current seed. A warm copy from an older seed is never taken: the pool replaces it.\n  %%> Seed:\n  %%> - Each new copy starts from its own Node's seed: the default's seed pair on the default's Node, the seed holder on a CI Node (TaskStart::seed).\n  SW->>CP: Stop idle runners<br/>orbit-ci-runner-hold hold\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - A place runs one runner job or one proof copy. A Node's idle runners would take new jobs in the places that the proof holds.\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - In each copy's create, before the copy starts its inner fleet. Under one lock per Node, the copy records its hold. A warm copy took its hold in its own create, so a proof that takes it stops no runner.\n  %%> How many:\n  %%> - Idle runners stop only down to a limit: the running runners must not exceed ci_places minus the proof holds on the Node. Idle runners stop down to that number, and only those are recorded. With 5 places, no busy runner and 2 holds, 2 idle runners stop and 3 run.\n  %%> Busy runners:\n  %%> - A runner whose Runner.Worker process runs is busy. It finishes its job and is never stopped.\n  GW->>GW: Hold admitted child starts\n  %%> Execution copies must all succeed before the first start is queued. A child is not due until its copy is `active`: Orbit checks again every 20 seconds. A taken warm copy is already active. A copy whose create ended without success (an expired budget only once its expiry is settled, as Swamp can still reopen it), whose completion failed, or that is gone, fails its child with proof.run_failed; its cases are unrun, and the child's end cancels its group, which destroys the copy and releases its CI place.\n  par each admitted child\n    GW->>SW: Start one run per copy\n    %%> Queue every due start at once. A lost confirmation never permits resubmission: recovery binds the run or the child ends, bounded by the released child's 600-second admission deadline. A refusal or definite start failure of one child does not hold its siblings. Before submission, an ended or cancelled parent cancels the child through normal cancellation and group cleanup.\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - A gap between starts adds to the wall time of the proof for each child, and the proof holds the only proof place meanwhile.\n    %%> A held child has no native admission deadline. Release starts its normal 600-second admission budget and preserves its captured execution budget. A lost start uses the existing watchdog: after the parent's allowance, all children quiet for 300 seconds makes unstarted children fail with proof.admission_interrupted and cleans up their groups.\n  end\n  SW->>CP: Bring in the commit\n  %%> One Ansible phase from the copy's Node, as `agent` in the copy (ADR 0268): fetch the commit by SHA from the public Orbit repository through the copy's own egress, check it out detached, and run each of the Project's development deploy steps whose inputs changed since the seed. A failed fetch fails by name after 60 s. No retry.\n  %%> No mirror:\n  %%> - A proof copy needs no Git mirror of the Project on its Node, so a CI Node keeps none. Its clone brings no branch into a Node mirror; this fetch is the only way the commit comes in, on every Node.\n  SW->>SW: Check the workspace commit\n  %%> The workspace is at the commit under proof, or the run fails with proof.workspace_commit_mismatch before any case. Each batch's reset restarts and migrates the nested Gateway on this checkout, and the runner applies its grants and collections, so the fleet runs the tested code.\n  loop each assigned part, read-only batches first\n    SW->>CP: Read the command batches\n    SW->>SW: Select the assigned part<br/>batch and case ids\n    opt first part, or the part before it could change the fleet\n      SW->>CP: Reset the inner fleet\n      %%> A read-only batch leaves the fleet at `base`, so the part after it needs no reset. The runner refuses a case marked `read_only: true` that has a setup or a step that can change the fleet.\n      %%> `base` is the seed's fleet converged with the default scope at the seed's commit (the refresh's default converge). So a converge or role-add case finds nothing to change unless the commit under proof or the case's own setup changed an input.\n    end\n    SW->>CP: Run batch setup\n    SW->>CP: Run selected cases<br/>of the part\n    %%> A copy runs no replay of flagged cases (`fails_on_base: true`) on the merge base: the head's verdicts are the run's.\n    CP-->>SW: Verdict lines\n    %%> The Gateway records the verdict lines of each batch on its shard as the batch ends. A shard that ends at its run budget keeps them; only the cases of the batches that did not end are unrun.\n  end\n  SW-->>GW: Report verdicts<br/>full artifact\n  %%> Capture the full report, not its 4 KiB history tail. Identify command and file on each line. Missing, duplicate or unexpected case verdicts prevent a pass; a missing expected case is unrun.\n  GW->>GW: Remove the copy\n  %%> Every run end (passed, failed, cancelled, timed out, interrupted, never started) cancels its group with discard, which destroys the copy. A busy copy is asked again; the watchdog asks again for every group of a proof Operation that still owns a copy.\n  SW->>CP: Start held runners<br/>orbit-ci-runner-hold release\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - In each copy's destroy, so after every run end, also a failure or a cancel. Under the Node's lock the copy drops its hold.\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - When no hold is left on the Node, start exactly the runners that the holds stopped, and no other. A runner that is gone is skipped. A second release changes nothing.\n  GW->>GW: Refill the warm pool\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - After a warm copy is taken or destroyed, and after each seed refresh on a Node. Not while the pool is drained (proof:pool:drain), and not while a proof waits for its admission: that proof takes the free places first.\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - Start warm copies until the pool holds proof.warm_copies, on Nodes with a free place, the Nodes in turns. A used copy is never put back: each new warm copy is an `instance.create` from the Node's current seed, with its own hold. A refill never waits for a place. Its reads of the Nodes are slow, so it checks the drain and the waiting proofs again under the lock of the proof default before it starts a copy; proof:pool:drain records the drain under the same lock.\n  %%> Stale seed:\n  %%> - After a seed refresh, each idle warm copy from an older seed is destroyed first, so its place is free for the new one.\n  GW->>GW: Merge all shard verdicts\n  %%> Wait for every admitted child, require every expected case and every selected command, and close one proof result. Preserve a native interrupted shard as inconclusive interrupted coverage, even with all passed rows. A completed failed case or a non-interrupted child that did not pass takes precedence and fails the proof. Keep every case verdict, including synthesized unrun rows. Cancellation and admission interruption keep their existing failure semantics.\n  GW-->>CLI: One merged report\n  CLI->>CLI: Require the proof verdict\n  %%> The proof job is in Required checks. A selected command with a failed case or no verdict fails. An interrupted result stays interrupted in the receipt and exits nonzero, including with all passed rows. Malformed coverage or a nonzero CLI exit never passes. No selected command passes. Capacity is only a bounded wait, never a successful no-case result.\n  %%> A fork uses the outside proof's status on this exact head. A missing, pending or failed status fails Required checks; rerun the failed Required job after the approved outside proof succeeds.\n"
  },
  "route/create": {
   "kind": "route.create",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Gateway holds each Route-serving Node from admission to the terminal state; a second Route write on it waits (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run, certificate signing is the one step-to-Gateway call. docs/reference/routes.md#create-and-change-targets.\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the site child is one Ansible phase per Node (tags site), because each placed Swamp step costs about 1.2 s and the child ran 30 in series (47.6 s measured).\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): private DNS is published beside the site install, not last (it waited 4 to 7 s for the site and the probe it does not depend on).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: route:create\n  %% kind: route.create\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane V icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Route Nodes<br/>workload · Router\n  participant V as VPN Node<br/>private DNS\n  CLI->>GW: route:create<br/>INSTANCE DOMAIN\n  Note over GW: Validate, reserve domain<br/>Route pending\n  alt identical request, Route active\n    GW-->>CLI: Skipped<br/>route.exists · no run\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the command succeeds and changes nothing; a different request for the domain is refused with `route.retry_conflict`\n  else new or pending Route\n    Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - holds each Node that serves this Route's site (Caddy and PHP) from admission to the terminal state\n    %%> - a second Route write on one of those Nodes waits (task status `waiting`) and starts when the Node is free, oldest first\n    %%> Not held:\n    %%> - the DNS Node: its records are keyed, and the apply phase refuses a file that changed since the read\n    %%> Limit:\n    %%> - two Route writes on one Node run one after the other, so each waits for the other's whole run\n    %%> - a newer write waits behind an older waiting one that shares a Node, and a waiting write can be cancelled\n    %%> - when a waiting create gains its Nodes, the Gateway renders its Caddyfiles from the current site catalog before starting the run; the admitted resource and certificate identities stay fixed\n    %%> - a timed-out write keeps its Nodes until Swamp confirms that its run stopped, or for 15 minutes\n    %%> - task provision and cancel, instance:register and the Cluster Router writes hold the Nodes for their Route-publish step only, through the `route-hold` call\n    %%> - a task that waits starts from the Node's live files; a site block that matches none of its captured states still stops it with `owned_revision_conflict`\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-route-create\n    %%> On the Gateway engine (ADR 0271):\n    %%> - with `route.create` in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`, the same steps run as host catalogue primitives that the Gateway composes (route/engine/create.mmd); a Route with Caddy access rows runs on Swamp until `posix.acl.ensure` takes a tree scope\n    %%> - a Route that needs the Ingress edge, a LAN rule, a production `.env`, or a Node without the route primitives runs on Swamp\n    %%> - the engine's probe waits for the sites alone, so a failed DNS publish does not skip it; the Route still fails with the DNS step's code\n    %%> - step names, failed steps and error codes stay the chart's (docs/reference/gateway-engine.md#routecreate)\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    SW->>N: Make a CSR on the Node<br/>openssl req · key stays on Node\n    %%> Key custody:\n    %%> - the CSR action creates the leaf directory and gives its Node-local key root:caddy ownership and mode 0640 before it returns\n    SW->>GW: Sign the CSR<br/>operation:direct:certificate-sign\n    %%> Why a call to the Gateway:\n    %%> - certificate signing is the one allowed step-to-Gateway call; the Orbit CA stays in the Gateway\n    GW-->>SW: Signed certificate\n    opt Cluster-scoped Route\n      SW->>N: Allow the Router on LAN<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall\n    end\n    SW->>N: Install cert, publish site<br/>child route-create-site · Ansible\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - a caller that already wrote the admitted route intent shares that read-only input and shipped assets; the site child keeps its own scratch folder\n    %%> - one Node call converges certificates, their links and account copies, trust and the Caddyfile; validation, reload and TLS proof keep their order\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - grant checkout access only on existing paths; missing optional Laravel storage paths are skipped, never created; a missing web root fails\n    %%> - failures record redacted Ansible stderr, never certificate keys or environment contents\n    %%> - each PHP-FPM unit is reloaded and enabled; a stopped one starts (a task VM image keeps PHP-FPM off)\n    opt domain outside the TLD\n      SW->>V: Publish private DNS<br/>records + catalog, then restart\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - beside the site install, not after the probe: it needs nothing from the site and waits only for the earlier steps. A failed install can leave a record that points at a Node without the site, and a failed DNS publish now skips the probe and the Ingress edge; a rerun or the Route removal replaces the record\n    end\n    SW->>N: Probe the site over TLS<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · SNI domain\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the probe checks the hostname and the trusted chain; it does not need a successful application HTTP response\n    opt --publication=public\n      SW->>N: Open the Ingress edge<br/>Ingress Caddy · ufw 80/443\n    end\n    alt every step succeeds\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event + outputs\n      Note over GW: Record result<br/>Route active\n    else a step fails\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      Note over GW: Record the failure<br/>Route pending · failed step\n      %%> On the Nodes:\n      %%> - a failed validation leaves the live Caddyfile unchanged, removes the candidate and skips reload; other changes stay; there is no rollback\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - fix the cause and run the same command again; it resumes the pending Route\n      %%> - `route:destroy` removes the Route and its files\n    end\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "route/engine/create": {
   "kind": "route.create",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% route.create on the Gateway engine: the steps of route/create.mmd (workflow operation-route-create) as host catalogue primitives composed in the Gateway (RouteEngineFlow, RouteHostSteps). ADR 0271, Routes rewrite PR B (ORB-6352).\n  %% Pending A4: Caddy's recursive access needs posix.acl.ensure with `scope: tree`; a Route with access rows runs on Swamp until it lands.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: route:create\n  %% kind: route.create\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane EN icon=workflow pill=engine tone=purple\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=executor tone=amber\n  %% lane V icon=server pill=executor tone=green\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant EN as Engine<br/>Gateway and NATS\n  participant N as Route Node<br/>executor\n  participant V as VPN Node<br/>executor\n  CLI->>GW: route:create<br/>INSTANCE DOMAIN\n  Note over GW: Validate, hold Nodes<br/>render whole files\n  %%> Same guards and admitted plan as route/create.mmd (`route_create`, NativeRouteCreatePlan)\n  %%> The publication hold covers every Node of the plan: site and leaf Nodes and each VPN Node with DNS work, from the reads to the guarded writes, also after an admission wait (RoutePublicationHolds)\n  %%> Each account the Node's steps write for is read on the Node first (`account.read`): home, primary group and gid by NSS; pools are rendered with the group's name\n  alt the engine admits the whole Operation\n    %%> Admission, one decision per Operation (EngineKinds::routes):\n    %%> - `route.create` is in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`, and every Node a step touches reports each step's primitive in `$KV.NODES` (the `host` family with `account.read`, agent 0.37.0), with `tls.csr_input` on the leaf Node; every build with the host family has file.ensure `source_path`, so the key copy needs no feature\n    %%> - the flow accepts the plan shape; an Ingress edge, a LAN rule, a production `.env`, awake markers, Caddy access rows, or a pool outside the two pool kinds throws `EngineUnsupported`\n    %%> - the kind's own admission (RouteEngineFlow::admit) also refuses a Route with Caddy access rows, and a Node with work that lacks one host primitive, or a leaf Node that lacks `tls.csr_input`\n    %%> - an owner name outside the host primitives' pattern `^[a-z_][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}$` (the Route's account or another link owner, such as `Orbit` or `Dev.Team`) keeps the whole Operation on Swamp; a name NSS answers outside it refuses at the accounts check, before any request\n    %%> - production never moves: a site or leaf Node that is not an app-dev Node (app-prod, unclassified, no role), a DNS Node without the vpn role, a target Instance outside development, or an intent with a production placement or `production_control` keeps the whole Operation on Swamp\n    Note over GW: Bind steps, take the lock<br/>commit, publish the start\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    EN->>N: Read each account<br/>account.read · account-k\n    %%> First, on each site Node: the Node's account (leaf copies, pools) and each other link owner; the recorded answer keeps a retry byte for byte, and its canonical `user` owns the account's files\n    EN->>N: Read the trust anchor<br/>file.read · read-trust\n    Note over EN: Check the trust anchor<br/>Gateway step · trustcheck\n    %%> The anchor must hold this root or be absent; another root (a rotation) refuses here, before any request, as the Node's site step (`route.publish_failed`). After an Orbit root CA rotation, route.create stays off in ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS until the ca.trust.ensure rotation fix (ORB-6406) is released and pinned and has repaired the subject-hash link on every development Node. Swamp rotating the file alone is not enough.\n    Note over EN: Check the accounts<br/>Gateway step · accounts\n    %%> An account NSS does not resolve now (`absent`) refuses here, before any request, so nothing has changed; the step is the Node's site step, so the Route records `route.publish_failed`\n    EN->>N: Make a CSR on the Node<br/>tls.ensure_csr · csr-i\n    %%> The Gateway sends the folder, its root:caddy owner, modes 0750 and 0640, and the subject; the key never leaves the Node\n    Note over EN: Sign the CSR<br/>Gateway step · sign-i\n    %%> As `operation:direct:certificate-sign`; a pending cancel refuses\n    EN->>N: Set APP_URL and links<br/>dotenv.ensure · file.ensure\n    %%> `site-node-url-k`: the last `APP_URL=` line of an existing `.env` that the account owns, replaced or appended; `site-node-link-k`: a release link owned by its account\n    EN->>N: Read each pool file<br/>file.read · read-pool-k\n    %%> One path, read only: absent, or present with its type, owner, mode and, for a regular file, its SHA-256; never its content. The step records the answer\n    EN->>N: Write and test the pools<br/>config.apply · site-node-pools\n    %%> Under `/etc/caddy/.orbit-publish.lock`: each pool rendered whole with the account's group and home, root:root 0644, with the digest its read recorded as `from_sha256` (none when absent); `php-fpm` validators from the fixed list; a failed test restores the files\n    EN->>N: Start each pool<br/>systemd.unit.ensure\n    EN->>N: Reload the pools<br/>config.apply · site-node-reload\n    %%> The same files with the digest of the bytes the install left as their guard, so it writes nothing and refuses a change made since; validators, then each pool unit reloads on every run, as route.publish_site did\n    EN->>N: Install leaf, copies<br/>file.ensure · cert-i …\n    %%> `cert-i`: the signed leaf, root:caddy 0644; `current-i`: the version link; `folder-i`: the account copy's folder, root 0755 as `install -D` made it; `key-i`: the key copied on the Node (`source_path`), account 0600; `copy-i`: the leaf, account 0600; `copylink-i`: the account's version link\n    EN->>N: Trust the Orbit root<br/>ca.trust.ensure · trust\n    %%> The one trust anchor `orbit-managed-root-ca.crt`, root:root 0644, as main and Swamp place it; it already holds this root in every create outside a rotation, so nothing changes (the update runs only after a change); unrelated roots are never named\n    EN->>N: Read the Caddyfile<br/>file.read · read-caddy\n    EN->>N: Apply the Caddyfile<br/>config.apply · site-node-caddy\n    %%> Under `/etc/caddy/.orbit-publish.lock`: the whole Caddyfile, root:caddy 0640, with the digest its read recorded; validated as `caddy`; Caddy reloads on every run; a failed validation or reload restores the file\n    EN->>V: Read records, catalog<br/>file.read · dns-i-read-…\n    %%> The reads find a link in a file's place, which the unlink step removes first\n    EN->>V: Publish private DNS<br/>config.apply · dns-i\n    %%> Beside the sites, after the signatures. Under `/run/lock/orbit-dnsmasq.lock`: records and catalog, root:root 0644, each guarded by its admission digest, so a payload that went stale while the create waited refuses without writing (`private_dns.publication_failed`); a file already holding the payload is kept; the `dnsmasq` validator over `/etc/dnsmasq.conf` and `/etc/dnsmasq.d`; dnsmasq and the private DNS listener restart on every run\n    Note over EN: Probe the site over TLS<br/>Gateway step · probe-i\n    %%> After each Node's last site step: a TLS handshake by WireGuard address, trusting only the Orbit root\n    %%> A read that finds a link in a file's place runs that file's unlink step (`file.ensure` absent, never following it), and the write then sends no guard; for any other read the unlink step is done without an order (EngineSkip)\n    %%> Every config.apply input comes from the admitted plan and the recorded reads, so a resend carries the same bytes, the order's identity; each step has its own `recovery_dir` under `/var/lib/orbit/executor/config-recovery` (`pool-install`, `pool-reload`, `caddy`, `dns`)\n    %%> The writes overwrite whatever the Node held at the read, as main's route.create did: a pool with a numeric gid or a catalog in another key order is replaced; the guard refuses only when a file changed between its read and its write, under the Node publication hold and the lock\n    alt every step succeeds\n      EN->>GW: Route active<br/>same projection\n    else a step fails\n      EN--xGW: Failed step<br/>chart name · main's code\n      %%> A Node's site steps record `Install cert, publish site-<node>` with `route.publish_failed`; `dns-i` records `Publish private DNS-i` with `private_dns.publication_failed`, main's codes; a guard refusal records its chart step's code too, as main maps it\n    end\n  else the engine does not admit it\n    Note over GW: Start the Swamp run<br/>operation-route-create\n    %%> No step runs on the engine; an Operation never splits across engines\n    %%> A Route with Caddy access rows runs here, with Swamp's recursive access, until A4 lands; PR D keeps the old actions until then\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "route/destroy": {
   "kind": "route.destroy",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): a withdrawal clears the Router answers of the Route from the private DNS catalog in the same step, not only its records.\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Gateway holds each Route-serving Node from admission to the terminal state; a second Route write on it waits (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run, official cfgmgmt behaviors and SSH argv commands. docs/reference/routes.md#remove-a-route.\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Node work of the site child is one Ansible phase (see route:create).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: route:destroy\n  %% kind: route.destroy\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane V icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Route Nodes<br/>workload · Router\n  participant V as VPN Node<br/>private DNS\n  CLI->>GW: route:destroy ROUTE\n  Note over GW: Guard, capture placements<br/>Route unchanged\n  %%> Replacement that never activated:\n  %%> - the run also restores the old domain's URL and DNS; the replaced Route stays\n  Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - holds each Node that serves this Route's site (Caddy and PHP) from admission to the terminal state\n  %%> - validates a same-directory candidate before atomic publication; invalid bytes never replace the live file\n  %%> - a second Route write on one of those Nodes waits (task status `waiting`) and starts when the Node is free, oldest first\n  %%> Not held:\n  %%> - the DNS Node: its records are keyed, and the apply phase refuses a file that changed since the read\n  %%> Limit:\n  %%> - two Route writes on one Node run one after the other, so each waits for the other's whole run\n  %%> - a newer write waits behind an older waiting one that shares a Node, and a waiting write can be cancelled\n  %%> - a timed-out write keeps its Nodes until Swamp confirms that its run stopped, or for 15 minutes\n  %%> - task provision and cancel, instance:register and the Cluster Router writes hold the Nodes for their Route-publish step only, through the `route-hold` call\n  %%> - a task that waits starts from the Node's live files; a site block that matches none of its captured states still stops it with `owned_revision_conflict`\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-route-removal\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n  opt --offline\n    SW->>N: Check each Node answers<br/>@swamp/ssh · true\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a Node that does not answer is skipped and keeps its residue for `node:converge`; the VPN Node is never skipped\n  end\n  opt domain outside the TLD\n    SW->>V: Remove private DNS<br/>records + catalog, then reload\n    SW->>SW: Wait the DNS grace<br/>31 s: TTL 30 s + 1 s\n  end\n  SW->>N: Withdraw site, remove cert<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file absent\n  opt development targets\n    SW->>N: Converge PHP-FPM pools<br/>child route-create-site · Ansible\n  end\n  opt Cluster-scoped or public\n    SW->>N: Remove unused LAN rules<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall absent\n  end\n  SW->>N: Check nothing is left<br/>cfgmgmt check · native assert\n  alt every step succeeds\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event + outputs\n    Note over GW: Delete the Route record<br/>residue per skipped Node\n  else a step fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    Note over GW: Record the failure<br/>Route unchanged\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the task carries the failed step; the Route stays as it was, because a failed Route would render no site\n    %%> On the Nodes:\n    %%> - finished steps stay; there is no rollback\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again after the fix; every step runs again from the start\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "route/engine/destroy": {
   "kind": "route.destroy",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% route.destroy on the Gateway engine: the steps of route/destroy.mmd (workflow operation-route-removal) composed in the Gateway from host primitives (`file.read`, `config.apply`, `http.probe`). Built for ADR 0271 (RouteDestroyEngineFlow, Routes rewrite PR C).\n  %% Every path, lock, owner, validator and unit arrives in the order; the Gateway renders each whole file. instance/container/destroy-engine.mmd still withdraws its Routes with `dns.publish_records` and `route.withdraw_site` until its own rewrite.\n  %% status: built\n  %% command: route:destroy\n  %% kind: route.destroy\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane EN icon=workflow pill=engine tone=purple\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=executor tone=amber\n  %% lane V icon=server pill=executor tone=green\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant EN as Engine<br/>Gateway and NATS\n  participant N as Route Node<br/>executor\n  participant V as VPN Node<br/>DNS executor\n  CLI->>GW: route:destroy ROUTE\n  Note over GW: Guard, capture placements<br/>Route unchanged\n  %%> Same guards and captured states as route/destroy.mmd\n  Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes\n  %%> Same hold as Swamp: each Node that serves the site, from admission to the terminal state; a second Route write on one of them waits\n  alt the engine admits the whole Operation\n    %%> Admission, one decision per Operation (EngineKinds::routes):\n    %%> - `route.destroy` is in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`\n    %%> - every Node a step touches reports its action in `$KV.NODES`: `file.read`, `file.ensure` and `config.apply` on each VPN Node, and `http.probe` too on each Route Node (agent 0.36.0)\n    %%> - the flow accepts the plan shape; a part it does not cover throws `EngineUnsupported` before any order\n    %%> - production gate (as ORB-6337): each serving Node has the active `app-dev` role and each DNS Node the `vpn` role; no `production_control`, and no production Instance on a serving Node\n    %%> - eligible plan: an ordinary private Route with no URL write; `--offline`, Caddy access rows (`posix.acl.ensure` with scope `tree`, A4) or link writes, a never-activated replacement, LAN rules, an Ingress edge, `url_lines` for a surviving Route and any `env_files` write throw `EngineUnsupported`\n    %%> - leaf folders and pools need the account's home and group, which `account.read` (agent 0.37.0) reads on the Node; a Route Node with either must report it\n    %%> - also refused: a public Route or a Process proxy (`route_private_development`), and a PHP version whose last shared pool would leave\n    Note over GW: Bind steps, take the lock<br/>hold every Node, publish the start\n    %%> - the step rows and the Operation lock commit in one transaction before the start event; an Operation never moves between engines after this\n    %%> - in the same transaction the start refuses `route.publication_held` while another open Route write, on either engine, writes or holds one of its Nodes, the DNS-only VPN Nodes included (RouteWriteNodes); an engine removal fences later Route writes the same way\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    opt a leaf or a pool to change\n      EN->>N: Read the account<br/>account.read · account-node\n      %%> The account's uid, gid, group and home as the Node's NSS answers them; `identity-node`, a Gateway step, refuses an absent or unreadable account before any step below changes a file\n      N-->>EN: Account\n    end\n    opt domain outside the TLD\n      EN->>V: Read records and catalog<br/>file.read · dnsread-i\n      %%> Two reads, never following a link: the digest, type, owner and mode of `/etc/dnsmasq.d/orbit-records.conf` and `/var/lib/orbit/private-dns/catalog.json`; the receipt is the step's output, so a retry sends the same guard\n      %%> A link in a file's place is removed without following it (`file.ensure` absent, `dnsunlink-i-file`), only then; the step is done without an order otherwise; any other kind refuses\n      V-->>EN: Digest or absent\n      EN->>V: Write records and catalog<br/>config.apply · dns-i\n      %%> The records and catalog rendered without the Route, owner `root:root 0644`, under the DNS lock `/run/lock/orbit-dnsmasq.lock`; each guarded (`from_sha256`) by the digest of the bytes the Gateway rendered from at admission (`records`, `catalog_file`), or by the read digest when the read already found the desired bytes; a file that was a link is written unguarded\n      %%> A file changed after admission, by another Route or any other publisher, refuses before any write (`owned_revision_conflict`), as on main; a sibling added since is never erased\n      %%> `dnsmasq --test` with `/etc/dnsmasq.conf` and `/etc/dnsmasq.d`, then `dnsmasq` and `orbit-private-dns` restart on every run; a failed test or restart puts the old files back and loads them again\n      %%> The prior state is recorded in `/var/lib/orbit/executor/recovery/operation-<id>/dns-<i>`, so a run that stopped is undone by its replay\n      V-->>EN: Files and restart<br/>applied\n      Note over EN: Wait the DNS grace<br/>31 s: TTL 30 s + 1 s\n      %%> A Gateway step (`grace`) after every `dns-i`: it runs no earlier than 31 s after the last recorded DNS receipt (`done_ms`), as Swamp's `route-dns-grace`; a redelivered receipt or a resend never moves that time\n      %%> The engine keeps that time on the step (`EngineWait`) and schedules its deadline on the bus; no process sleeps, so a restart resumes only what is left (ORB-6333)\n      %%> A cancel during the wait ends the Operation `cancelled` with nothing withdrawn from the Route Node\n    end\n    EN->>N: Read files and account<br/>file.read · account.read\n    %%> `caddyread-node`, and `poolread-node-k` for each pool; the account was read first (`account-node`, `account.read`: uid, gid, group, home), and `identity-node` refused an absent or unreadable one before any DNS or Caddy step\n    N-->>EN: Digests · account\n    Note over EN: Guard the Caddyfile<br/>guard-node\n    %%> A Gateway step: the read digest must be the Caddyfile rendered at admission (`route_guards`) or the desired one, else `owned_revision_conflict` and nothing is written; a missing Caddyfile or another kind refuses too\n    %%> Each pool must hold its render at admission or the desired one in a writer's form (each scope's group as gid or name, with or without a trailing separator; at most 8 scopes, more runs on Swamp), else `owned_revision_conflict`\n    %%> A link in its place passes: `caddyunlink-node` removes it without following it (`file.ensure` absent), and the write below goes unguarded; the step is done without an order otherwise\n    EN->>N: Write and reload Caddy<br/>config.apply · withdraw-node\n    %%> The whole Caddyfile without the Route, guarded by the digest read, owner `root:caddy 0640`, under the publication lock `/etc/caddy/.orbit-publish.lock`\n    %%> `caddy validate` as `caddy`, then `caddy.service` reloads on every run, also a replay that finds nothing to change; a failed validation or reload puts the old file back and reloads it\n    %%> Recovery record in `/var/lib/orbit/executor/recovery/operation-<id>/withdraw-<node>`\n    N-->>EN: applied\n    EN->>N: Poll Caddy's admin API<br/>http.probe · poll-node\n    %%> `http://127.0.0.1:2019/config/` until no withdrawn name appears in it, within 5 s (ORB-6335); unmet fails the chart's withdrawal step\n    N-->>EN: met\n    opt a pool or a leaf to change\n      EN->>N: Pools and leaves<br/>config.apply · file.ensure\n      %%> `pools-node`: each pool rendered whole by the Gateway (in place of withdraw.rs `merge_pool`), the gid and home from `account.read`, guarded by its read, under `/etc/caddy/.orbit-publish.lock`; `php-fpm -t` per pool, then each pool reloads; a failure restores them, `caddyback-node` puts back the Caddyfile captured at admission (guarded by the withdrawal just written), and the run fails `Converge PHP-FPM pools`; no order when every guarded pool is absent\n      %%> `leaf-node-k`, `leafhome-node-k`: `file.ensure` absent, recursive, of `/etc/caddy/orbit-certificates/<scope>` and `<home>/.orbit/certificates/<scope>`, only once Caddy no longer names them\n      N-->>EN: applied\n    end\n    EN->>N: Probe a sibling Route<br/>TLS over WireGuard\n    %%> A Gateway step (`sibling-node`) as route.create's probe: a TLS handshake with another Route on the same Node, trusting only the Orbit root, proves Caddy still serves the rest; skipped when the Node serves no other Route\n    opt the receipt is lost\n      EN->>N: Resend the same step<br/>fresh nonce · same step ID\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - the step passes its resend deadline with no receipt; two sends, one execution\n      %%> - the Operation keeps its lock, so the Route and its held Nodes stays fenced; no other Operation and no Swamp run acts on it meanwhile\n      %%> On the Node:\n      %%> - the journal holds `done` for this Operation step, action and target: the executor answers the stored result with `repeat: true` and does not act again\n      %%> - an order still `open` replays under the target lock first; an order that expired before the lock did nothing (`executor.order_expired`), and the step is sent again\n      N-->>EN: Stored result<br/>repeat: true\n    end\n    alt every step verified\n      EN->>GW: Delete the Route record<br/>free the held Nodes\n      %%> - the kind's own projection records the result, the same rows a Swamp run records: the Route goes `retiring` before its record is deleted, and the only authoritative Route of an active Instance stays (ORB-6339); a projection that throws is retried twice, then fails `engine.projection_failed`\n    else a step fails\n      EN--xGW: Failed step<br/>error_code · redacted detail\n      %%> - a deterministic refusal is a terminal receipt too; the journal keeps it and a resend repeats it\n      Note over GW: Record the failure<br/>Route kept · failed step\n      %%> - what was withdrawn stays withdrawn; no rollback\n      %%> - each step maps onto the Swamp chart step it is part of (reads, unlinks, guard and poll onto `Withdraw site, remove cert` or `Remove private DNS`), so the projection records main's `failed_step` and that step's code: a guard conflict (`owned_revision_conflict` in the receipt) persists `route.withdraw_failed` or `private_dns.publication_failed`, as on main\n      %%> - retry: the same command; its reads take the files as they are and each write converges\n    end\n  else the engine does not admit it\n    Note over GW: Start the Swamp run<br/>operation-route-removal\n    %%> - the kind is not enabled, a Node lacks the action, `$KV.NODES` is unreadable, or the flow throws `EngineUnsupported`: the whole Operation runs operation-route-removal (route/destroy.mmd)\n    %%> - no step of it runs on the engine; an Operation never splits across engines\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "route/caddy-converge": {
   "kind": "route.caddy_converge",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: complete captured Caddy publication, official cfgmgmt file and pinned SSH argv. docs/reference/caddy-configuration.md#native-publication.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten rules 9, 14, 22. No result printer. The official file check feeds the native end assertion.\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: none · Node Caddy build\n  %% kind: route.caddy_converge\n  autonumber\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>over SSH\n  Note over GW: Render, record intent<br/>whole-Node Caddyfile\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - an operator runs `orbit:caddy-build NODE` on the Gateway; there is no CLI command\n  %%> Refused before a task starts:\n  %%> - the Node is unknown, the render has a problem (`caddy.render_failed`), or the Node has no SSH address, user or host key, or the control identity is missing (`swamp.ssh_target_unavailable`, `swamp.ssh_identity_unavailable`)\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-route-caddy\n  %%> What the operator gets:\n  %%> - the Operation ID at once; completion is read from the Operation\n  Note over SW: Read the input\n  %%> Why first:\n  %%> - the captured content hash is verified before the Node is contacted\n  SW->>N: Check the connection\n  SW->>N: Write the Caddyfile\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - writes the rendered bytes to a run-scoped candidate beside `/etc/caddy/Caddyfile`, `root:caddy`, mode 0640\n  SW->>N: Validate the Caddyfile\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - `caddy validate` runs as the `caddy` user against the candidate, then atomic rename publishes it; failure leaves the live file unchanged and skips reload\n  SW->>N: Reload Caddy\n  SW->>N: Check the Caddyfile\n  Note over SW: Check the publication\n  alt every step succeeds\n    SW-->>GW: Native terminal status<br/>captured publication\n    Note over GW: Record the version<br/>after the end check\n  else a step fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    Note over GW: Record the failure\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - validation failure removes the candidate and keeps the live file; after publication, reload or end-check failure leaves the validated new file\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - fix the cause and run `orbit:caddy-build NODE` again; a Caddyfile that fails validation needs the stored intent corrected first\n  end\n"
  },
  "route/target-set": {
   "kind": "route.target_set",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Gateway holds each Route-serving Node from admission to the terminal state; a second Route write on it waits (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run, certificate signing is the one step-to-Gateway call. docs/reference/routes.md#change-the-target-of-a-route.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: route:target:set\n  %% kind: route.target_set\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane V icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Route Nodes<br/>workload · Router\n  participant V as VPN Node<br/>private DNS\n  CLI->>GW: route:target:set<br/>ROUTE TARGET\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit route:target:set ROUTE TARGET`\n  %%> - syntax: `<route> <target> [--targets=ID]... [--reassign=ID:ROUTE]... [--remove=ID]... [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Route and its target Instance; the options send the complete target set of a production pool and a disposition for each detached Instance\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Guard, lock, record intent<br/>targets unchanged\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the Route's Nodes\n  %%> - the target is an active Instance of the Route's Project; a production pool is a Cluster-scoped explicit Route with active production Instances on distinct active `app-prod` Nodes\n  %%> - the change does not take an Instance from another Route, or detach an active Instance from its only Route (`route.target_conflict`); a detached Instance has a disposition (`route.target_disposition_required`)\n  %%> - no other target-set change is recorded on the Route (`route.target_set_conflict`) and no other operation holds the projection (`app-dev.projection_busy`)\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - a single-target change stores the association before the run; a production pool records the requested set and keeps the associations unchanged until the run's result\n  %%> - an `active` Route refuses a single-target change (`route.reconciliation_required`)\n  %%> - `--remove` is refused today (`route.target_cleanup_unavailable`, 503)\n  alt unpublished development Route\n    Note over GW: Record the target<br/>R1: no run\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - changes only Orbit's record; no Node changes and no run starts\n    %%> Repeat:\n    %%> - setting the current target again is skipped: `Skipped — target already set (route.target_unchanged)`\n    GW-->>CLI: Target set<br/>task succeeded\n  else published Route\n    Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - holds each Node that serves this Route's site (Caddy and PHP) from admission to the terminal state\n    %%> - a second Route write on one of those Nodes waits (task status `waiting`) and starts when the Node is free, oldest first\n    %%> Not held:\n    %%> - the DNS Node: its records are keyed, and the apply phase refuses a file that changed since the read\n    %%> Limit:\n    %%> - two Route writes on one Node run one after the other, so each waits for the other's whole run\n    %%> - a newer write waits behind an older waiting one that shares a Node, and a waiting write can be cancelled\n    %%> - a timed-out write keeps its Nodes until Swamp confirms that its run stopped, or for 15 minutes\n    %%> - task provision and cancel, instance:register and the Cluster Router writes hold the Nodes for their Route-publish step only, through the `route-hold` call\n    %%> - a task that waits starts from the Node's live files; a site block that matches none of its captured states still stops it with `owned_revision_conflict`\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-route-target-set\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the task ID, at once\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n    SW->>N: Make CSRs on new Nodes<br/>openssl req · key stays on Node\n    %%> Key custody:\n    %%> - the CSR action creates the leaf directory and gives its Node-local key root:caddy ownership and mode 0640 before it returns\n    SW->>GW: Sign the CSRs<br/>operation:direct:certificate-sign\n    %%> Why a call to the Gateway:\n    %%> - certificate signing is the one allowed step-to-Gateway call; the Orbit CA stays in the Gateway\n    GW-->>SW: Signed certificates<br/>Orbit CA key stays in Gateway\n    opt published development Route\n      SW->>N: Install cert, publish site<br/>route:create's publish step\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - moves the site to the new checkout; the target stays set if this fails (`route.publish_failed`), and the request is run again\n    end\n    opt production pool\n      SW->>N: Stage the new targets<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file · firewall\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - stages each added target's runtime, certificates, Caddy site and `.env`\n      SW->>N: Probe each new target<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · SNI domain\n      %%> Why:\n      %%> - the probe verifies the Route's hostname and the Orbit CA over TLS; application HTTP errors do not fail it, and a failed probe stops the switch\n      SW->>N: Switch the Router pool<br/>Router Caddy · reload\n      SW->>N: Retire detached targets<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt · absent\n    end\n    opt Node-scoped, outside TLD\n      SW->>V: Move private DNS<br/>records + catalog, then reload\n    end\n    alt every step succeeds\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n      Note over GW: Write the new targets<br/>from result\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the new associations are written, and a destination Route that received a reassigned Instance becomes `active`\n    else a step fails\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      Note over GW: Mark the change failed<br/>targets from failed step\n      %%> Result for a published development Route:\n      %%> - the new target stays set and the failed step is `Install cert, publish site`; run the same request again\n      %%> Result for a production pool:\n      %%> - before the Router switch, the original associations stay and the staged sites serve no traffic\n      %%> - after the switch, the new associations are written, because the new pool serves, and the recorded set and `failed_step` stay\n      %%> On the Nodes:\n      %%> - finished steps stay; there is no rollback\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same request again; a different request is refused until the change completes (`route.target_set_conflict`); a request with dispositions can be refused with `route.target_disposition_required` once the detached Instances are gone\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "route/update": {
   "kind": "route.update",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Gateway holds each Route-serving Node from admission to the terminal state; a second Route write on it waits (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run, certificate signing is the one step-to-Gateway call. docs/reference/routes.md#change-an-explicit-domain.\n  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Node work of the site child is one Ansible phase (see route:create).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: route:update\n  %% kind: route.update\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane V icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution\n  participant N as Route Nodes<br/>workload · Router\n  participant V as VPN Node<br/>private DNS\n  CLI->>GW: route:update ROUTE<br/>at least one option\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit route:update ROUTE --domain=DOMAIN`\n  %%> - syntax: `<route> [--domain=DOMAIN] [--publication=private|public] [--web-root=PATH] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Route and at least one change; `--web-root` is separate from the others\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, lock, record<br/>replacement pending\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the Route's Node, or to any member Node for a Cluster-scoped Route\n  %%> - the Route is an app Route (`route.kind_unsupported`); a generated Route cannot change its domain (`route.domain_immutable`); a Route with a web root keeps its domain (`route.web_root_domain_immutable`)\n  %%> - the new domain is free and not reserved (`route.domain_conflict`), and no other domain change runs (`route.domain_change_conflict`)\n  %%> - public publication needs production targets (`route.reconciliation_required`)\n  %%> - no other operation holds the projection lock; Orbit waits up to 30 seconds, then refuses with `app-dev.projection_busy`\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - a domain change records a replacement Route as `pending`; the current Route stays authoritative until the cutover\n  alt Node work needed\n    Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - holds each Node that serves this Route's site (Caddy and PHP) from admission to the terminal state\n    %%> - a second Route write on one of those Nodes waits (task status `waiting`) and starts when the Node is free, oldest first\n    %%> Not held:\n    %%> - the DNS Node: its records are keyed, and the apply phase refuses a file that changed since the read\n    %%> Limit:\n    %%> - two Route writes on one Node run one after the other, so each waits for the other's whole run\n    %%> - a newer write waits behind an older waiting one that shares a Node, and a waiting write can be cancelled\n    %%> - a timed-out write keeps its Nodes until Swamp confirms that its run stopped, or for 15 minutes\n    %%> - task provision and cancel, instance:register and the Cluster Router writes hold the Nodes for their Route-publish step only, through the `route-hold` call\n    %%> - a task that waits starts from the Node's live files; a site block that matches none of its captured states still stops it with `owned_revision_conflict`\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-route-update\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the task ID, at once\n    %%> Next:\n    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n    opt --domain\n      SW->>N: Make a CSR on the Node<br/>openssl req · key stays on Node\n      %%> Key custody:\n      %%> - the CSR action creates the leaf directory and gives its Node-local key root:caddy ownership and mode 0640 before it returns\n      SW->>GW: Sign the CSR<br/>operation:direct:certificate-sign\n      %%> Why a call to the Gateway:\n      %%> - certificate signing is the one allowed step-to-Gateway call; the Orbit CA stays in the Gateway\n      GW-->>SW: Signed certificate<br/>Orbit CA key stays in Gateway\n    end\n    SW->>N: Install cert, publish site<br/>child route-create-site · Ansible\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - publishes the replacement site beside the old one; Caddy reloads after validation\n    %%> - for `--web-root`, prepares the workload for the new root (Caddy access, PHP pool) and publishes with it\n    opt --domain\n      opt Laravel target\n        SW->>N: Write the new APP_URL<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/line\n      end\n    end\n    SW->>N: Probe the site over TLS<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · SNI domain\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the probe verifies the hostname and the Orbit CA; an application HTTP error does not fail it, and a failed probe stops the cutover\n    opt --publication, or a domain change on a public Route\n      SW->>N: Open or close the edge<br/>Ingress Caddy · ufw 80/443\n    end\n    opt --domain outside the TLD\n      SW->>V: Move private DNS<br/>records + catalog, then reload\n      Note over SW: Wait the DNS grace<br/>31 s: TTL 30 s + 1 s\n      %%> Why:\n      %%> - the old DNS answer has 31 seconds to expire before the old site is withdrawn\n    end\n    opt a lasting site is replaced\n      SW->>N: Withdraw the old site<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/file absent\n    end\n    alt every step succeeds\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event + outputs\n      Note over GW: Record result<br/>replacement active\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - a domain change makes the replacement Route active and deletes the old one; a publication-only or web-root change keeps the Route ID\n    else a step fails\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n      Note over GW: Mark the change failed<br/>rerun resumes\n      %%> Result before the cutover:\n      %%> - the old Route stays authoritative and the replacement is `failed`; a failed Caddy validation leaves the live file unchanged, removes the candidate and skips reload\n      %%> Result after the cutover:\n      %%> - a failure in the DNS grace or the old site's withdrawal leaves the replacement authoritative and the old Route `retiring`; a rerun finishes the withdrawal\n      %%> What `route:show` shows:\n      %%> - for a domain change, both Routes with `replacement_step`, `failed_step` and `error_code`; otherwise the one Route with `failed_step` and `error_code`\n      %%> On the Nodes:\n      %%> - finished steps stay; there is no rollback\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same request again to resume; a different domain or publication request changes nothing\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    end\n  else nothing to do on a Node\n    GW-->>CLI: 200 OK<br/>nothing to change\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the domain, publication or web root already has the requested value, or the Route is not yet published, so the replacement is created at once\n    %%> Skipped:\n    %%> - an identical request is reported as `Skipped — nothing to change (route.update_unchanged)`; no run starts\n  end\n"
  },
  "route/engine/update": {
   "kind": "route.update",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% route.update on the Gateway engine: a `--domain` change of a private Route in route/update.mmd (workflow operation-route-update), built from route.create's primitives and route/engine/destroy.mmd's withdrawal and sibling check. Design for ADR 0267 and ADR 0271; RouteUpdateEngineFlow implements it; its admission captures the guarded DNS input's rendered-from digests (`route_update.dns_guards`). Step names describe effects; the implementation settles action names unless an existing action is named.\n  %% Existing actions: `tls.ensure_csr`, `route.publish_site`, `dns.publish_records` (docs/reference/gateway-engine.md#routecreate). From route/engine/destroy.mmd: `route.withdraw_site`, sent over the lasting Caddyfile, and the guarded input of `dns.publish_records` (`dns.guarded_records`).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: route:update\n  %% kind: route.update\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane EN icon=workflow pill=engine tone=purple\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=executor tone=amber\n  %% lane V icon=server pill=executor tone=green\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant EN as Engine<br/>Gateway and NATS\n  participant N as Route Node<br/>executor\n  participant V as VPN Node<br/>DNS executor\n  CLI->>GW: route:update ROUTE<br/>at least one option\n  Note over GW: Validate, lock, record<br/>replacement pending\n  %%> Same validation as route/update.mmd; a domain change records the replacement Route `pending`, and the current Route stays authoritative until the cutover\n  alt nothing to do on a Node\n    Note over GW: Record the result<br/>no executor order\n    %%> As route/update.mmd: the value is already set, or the Route is not yet published and its replacement settles at once; the Gateway records the Operation's result and answers `202` with its Operation data, as RoutesController::update does for every update; an identical request is `route.update_unchanged`; neither starts a run\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation data, settled\n    %%> The engine admits nothing here; no run, no Node order\n  else Node work needed\n    Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes\n    %%> Admission holds the serving Nodes, as route/update.mmd; the engine start refuses `route.publication_held` in its bind transaction while any other open Route write writes one of its serving, edge, retired or VPN Nodes, and such a write is refused while this update is open\n    alt the engine admits the whole Operation\n      %%> Admission, one decision per Operation (EngineKinds::routes):\n      %%> - `route.update` is in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`\n      %%> - every Node a step touches reports its action in `$KV.NODES`: `tls.ensure_csr`, `route.publish_site` and the site withdrawal on each Route Node, `dns.publish_records` on each VPN Node\n      %%> - each VPN Node also lists the guarded DNS input in its reported `features` (as `instance.t3_pairing` is listed); the existing decoder refuses unknown fields, so without the feature the whole update stays on Swamp and never fails after a step acted\n      %%> - eligible plan: `--domain` on a private Route with no URL write; `APP_URL` lines, `--publication`, a public Route's domain change (Ingress edge, ufw 80/443), LAN rules, a whole `env_files` write (a private production domain) and any other URL write throw `EngineUnsupported`\n      Note over GW: Bind steps, take the lock<br/>commit, publish the start\n      %%> - the step rows and the Operation lock commit in one transaction before the start event; an Operation never moves between engines after this\n      GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n      Note over EN: Check the Route Nodes<br/>Gateway step · current\n      %%> Under the Node hold and outside any transaction: the stored Caddyfiles still equal what the catalog renders now, else `route.publication_stale` before any order; every other step waits for it\n      EN->>N: Make the key and CSR<br/>tls.ensure_csr · csr-i\n      %%> The key is made once and never leaves the Node; a resend answers a request for the same key\n      N-->>EN: CSR<br/>output\n      Note over EN: Sign the CSR<br/>Gateway step · sign-i\n      %%> `operation:direct:certificate-sign`; the Orbit CA key stays in the Gateway; a pending cancel refuses\n      EN->>N: Publish the new site<br/>route.publish_site · site-node\n      %%> Leaf, Caddy site, PHP-FPM pool and the admitted `APP_URL` lines, as route.create; the Caddyfile is validated before the move, and Caddy reloads on every publication, also on a replay, so a crash after the move still makes it live\n      N-->>EN: Files placed<br/>applied\n      EN->>N: Probe the new site<br/>Gateway step · SNI new domain\n      %%> TLS handshake over the WireGuard address, trusting only the Orbit root, as route.create's probe\n      opt domain outside the TLD\n        EN->>V: Move the DNS answers<br/>dns.publish_records · dns-i\n        %%> Records and catalog with the new name and without the old one; the guarded input carries, per file, the digest the Gateway rendered from and the digest of the desired bytes\n        %%> Per file: live equals desired (this step already wrote it, also one half of a pair) is kept; live equals the rendered-from digest is replaced; anything else refuses `owned_revision_conflict` and writes nothing; then `dnsmasq --test` and the restart run every time\n        V-->>EN: Files and restart<br/>applied · digests\n      end\n      Note over EN: Record the cutover<br/>replacement authoritative\n      %%> A Gateway step after the probe and, when present, the DNS move: from here the replacement is authoritative and the old Route `retiring`\n      opt domain outside the TLD\n        Note over EN: Wait the DNS grace<br/>31 s: TTL 30 s + 1 s\n        %%> A timed Gateway step from the stored DNS receipt (`done_ms`): until it ends the step waits as `waiting` without a worker, and a NATS schedule at its end runs it again; a resend or a retry never shortens it; the old site serves until it ends\n      end\n      EN->>N: Write the lasting site<br/>route.publish_site · final-node\n      %%> The Caddyfile without the old name and the late leaves, validated before the move and read back; no `APP_URL`\n      N-->>EN: Files placed<br/>applied\n      opt a lasting site is replaced\n        EN->>N: Withdraw the old site<br/>route family · withdraw-node\n        %%> The primitive of route/engine/destroy.mmd with the old domain's captured states; it never touches the new site; Caddy reloads on every run\n        N-->>EN: Old site absent<br/>read back\n        EN->>N: Probe a sibling Route<br/>TLS over WireGuard\n        %%> The sibling check of route/engine/destroy.mmd on each Node that served the old site, after its withdrawal: another Route on that Node still answers; skipped only for a Node that serves no other Route\n      end\n      opt the receipt is lost\n        EN->>N: Resend the same step<br/>fresh nonce · same step ID\n        %%> When:\n        %%> - the step passes its resend deadline with no receipt; two sends, one execution\n        %%> - the Operation keeps its lock, so the Route, its replacement and the held Nodes stay fenced; no other Operation and no Swamp run acts on them meanwhile\n        %%> On the Node:\n        %%> - the journal holds `done` for this Operation step, action and target: the executor answers the stored result with `repeat: true` and does not act again\n        %%> - an order still `open` replays under the target lock first; an order that expired before the lock did nothing (`executor.order_expired`), and the step is sent again\n        %%> - a replayed site or withdrawal step reloads Caddy again and a replayed DNS step restarts dnsmasq again, so files placed before a crash always become live\n        N-->>EN: Stored result<br/>repeat: true\n      end\n      alt every step verified\n        EN->>GW: Record replacement active<br/>delete the old Route\n        %%> The kind's own projection, the same rows a Swamp run records; the held Nodes are freed\n      else failure before the cutover\n        EN--xGW: Failed step<br/>error_code · redacted detail\n        Note over GW: Old Route authoritative<br/>replacement failed\n        %%> Gateway authority: the old Route stays authoritative and the replacement is `failed`\n        %%> What resolvers serve, per VPN Node; finished steps stay, no rollback:\n        %%> - a Node whose DNS step succeeded answers the new name and no longer the old one\n        %%> - a Node whose DNS step never reached it is untouched and still answers the old name\n        %%> - a Node whose `dns.publish_records` apply failed gets no promised answer: after `dns.restart_failed` its new files are already installed and not restored, so it may answer the new name or not answer at all\n        %%> Only a successful forward retry brings every VPN Node to the new records\n      else failure after the cutover\n        EN--xGW: Failed step<br/>error_code · redacted detail\n        Note over GW: Replacement authoritative<br/>old Route retiring\n        %%> A failure in the grace, the lasting site, the old site's withdrawal or the sibling check; the cutover step's `done` row is its acknowledgement, so every later failure is post-cutover and never demotes the replacement\n      end\n      %%> Retry is forward only: the same request resumes; before the cutover it publishes the replacement again (a made key is reused, files repeat with `applied: 0`), after it only the grace remainder, the lasting site, the withdrawal and the sibling check run, from the stored plan of the Operation that committed the cutover\n      %%> One chain, one engine: a retry of a Swamp-started update stays on Swamp; a Swamp start of an engine-started chain refuses `route.update_engine_chain`\n    else the engine does not admit it\n      Note over GW: Start the Swamp run<br/>operation-route-update\n      %%> The kind is not enabled, a Node lacks an action or the DNS feature, `$KV.NODES` is unreadable, or the flow throws `EngineUnsupported`: the whole Operation runs route/update.mmd; it never splits across engines\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "schedule/create": {
   "kind": "schedule.create",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 native Swamp schedules, one Swamp workflow per Schedule (Nick 2026-11-02), retained production Node timers use official resources; R1-R3 and rule 14.\n  %% Rule 23: 2026-11-03 required-only main path.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: schedule:create\n  %% kind: schedule.create\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: schedule:create report<br/>--instance=42 --calendar=@daily\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit schedule:create report --instance=42 --calendar='0 3 * * *' --command='php artisan report:send'`\n  %%> - syntax: `<name> (--node=ID | --instance=ID | --project=ID --for=production) --calendar=CALENDAR --command=COMMAND [--timeout=SECONDS] [--no-start] [--idempotency-key=KEY]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the name as the argument, unique for its Node or Instance\n  %%> - exactly one owner: a Node, an Instance, or a Project definition\n  %%> - the calendar and the command line\n  %%> Calendar:\n  %%> - a cron expression, the format Swamp schedules use, for example `0 3 * * *`, a 6-field form with seconds such as `20 * * * * *`, or a shorthand such as `@hourly` or `@daily`\n  %%> - it fires in serve's time zone (UTC); a Swamp trigger carries no time zone\n  %%> - existing systemd calendars are converted once during the migration\n  %%> Optional:\n  %%> - `--timeout=SECONDS`: the execution timeout; the default is 3,600 seconds; it adds no step\n  %%> - `--no-start`: create a Node or Instance Schedule disabled; it removes the trigger instead of setting it\n  %%> - `--idempotency-key` and `--json` change no step\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Validate, record Schedule<br/>Schedule 7f3c… · Instance 42\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs Instance 42\n  %%> - Instance 42 is active; a production Instance has a selected release\n  %%> - the name, calendar, command and timeout are valid\n  %%> - Instance 42 has no Schedule with this name and another specification (`schedule.retry_conflict`)\n  %%> Derives from the Placement:\n  %%> - the user, the working directory and the shell the command runs with\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - Schedule 7f3c… recorded, with its desired state `enabled`, or `disabled` with `--no-start`\n  %%> - an identical create returns the existing Schedule\n  %%> With `--project`:\n  %%> - only a Project Schedule definition is recorded (R1); the answer comes at once\n  %%> - production preparation later copies it into a disabled Instance Schedule; a cron calendar makes a native copy, a systemd calendar a Node timer\n  %%> Exception:\n  %%> - production's per-minute schedulers (`laravel-scheduler`, `powersync-health`) keep their systemd timer on the Node until the serve load and outage test passes (ADR 0244)\n  %%> - all other Node and Instance Schedules are native Swamp schedules on 209\n  alt a retained production timer copy\n    GW->>SW: Start timer installation<br/>operation-schedule-create\n    %%> Inputs:\n    %%> - the captured public Schedule intent for the Node timer exception\n    Note over SW: units\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - render the public service and timer units without command bytes\n    SW->>N: files\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - apply both unit files through official file resources\n    SW->>N: reload\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - reload systemd after the unit files are written\n    SW->>N: timer\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - apply the captured enabled and running state through official systemd\n    SW->>N: remove_obsolete\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - remove the obsolete script and completion files through official file resources\n    SW->>N: check_files\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - check both unit files and the absence of the two obsolete files\n    SW->>N: check_timer\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - check the captured timer state through official systemd\n    Note over SW: Check the installation\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - native assert requires every resource check to be compliant\n    SW-->>GW: Native completion<br/>resource checks passed\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Gateway records readiness from the same terminal history read\n  else a native cron Schedule\n    GW->>SW: Store the command<br/>vault: schedule-command-7f3c…\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - pushes the command line to a Swamp vault key on 209 (R3)\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the command is operator input; it never goes into workflow files, run inputs or step output\n    GW->>SW: Create the workflow<br/>workflow.create schedule-7f3c…\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - creates one small Swamp workflow for this Schedule on serve (Node 209), then writes its content with `workflow.edit`\n    %%> - its one step calls the shared `task-command-run` as a nested workflow\n    %%> - nothing is installed on Node 12; there is no Node work, so no run starts (R1)\n    %%> Fixed inside:\n    %%> - the full intent: Schedule 7f3c…, Node 12's address and SSH host keys, the user, the working directory, the shell and the timeout\n    %%> - the command's vault key name, never the command itself\n    %%> Stored:\n    %%> - in serve's `workflows/` directory beside the checked-in workflows; a release load keeps it\n    %%> - the Gateway's Schedule record stays the source; an identical create writes it again\n    GW->>SW: Set its trigger<br/>workflow.trigger.set\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - sets the cron trigger of `schedule-7f3c…` on serve\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - always, unless `--no-start` replaces it with the next step\n    opt --no-start\n      GW->>SW: Remove the trigger<br/>workflow.trigger.remove\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - replaces \"Set its trigger\": removes any trigger of `schedule-7f3c…` on serve\n      %%> - the Schedule is recorded `disabled`; `schedule:enable` sets the trigger later\n    end\n    alt Swamp confirms\n      SW-->>GW: workflow and trigger set<br/>next fire 03:00\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the workflow exists and its trigger is active, with its next fire time\n      GW-->>CLI: Schedule created<br/>active · next fire 03:00\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - Schedule 7f3c… becomes active, only after Swamp confirms\n      %%> - with `--no-start`: active and disabled, with no trigger\n      %%> - task #5140 is recorded as succeeded at once (R1)\n      %%> What you get:\n      %%> - the Schedule and its next fire, at once; there is nothing to follow\n    else Swamp refuses or is down\n      SW--xGW: no confirmation<br/>refused or unreachable\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - Swamp refuses the calendar, or serve on 209 does not answer\n      Note over GW: Mark the Schedule failed<br/>rerun the same create\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - Schedule 7f3c… stays recorded, marked failed, with its error code\n      %%> - the vault key and a created workflow without a trigger may stay; they start nothing\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same command again; an identical create writes the workflow and its trigger again\n      GW--xCLI: create failed<br/>schedule.calendar_invalid or 503\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - `schedule.calendar_invalid` (422) for a refused calendar\n      %%> - `swamp.schedule_publish_unavailable` (503) when serve does not answer\n    end\n    loop each calendar fire\n      Note over SW: Start a root run<br/>schedule-7f3c…\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - serve fires the trigger and starts one root run of `schedule-7f3c…` on 209\n      %%> - its step runs `task-command-run` with the fixed intent, within the Schedule's timeout\n      %%> Timing:\n      %%> - serve runs up to 4 scheduled runs at once; a fire can start late when all are busy\n      %%> - a Schedule never overlaps itself; a fire due while the last one runs waits\n      SW->>N: Run the command<br/>@swamp/ssh, as the Schedule's user\n      %%> How:\n      %%> - checks the user and the working directory on Node 12\n      %%> - reads the command with `vault.get` and sends it on stdin\n      %%> Runs as:\n      %%> - the Schedule's user, in its working directory, with its shell\n      N-->>SW: exit code, output\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the exit code; the output stays in the Swamp step\n      SW-->>GW: Run finished<br/>read each minute\n      %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n      %%> - a Gateway job lists the runs that finished, once a minute; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n      Note over GW: Record the fire<br/>task #5141 · trigger: schedule\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - each fire becomes a task, with trigger `schedule`, recorded when it ends\n      %%> - when it ends, the task gets the nested result, such as `schedule.target_unavailable` or `schedule.run_failed`\n      %%> - `last_run_at` and `last_run_status` of Schedule 7f3c… are set when it ends\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "schedule/destroy": {
   "kind": "schedule.destroy",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 native Swamp schedules, one Swamp workflow per Schedule (Nick 2026-11-02), nothing installed on Nodes; R1-R3.\n  %% Rule 23: 2026-11-03 required-only main path.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: schedule:destroy\n  %% kind: schedule.destroy\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: schedule:destroy 7f3c…<br/>Schedule UUID\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit schedule:destroy 7f3c9a2e-41d0-4b8a-9c11-2f6e0d5b7a10`\n  %%> - syntax: `<schedule> [--project=ID] [--yes] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the UUID of the Schedule\n  %%> Other form:\n  %%> - `<name> --project=ID` deletes a Project Schedule definition; see below\n  %%> Optional:\n  %%> - `--yes` skips the prompt; `--idempotency-key` and `--json` change no step\n  %%> Confirmation:\n  %%> - without `--yes`, the CLI asks first; the default is No\n  %%> - without a terminal, or with `--json`, `--yes` is required\n  %%> With `--project`:\n  %%> - only the Project Schedule definition is deleted (R1); Instance copies stay\n  Note over GW: Validate, mark removing<br/>Schedule 7f3c… · Instance 42\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs Instance 42\n  %%> - Schedule 7f3c… exists; a failed or disabled Schedule can be destroyed too\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - Schedule 7f3c… is marked removing; its trigger and workflow are removed next\n  %%> - there is no Node work, so no run starts (R1)\n  %%> Not refused:\n  %%> - a running fire no longer blocks destroy; `schedule.run_active` is gone\n  alt retained Node timer copy\n    GW->>SW: Start one run<br/>operation-schedule-destroy\n    opt standalone removal\n      SW->>N: idle\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - one bounded SSH argv reads the service state\n      Note over SW: Check the service is idle\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - native assert accepts inactive, failed or unknown; active and transitional states refuse removal before mutation\n    end\n    SW->>N: stop_timer\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - official systemd stops and disables the timer\n    opt Instance cascade\n      SW->>N: stop_service\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - one bounded SSH argv stops the service, including an activating oneshot; standalone removal skips this step\n    end\n    SW->>N: remove_files\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - official file resources remove four managed paths in parallel\n    SW->>N: reload\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - one bounded SSH argv reloads systemd after file removal\n    SW->>N: check_files\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - official file checks confirm all four paths are absent, in parallel with the timer check\n    SW->>N: check_timer\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - official systemd checks the timer is stopped and disabled\n    Note over SW: Check the removal\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - native assert requires absent files and a compliant, unloaded timer\n    SW-->>GW: Native completion<br/>removal check passed\n    Note over GW: Delete the timer copy\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - delete only after success; failure keeps the copy recorded as removing\n  else a native cron Schedule\n    GW->>SW: Remove the trigger<br/>workflow.trigger.remove\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - removes the cron trigger of `schedule-7f3c…` from serve (Node 209); serve starts no new fire\n  %%> - removing a trigger that is gone is safe, so a disabled Schedule takes the same path\n  GW->>SW: Delete the workflow<br/>workflow.delete schedule-7f3c…\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - deletes the Schedule's own workflow from serve\n  %%> - a fire that already started keeps running from its own copy\n  GW->>SW: Delete the command key<br/>vault.delete\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - deletes the Schedule command key after its trigger and workflow\n  alt Swamp confirms\n    SW-->>GW: trigger and workflow gone\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - serve starts no new fire for Schedule 7f3c…\n    Note over GW: Delete the Schedule<br/>its tasks stay as history\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Schedule 7f3c…'s record is deleted\n    %%> - its tasks stay, so past fires remain visible\n    %%> - task #5141 is recorded as succeeded at once (R1)\n    %%> Vault key:\n    %%> - the command's vault key is deleted; a fire that already started read it when it began\n    GW-->>CLI: Schedule destroyed<br/>202 Accepted · task succeeded\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the result at once; there is nothing to follow\n  else Swamp refuses or is down\n    SW--xGW: no confirmation<br/>refused or unreachable\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - serve on 209 does not answer\n    Note over GW: Keep Schedule removing<br/>rerun the same destroy\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Schedule 7f3c… stays recorded, marked removing\n    %%> - the trigger or the workflow may still be set; a fire it starts still runs and is recorded as a task\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again; removing a trigger or workflow that is gone is safe\n    GW--xCLI: 503 removal unconfirmed<br/>swamp.schedule_publish_unavailable\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the error code; the Schedule stays removing\n  end\n  opt a fire is still running\n    SW->>N: Finish the running fire<br/>@swamp/ssh, own timeout\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the fire that already started runs to its end, within the Schedule's timeout\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - stopping a command halfway can leave the application in a worse state\n    SW-->>GW: Run finished<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record the fire<br/>task #5139 · trigger: schedule\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the fire's task, recorded when it started, gets its result, although the Schedule is gone\n  end\n  end\n"
  },
  "schedule/disable": {
   "kind": "schedule.disable",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 native Swamp schedules, one Swamp workflow per Schedule (Nick 2026-11-02), nothing installed on Nodes; R1.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: schedule:disable\n  %% kind: schedule.disable\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: schedule:disable 7f3c…<br/>Schedule UUID\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit schedule:disable 7f3c9a2e-41d0-4b8a-9c11-2f6e0d5b7a10`\n  %%> - syntax: `<schedule> [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the UUID of the Schedule, as `schedule:list` shows it\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - to stop the calendar fires for a while; `schedule:enable` starts them again\n  %%> Not stopped:\n  %%> - a fire that already started finishes, and its result is still recorded\n  Note over GW: Validate the Schedule<br/>Schedule 7f3c… · Instance 42\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs Instance 42\n  %%> - Schedule 7f3c… is active, not failed or removing\n  %%> - its trigger is native; a per-minute production scheduler keeps its Node timer and cannot be disabled here\n  %%> Already disabled:\n  %%> - the trigger is removed again; disable is idempotent\n  GW->>SW: Remove the trigger<br/>workflow.trigger.remove\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - removes the cron trigger of `schedule-7f3c…` on serve (Node 209); the workflow stays\n  %%> - there is no Node work, so no run starts (R1)\n  alt Swamp confirms\n    SW-->>GW: trigger removed<br/>no next fire\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the workflow has no trigger; serve starts no new fire\n    Note over GW: Record result<br/>desired state: disabled\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Schedule 7f3c… is recorded as disabled, only after Swamp confirms\n    %%> - task #5140 is recorded as succeeded at once (R1)\n    GW-->>CLI: Schedule disabled<br/>no next fire\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the result at once; there is nothing to follow\n  else Swamp refuses or is down\n    SW--xGW: no confirmation<br/>refused or unreachable\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - serve on 209 does not answer, or refuses the trigger\n    Note over GW: Keep the prior state<br/>desired state: enabled\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Schedule 7f3c… stays enabled; nothing changed\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again; it is safe\n    GW--xCLI: 503 publish unavailable<br/>swamp.schedule_publish_unavailable\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the error code; the Schedule is unchanged\n  end\n"
  },
  "schedule/enable": {
   "kind": "schedule.enable",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 native Swamp schedules, one Swamp workflow per Schedule (Nick 2026-11-02), nothing installed on Nodes; R1.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: schedule:enable\n  %% kind: schedule.enable\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: schedule:enable 7f3c…<br/>Schedule UUID\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit schedule:enable 7f3c9a2e-41d0-4b8a-9c11-2f6e0d5b7a10`\n  %%> - syntax: `<schedule> [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the UUID of the Schedule, as `schedule:list` shows it\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - after `schedule:create --no-start`, or for a copy that production preparation created disabled\n  %%> Disable:\n  %%> - `schedule:disable` is the same flow in reverse; see its own chart\n  Note over GW: Validate the Schedule<br/>Schedule 7f3c… · Instance 42\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs Instance 42\n  %%> - Schedule 7f3c… is active, not failed or removing\n  %%> - its trigger is native; a per-minute production scheduler keeps its Node timer, and enable starts that timer as before\n  %%> - Instance 42 is still on Node 12\n  %%> Already enabled:\n  %%> - the trigger is set again; enable is idempotent\n  alt a retained production Node timer\n    GW->>SW: Start one run<br/>operation-schedule-control\n    %%> Inputs:\n    %%> - the admitted Schedule UUID and Node SSH host pins\n    SW->>N: timer\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - official systemd enables and starts the retained timer\n    SW->>N: check_timer\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - official systemd checks that the timer is enabled and active\n    Note over SW: Check the timer\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - native assert requires the systemd check to be compliant\n    SW-->>GW: Native completion<br/>timer check passed\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - record the desired state from the existing terminal history read\n  else a native cron Schedule\n    opt copy has no workflow yet\n      GW->>SW: Store the command<br/>vault: schedule-command-7f3c…\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - stores the copied definition's command in the vault before creating its workflow\n      GW->>SW: Create the workflow<br/>workflow.create, workflow.edit\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - creates the copied Schedule's workflow with its fixed intent and command key\n      %%> - an existing workflow is kept as it is\n    end\n    GW->>SW: Set its trigger<br/>workflow.trigger.set\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - sets the cron trigger of the Schedule's own workflow `schedule-7f3c…` on serve (Node 209)\n    %%> - there is no Node work, so no run starts (R1)\n    %%> Inputs:\n    %%> - the workflow name and the calendar; the intent stays fixed inside the workflow\n    alt Swamp confirms\n      SW-->>GW: trigger set<br/>next fire 03:00\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the trigger is active, with its next fire time\n      Note over GW: Record result<br/>desired state: enabled\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - Schedule 7f3c… is recorded as enabled, only after Swamp confirms\n      %%> - task #5140 is recorded as succeeded at once (R1)\n      GW-->>CLI: Schedule enabled<br/>next fire 03:00\n      %%> What you get:\n      %%> - the result at once; there is nothing to follow\n    else Swamp refuses or is down\n      SW--xGW: no confirmation<br/>refused or unreachable\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - serve on 209 does not answer, or refuses the trigger\n      Note over GW: Keep the prior state<br/>desired state: disabled\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - Schedule 7f3c… stays disabled; nothing changed\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - run the same command again; it is safe\n      GW--xCLI: 503 publish unavailable<br/>swamp.schedule_publish_unavailable\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the error code; the Schedule is unchanged\n    end\n    loop each calendar fire\n      Note over SW: Start a root run<br/>schedule-7f3c…\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - serve fires the trigger and starts one root run of `schedule-7f3c…` on 209\n      %%> - its step runs `task-command-run` with the fixed intent, within the Schedule's timeout\n      %%> Timing:\n      %%> - serve runs up to 4 scheduled runs at once; a fire can start late when all are busy\n      SW->>N: Run the command<br/>@swamp/ssh, as the Schedule's user\n      %%> How:\n      %%> - checks the user and the working directory on Node 12\n      %%> - reads the command with `vault.get` and sends it on stdin\n      %%> Runs as:\n      %%> - the Schedule's user, in its working directory, with its shell\n      N-->>SW: exit code\n      %%> Returns:\n      %%> - the exit code; command output stays in the Node journal\n      SW-->>GW: Run finished<br/>read each minute\n      %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n      %%> - a Gateway job lists the runs that finished, once a minute; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n      Note over GW: Record the fire<br/>task #5141 · trigger: schedule\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - each fire becomes a task, with trigger `schedule`, recorded when it ends\n      %%> - `last_run_at` and `last_run_status` of Schedule 7f3c… are set\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "schedule/engine/enable": {
   "kind": "schedule.enable",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% schedule.enable of a retained host Node timer on the Gateway engine: the timer path of schedule/enable.mmd (workflow operation-schedule-control) as one executor primitive. A `production_control` plan (workflow production-schedule-control: the timer inside the selected live release) stays on Swamp. ADR 0267 and ADR 0271; ScheduleEnableEngineFlow, primitive `schedule.ensure_timer`. Runtime proof on a nested fleet is pending.\n  %% A native cron Schedule has no Node timer: it keeps the synchronous Swamp trigger set and its succeeded Operation, with no executor order.\n  %% status: built\n  %% command: schedule:enable\n  %% kind: schedule.enable\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane EN icon=workflow pill=engine tone=purple\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=executor tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant EN as Engine<br/>Gateway and NATS\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>executor\n  CLI->>GW: schedule:enable 7f3c…<br/>Schedule UUID\n  Note over GW: Validate the Schedule<br/>Schedule 7f3c… · Instance 42\n  %%> Same validation as schedule/enable.mmd\n  alt native cron Schedule\n    Note over GW: Set the Swamp trigger<br/>no executor order\n    %%> As schedule/enable.mmd (NativeSchedules::enable): the Gateway sets the workflow trigger; after Swamp confirms it records a succeeded `schedule.enable` Operation with its result and answers with its ID; a refusal keeps the prior state and records none\n    %%> The engine admits nothing here; no run, no Node order\n  else production_control plan\n    Note over GW: Refuse the engine<br/>EngineUnsupported\n    %%> A timer inside a production release container (`production_control`): verifying the selected live release, enabling the timer inside it and updating its release record stay on production-schedule-control; the whole Operation runs there\n  else retained host Node timer\n    alt the engine admits the whole Operation\n      %%> Admission, one decision per Operation (EngineKinds::routes):\n      %%> - `schedule.enable` is in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`\n      %%> - every Node a step touches reports its action in `$KV.NODES`: the timer action of the Schedule's Node\n      %%> - the flow accepts the plan shape; a part it does not cover throws `EngineUnsupported` before any order\n      %%> - eligible plan: a host timer on the Schedule's Node, no `production_control` and no `container`\n      Note over GW: Bind steps, take the lock<br/>commit, publish the start\n      %%> - the step rows and the Schedule's own Operation lock (`schedule:<uuid>`; the owner's other Schedules stay free) commit in one transaction before the start event; an Operation never moves between engines after this\n      %%> - binding refuses `operation.target_busy` while another open Operation acts on this Schedule; while the lock is held, any admission for this Schedule, a create's reservation included, refuses `operation.target_busy` before it changes the row\n      GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n      EN->>N: Enable and start the timer<br/>owned unit only\n      %%> Desired state: the Schedule's own timer is enabled and active; an already-running timer only gets its enablement checked\n      %%> The unit name comes from the admitted Schedule; any other unit refuses with `schedule.input_invalid`\n      N-->>EN: Observed timer<br/>enabled · active · next elapse\n      %%> Verification read-back in the same primitive, as Swamp's `Check the timer`: LoadState=loaded, UnitFileState=enabled, ActiveState=active, and NextElapseUSecRealtime answered (empty when the calendar has none left); a property systemd leaves out fails the step\n      opt the receipt is lost\n        EN->>N: Resend the same step<br/>fresh nonce · same step ID\n        %%> When:\n        %%> - the step passes its resend deadline with no receipt; two sends, one execution\n        %%> - the Operation keeps its lock, so Schedule 7f3c… stays fenced; no other Operation and no Swamp run acts on it meanwhile\n        %%> On the Node:\n        %%> - the journal holds `done` for this Operation step, action and target: the executor answers the stored result with `repeat: true` and does not act again\n        %%> - an order still `open` replays under the target lock first; an order that expired before the lock did nothing (`executor.order_expired`), and the step is sent again\n        N-->>EN: Stored result<br/>repeat: true\n      end\n      alt every step verified\n        EN->>GW: Record Schedule enabled<br/>desired state: enabled\n        %%> - the kind's own projection records the result, the same rows a Swamp run records\n      else a step fails\n        EN--xGW: Failed step<br/>error_code · redacted detail\n        %%> - a deterministic refusal is a terminal receipt too; the journal keeps it and a resend repeats it\n        %%> - the step keeps a code for its phase (`schedule.input_invalid`, `schedule.timer_missing`, `schedule.apply_failed` for the change, `schedule.read_failed` for the read-back read, `schedule.state_mismatch` for a read of another state); the Operation records what Swamp records: `operation.native_failed`, failed step `timer`, `check_timer` or `Check the timer`; a cancel records no error code, the step code in the failure's detail\n        Note over GW: Keep the prior state<br/>desired state unchanged\n        %%> - retry: the same command; enabling an enabled timer changes nothing\n      end\n    else the engine does not admit it\n      Note over GW: Start the Swamp run<br/>operation-schedule-control\n      %%> - the kind is not enabled, a Node lacks the action, `$KV.NODES` is unreadable, or the flow throws `EngineUnsupported`: the whole Operation runs operation-schedule-control (schedule/enable.mmd)\n      %%> - no step of it runs on the engine; an Operation never splits across engines\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "schedule/install-instance-definitions": {
   "kind": "schedule.install_instance_definitions",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: captured Instance Schedule definitions, flow-tighten rule 14. Native child completion is the installation receipt.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% kind: schedule.install_instance_definitions\n  autonumber\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>Instance runtime\n  Note over GW: Capture definitions<br/>Instance 42\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - capture the existing definition copies and their public runtime intent\n  %%> - keep the admitted owner and Node for every member\n  GW->>SW: Start one run<br/>captured members\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - each Schedule UUID, desired state and SSH host pins\n  %%> - no Schedule command or execution credential is copied into the parent\n  loop each captured copy\n    SW->>N: members\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the existing child installs the captured service and timer with official cfgmgmt resources\n    %%> - copies share one Node and converge in sequence; a disabled copy stays stopped\n  end\n  Note over SW: Check the members\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - native assert confirms that all expanded member steps succeeded\n  %%> - an empty captured set completes without Node work\n  alt native run succeeded\n    SW-->>GW: Native completion<br/>all members installed\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the existing history read confirms the end check; no shell prints a completion marker\n    Note over GW: Record installed copies<br/>keep desired state\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - mark the captured copies active without enabling a disabled timer\n  else a member failed\n    N--xSW: Native member failure\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - keep completed Node changes and the captured definition records\n    SW--xGW: Native failed result<br/>failed step and error code\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - mark the captured copies failed so the owner can investigate and rerun\n  end\n"
  },
  "schedule/logs": {
   "kind": "schedule.logs",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: schedule:logs\n  %% kind: schedule.logs\n  autonumber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP\n  participant GW as Gateway\n  participant SW as Swamp\n  participant N as Node\n  alt CLI --follow\n    Note over CLI: Refuse Schedule follow<br/>schedule.follow_unavailable\n  else bounded snapshot\n    CLI->>GW: schedule:logs <schedule>\n    alt migrated Instance Schedule\n      Note over GW: Stopped container refuses<br/>schedule.target_inactive · no wake\n      GW->>SW: Read container journal<br/>shared runtime inspection\n      SW->>N: Read Schedule logs<br/>Ansible · schedule-logs\n      N-->>SW: Bounded journal read\n      %%> Limits:\n      %%> - complete entries within 4 MiB of UTF-8 bytes and the 8 MiB JSON transport limit, with 16 KiB reserved for the envelope\n      SW-->>GW: Private vault reference\n      Note over GW: Read private output<br/>Redact stored values\n    else other Schedule\n      Note over GW: Read pinned Agent journal<br/>Redact stored values\n    end\n    GW-->>CLI: Snapshot and request ID\n  end\n"
  },
  "schedule/run": {
   "kind": "schedule.run",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 one task system, native Swamp schedules; DECISIONS Schedules (Nick 2026-11-02), R2-R4.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 5, 14, 15, 22 (no check script, no report step; context enforced by the transient unit).\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: schedule:run\n  %% kind: schedule.run\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: schedule:run 7f3c…<br/>Schedule UUID\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit schedule:run 7f3c9a2e-41d0-4b8a-9c11-2f6e0d5b7a10`\n  %%> - syntax: `<uuid> [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the UUID of the Schedule, as `schedule:list` shows it\n  %%> Not changed:\n  %%> - the Schedule's timing; the next calendar fire stays as it was\n  %%> - an enabled Schedule stays enabled, and a disabled one stays disabled\n  Note over GW: Admit a task from template<br/>task #5140 · trigger: operator\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs Instance 42\n  %%> - Schedule 7f3c… is active, enabled or disabled\n  %%> - Instance 42 is still on Node 12\n  %%> - the Schedule still targets the recorded Node, user and working directory\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5140 recorded from the Schedule's template, with trigger `operator`\n  %%> - the same intent a calendar fire gets: user, working directory, shell, timeout and command\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5140\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  opt Instance migration holds admission\n    Note over GW: Wait for migration<br/>Then admit final context\n  end\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>task-command-run\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one root run of `task-command-run` on serve (Node 209), the workflow each calendar fire of `schedule-7f3c…` runs nested\n  %%> Why not `schedule-7f3c…` itself:\n  %%> - its inputs are fixed and carry no task number; the task's own run carries it, so the Gateway projects it like any task\n  %%> - every root run of `schedule-7f3c…` is then a calendar fire\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role\n  %%> - the user, the working directory, the shell and the timeout\n  %%> - the command's vault key name, never the command (R3)\n  SW->>N: Run the command<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · systemd-run\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - one `@swamp/ssh exec`: `sudo systemd-run --uid=<user> --wait --pipe --collect` with the admitted directory and timeout\n  %%> - reads the command with `vault.get` and sends it to Node 12 on stdin, never in argv; systemd-cat journals output on the Node\n  %%> Runs as:\n  %%> - the Schedule's user, in its working directory, with its shell\n  %%> - production Instances resolve `current` at start, so a new release changes later runs\n  %%> Timeout:\n  %%> - the Schedule's timeout is the run's time budget\n  alt exit 0 in time\n    N-->>SW: exit 0\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the exit code; command output stays in the Node journal\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record result<br/>task succeeded · last run\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - task #5140 succeeds\n    %%> - `last_run_at` and `last_run_status` of Schedule 7f3c… are set\n  else fails or times out\n    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or timeout\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - SSH fails, `sudo` or `cd` fails, the command exits non-zero, or it runs past its timeout\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the step ends the run with its own exit code; there is no report step\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - the exit code, mapped to `schedule.run_failed`; the failed step preserves native setup or transport diagnostics\n    Note over GW: Mark the task failed<br/>Schedule state unchanged\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - task #5140 fails; the Schedule's last run is marked as an error\n    %%> - the Schedule itself and its trigger stay as they were\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - whatever the command changed before it failed stays; Orbit does not roll it back\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run `schedule:run` again after fixing the cause; it is a new task\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5140\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "task/cancel": {
   "kind": "tasks.cancel",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: orchestrator 2026-11-08: keeping approved work is a precondition of the cancel, not a step of it. 2026-11-15: the task VMs and their release are removed (docs/reference/old-paths.md); a cancel starts no run. 2026-11-16: the place release workflows are deleted; `operation-task-cancel` keeps its inputs and its end check, and calls no release.\n  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-13: a task copy ends through its Instance destroy. Orchestrator 2026-11-14: work that only the copy holds, or a copy that cannot be read, refuses the cancel unless the caller discards it; the cancel is recorded under the group lock, in one transaction with the destroy's admission, after the destroy's reads. A commit a publish pushed counts as on origin. 2026-11-16 (group 5615): published means content on the default branch, not commit SHAs: the copy's content read, made by the `base` phase that task:sync runs, decides for committed work, so a squash-merged or hand-carried copy cancels without discard.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: task:cancel\n  %% kind: tasks.cancel\n  autonumber\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant GH as GitHub<br/>repository\n  Note over GW: Check before any change<br/>cancellable\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the group is cancellable; otherwise `tasks.not_cancellable`\n  %%> - every refusal here and in the next step comes before the cancel stops running work, so a refused cancel changes nothing\n  GW->>GH: Compare the task branch<br/>approved...task-42\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - resolves the group's latest approved commit: the latest commit Orbit reviewed (ADR 0203), or else the commit of the latest approval\n  %%> - reads origin's task branch head and compares it with that commit, with a `contents: read` token\n  %%> - only when GitHub answers that the branch does not exist (404) and the group's pull request merged does that pull request's head stand in; the pull request is read with a `pull_requests: read` token\n  %%> - skipped when the group has no approval, or holds no workspace\n  %%> - for a task copy, content on the default branch counts too: the copy's latest content read (`work`, of a `task:sync`) is of the copy's current head, holds the approved commit in its history and found every committed path on the default branch\n  GH-->>GW: Branch reaches it\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - otherwise the cancel refuses with HTTP 409 `tasks.approved_work_unpublished`, names the commit and the branch, and admits nothing: run `task:publish` first\n  %%> - every other outcome refuses the same way: a GitHub read that fails, a branch that is gone without a merged pull request\n  alt the task owns a task copy\n    Note over GW: Read the copy's workspace<br/>one bounded read\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - one SSH call to the copy's Node, bounded at 4 s, reads what the copy holds beyond its seed: commits that origin lacks, uncommitted, untracked and stashed files\n    %%> - a copy never fetches, so the commits that the group's publishes pushed are named to the read as on origin, with their history\n    %%> - a stopped or unreachable copy reads `unknown` with its reason\n    %%> - committed work counts by content, not by commit id: when the copy's latest content read (`work` of a `task:sync`, docs/reference/tasks.md#published-work-of-a-copy) is of the head this read sees and found every committed path on the default branch, the checked-out branch's own commits count as published, as after a squash merge or a hand-carry; a commit that only another local branch holds still counts\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - unless the caller discards the work, a copy that holds work (`dirty`: unpublished commits, uncommitted or untracked files, stashes) refuses the cancel with HTTP 409 `tasks.copy_unpublished_work`; its details carry the counts, the head, the commits the read counted and the content verdict `work`: `on_default`, `work_not_on_default`, or `work_unread` when no content read is of this head (run `task:sync`, then cancel again)\n    %%> - unless the caller discards it, a copy that cannot be read (`unknown`) refuses with HTTP 409 `tasks.copy_workspace_unknown`, naming the reason\n    %%> - a `clean` copy goes on; both refusals come before the cancel stops running work, so they change nothing\n    Note over GW: Record, then admit destroy<br/>one transaction · group lock\n    %%> Requirements:\n    %%> - no Operation holds the copy, such as its create or an earlier destroy; otherwise HTTP 409 `operation.target_busy`, naming it\n    %%> - after the group's running work was asked to stop, no Operation still holds the group, such as a timed-out one whose run nothing has proven ended; otherwise HTTP 409 `operation.target_busy`, naming it, and no destroy is admitted\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the destroy's checks and the Schedule stop come first, outside any transaction: its replication slot check over SSH, then the stop of the copy's Schedules (`schedule.destroy` Operations and their removal in Swamp)\n    %%> - then, in the destroy's one transaction, records the cancel under the group's lock key first and admits the normal `instance.destroy` of the copy with force\n    %%> - an Operation that takes the group lock first makes the record refuse with HTTP 409 `operation.target_busy`; the transaction rolls back, so a refused cancel never admits the destroy; the copy's Schedules stay stopped\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the cancel is recorded `running` without the group lock; its result names the destroy Operation and holds the workspace read\n    %%> - the destroy runs as every Instance destroy does; the cancel ends when it ends and has no budget of its own: the destroy's budget bounds both, except a signed clone removal on the Gateway engine, which stays fenced past its budget until its Node returns the result\n    %%> - while it waits, the cancel blocks no other admission of the task; `operation:cancel` on it fails with HTTP 409 `tasks.cancel_waits_for_destroy`, naming the destroy; the reconciler ends one whose destroy ended without it\n    %%> - when the destroy succeeds, the task, a `failed` one included, and its open subtasks are `cancelled`, the task lets go of the copy, and the cancel succeeds; a task that a completion completed meanwhile stays `completed`\n    %%> - when it fails or its budget expires, the cancel fails with the destroy's error code, such as `instance.removal_incomplete` or `operation.timed_out`; a later receipt that corrects the destroy's end corrects the cancel; the task runs on and keeps the copy; the next cancel admits the destroy again; a stopped or half-removed copy may read `unknown`, so that cancel may need the discard\n  else no task copy and no workspace\n    Note over GW: End the task at admission<br/>R1: no run\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the task holds no workspace, such as a `todo` task or one whose task VM was removed; its open Operations are cancelled first\n    %%> - a task that still holds a workspace that is no task copy (a task VM workspace) refuses with HTTP 409 `tasks.workspace_ownership_refused`: nothing can release it any more\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - in the admission transaction, the task and its open subtasks are `cancelled` and the Operation is recorded as succeeded; no Swamp run starts (R1)\n    %%> - the HTTP 202 already carries the succeeded Operation, so `task:show` reads `cancelled` from then on\n  end\n"
  },
  "task/check": {
   "kind": "tasks.check",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: docs/reference/tasks.md#check-an-external-group and the Instance workspace phases in docs/reference/task-instance-workspace.md.\n  %% Execution: task-instance-check owns the copy phases and the typed check result.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: task:check\n  %% kind: tasks.check\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane I icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant I as Instance<br/>via its Node\n  CLI->>GW: task:check 7<br/> --phase=handoff\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit task:check 7 --phase=handoff`\n  %%> - syntax: `<task> --phase=baseline|handoff [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  Note over GW: Admit the check<br/>check 17 · task #5170\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - group 42 is open and external, with its own task copy, else `tasks.no_workspace` (\"The group requires its task copy.\"): task VM workspaces are removed\n  %%> - subtask 7 is the current open subtask, else `tasks.subtask_not_current`\n  %%> - a baseline only before any implementer started, else `tasks.baseline_done`\n  %%> - `task:start` of a Project whose default is a container, or of a nested-fleet Project, creates that ownership with a task copy\n  %%> - that workspace's last completion did not fail, else `tasks.copy_completion_failed` with its Operation, failed step, error code and the retry `orbit instance:complete ID`; and it is `active`, else `tasks.copy_not_ready`\n  %%> - that workspace needs a running runtime, else `tasks.instance_not_running`, and a recorded branch, else `tasks.no_workspace`\n  %%> - that workspace refuses a handoff that needs hosted CI (`tasks.instance_hosted_check_unavailable`), before a check row exists\n  %%> - its baseline has an empty setup list: the copied seed already ran the Project's setup steps\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - check 17 recorded as running; the full intent holds the Project check, setup (baseline), deliverables (handoff), base commit and the Instance Node's SSH target\n  %%> - `workspace` holds the recorded Incus project and container, the Instance branch and a new scratch ID; the SSH target is its Node\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-check\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - the copy intent; hosted CI is refused at admission\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5170\n    SW->>I: Check in the Instance\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - one child workflow runs the whole check through Ansible phases on the Instance Node; every repository command runs in the container as `agent` in `/srv/workspace`, with the agent's HOME and login shell\n    %%> - a scoped Project check such as `bin/task-check` prints one summary line per part; its `ci-policy` part runs the CI policy tests (`python3 -m unittest discover -s tools -p 'test_ci_*.py'`) when the change touches what those tests read: the CI registry and its fixture tomls, the workflows, the CI scripts or a Swamp test file, before Pest and the fixtures, within the check's budget\n    %%> - see [task Instance workspace](/reference/task-instance-workspace#check-phases)\n    %%> Base first (handoff only):\n    %%> - its child first runs `task-copy-base`, phase `base`, the same one `task:sync` runs ([Bring main into a copy](/reference/tasks#bring-main-into-a-copy)): the Node fetches the current default branch head into the copy's mirror and the copy gets it in `refs/remotes/origin/<default>`\n    %%> - the read token follows the sync's rules: the Gateway mints it as the run starts, the Node's one fetch reads it as a Git header through stdin, and it never reaches the copy\n    %%> - a failed base (mirror or GitHub down) does not block the check: the check runs on the tree as it is, and the result says `base: unavailable` with `base_reason`; the base's scratch cleanup runs after it, whatever it did\n    %%> - then the `base-merge` phase, as `agent` in the copy: `git merge-tree --write-tree HEAD refs/remotes/origin/<default>`; HEAD already holds the head: nothing, `merged_default` is null\n    %%> - a clean merge: any uncommitted work is committed first with the check's message, then one merge commit, parents HEAD then the head, message `Merge main <full sha> before the check`, author and committer `Orbit <orbit@users.noreply.github.com>`\n    %%> - only the copy's own workflow changes refuse at publish (`git diff <merge-base>...<commit>`): the default branch's workflow changes that the merge brings in are never the copy's\n    %%> - a conflict refuses with `tasks.check_conflict` and `conflicting_paths`, at most 100 in at most 3,000 bytes, and `conflicting_more`, before the long parts run; the workspace is left exactly as it was\n    %%> - every refusal comes before the copy changes: the work and merge commits are objects no ref names; a merge that would add a file over an untracked or ignored one fails with `tasks.workspace_phase_failed`; only then the working tree, the index and the branch move\n    %%> - after the merge step succeeds, HEAD holds the default head, whether this check merged it or an earlier check, or a sync and a hand merge, did; file deliverables and `fails_on_base` runs compare with the subtask's start plus that head, so main's changes are never the subtask's work\n    %%> - the base-merge, check, commit and commit-ref phases run as the behavior `task-instance-check-NODE-GROUP`, for up to 900 s, 10,800 s, 600 s and 600 s, so the check of another group's copy on this Node never waits for its lock; its short steps keep their Node names\n    %%> Refuses:\n    %%> - a changed checkout identity or branch: `tasks.workspace_identity_changed`\n  Note over SW: Read the result\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - reads the receipt of `Check in the Instance`\n  %%> - a direct data step exposes the Instance check's result JSON to a calling workflow\n  %%> - it keeps the complete check receipt on passed, failed and changed outcomes\n  Note over SW: Check the result\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - native assert checks the Instance receipt for an execution error\n  %%> - the Gateway captures that receipt in the same history read; no shell prints it again\n  opt hosted CI is pending\n    Note over GW: Record scheduled CI\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - on each native schedule tick, `collect` shapes public observations for admitted checks after their SSH snapshots\n    %%> - queued or running GitHub Actions jobs on the candidate keep CI pending even before Required checks appears\n    %%> - another head or App cannot extend the wait; missing CI still expires after ten minutes, active CI after 55 minutes\n    %%> - the existing reader takes these rows directly from the collector output; no reporting shell runs\n    %%> - Swamp inlines only a small step output in the run history; a larger one, such as a check with long output, is read as the `output` data artifact of `task-check-ci-data` with `data.get`, at the version the history names\n  end\n  alt every step passes\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>outcome · commit · ci\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - a copy's handoff check also names `default_commit`, the default head it judged against, `merged_default`, the head it merged or null, and `base`: `current`, or `unavailable` with `base_reason`\n    Note over GW: Record check 17<br/>passed, failed or changed\n    %%> Rules:\n    %%> - a check preserves its admitted subtask base as the start commit; the candidate HEAD is never a new base for a retry\n    %%> - a passed handoff check records the group's pull request mode in effect at that moment (`pr_mode`); only a check that recorded `pr_when_done` lets the `task:pr-when-done` tick publish by itself. Recording it starts nothing (docs/reference/tasks.md#where-the-mode-comes-from)\n  else a step fails\n    I--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or no reply\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    Note over GW: Record the failed check<br/>group lifecycle stays external\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5170\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "task/complete": {
   "kind": "tasks.complete",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: docs/reference/tasks.md#complete-and-cleanup. Orchestrator 2026-11-08: keeping approved work is a precondition of completion, not a step of it. Orchestrator 2026-11-14: a task copy goes through its normal instance.destroy, as on cancel. 2026-11-16 (group 5615): a copy group whose committed work is on the default branch completes without an ended watched pull request (a hand-carry); the copy's content read decides, and an empty group still needs cancel. 2026-11-16 (group 5615, subtask 2): complete reads the copy first and never destroys one that holds uncommitted, untracked, stashed or other-branch work; a copy group whose watched pull request merged completes by itself from the task:copy-settle tick, in every Project. 2026-11-15: the task VMs and their release are removed (docs/reference/old-paths.md).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: task:complete\n  %% kind: tasks.complete\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant GH as GitHub<br/>repository · PRs\n  CLI->>GW: task:complete 42\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit task:complete 42 --yes`\n  %%> - syntax: `[task] [--yes] [--json]`\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the group is `settling` or `waiting_for_review`, or `running` or `reviewing` with a watched pull request that merged or closed; a `completed` group retries its removal\n  %%> - or a `running` or `reviewing` group with a task copy and no ended watched pull request, whose latest content read (`work`, of a `task:sync`) is of the copy's current head, found every committed path on the default branch, and is not `empty`: its work was carried to the default branch by hand\n  %%> - otherwise HTTP 409 `tasks.not_settling`, whose `reason` detail says why: `status`, `pull_request_not_ended`, `work_unread` (run `task:sync` first), `work_not_on_default` or `work_empty` (cancel the group instead)\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Gateway\n  %%> - the `task:copy-settle` tick runs this same completion by itself, every five minutes in every Project, for a copy group whose watched pull request merged; it reads the pull request and records `merged` and `merged_at` first, so this completion reads it no more; it skips a group with an open Operation, or whose copy an Operation holds, and the next tick tries again\n  GW->>GH: Compare the task branch<br/>approved...task-42\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - resolves the group's latest approved commit: the latest commit Orbit reviewed (ADR 0203), or else the commit of the latest approval\n  %%> - reads origin's task branch head and compares it with that commit, with a `contents: read` token\n  %%> - only when GitHub answers that the branch does not exist (404) and the group's pull request merged does that pull request's head stand in; the pull request is read with a `pull_requests: read` token\n  %%> - skipped when the group has no approval, or holds no workspace\n  %%> - for a task copy, content on the default branch counts too: the copy's latest content read (`work`, of a `task:sync`) is of the copy's current head, holds the approved commit in its history and found every committed path on the default branch\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the destroy deletes the copy, and a commit that only the copy holds would go with it\n  GH-->>GW: Branch reaches it\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - otherwise completion refuses with HTTP 409 `tasks.approved_work_unpublished`, names the commit and the branch, and changes nothing: run `task:publish` first\n  %%> - every other outcome refuses the same way: a GitHub read that fails, a branch that is gone without a merged pull request\n  opt the task owns a task copy\n  Note over GW: Read the copy's workspace<br/>one SSH read · as on cancel\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - reads what the copy holds beyond origin and the default branch, as `task:cancel` does (docs/reference/tasks.md#published-work-of-a-copy)\n  %%> - skipped for a group that is already `completed`: the completion that ended it read the copy\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - a copy that holds uncommitted, untracked, stashed or other-branch work refuses with HTTP 409 `tasks.copy_unpublished_work` and the read's counts, and changes nothing\n  %%> - a copy that cannot be read refuses with HTTP 409 `tasks.copy_workspace_unknown`\n  %%> - the settle tick does not destroy the copy: it records `failure` assistance once for unpublished work or a stopped, asleep or broken copy, and retries any other refusal, such as a read that timed out, on its next tick\n  end\n  Note over GW: Complete the group<br/>stop work · subtasks cancelled\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - stores how the watched pull request ended, if one did, stops the running implementer, reviewer and checks, and marks the group `completed`\n  %%> - a group without a workspace ends here\n  %%> - a group that still holds a workspace that is no task copy (a task VM workspace) is `completed` too, and asks for `removal_failure` assistance: task VMs and their release are removed, so nothing removes that workspace\n  alt the task owns a task copy\n  Note over GW: Admit the copy's destroy<br/>instance.destroy · force\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - no other Operation holds the copy; an open destroy of it is reused, so a repeated complete admits no second one\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - admits the normal `instance.destroy` of the copy with force, the destroy that `task:cancel` admits\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - origin already has the approved work, which the compare or the content read above proved, and the workspace read found nothing else, so complete needs no `--discard`\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the group is `completed` at once and still names the copy; when the destroy succeeds the group lets go of it and stays `completed`\n  %%> - when the destroy fails, the group stays `completed` and keeps the copy; a later complete admits the destroy again\n  end\n"
  },
  "task/egress": {
   "kind": "tasks.egress",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design (agreed with Nick, 2026-11-07): the outside hosts a task VM connects to, per VM and per task group, log only. The Node agent of the VM's host observes the bridge logs, the Gateway attributes the rows to the group that holds the VM, and this command reads them. Step 2, an allowlist enforced by a proxy outside the VM, starts from this record. The host part and the agent fact: docs/reference/task-vm-egress-log.md.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: task:egress\n  %% kind: tasks.egress\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane A icon=server pill=observer tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant A as Node agent<br/>orbit-agent\n  CLI->>GW: task:egress GROUP\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit task:egress GROUP`\n  %%> - syntax: `<task> [--json]`\n  alt the tasks extension is off\n    GW--xCLI: 409 extension.disabled<br/>nothing is read\n    %%> Requirements:\n    %%> - the tasks extension is enabled\n    %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Gateway\n  else the extension is on\n    Note over GW: Read the group's rows<br/>by destination, port\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - one row for each destination, port and protocol, in that order\n    %%> - a name that nothing connected to is listed only until the VM connects to it\n    GW-->>CLI: 200 with the rows<br/>possibly an empty list\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - each row: the destination and its kind, the port and protocol, the addresses seen, the sources `conn` and `dns`, the flags `dot`, `dns` and `doh`, the VM and the first-seen time\n    %%> - destination names are text that a VM chose: treat them as data\n    %%> - the list is empty until the host's agent has reported the VM, or when the VM made no outside connection\n  end\n  loop while the group holds a VM, every 5 s on change\n    Note over A: Read the bridge logs<br/>DNS · new flows · leases\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - runs on the task VM host; the host phase turns the logs on, and the VM cannot edit them\n    %%> - names each VM by its MAC address through Incus's own hosts file, and joins a flow with the name the same VM resolved for its address\n    A->>GW: Report the egress fact<br/>one per VM · on change\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - sent within 15 s of a change, with at most 1,500 rows, under 1 MiB\n    %%> - the Gateway accepts it only from a task VM host, for that host's bridge\n    Note over GW: Add the rows to the group<br/>that holds the VM\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - finds the group by host Node and VM name, adds new rows and merges addresses, sources and flags into known ones\n    %%> - a VM that no group holds adds nothing, and a row first seen before the claim is left out\n    %%> - the group keeps its rows after its VM and Node are deleted\n  end\n"
  },
  "task/pr-merge": {
   "kind": "tasks.pr_merge",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Nick 2026-11-06: a task VM never holds a GitHub credential, and the step mints its token when it runs, not at admission.\n  %% Target design: stage 4 of the command development pipeline (docs/reference/pipeline/4-pr-and-ci.mmd, \"Admit the merge step\"), R2-R3. The Gateway admits and names the vault key, the step mints its token, and Swamp calls GitHub. docs/reference/tasks.md#ready-and-merge-a-task-pull-request.\n  %% Task group modes (ADR 0257): a group whose workspace is a task copy is never merged here; the refusal comes before any GitHub read. docs/reference/tasks.md#no-merge.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: none · API / MCP (task-pr-merge)\n  %% kind: tasks.pr_merge\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as API / MCP<br/>typed API request\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant GH as GitHub<br/>repository · PRs\n  CLI->>GW: Merge the PR of GROUP<br/>head SHA\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `POST /api/v1/tasks/GROUP/pr-merge` with `head_sha` and an `Idempotency-Key`\n  %%> - syntax: the MCP tool `task-pr-merge` with `group`, `head_sha`\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - a Node with an access grant to the Gateway; there is no `orbit` command\n  %%> Refused at once:\n  %%> - a group whose workspace is a task copy, or that published from one, or whose watched PR a copy of any Project on the repository opened: `tasks.copy_pr_no_merge` (409), before any GitHub read; once read, also a PR whose head is a copy group's `task-<id>` branch; a person merges its PR on GitHub\n  GW->>GH: Read the PR reviews<br/>short poll · approval on head\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - reads the PR, its reviews and the check runs of the head with read-only App tokens (`pull_requests: read`, `checks: read`)\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the Gateway is private and its App receives no webhooks, so a caller that polls this command sees the approval\n  GH-->>GW: Approval on the head\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the reviewer's latest decision is an approval of exactly this head; a later push moves the head and voids it\n  Note over GW: Check the gate<br/>approval · CI green · key name\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the tasks extension is on, and the group is an open external group with a published PR that is open and no longer a draft\n  %%> - the PR head is exactly `head_sha`, and the Project's merge check (`Required checks`) passed on that head\n  %%> - the gate holds: the configured reviewer's latest review is an approval on this exact head (manual mode); a missing approval, a request for changes or a moved head refuses with its own code\n  %%> - no other task runs on the group; the Gateway holds one lock per group\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - names the vault key `pr-merge-write-<uuid>` for the write token; it mints no token\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - a token lives one hour and an approval can take longer, so the merge step mints its own token when it runs\n  %%> Not built:\n  %%> - the automatic gate, a fresh self-review agent with at most 3 rounds; the approval is the only gate\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-pr-merge\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209); no Node is a target\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - the repository, PR number and expected head\n  %%> - the vault key name of the token, never the token\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task id\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the caller follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>GW: Mint the token<br/>operation:direct:github-token\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the first step posts the run id to the Gateway; the Gateway checks the Operation, the run and the repository, mints a token with only `contents: write` and `pull_requests: write`, and writes it to the vault key that admission named\n  %%> Why here and not at admission:\n  %%> - an installation token lives one hour; the token is minted when the step that uses it runs\n  %%> - no task VM holds a GitHub credential\n  SW->>GH: Squash on expected head\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - one squash merge straight into the base branch, with the admitted `sha`; a moved head refuses without merging\n  %%> - no read of the PR comes first: admission read the head, and the `sha` of the call is the guard for the seconds in between\n  %%> - no merge queue and no GitHub auto-merge\n  %%> Refuses:\n  %%> - a conflict with the base branch; the author rebases, which reruns CI and the proof, and the gate starts again\n  SW->>GH: Clean up the token<br/>task-github-cleanup\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - runs the shared cleanup child: it revokes the token, best effort because the token expires within one hour, and removes the private GitHub CLI configuration directory of this run\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - always, on success and failure\n  Note over SW: Confirm the merge\n  alt merged\n    GH-->>SW: PR merged · sha\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the merge commit SHA\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event · merge sha\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record the merge<br/>task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the task succeeds with the merge SHA; CI then runs on the new base commit, and nothing is deployed here\n  else refused or failed\n    GH--xSW: head moved or conflict\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the head moved, the PR conflicts with the base branch, or GitHub refused the merge\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code, such as `tasks_pr.merge_refused`\n    Note over GW: Mark the task failed<br/>PR open · author fixes\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the task fails; the PR stays open, unmerged\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - a new request for the same head is a new task with a new token; the gate is read again\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "task/pr-ready": {
   "kind": "tasks.pr_ready",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Nick 2026-11-06: a task VM never holds a GitHub credential, and the step mints its token when it runs, not at admission.\n  %% Target design: stage 4 of the command development pipeline (docs/reference/pipeline/4-pr-and-ci.mmd, \"Admit the ready step\"), R2-R3. The Gateway admits and names the vault key, the step mints its token, and Swamp calls GitHub. docs/reference/tasks.md#ready-and-merge-a-task-pull-request.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: none · API / MCP (task-pr-ready)\n  %% kind: tasks.pr_ready\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as API / MCP<br/>typed API request\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant GH as GitHub<br/>repository · PRs\n  CLI->>GW: Ready the PR of GROUP<br/>head SHA · mode MODE\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `POST /api/v1/tasks/GROUP/pr-ready` with `head_sha`, `mode` (`manual` or `automatic`) and an `Idempotency-Key`\n  %%> - syntax: the MCP tool `task-pr-ready` with `group`, `head_sha`, `mode`\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - a Node with an access grant to the Gateway; there is no `orbit` command\n  GW->>GH: Read the PR and checks<br/>head · draft · CI\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - reads the PR and the check runs of the head with read-only App tokens (`pull_requests: read`, `checks: read`)\n  GH-->>GW: PR state · check runs\n  Note over GW: Check the PR<br/>head · CI green · key name\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the tasks extension is on, and the group is an open external group with a published PR that is open\n  %%> - the PR head is exactly `head_sha`, and the Project's merge check (`Required checks`) passed on that head\n  %%> - in manual mode, the PR author is not the reviewer, because GitHub refuses a review request from the author\n  %%> - no other task runs on the group; the Gateway holds one lock per group\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - names the vault key `pr-ready-write-<uuid>` for the write token; it mints no token\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - a task recorded with the full intent\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-pr-ready\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209); no Node is a target\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - the repository, PR number, expected head, mode and the reviewer's GitHub login\n  %%> - the vault key name of the token, never the token\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task id\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the caller follows the task with `operation:show`\n  SW->>GW: Mint the token<br/>operation:direct:github-token\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the first step posts the run id to the Gateway; the Gateway checks the Operation, the run and the repository, mints a token with only `pull_requests: write`, and writes it to the vault key that admission named\n  %%> Why here and not at admission:\n  %%> - an installation token lives one hour; the token is minted when the step that uses it runs\n  %%> - no task VM holds a GitHub credential\n  SW->>GH: Read the PR\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - one `gh api` read of state, draft flag, head SHA and GraphQL node id\n  Note over SW: Check the PR head\n  %%> Refuses:\n  %%> - a head other than the admitted one, or a PR that is no longer open, with `tasks_pr.head_moved`; nothing is changed\n  SW->>GH: Take the PR out of draft\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - marks the PR ready for review with the App token, passed by vault key name\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - only while the PR is a draft; a PR that is already ready skips this step, so a rerun is safe\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - a draft means the work is still moving; leaving draft says the spec, code and proof cases are done\n  opt manual mode\n    SW->>GH: Request the review\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - requests the review of the reviewer's GitHub login, so GitHub notifies the reviewer that the PR waits\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - in manual mode the reviewer's approval on the exact head is the gate of the merge step\n  end\n  SW->>GH: Read the PR result\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - reads the head, the draft flag and the requested reviewers; the Gateway keeps this as the task result\n  SW->>GH: Clean up the token<br/>task-github-cleanup\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - runs the shared cleanup child: it revokes the token, best effort because the token expires within one hour, and removes the private GitHub CLI configuration directory of this run\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - always, on success and failure\n  Note over SW: Confirm the ready step\n  alt ready\n    GH-->>SW: PR ready · head sha\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the head SHA, the draft flag and the requested reviewers\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event · PR state\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record the result<br/>task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the task succeeds with the PR number, head and requested reviewers; the PR now waits for the gate\n  else refused or failed\n    GH--xSW: head moved or refused\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the head moved, GitHub refused the draft change, or GitHub refused the review request\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code, such as `tasks_pr.head_moved`\n    Note over GW: Mark the task failed<br/>PR kept as it is\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the task fails; the PR stays as GitHub left it, and the Gateway changes no task state\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - a new request for the same head is a new task with a new token; a step that already happened is skipped\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "task/publish": {
   "kind": "tasks.publish",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, R2-R4, swamp-tasks-model. Nick 2026-11-06: a task VM never holds a GitHub credential; the push step mints its token when it runs, not at admission (an installation token lives one hour). docs/reference/tasks.md#publish-an-external-group.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rule 16 (no tip read: a push without force refuses a divergent tip and pushes nothing for an identical one); rule 22 (no shipped scripts: task VM work is `@swamp/ssh exec` argv, 209 work is inline `git` and `gh api`; one workspace check, since import checks the bundled commit and tree).\n  %% Task copy (2026-11-14): a group whose workspace is a task copy exports through its container's workspace phase; the push stays on the worker. The .env Orbit wrote never leaves a copy: the range from the copy's start is checked before the mint. docs/reference/tasks.md#task-copy.\n  %% Task group modes (ADR 0257): a copy group's done check publishes without --check, by a person's release or by the task:pr-when-done tick (docs/cli/task/pr-when-done.mmd), with the same admission and run. docs/reference/tasks.md#task-group-modes.\n  %% Base (2026-11-14): a copy's run fetches the default branch into its scratch repository after the mint and before the push, states the checked base, how far it is behind and the overlapping paths, and refuses only a real merge conflict. It reads only intent keys the publish already has. docs/reference/tasks.md#the-base-of-a-copy-publish.\n  %% Workflows (2026-11-16): the workflow judgement moved from the copy check to the base read, against the real default head that the publish fetches, not the copy's own remote refs (Operation 24405): a commit that head holds, synced or carried in by hand, is never a change of the copy. It is the net tree diff of `.github/workflows/` between that head and the candidate, the comparison GitHub makes, not the copy's commits (Operation 24460): a copy behind a workflow change of the head refuses too. docs/reference/tasks.md#the-base-of-a-copy-publish.\n  %% Own change, merge at publish (2026-11-16, group 5429): a workflow change merged on main after the copy's sync refused copies (#3867), so only the copy's own change refuses: the workflow paths it changed since the merge base (`git diff head...candidate`). GitHub still judges the pushed tree against the default branch (Operation 24460), so a copy only behind a workflow change publishes through a clean merge the worker makes at publish, `Merge main <sha> at publish`; a republish adds the earlier publish merge as a third parent, so its push is a fast-forward. docs/reference/tasks.md#merge-main-at-publish.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: task:publish\n  %% kind: tasks.publish\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Instance Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  participant GH as GitHub<br/>repository · PRs\n  CLI->>GW: task:publish 42<br/>--check=17\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit task:publish 42 --check=17`\n  %%> - syntax: `[task] [--check=ID [--title=TEXT --body=TEXT [--draft]]] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the task group, and the passed handoff check whose commit is published\n  %%> - optionally a title and body together, to open or update the pull request; without them it only pushes\n  %%> - a task copy's group may omit `--check`, `--title` and `--body`: the release of its done check, with the reviewer's title and body, as a ready pull request; on a group that has its pull request, the release pushes its done check again and updates the pull request's title and body with the reviewer's text when its newest turn sent one for that check, else it only pushes\n  %%> - a task copy's `--check` without `--title` and `--body`, on a group without a pull request: when the check is the reviewed done check with the reviewer's text, the same Operation opens the pull request, as the release does; any other check only pushes\n  %%> - a task copy's `--check` without `--title` and `--body`, on a group that has its pull request: when the check is the done check with the reviewer's text, the same Operation updates the pull request with it, as the release does; any other check only pushes\n  Note over GW: Check the candidate<br/>task #5165 · record the key names\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Gateway\n  %%> - group 42 is an open external group with its own task copy whose container runs (`tasks.instance_not_running`) and has a start commit; a group without a task copy gets `tasks.no_workspace`: task VM workspaces are removed\n  %%> - a task copy's Project is released for publishing: a nested-fleet Project, such as the Orbit Project, or listed in the Gateway's `ORBIT_TASKS_COPY_PUBLISH_PROJECTS`; otherwise HTTP 409 `tasks.copy_publish_held`, and the checked commit stays in the copy\n  %%> - a task copy has no earlier turn that may still run (`tasks.copy_turn_unended`)\n  %%> - a task copy's last completion did not fail (`tasks.copy_completion_failed`, with its Operation, failed step, error code and the retry `orbit instance:complete ID`), and the copy is `active` (`tasks.copy_not_ready`)\n  %%> - check 17 passed for group 42, and required hosted CI passed on its commit, else `tasks.commit_not_checked`\n  %%> - without `--check`: the group's work is done, else `tasks.not_done`, also on a group that has its pull request; the done check is the check, and the request is the same from here on (docs/reference/tasks.md#when-the-work-is-done)\n  %%> - without `--check`, on a group without a pull request: the reviewer thread's newest turn sent its pull request text on the done check, else `tasks.pr_text_missing`; that text is the title and body, with `Closes #N` added for a group that names issue N and whose body does not close it (docs/reference/tasks.md#the-reviewers-pull-request-text)\n  %%> - no other task runs on group 42; the Gateway holds one lock per group\n  %%> - a release, and a `--check` that takes the reviewer's text: the done check is read again under the group lock, else `tasks.not_done`\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - captures the repository, default branch, commit, checked tree and the copy Node's SSH target\n  %%> - for a task copy: its Node's SSH target, its container, its start commit and the path of the `.env` Orbit wrote, from the Instance's record\n  %%> - names two `task-publish-token-<uuid>` vault keys for the token and the Git authorization header; it mints and writes no value at admission\n  %%> - the `task:pr-when-done` tick admits through this same step, keyed `pr-when-done:<group>:<check>`; its refusal is stored on the group as the publication's error code\n  %%> - a copy's `--check` that only pushes records why no pull request opens, `pull_request_withheld`, on the Operation; the run's intent does not carry it\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5165 recorded with the full intent\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-publish\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - the commit, tree, branch `task-42`, default branch and the optional title and body\n  %%> - the vault key names of the token and Git authorization header, never their values; no token exists yet\n  %%> - for a task copy: the `.env` the Gateway renders from the Instance's stored values, as the vault copy `task-publish-environment-<operation>`; the agent cannot change it\n  %%> - for a copy in the nested-fleet project: the Club key's one vault entry `nested-fleet-club-api-key`, which every nested start reads, when the Gateway holds a key\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5165\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  Note over SW: Create publication scratch\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - a private worker directory holds the bundle, bare repository and bounded CLI replies\n    SW->>N: Preflight the copy\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the workspace phase reads the checkout as `agent` in the container through its Node; HEAD must be the checked commit\n    SW->>N: Export the copy\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the workspace phase bundles the branch as `agent`, checks its head and tree against the candidate, and fetches it to this worker; the Node keeps no copy\n    %%> - the phase runs as the behavior `task-workspace-NODE-GROUP` for up to 1,800 s, so a publish of another group's copy on this Node never waits for its lock; the content check, import and push name their group too\n    %%> - the Node also pulls the copy's `.env` as a regular file and fetches it beside the bundle; its bytes enter no result or log\n    %%> - and the copy's proposed pull request body for the task branch, `.git/orbit/proposed-pr-bodies/task-GROUP.md`, when it is a regular file of at most 64 KiB: data, never run; without it the publish sends the caller's text\n    SW->>N: Check the copy\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the workspace phase `scan` runs `task_copy_scan.py` in the container as root; root reads the copy's secrets, Git runs as `agent` (docs/reference/tasks.md#the-copy-check)\n    %%> - the secrets: the Club key in `/run/orbit-club`, the operator WireGuard key, and of each inner machine its Gateway `.env` values, Swamp tokens, Orbit tokens and WireGuard keys; values under 16 bytes and values the start tree holds are skipped and counted\n    %%> - every object new from the copy's start to the candidate, and the proposed body, are searched for each value and for a private key block with a real body\n    %%> - the evidence names paths, lines, secret names and the digest of the objects read, never a value; a nested-fleet copy without its inner machines is unchecked\n    SW->>SW: Read the copy check\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - typed data reads the evidence: a secret refuses with `tasks.publish_secret_value`, naming its items; an error refuses with `tasks.publish_secret_unchecked`\n  Note over SW: Init the bare repo\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - one bounded native command on the worker; no shipped script\n  Note over SW: Import the bundle\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - one bounded native command on the worker; no shipped script\n  Note over SW: Read bundle contents\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - one bounded native command on the worker; no shipped script\n  Note over SW: Check imported commit\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - typed data compares the imported commit and tree without a second workspace observation\n    Note over SW: Add the waiver lines\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - typed data writes the PR title and body: the caller's body, then each `Docs-unaffected:` line of the proposed body that the body lacks, in the file's order, while the body stays within 65,536 characters\n    %%> - the next checks read this text, so the PR gets exactly what they checked; a copy without a pull request skips the step\n    Note over SW: Check the environment file\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - every commit from the copy's start to the candidate must hold the `.env` path exactly as the start does; a commit that adds it, even one a later commit removes, refuses with `tasks.publish_secret_path` before any token exists\n    Note over SW: Check the .env content\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - every object new in the range (file contents, file names, commit messages) and the final pull request title and body, waiver lines included, are searched for the whole `.env` and for each of its values of 8 bytes or more that the start tree does not hold, from the copy's file and from the Gateway's render; a nested-fleet copy's Club key counts as one more value\n    %%> - a hit refuses with `tasks.publish_secret_content`, naming the commit and the object, and the file only when its path holds no value; any error refuses with `tasks.publish_secret_unchecked`\n    %%> - the range's object digest must equal the one the copy check read, else `tasks.publish_secret_unchecked`: the copy check read the objects this bundle pushes\n    Note over SW: Read the .env check\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - typed data reads the check's exit code and its one refusal line from the private scratch, because Swamp keeps no stderr of a failed command step\n    %%> - exit 0 passes; a refusal fails the step with that line as its detail; anything else fails with `tasks.publish_secret_unchecked`; a copy mints only after this step and \"Read the copy check\" succeeded\n  SW->>GW: Mint the push token<br/>operation:direct:github-token\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - one bounded `curl` step posts the run id to the Gateway when the push is next; the run id proves the caller\n  %%> - the Gateway checks the Operation, the run and the repository, mints a token for that one repository with `contents: write` and `pull_requests: write`, never `workflows`, and writes the two vault values; the reply holds the vault descriptors only\n  %%> - no step writes the token into the copy; it receives no credential, in the bundle, the Git configuration or the environment\n    SW->>GH: Read the base\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - one bounded fetch of the default branch head into the scratch repository, with the minted token as an extra header, so the head is the one at publish time\n    %%> - `git merge-base` of the candidate and that head is the checked base; `git rev-list --count candidate..<default>` is `behind_by`\n    %%> - the overlapping paths are the paths that both the candidate and the default branch changed since the base\n    %%> - the copy's own workflow paths are the paths under `.github/workflows/` that the candidate changed since the merge base (`git diff --name-only <base> candidate`, which is `git diff head...candidate`); a commit that head holds, synced or carried in by hand, is never a change of the copy\n    %%> - `git merge-tree --write-tree` merges the two in the repository alone; no checkout and no workspace is touched\n    %%> - it also fetches the task branch's tip on origin, when it exists\n    %%> - with no own workflow path and a clean merge, it writes a merge commit in the scratch repository when the trees under `.github/workflows/` of the head and the candidate differ: the merge-tree result, parents the candidate then the head, message `Merge main <head sha> at publish` with the default branch's name, author and committer `Orbit <orbit@users.noreply.github.com>`; the push sends it, because GitHub judges the pushed tree against the default branch\n    %%> - a republish: a tip that is not an ancestor of the candidate, but an earlier publish merge whose first parent is an ancestor of the candidate and whose second parent is an ancestor of the head, always gets a merge with parents the candidate, the head and that tip, so the push is a fast-forward; a tip whose first two parents already are the candidate and the head is pushed again as it is\n    %%> - with no workflow difference and no such tip, it makes no merge and the push sends the candidate\n    %%> - a copy's handoff check already merged the default head it judged against (`Merge main <sha> before the check`): when the default branch did not move since that check, `behind_by` is 0 and no merge at publish is needed; the merge at publish stays for a default branch that moved after the check\n    %%> - of the intent it reads only `remote`, `base`, `branch` and `node_id`, which the publish intent already had; the candidate is the bundle it imported\n    %%> - it writes its statement and exit code to the private scratch; Swamp keeps no stderr of a failed command step\n    Note over SW: Check the base\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - typed data reads the statement: `base_commit`, `default_commit`, `behind_by` and `overlapping_paths`, at most 100 paths in at most 3,000 bytes, with `overlapping_more` counting the rest, and `merged_default`, the head of a merge at publish or null\n    %%> - an own workflow path fails with `tasks.publish_workflow_change`, naming the paths in the same bounds and the count beyond, before the conflict check and the push: the token has no `workflows` permission\n    %%> - it passes the merge commit to the push, when the read made one\n    %%> - a conflict of the merge fails with `tasks.publish_conflict`, naming at most 100 conflicting paths in at most 3,000 bytes, so the line fits the 4,096 bytes of detail the Gateway keeps, and the count beyond, before the push; any other error fails with `tasks.publish_failed`\n    %%> - a copy pushes only after this step succeeded; a skip of it is never a pass\n    %%> - `behind_by` and overlapping paths never refuse on their own\n  SW->>GH: Push the branch\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - one bounded push without force; authorization is a vault-backed extra header in the environment\n  %%> - a task copy's merge at publish is pushed instead of the candidate: `<merge>:refs/heads/task-42`\n  Note over SW: Read push result\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - typed data reads private replies and preserves fast-forward and Workflows refusal codes; for a task copy the Workflows refusal is only a fallback, for a reason the base read did not see\n  opt --title and --body\n    SW->>GH: Read open PRs\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - one bounded native command on the worker; no shipped script\n    Note over SW: Plan the PR\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - typed data selects an open PR or creation and writes unchanged caller prose into a JSON input file\n    %%> - with `--draft` the creation carries `draft: true`; an update of an open PR sends only title and body, so a PR keeps its state\n    %%> - for a task copy, a fixed `## Base` section follows the caller's body: the base commit, the default head, how far behind, `- Merged main <sha> at publish` after a merge at publish, and the overlapping paths or none\n    %%> - an Orbit marker opens the section, so a caller body that carries an earlier one gets it replaced: a re-publish states it once\n    %%> - the section follows the text the .env check read: the caller's body and its waiver lines; the whole stays within 65,536 characters, naming only the count of the paths when their list does not fit\n    SW->>GH: Write the PR\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - one bounded native command on the worker; no shipped script\n    Note over SW: Read PR result\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - typed data keeps only the PR number and URL\n  end\n  SW->>GH: Revoke the token\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - one bounded gh api call, only after a token was minted; a refused revoke is best effort and the token expires within an hour\n    SW->>N: Discard the copy scratch\n    %%> How:\n    %%> - the workspace phase removes its guest scratch after failure too, once the preflight passed\n  Note over SW: Remove local scratch\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - one bounded command removes the private worker directory and CLI configuration\n  Note over SW: Confirm publication\n  %%> How:\n  %%> - the native assert requires the push, optional PR reply and worker cleanup to be complete\n  alt pushed\n    GH-->>SW: Branch pushed · head sha\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the branch, commit, and the PR number and URL, or null PR fields for a push alone\n    %%> - for a task copy also `base_commit`, `default_commit`, `behind_by`, `overlapping_paths`, `overlapping_more` and `merged_default`; `commit` stays the checked candidate after a merge at publish; a run that started on the workflow before the base step has none of them and no `## Base` section\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record the publication<br/>branch · commit · task done\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - a PR receipt makes the group watch its PR; a branch-only push keeps its workspace\n    %%> - a task copy stays until `task:cancel` or the group's end; its group keeps its status\n    %%> - Orbit merges no pull request that a task copy's publish opened (`tasks.copy_pr_no_merge`); a person merges it on GitHub\n    %%> - task #5165 succeeds\n  else a step fails\n    GH--xSW: refused or no reply\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the workspace moved, the tip diverged, the push or PR write was refused, or a reply was lost\n    %%> - a commit of a task copy holds the `.env` Orbit wrote, or the copy's start is gone from the branch history: `tasks.publish_secret_path`; cancel the copy and start the task again\n    %%> - a commit of a task copy holds the `.env` content or one of its values under any name: `tasks.publish_secret_content`; remove it from the branch history and check again\n    %%> - the content check or the copy check could not run: `tasks.publish_secret_unchecked`; nothing is pushed\n    %%> - a commit or the proposed body holds a secret value of the copy or a private key block: `tasks.publish_secret_value`, with `refused` (path:line and secret name) and `refused_more`; nothing is pushed; remove it from the branch history and check again\n    %%> - a task copy's candidate changed a path under `.github/workflows/` since the merge base: `tasks.publish_workflow_change`, with the paths in `refused` and `refused_more`; nothing is pushed; a person makes a workflow change of the copy's own in a pull request of their own (docs/reference/tasks.md#the-base-of-a-copy-publish)\n    %%> - the task branch's tip is neither an ancestor of the candidate nor an earlier publish merge of it: `tasks.not_fast_forward`; nothing is pushed\n    %%> - a task copy's candidate conflicts with the default branch head: `tasks.publish_conflict`, with `conflicting_paths` and `conflicting_more`; nothing is pushed; the `task:merge-round` tick then syncs, merges in one implementer turn, checks, reviews the merge and publishes again (docs/reference/tasks.md#the-merge-round)\n    %%> - a step waited 60 s for a behavior lock that another run held: `tasks.publish_lock_timeout`; a step after the push can fail so too, so the branch may be pushed already\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its error code, such as `tasks.push_failed`\n    Note over GW: Mark the task failed<br/>group kept · rerun after a fix\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - task #5165 fails; group 42 and its workspace stay as they are\n    %%> - a publish that the `task:pr-when-done` tick started shows the group's publication as `refused` with this code; the tick does not start the same done check again\n    %%> - except `tasks.publish_lock_timeout`: the publication stays `publishing` and the tick starts the done check again, at most 3 attempts in all; a retry is safe, because an identical tip pushes nothing and the PR plan updates an open PR\n    %%> On GitHub:\n    %%> - a push or PR that started stays; there is no rollback\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again: a new task mints a new token at its own push step; an identical tip pushes nothing, and the PR is looked up again before any write\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5165\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n    %%> - a copy's `--check` that only pushed names why in `pull_request_withheld`\n  end\n"
  },
  "task/start": {
   "kind": "tasks.provision",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: a task starts as a copy of its Project's default Instance, a clone `task-<id>` that the task owns (nested-fleet Projects since 2026-11-13).\n  %% Nick 2026-11-14: every Project whose default Instance is a container gets task groups as copies too; a plain clone, with no inner fleet.\n  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-15 (step 5): task VMs are removed. A Project whose default is no container is refused by name; the VM claim and its queue are gone.\n  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-15 (task small fixes): the CLI waits for an active copy by default, as instance:create --wait-active; the API stays asynchronous.\n  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-16 (task 5634): a failed create no longer ends the task: it goes back to todo, the create starts once more after the copy is gone, and a second failure asks for assistance.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: task:start\n  %% kind: tasks.provision\n  autonumber\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  Note over GW: Decide where the task runs<br/>copy or refusal\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - `InstancePlacement::taskCopy` decides once: a Project with `nested_fleet` on, or whose default Instance is a container, starts the task as a copy of that default; any other Project is refused\n  %%> - no other step decides it; later steps follow the group's own workspace\n  alt the Project's tasks run as copies\n    Note over GW: Admit the copy's create<br/>instance.create · task-TASK\n    %%> Requirements:\n    %%> - the task is in todo and holds no workspace; otherwise `tasks.not_todo` or `tasks.group_closed`\n    %%> - the Project's default Instance is active, with a seed snapshot, and runs or sleeps; otherwise HTTP 409 `tasks.copy_source_unavailable`, also for a default stopped by hand, and nothing is recorded\n    %%> - before the transaction, one bounded read of the default's container (at most about 6 s) confirms it runs or sleeps: its power record can be stale after a stop by hand; a container that does not run and has no asleep marker, or that cannot be read, refuses the same way\n    %%> - an asleep default stays asleep: the copy clones its seed snapshot, which needs no running container\n    %%> - no Instance is named `task-TASK` yet; otherwise `tasks.copy_name_taken`\n    %%> - the task holds no copy of a failed create that Orbit still removes; otherwise `tasks.copy_removing`\n    %%> - for a nested-fleet Project only: the default's Node holds fewer nested-fleet task copies than the Gateway's `ORBIT_TASKS_COPIES_MAX` (default 1); every nested copy that a task owns counts, also one that fails or is being destroyed; otherwise `tasks.copy_capacity_full`, with no wait. A copy of any other Project is a plain clone: it neither counts nor waits\n    %%> - before the transaction, one bounded read of the Node's ID ranges (at most about 4 s): root's subordinate range and every container's ID map, stopped ones too; an unreadable Node refuses, `tasks.copy_id_ranges_unreadable`\n    %%> - with the copy placed as Incus places ranges, a free isolated ID range stays for the next seed of every default on the Node; a container that an open create admitted counts as held; otherwise `tasks.copy_id_range_full`, with no wait, also for a plain clone\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - one transaction: the clone `task-TASK` on branch `task-TASK`, owned by the task and bound to it, the task `reserved`, and the `instance.create` Operation that the API returns at once\n    %%> - the CLI follows that create and then its completion, `result.completion_operation_id`, and reads the copy: it exits 0 only when the copy is `active`, as `instance:create --wait-active` does. `--no-wait` ends at the create. An API caller polls the same: the create, the completion, then the Instance's `status`\n    %%> - Swamp starts the run only after that commit: a Gateway that stops before it leaves no record and no run\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the create runs as every clone's does: the copy of the default's seed, its own database, Route and Processes, its inner fleet when the Project has one, its T3 server registered with Conn (the container create chart)\n    %%> - the copy presents itself as `task-<task id>.<project slug>`: its T3 server's name, and its hostname when the slug is a lowercase DNS label and the name fits 63 characters, else the long form; its Incus name stays `instance-<id>`\n    %%> - the copy's agent gets the fixed task model key, as every clone does: it is not per task\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-create\n    alt the create succeeds\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>thread usable\n      Note over GW: Keep the task reserved<br/>the completion runs\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - a succeeded create means the copy's thread is usable: its T3 server is registered; the copy stays `source_resolved` and its `instance.complete` sets up the database, the Processes and a nested copy's inner fleet\n      %%> - while the completion runs, a turn, a check, a publish and a proof answer `tasks.copy_not_ready`\n      alt the completion succeeds\n        SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>copy active\n        Note over GW: Run the task<br/>running\n        %%> When:\n        %%> - the completion succeeded and the copy is `active`: fully set up; a nested copy's fleet may still be starting (`instance.fleet`, see `fleet_ready_at`), and a proof waits for it\n        %%> - after a retried completion, the task also drops the assistance that the failed completion recorded, and only that request\n      else the completion fails\n        SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step\n        Note over GW: Keep the copy<br/>ask for assistance\n        %%> On failure:\n        %%> - every end of the completion but success and an expired run budget counts: a failed or interrupted run, a completion the create could not admit, a cancel\n        %%> - an agent may already have worked in the copy, so Orbit removes nothing: no destroy is queued\n        %%> - the task stays `reserved` and keeps its copy; `task:cancel` still works, with its read of the copy's work\n        %%> - the task records a failure assistance request, unless a direction request is open: it names the completion Operation, its failed step, its error code and the retry `orbit instance:complete ID`; `task:show` and `task:status` show it\n        %%> - a turn, a check, a publish and a proof of the task answer HTTP 409 `tasks.copy_completion_failed`, with `operation_id`, `failed_step`, `error_code` and `retry` in the details: the copy's Processes and inner fleet did not start, and a retry may run at the same time\n        %%> - `task:start` keeps refusing the reserved task; the retry is `orbit instance:complete ID`, and while it runs a turn, a check, a publish and a proof answer `tasks.copy_not_ready`\n        %%> - an expired budget changes nothing: the run may still be stopping, so the task stays `reserved` with its copy\n      end\n    else the create fails\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step\n      Note over GW: Task back to todo<br/>remove copy, retry once\n      %%> On failure:\n      %%> - every end of the create but success, a cancel and an expired run budget counts: a failed run, a failed register or baseline read, a start that Swamp refuses\n      %%> - the task goes back to `todo` and still owns the copy until its destroy; a queued job removes the copy with `instance:destroy` with force\n      %%> - the first failure is recorded on the task: its Operation, failed step and error code; when the destroy succeeds, Orbit starts the create once more for the same task, as its first caller\n      %%> - the retry's failure, or a retry that the start refuses, leaves the task in `todo` with no workspace and records a failure assistance request that names both Operations, their failed steps and their error codes; `task:show` shows it; no third create starts\n      %%> - `task:start` of that task works once the copy is gone; while it is still bound, `tasks.copy_removing`; a copy that comes up `active` clears that request\n      %%> - a cancel of the create, and the create of a proof's copy, fail the task as before: nothing starts again\n      %%> - `task:cancel` of the `todo` task while it holds the failed copy cancels it when the copy's destroy succeeds; a task moved to `backlog` before then owes no retry\n      %%> - the job destroys at once only when the records prove that no agent could have worked in the copy: the create did not succeed and no T3 server is registered for it\n      %%> - otherwise it first reads the copy's work as `task:cancel` does and destroys only a clean copy; a copy with work origin lacks, or a read that does not answer, stays, and the task records a `removal_failure` assistance request with the unpushed, uncommitted, untracked and stash counts, or the read's reason, and `orbit task:cancel TASK`, with `--discard` if the work is not wanted\n      %%> - an expired budget changes nothing: the run may still be stopping, so the task stays `reserved` with its copy\n      %%> - while the task keeps the copy, `task:cancel` admits its destroy, and `orbit instance:destroy ID --yes --force` does too\n    end\n  else the Project's default is no container\n    Note over GW: Refuse by name<br/>tasks.vm_runtime_removed\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - HTTP 422 `tasks.vm_runtime_removed`: task VMs are removed, and a task runs only in a copy of its Project's default container Instance\n    %%> - the message says what to do: move a checkout default into a container with instance:migrate, or create a container default with instance:create, then start the task and work in its copy\n    %%> - nothing is recorded: the task stays `todo` with no workspace, and no Operation, VM, identity or wait exists\n    %%> - every task VM command answers the same refusal first: a turn of a group without a copy, package and script; prep, prep-fix, build and review round are removed (docs/reference/old-paths.md#the-pi-task-loop)\n  end\n"
  },
  "task/sync": {
   "kind": "tasks.sync",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: docs/reference/tasks.md#bring-main-into-a-copy. Fetch the default branch through the copy's Node mirror and an incremental bundle. No credential enters the copy; sync never merges or rebases its branch.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: task:sync\n  %% kind: tasks.sync\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Instance Node<br/>reached over ssh\n  participant GH as GitHub<br/>origin\n  CLI->>GW: task:sync 42<br/>refresh the refs\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit task:sync 42 [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`, the MCP tool `task-sync`, or `POST /api/v1/tasks/42/sync` with an `Idempotency-Key` and no body; all three call the same route\n  %%> - the CLI sends a fresh key when none is given, follows the Operation until it ends, and prints `default_commit`, `behind_by` and `branch_commit`\n  Note over GW: Admit the sync<br/>group 42 · capture the intent\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to the Gateway\n  %%> - group 42 is open and external, with its own task copy; a group without one gets `tasks.no_workspace`: task VM workspaces are removed, and the Gateway sends no task VM intent any more\n  %%> - a task copy's container runs, else `tasks.instance_not_running`, and no copy turn of the group is unended, else `tasks.copy_turn_unended`\n  %%> - no other task runs on group 42, so no check or publish; the Gateway holds one lock per group\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - captures the repository, the default branch, the branch `task-42`, the workspace path and the SSH target of the copy's Node\n  %%> - for a task copy, adds `copy`: its Node's SSH target, its container, its start commit and its Node mirror\n  %%> - names the vault key of the read credential\n  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review:\n  %%> - no Swamp step calls the Gateway: the Gateway mints the read token (`contents: read`, valid one hour) as it starts the run and writes it to that vault key, as it does for the Pi token of a turn; the run gets only the key name\n  %%> - each run mints its own credential; a later run never reuses it\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation recorded\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the Operation with `operation:show`\n    GW->>SW: Start the copy's run<br/>operation-task-sync-copy\n    %%> Inputs:\n    %%> - the copy intent and the vault key name of the read token, never the token; every expression of the workflow reads only its keys\n    SW->>N: Bring in the base<br/>task-copy-base · base\n    %%> Why its own child:\n    %%> - only this path and the handoff check of a copy call `task-copy-base`; the shared workspace child of turns, checks and publishes takes no secret and no base input\n    %%> - the handoff check of a copy runs the same `base` phase with its own read token, under the same rules, before it merges the head ([task:check](/cli/task/check#how-it-works))\n    %%> On the Node:\n    %%> - first removes what an earlier, cancelled sync of the copy left in the copy's Node scratch and bundle folder, which the copy's Instance names\n    %%> - fetches `+refs/heads/<default>` from the Project's repository into the copy's own mirror, `/var/lib/orbit/instance-repositories/<instance>.git`; a missing mirror is made from the default's mirror, which only then is needed\n    %%> - the read token is the sync's Git header: it reaches that one `git fetch` only as its environment, through the stdin of a pipelined task; never argv, a file or a stored output, so a killed run leaves none behind\n    %%> - bundles the default branch without the commits the copy already holds (`^origin/<default>`, `^task-42`, where the mirror holds them); when the copy holds the head already, no bundle is made\n    %%> In the container:\n    %%> - root makes the bundle folder fresh and owns it and the bundle; `agent` can read it and cannot replace it\n    %%> - as `agent`, `git fetch` of the bundle into `refs/remotes/origin/<default>` only\n    %%> - reads the default head, the branch commit, `git rev-list --count task-42..origin/<default>` and the copy's start\n    %%> - then one content read, `work`: whether every path HEAD changed since its merge base with the default head holds the same content on the default head or in a published commit, and the uncommitted, untracked and stashed counts (docs/reference/tasks.md#published-work-of-a-copy); `task:cancel` and `task:complete` read it\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - HEAD, the working tree, the index and the local branches are not touched; nothing is merged or rebased\n    %%> Source:\n    %%> - the Project's repository, for every Project; a Project whose repository receives main only at a release sees the last released main\n    SW->>N: Discard the base scratch<br/>task-copy-base · base-cleanup\n    %%> Clean-up rule:\n    %%> - the bundles on the Node and in the copy go when the base ends, here after the run, at the next sync of the copy, or at the copy's destroy, whichever comes first; no credential is ever written, so none is left to remove\n    %%> Test:\n    %%> - a failed fetch, a cancel during the fetch and a cancel after the push leave no credential anywhere and no bundle in the copy once this step or the next base ran\n  Note over SW: Confirm the sync\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - native assert: the base and its discard succeeded\n  alt synced\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event · commits\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Record the result<br/>refs · behind_by\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - a task copy: `default_branch`, `default_commit`, `branch`, `branch_commit`, `behind_by`, `start` and `work`\n  else a step failed\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code · failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed; a branch that does not exist on origin fails the clone step\n    Note over GW: Mark the Operation failed<br/>workspace unchanged\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run it again: a new run mints a new read token, and a fetch that already happened makes the next bundle smaller\n  end\n  loop until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show<br/>Operation number\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the Operation status, and the commits and `behind_by` when the run succeeded\n  end\n"
  },
  "task/engine/sync": {
   "kind": "tasks.sync",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% tasks.sync of a task copy on the Gateway engine: the steps of task/sync.mmd (workflow operation-task-sync-copy, child task-copy-base) as executor primitives on the copy's Node. ADR 0267 and ADR 0271, implemented by TaskSyncEngineFlow: steps `fetch` (`instance.fetch_branch`), `sync` (`instance.sync_base`), `clear` and the failure step `clear_after_failure` (`instance.clear_base`). Runtime proof on a nested fleet is outstanding.\n  %% Same contract as the Swamp run (docs/reference/tasks.md#bring-main-into-a-copy): fetch the default branch into the copy's Node mirror, import only the missing objects, move only refs/remotes/origin/<default>. Nothing is merged or rebased; the credential never enters the copy.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: task:sync\n  %% kind: tasks.sync\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane EN icon=workflow pill=engine tone=purple\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=executor tone=amber\n  %% lane C icon=server pill=copy tone=green\n  %% lane GH icon=server pill=origin tone=gray\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant EN as Engine<br/>Gateway and NATS\n  participant N as Instance Node<br/>executor\n  participant C as Task copy<br/>via executor\n  participant GH as GitHub<br/>origin\n  CLI->>GW: task:sync 42<br/>refresh the refs\n  Note over GW: Admit the sync<br/>group 42 · capture the intent\n  %%> Same admission as task/sync.mmd: an open external group with its own running task copy (`tasks.instance_not_running`), no unended copy turn (`tasks.copy_turn_unended`), and the group lock: no check or publish runs beside it\n  %%> The intent pins the repository, the default branch, `task-42`, the copy's Instance, its Node mirror `/var/lib/orbit/instance-repositories/<instance>.git` and its start commit\n  alt the engine admits the whole Operation\n    %%> Admission, one decision per Operation (EngineKinds::routes):\n    %%> - `tasks.sync` is in `ORBIT_ENGINE_KINDS`\n    %%> - the copy's Node reports `instance.fetch_branch`, `instance.sync_base` and `instance.clear_base` in `$KV.NODES`, and the feature `instance.fetch_seed`\n    %%> - the copy, its runtime, its branch, its mirrors and its published and approved commits match the flow's shape and the group's own copy on that Node; a Project that reads with the Gateway's GitHub CLI login runs on Swamp\n    %%> - the flow accepts the plan shape; a part it does not cover throws `EngineUnsupported` before any order\n    %%> - eligible plan: a task copy (`copy` in the intent); a group without one keeps `tasks.no_workspace`\n    Note over GW: Bind steps, take the lock<br/>commit, publish the start\n    %%> - the copy's Instance lock commits with the step rows; a Swamp sync takes none; while the kind is enabled, a confirmed removal of the group's copy (`removing`, or a removal not ended) refuses the sync (`operation.target_busy`) before routing, so neither the engine nor the Swamp fallback starts it; an intent naming no copy of the group falls back as before; the step rows commit before the start event, from the pinned intent alone; an Operation never moves between engines after this\n    %%> - the group stays fenced by its running Operation and the copy by the Instance lock: no removal or other writer of the copy starts until the sync ends\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID\n    EN->>N: Fetch the default branch<br/>instance.fetch_branch · mirror\n    %%> Reuses `instance.fetch_branch` with the default branch: routing mints the one-hour read token (`contents: read`), keeps it encrypted in the Gateway cache for the journal's replay window or until the Operation ends, and keeps its digest with the Operation. Once a `fetch` order is published, every resend names that same credential, from the cache or by its digest, and none mints again. Only when the cache lost it before the first publication, so no Node ever received it, the first order mints it once more with the admitted access mode and uploads it: unlike a resend, no order can repeat a credential that was never sent\n    %%> The credential is written root-only beside the mirror in a file of this fetch's own for the Git calls and removed after them, whatever they did; a sibling fetch keeps its own file. It never reaches argv, the copy or a receipt\n    %%> Desired state: the mirror holds `refs/heads/<default>` at origin's head; a repeat fetches nothing new. A missing mirror is made bare from the default's mirror (`seed`) in the copy's staging repository `<mirror>.seeding`, which takes the mirror's name only when complete and which the copy's cleanup and destroy remove; a folder without `HEAD` is no mirror, the default's mirror is checked before anything changes, only an empty folder is replaced and any other is left as it is and refused, as the Swamp base phase makes it; with no default's mirror the fetch refuses\n    N->>GH: Fetch the default head<br/>HTTPS · credential on the Node\n    GH-->>N: Default head objects\n    N-->>EN: Mirror head<br/>output: commit\n    %%> `instance.fetch_branch` answers `commit: null` as success when origin lacks the branch (a create starts from the seed then); for a sync that is a failure: a null commit fails the Operation as `tasks.default_branch_missing`, and no old mirror head is ever imported\n    EN->>C: Import missing objects<br/>bundle · remote ref only\n    %%> Input: the non-null mirror head that the fetch receipt returned, pinned in the step input, so a resend imports the same commit even when origin moved\n    %%> On the Node: takes the copy's `origin/<default>` and `task-42` commits, keeps as prerequisites only those the mirror also holds (`cat-file -e`, as task-instance-workspace-phase.yml `Read which of the copy's commits the mirror holds`), pins `<head>` as `refs/orbit/task-base-<instance>` in the mirror, and bundles that ref excluding them; an unpublished `task-42` commit the mirror lacks is never a prerequisite\n    %%> In the copy: root makes the bundle folder fresh and owns it; as `agent`, `git fetch` of the bundle into the object store. The publish history reaches the step as a blob\n    alt the copy already holds the head\n      Note over C: No bundle to fetch<br/>the ref may still be stale\n      %%> A copy can hold the pinned commit (an earlier interrupted sync, a merge into task-42) while `refs/remotes/origin/<default>` still names an older commit\n    end\n    C->>C: Set only the remote ref<br/>update-ref, old value checked\n    %%> As `agent`: `git update-ref --no-deref refs/remotes/origin/<default> <head> <old>` with the value read before, refused first when the ref is symbolic, with or without a bundle; no other ref, HEAD, the index, the working tree or stashes is written\n    %%> A retry finds the ref already at the head and changes nothing; a ref that moved since the read refuses as `tasks.base_ref_mismatch` rather than overwriting it; any other update failure keeps the ordinary code and its diagnostic\n    C-->>EN: Refs and work state<br/>commits · behind_by · work\n    %%> The Gateway takes this receipt only when its evidence names the pinned head, the admitted branch and start, and a whole `work` read on that head. Read-back in the same primitive: `refs/remotes/origin/<default>` equals the pinned head, else `tasks.base_ref_mismatch`; HEAD and every local branch equal their values before the step; the branch commit, `git rev-list --count task-42..origin/<default>`, the copy's start and the `work` read (docs/reference/tasks.md#published-work-of-a-copy)\n    EN->>N: Clear the base scratch<br/>scratch · pin · bundle folder\n    %%> Two steps of one action: `clear` after the import, and `clear_after_failure` after a failed or cancelled fetch or import, as the engine runs no ordinary step after a failure. Each removes `/var/tmp/orbit-task-base-<instance>`, the mirror pin and the copy's bundle folder, and answers only when all three are gone; the import also clears right after its bundle. A later sync or the copy's destroy removes what an interruption left\n    N-->>EN: Scratch absent\n    opt the receipt is lost\n      EN->>C: Resend the same step<br/>fresh nonce · same step ID\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - the step passes its resend deadline with no receipt; two sends, one execution\n      %%> - the Operation keeps its lock, so group 42 and its copy stays fenced; no other Operation and no Swamp run acts on it meanwhile\n      %%> On the Node:\n      %%> - the journal holds `done` for this Operation step, action and target: the executor answers the stored result with `repeat: true` and does not act again\n      %%> - an order still `open` replays under the target lock first; an order that expired before the lock did nothing (`executor.order_expired`), is not journaled as a result, and the step is sent again\n      %%> - a result goes back only once its `done` record is durable; content the executor could not read never settles a step\n      %%> - the Gateway takes only each step's own evidence: the mirror head, the base evidence or the cleanup; anything else fails the step as `engine.receipt_invalid`\n      C-->>EN: Stored result<br/>repeat: true\n    end\n    alt every step verified\n      EN->>GW: Record refs and work<br/>default_commit · behind_by\n      %%> - the kind's own projection records the result, the same rows a Swamp run records\n    else a step fails\n      EN--xGW: Failed step<br/>error_code · redacted detail\n      %%> - a deterministic refusal is a terminal receipt too; the journal keeps it and a resend repeats it\n      Note over GW: Mark the sync failed<br/>workspace unchanged\n      %%> - a cancel sends no new step: it waits for the published order's receipt, clears the scratch and ends cancelled with no field and no error\n      %%> - retry: the same command; a new Operation mints a new token, and an earlier fetch makes the next bundle smaller\n      %%> - a failed fetch or import leaves the copy's branches and work as they were; only the remote ref may already be newer\n    end\n  else the engine does not admit it\n    Note over GW: Start the Swamp run<br/>operation-task-sync-copy\n    %%> - the kind is not enabled, a Node lacks the action, `$KV.NODES` is unreadable, or the flow throws `EngineUnsupported`: the whole Operation runs operation-task-sync-copy (task/sync.mmd)\n    %%> - no step of it runs on the engine; an Operation never splits across engines\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "task/copy-turn": {
   "kind": "tasks.copy_turn",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design (orchestrator, 2026-11-13, plan A; 2026-11-14 one ongoing thread per role, continued by default, effort per role through T3 provider instances; 2026-11-14 provider, model and effort per role from the group, the Project or the Gateway, checked against the copy's offer): a turn of a task that runs in a copy of its Project's default Instance. Swamp runs T3's own CLI in the copy as agent; stock T3 accepts only full-access MCP sessions it issues itself, so no T3 session leaves the container. The implementer and the reviewer each keep one T3 thread that later turns continue. 2026-11-14: a question ends the turn: Orbit stores it, then stops the run through the stock T3 server's own RPC, and the answer comes as the next turn.\n  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-15 (step 5, S-Pi b): the Pi turn on a task VM is removed with the Pi task loop; this is the only turn of task:turn.\n  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-15 (task small fixes): the admission starts the subtask, todo to running; completion stays the caller's.\n  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-16 (group 5321, Operation 24161): Orbit stops a turn's run. A turn cut by its bound stops its own run in its phase, and the next turn stops the run of an earlier turn that is over in Orbit (timed out, cancelled, lost) before it settles it, so `operation:cancel` and a new turn stop a run.\n  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-16 (group 5321 evidence): every turn gets the group's title and brief first and Orbit's fixed copy rules, so no agent guesses the scope or leaves a command running past its turn.\n  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-16 (reopen): the caller can reopen the last completed subtask of an open group; it is the current subtask again and its turns continue its threads.\n  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-16 (group 5425): the prompt gives the turn's clock, admission time and end; the result names the agent thread's id and the agent's bounded reply, so a driver reads a turn in one call.\n  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-19 (group 6005): each role gets its own fixed prompt text; the implementer runs the task check before it hands off, the reviewer gets the check's results and the handoff note, and a re-review covers only what changed since the last finished review.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: task:turn\n  %% kind: tasks.copy_turn\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve\n  participant N as Task copy<br/>incus exec · T3\n  CLI->>GW: task:turn TASK<br/>--role · --thread\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit task:turn SUBTASK --role=implementer --prompt=\"Run Doctor and commit a note.\"`\n  %%> - `orbit task:turn SUBTASK --role=implementer --prompt=\"Start over from the brief.\" --thread=new`\n  %%> - `orbit task:turn SUBTASK --role=reviewer --prompt=\"Review the checked commit.\" --check=CHECK`\n  Note over GW: Admit the turn<br/>owned copy · subtask · role thread\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - a group that owns a task copy, or whose Project has `nested_fleet` on, turns in its copy as `tasks.copy_turn`. Task VMs are removed: every other group answers `tasks.vm_runtime_removed` (422), and nothing is recorded. A nested-fleet group without a copy refuses here with `tasks.no_workspace`\n  %%> - composes the prompt from the fixed text of the turn's role (docs/reference/tasks.md#run-an-external-turn): the group's title and brief in `<task_brief>`, the subtask's in `<subtask_brief>`, its deliverables in `<deliverables>` (left out when it has none), the role's rules, the caller's prompt in `<turn>`, then the line `Orbit ends this turn at END.`, where END is the admission's time in UTC plus the 3,600 s bound. No role gets a rule of the other role; the texts name nothing of one Project but its task check command. The 100000-character limit covers the whole text; a longer one refuses `tasks.prompt_too_large`, and nothing is recorded\n  %%> - an implementer's rules: how to work in the copy, then before it hands off: check each deliverable, run the Project's task check and fix what it reports (left out, and the later steps numbered on, for a Project without one), give the asked proof with no stand-in or ask, clean up, commit. Its last message is the handoff note. It asks with the question tool; a question ends the turn, and the answer comes as the next turn\n  %%> - a reviewer's text names its check, the check's commit and the base that check compared against (the start, or the tree of `git merge-tree --write-tree START HEAD` when the check merged the default head first), and each deliverable's result in that check (`passed`, `failed`, `not_checked`). It gives the implementer's handoff note: the reply of the latest finished implementer turn on the subtask that did not ask, as stored, or no block when there is none. Then the reviewer rule (docs/reference/tasks.md#the-reviewer-rule), the pull request text rule (docs/reference/tasks.md#the-reviewers-pull-request-text) and the fixed verdict line\n  %%> - the first reviewer turn on a subtask gets the first-review lines; every later one gets the re-review lines, with its round and the commit of the latest finished reviewer turn on the subtask. A finished turn succeeded and did not ask; a reviewer turn that failed or was cancelled does not count, and one that asked finished no review, so the turn that answers it keeps its round. Orbit composes each turn from the records, so a thread that started under an older prompt gets a valid prompt\n  %%> - a turn that continues a thread whose last turn asked quotes that question, each with its options, first inside `<turn>`, before the caller's prompt: the prompt is the answer. Its intent also names exactly those stored request ids, once per request\n  %%> - without `--thread`, the turn continues its role's thread: the implementer's thread of the current subtask, or the group's reviewer thread, when it is a `t3` thread of this copy. A role with no such thread starts one. `--thread=new` starts a new one; `--thread=ID` must name the thread the turn would continue\n  %%> - a new thread takes the role's provider, model and effort, each from the group, else the Project's default, else the Gateway's `ORBIT_TASKS_COPY_<ROLE>_PROVIDER`, `_MODEL` and `_EFFORT` (shipped: `claude`, `claude-opus-5-5`, `medium` for the implementer and `high` for the reviewer)\n  %%> - the provider and the effort name the T3 provider instance that carries the effort, as `claudeMedium`; the thread records the driver `t3`, the copy's Node, the model, the effort and that instance\n  %%> - a continued thread keeps its model and effort: T3 keeps the thread's provider instance\n  %%> - a reviewer turn names the subtask's checked candidate with `--check`; the commit goes into the turn\n  %%> - the current subtask moves from `todo` to `running` in the admission's transaction, under the group's row lock; a refused turn moves nothing. No turn moves it further: the caller completes it (docs/reference/tasks.md#subtask-status-in-a-task-copy), and may reopen the last one with `task:update SUBTASK --status=running`, which makes it the current subtask again (docs/reference/tasks.md#reopen-the-last-subtask)\n  %%> - the intent names the copy's container, the T3 command, whether the turn starts or continues its thread, the provider instance, model and model options, the candidate, the 3,600 s bound and a title that names the Orbit thread; no credential and no vault key\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the group owns its copy and the copy is `active`; otherwise `tasks.no_workspace` or `tasks.copy_not_ready`, and nothing is recorded\n  %%> - the copy's last completion did not fail; otherwise `tasks.copy_completion_failed` with its Operation, failed step, error code and the retry `orbit instance:complete ID`, and nothing is recorded\n  %%> - the role is `implementer` or `reviewer`, and the request names no model: the role `consult` and a `model` were options of the removed Pi turn, and either answers `validation.failed` (422) on that field\n  %%> - `--thread=ID` names the role's thread that the turn would continue; otherwise `tasks.thread_role_mismatch`\n  %%> - a reviewer's check is the subtask's checked candidate; otherwise `tasks.commit_not_checked`\n  %%> - a new thread's resolved provider, model and effort are ones the provider honours (docs/reference/tasks.md#copy-turn-agents): `claude` at `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh` or `max`, `codex` at those or `ultra`, each with a model name; `antigravity` with one of its models at that model's efforts. Otherwise `tasks.agent_unsupported` (422) naming the field, the value, its source and what the provider accepts, and nothing is recorded\n  %%> - a new thread's provider and effort name a provider instance that Orbit writes into the copy's T3 settings: one per Claude and Codex effort, and the one `antigravity` instance in a copy whose create settings hold it. Otherwise `tasks.copy_turn_unoffered` (422) with what the copy offers in `details.offered`, and nothing is recorded. Orbit owns this list, so the check reads nothing in the copy\n  %%> - a new Antigravity thread starts on the tiered model `<model>-<effort>`, such as `gemini-4-neon-medium`; the intent also names the base model, which T3's catalog lists. A continued Antigravity thread whose first turn started on its bare model is refused with `tasks.copy_thread_untiered` (422): start a new thread with `--thread=new`\n  %%> - no other Operation of the group runs; otherwise `operation.target_busy`\n  %%> - one rule settles a turn: its run is over, and a reviewer's detach is undone. A thread is its captured T3 ID, whatever its title; only a turn with no ID that T3 holds is found by its title. A recorded run is over only when a readback shows that exact run in a status that ends it; an accepted interrupt, or a run or thread T3 does not show, is no proof. A turn with no recorded run is over when T3 lists its thread in no active state. A turn whose result shows that, or that a later turn's phase found so, is settled\n  %%> - every turn of the group that started a run and is not settled goes into the intent, to be settled first: its Operation, its title, and for a reviewer's turn the task branch. A check, a publish or a proof of the copy cannot read T3: it answers `tasks.copy_turn_unended` with that Operation in `details.operation_id`, and waits for the next turn, which stops the earlier run and settles it. `operation:cancel` ends a running turn's Operation, and the next turn stops its run. When the last subtask is completed, the caller reopens it (docs/reference/tasks.md#reopen-the-last-subtask) and its next turn settles the copy; when a completed subtask cannot reopen, no turn can run and `task:cancel` is the only way out\n  GW->>SW: Start one run<br/>operation-task-copy-turn\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation recorded\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - follow the Operation while Swamp runs the turn\n  Note over SW: input\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - writes the copy's container, the SSH target and the turn into the private run folder\n  SW->>N: Run the turn\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - one Ansible phase on the copy's Node: `incus exec` into the copy, then `runuser -u agent`, non-login, with the agent's groups\n  %%> - first one `true` in the copy: a copy the phase cannot reach fails before anything is launched, so its group stays free\n  %%> - before any checkout or start, a new thread checks its model against the slugs and aliases in T3's cached `model-manifest.json` for the driver named in `settings.json`. A match passes without a provider request\n  %%> - if the copy cannot read the provider instance's driver, the turn fails with `tasks.copy_turn_model_unverified` before checkout or launch\n  %%> - if the catalog does not confirm a Codex model, the copy sends one bounded, tool-free request to its configured Codex Responses provider. A usable response must name the requested model exactly. A provider rejection fails with `tasks.copy_turn_model_unoffered`. An inconclusive probe fails with `tasks.copy_turn_model_unverified`. Both leave the thread unlaunched\n  %%> - for other drivers, an unlisted model fails with `tasks.copy_turn_model_unoffered`; the listed slugs stay in the run folder's evidence, not in the error. Without a catalog for the driver, that check is skipped and the provider can reject the model in the run\n  %%> - then each earlier turn to settle: `t3 thread list` finds its thread by the captured thread ID, by title only when no ID is recorded or listed. A thread that T3 still shows active has the earlier turn's captured run stopped first: `t3_copy_rpc.mjs stop THREAD RUN` interrupts exactly that run and waits for its end, and refuses another run; a turn with no captured run is not stopped and stays open. A thread in a state that runs nothing (the result step's list), none of that title, or whose run the stop ended or found ended, has ended; a state Orbit does not know leaves its turn open; the evidence names each stopped run. A reviewer's detach in effect is marked in the copy by the ref `refs/orbit/review-detach`, at the tip the branch had. Once every earlier run has ended and the mark is set, a still detached checkout is restored as after a reviewer's turn (reset, clean, the task branch); it must then be clean on that branch at the mark's tip, and the mark is cleared. Without the mark the tree is not touched. A stop that fails leaves its turn unsettled: nothing is touched or launched, and the phase fails with `tasks.copy_turn_unended`; the work in the copy stays\n  %%> - a continued turn then finds its T3 thread by its title with `t3 thread list`. A list that fails or is not JSON fails with `tasks.copy_turn_failed`; when T3 holds not exactly one thread of the title, the phase fails with `tasks.copy_thread_missing`. Nothing is launched\n  %%> - before any reviewer checkout or launch, a continued turn that answers stored questions runs `t3_copy_rpc.mjs answered THREAD REQUEST...`. In one RPC session it cancels exactly those pending user-input requests with `runtime-request.respond`, decision `cancel`, without sending a reply message, then reads them back. Requests already resolved, cancelled or absent need no action. An intent admitted before `answered` was added treats its absence as an empty list. If a stored request stays pending, or its resolution cannot be confirmed, the phase fails with `tasks.copy_question_unresolved` and launches nothing. Interrupting a T3 run alone does not resolve every provider's questions\n  %%> - a reviewer turn then requires a clean checkout on a branch, reads the branch's tip, sets the copy's detach mark at it and checks the candidate out, detached; a checkout with changes fails with `tasks.copy_tree_dirty` before anything is launched. The detach mark is written as soon as the checkout is detached\n  %%> - a new thread's model options: a Codex thread carries its effort as the `reasoningEffort` option, since T3 0.1.7 sends Codex no other effort; a Claude thread carries none, and its instance's `CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL` sets its effort\n  %%> - a new thread is created first: `t3_copy_rpc.mjs create TITLE INSTANCE MODEL OPTIONS` makes it in T3's project at `/srv/workspace`, in full access, with no message, through T3's own RPC; stock `t3 thread start` takes no model options. A create that makes no thread runs nothing and fails with `tasks.copy_turn_failed` and T3's message\n  %%> - the run: `t3 thread send --wait --json THREAD -` on the created or continued thread. The prompt goes on stdin, bounded by `timeout` at 3,600 s\n  %%> - a wait that the bound cut (`timeout` exits 124, or 137 after the 30 s kill grace) leaves T3's run going. The start printed nothing, so Orbit does not know the run's ID and stops no run: the turn holds until T3 lists its thread in a state that runs nothing, or `task:cancel`\n  %%> - `t3 thread list` finds the thread the turn sent to by its ID, also when the bound cut the wait or a person renamed it; only a turn that made no thread is looked for by its title. `t3 thread show --json` reads it back, and `t3_copy_rpc.mjs usage THREAD` reads the token use T3 keeps on each provider turn of the thread from `orchestration.getThreadProjection`; a usage read that fails leaves the use unknown and fails nothing. Each read is bounded at 60 s\n  %%> - after a reviewer turn whose run is over, also a failed one, the phase discards what the reviewer changed and checks the branch out again, then reads that the checkout is clean, on its branch, and that the branch is at the tip read before the detach; a verified restore clears the copy's detach mark. A run that may still write, such as a cut one T3 lists running, is not restored: the mark stays, and the next turn restores the checkout once it proves the run over\n  %%> - a run that waits for an answer (`--wait` exits 2): first `t3_copy_rpc.mjs read` stores the thread's pending requests, each question with its id, header, text and options as the agent asked them. Only when every request is a question stored whole, and only after that evidence is written, `t3_copy_rpc.mjs interrupt` stops that run with T3's `run.interrupt` and waits for it to end within the helper's 42 s deadline. It stops the run only while it waits for exactly the stored requests and every request node belongs to that run: a run that someone answered, or that now asks something else, goes on. Each call is bounded at 60 s\n  %%> - `t3_copy_rpc.mjs stop THREAD` reads the thread, finds its active run, sends the same `run.interrupt` as `interrupt` and waits for the run's end within the same 42 s deadline. It answers whether the run stopped, its id and its last state; a thread with no active run answers `stopped: false` with no run. Both commands share one interrupt path\n  %%> - the helper runs on the copy's Node.js runtime, from stdin. It speaks the stock T3 server's own WebSocket RPC (`orchestration.getThreadProjection`, `orchestration.dispatchCommand`) with a session from `t3 auth session issue`: read-only for the read, one minute, held only in the helper's memory, revoked before it exits. T3 stays stock\n  %%> - writes each evidence file as soon as its command ends: the thread found to continue, the reviewer's checkout, the detach mark, the launch mark, the settle's stops, the start's end, the question, the interrupt, the stop of a cut run, the read, the restore; a cut phase still tells whether a turn was launched\n  %%> - T3's CLI issues its own full-access session inside the copy and revokes it when it exits; the replies stay in the run folder, never in a log\n  Note over N: Stored requests<br/>resolve · send\n  Note over N: T3 runs the turn<br/>provider instance · effort\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - the copy's T3 settings hold one `claudeAgent` provider instance per effort level, `claudeLow` to `claudeMax`, each with `CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL` in its environment; the copy's create writes them\n  %%> - Claude Code takes that variable over the `--effort` T3 passes, so the thread runs at its recorded effort: verified with Claude Code 2.1.311 and T3 0.1.7\n  Note over SW: result\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - maps the run's status: `completed` is done, `waiting` asks for input, any other status fails with `tasks.turn_failed`, a cut wait fails with `tasks.turn_timeout`, a start that T3 refused fails with `tasks.copy_turn_failed`, a thread to continue that T3 does not hold fails with `tasks.copy_thread_missing`, a stored answered request whose resolution is not confirmed fails before launch with `tasks.copy_question_unresolved`\n  %%> - a turn that asks keeps its question in the result. Its run stopped, it is `asking_for_input` and over. A question Orbit could not read fails with `tasks.copy_question_unread`, and a run Orbit could not stop with `tasks.copy_question_unstopped`; neither is over\n  %%> - names the provider instance, the model and the effort the thread started with, as the admission recorded them\n  %%> - a reviewer turn with the detach mark and no restore that reads clean on its branch at its tip fails with `tasks.copy_review_unrestored`, whatever its run did, also when it failed or nothing was launched; the error keeps the run's own error\n  %%> - keeps the message text of the thread, all its turns, redacted and bounded to 256 KiB with the newest kept; T3's CLI gives no tool items. A user message keeps only its ID, author and time, never its text: redaction does not catch an ordinary private prompt\n  %%> - names `sent` from the launch mark and `launched` only when the start reported the run it started; a send with no reported run is possibly sent. Each stored question names the run that owns its request as `run_id`; a read with a request of no owner stores no question, stops no run and fails with `tasks.copy_question_unread`. A start that printed no status names no end. The error keeps no text of T3 or a provider and nothing parsed from it. A run status outside T3's known statuses is not named. `ended` follows one rule on the turn's own run, row by row as the result step's `over()` names them: positive evidence of that run's end, never another run of the thread; a question not stored with its owner run or not confirmed stopped keeps the hold `spent` stays empty: the CLI driver proves no earlier run\n  %%> - records whether the turn is over: nothing was launched, the start printed an end other than a wait for an answer, T3 lists the thread the turn sent to, by its ID, in no active state, or, for a turn that made no thread, holds none of its title. A thread of the turn's ID that T3 does not list proves nothing. An unrestored reviewer turn is never over\n  %%> - names the earlier turns it found settled; the Gateway counts them settled from then on\n  %%> - names `usage`, the thread's token use summed over all its provider turns: `input_tokens` is T3's `inputTokens` less `cachedInputTokens`, `cached_input_tokens` is `cachedInputTokens`, `output_tokens` is `outputTokens`, `tokens` is `inputTokens` plus `outputTokens`, and `model_calls` the count of provider turns. No provider turn, a provider turn without `usageStatus` `complete`, or a failed read is null: unknown, never zeros\n  Note over SW: cleanup\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - removes the private run folder\n  Note over SW: outcome\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - native assert: the turn is done, or it asked and its run stopped\n  alt the turn is done or asked\n    SW-->>GW: Native terminal result<br/>capture result\n    Note over GW: Record the thread<br/>state · question · transcript\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the thread's state and error, T3's thread id as its external id, and the transcript in the Gateway's archive: `task:agent:show` reads it after the copy is gone\n    %%> - the external id's admission: the ID that capture set stays `admitted` until T3 reports it back, which makes it `confirmed`; a reported ID that replaced it, or any report on a row from before the mark, is `overwritten`\n    %%> - only a `launched` turn replaces the thread's questions; a thread whose stored request IDs are exactly the result's `spent` asks nothing any more\n    %%> - the archive keeps a user message only by its ID, author and time, also when an older worker sends its text\n    %%> - the error is the fixed sentence of its code, plus a fixed sentence that the group holds when the turn has not ended, never the worker's text; no diagnostic field is kept; a question without its owner run is not stored, records `tasks.copy_question_unread` unless the turn is `tasks.copy_review_unrestored`, and keeps the hold; a code outside the copy turn's codes is `tasks.copy_turn_failed`\n    %%> - a turn that asked keeps its question on the thread: `task:agent:list` shows it until the thread's next turn. The answer is the next `task:turn` of the role, or a message to the thread from a T3 client\n    %%> - the result names the provider instance, the model and the effort the thread started with, from its record\n    %%> - the result names `agent_id`, the agent thread's row that `task:agent:show GROUP AGENT_ID` reads, and `reply`: the text of the transcript's last `assistant` entry, its first 16,384 characters, then ` [cut: N more characters; read task:agent:show GROUP AGENT_ID]` when it is longer. No assistant entry, such as a transcript of user entries and Orbit's truncation notice only: `reply` is null. The transcript stays out of the result\n    %%> - a result's `usage` replaces the thread's `tokens`, `input_tokens`, `cached_input_tokens`, `output_tokens` and `model_calls`: it is the T3 thread's whole use, also for a continued thread. A null or absent `usage` leaves them as they are\n  else the turn failed or ran past its bound\n    SW--xGW: Native failed result<br/>error code\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the thread is `failed` with T3's status as its error, and the transcript T3 had\n    %%> - the result names `agent_id` and `reply` as for a turn that is done\n    %%> - a turn cut by its bound whose run Orbit stopped is over. A cancelled turn, a lost phase, or a cut turn whose stop failed may still run in the copy: the next turn stops its run, then settles it once T3 shows its run over and a reviewer's checkout is clean on the task branch. That turn fails with `tasks.copy_turn_unended` only when a stop fails or the checkout stays unclean. The work stays in the copy. `task:cancel` still ends it with the copy\n  end\n  loop until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show<br/>Operation number\n  end\n"
  },
  "tool/adopt": {
   "kind": "tool.adopt",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: Nick 2026-11-01, liveness 2026-11-02. Like doctor: only the Node agent reads Nodes; adopt checks its latest report. No task, no Swamp run, no SSH (R1).\n  %% status: reviewed\n  %% command: tool:adopt\n  %% kind: tool.adopt\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane A icon=server pill=observer tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant A as Node agent<br/>orbit-agent\n  loop on a short interval\n    A->>GW: Heartbeat\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Gateway marks the Node online, or offline when heartbeats stop\n  end\n  loop continuously, on change\n    Note over A: Observe packages<br/>per Tool Manager, apt sources\n    %%> What it observes:\n    %%> - the installed packages and versions for each Tool Manager on the Node\n    %%> - the apt sources and their signing keys\n    %%> Runs as:\n    %%> - read-only; the Node agent never changes a Node\n    A->>GW: Report observations<br/>with observed_at\n    %%> What it sends:\n    %%> - what changed since the last report, with the time it was observed\n    Note over GW: Store latest facts<br/>per Node, per Tool Manager\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Gateway always has a recent picture of what is installed on each Node\n  end\n  CLI->>GW: tool:adopt ripgrep<br/>--node=12 --manager=apt\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit tool:adopt ripgrep --node=12 --manager=apt --yes`\n  %%> - syntax: `<package> --node=ID --manager=MANAGER [--constraint=RANGE] [--repository=NAME] [--yes]`\n  %%> What adopting means:\n  %%> - Orbit takes over a package that is already installed, so it manages later updates and removal\n  %%> - it installs, updates, removes and repins nothing\n  %%> Confirmation:\n  %%> - without `--yes`, the CLI asks \"Take ownership of package ripgrep (apt on Node #12) and manage later updates and removal?\"; the default is No\n  Note over GW: Check against the facts<br/>installed · constraint · supported\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12\n  %%> - Node 12's platform supports the Tool Manager\n  %%> - the package isn't already a Tool on Node 12, and isn't protected (such as Caddy or PHP)\n  %%> - file mode requires an entry for the package and refuses definition overrides\n  %%> Checks in the agent's latest report:\n  %%> - the package is installed, and its version fits `--constraint`\n  %%> - in file mode the installed version matches the catalog coordinate\n  %%> - Orbit can manage this package later (no adoption block)\n  %%> - with `--repository`: the named apt repository of Node 12 is ready, and its source and signing key on the Node match its definition\n  alt package can be adopted\n    GW-->>CLI: Tool recorded<br/>Tool 31: installed 14.1.0\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Tool 31 recorded as installed, with the observed version\n    %%> - the Tool references the named apt repository, when one was given\n    %%> - first line: the agent's liveness, online or offline since <time>\n    %%> - the answer comes at once; there is no task to follow\n    %%> - when the agent is offline, the facts are marked \"possibly outdated, agent silent since <time>\"\n  else not installed or blocked\n    GW--xCLI: Refused<br/>tool.adoption_unsupported\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the package isn't installed, its version is outside the constraint, or Orbit can't manage it\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - first line: the agent's liveness, as above\n    %%> - nothing is recorded\n    %%> - when the agent is offline, the refusal is marked \"possibly outdated, agent silent since <time>\"\n  end\n"
  },
  "tool/apt-repository-add": {
   "kind": "tool.apt_repository_add",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% docs/reference/tools.md#apt-repositories\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: tool:apt-repository-add\n  %% kind: tool.apt_repository_add\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>control Node\n  participant N as Node 12<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: tool:apt-repository-add<br/>--node=12 --name=rex\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit tool:apt-repository-add --node=12 --name=rex --definition='{\"uris\":[…],\"suites\":[\"preview\"],\"components\":[\"main\"],\"signing_key\":{…}}'`\n  %%> - syntax: `--node=ID --name=NAME --definition=JSON [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  Note over GW: Validate, record rex<br/>adding · task #5140\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs the apt manager; no other apt write holds Node 12\n  %%> - no other repository of Node 12 has one of its URIs and suites (`tool.repository_conflict`)\n  %%> - a name keeps its definition until its removal succeeds (`tool.repository_conflict`); the same definition republishes it\n  %%> - file mode takes the definition only from the catalog (`tool.not_in_catalog`, `tool.file_override`)\n  GW->>SW: Start the Tools phase<br/>with only this repository\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5140\n  SW->>N: Hold its preference<br/>to its owned Tools\n  SW->>N: Verify the key, write<br/>keyring and source\n  %%> Key:\n  %%> - the downloaded key has exactly the recorded primary fingerprint, or `tool.repository_key_mismatch` before any keyring or source write\n  SW->>N: Read its package index\n  alt published\n    SW-->>GW: Report rex ready\n    Note over GW: rex: ready\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - Tool writes from rex are admitted from now on\n  else key, fetch or write failed\n    SW--xGW: Report rex failed<br/>error_code\n    Note over GW: rex: failed with the code\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again, or converge the Node; both run this publication\n    %%> - a run that never started leaves the repository as it was; a timed-out one leaves it failed\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5140\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "tool/apt-repository-remove": {
   "kind": "tool.apt_repository_remove",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% docs/reference/tools.md#apt-repositories\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: tool:apt-repository-remove\n  %% kind: tool.apt_repository_remove\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>control Node\n  participant N as Node 12<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: apt-repository-remove<br/>repository 7\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit tool:apt-repository-remove 7 --yes`\n  %%> - syntax: `<repository> [--yes] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  Note over GW: Validate, mark it removing<br/>task #5141 · rex on Node 12\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - no Tool references the repository (`tool.repository_in_use`); remove its Tools first\n  %%> - no other apt write holds Node 12\n  GW->>SW: Start the Tools phase<br/>removing only this repository\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5141\n  SW->>N: Remove its source,<br/>keyring and preference\n  alt removed\n    SW-->>GW: Report rex removed\n    Note over GW: Delete the record\n  else the removal failed\n    SW--xGW: Report failed<br/>error_code\n    Note over GW: rex: failed\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5141\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "tool/converge": {
   "kind": "tool.converge",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: tool:converge\n  %% kind: tool.converge\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP\n  participant GW as Gateway\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>control Node\n  participant N as Target Node\n  CLI->>GW: tool:converge --node=ID\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - syntax: `--node=ID [--tool-id=TOOL ...] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  Note over GW: Admit registered Tools<br/>with desired coordinates\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the Node is active and SSH-managed on Linux or macOS\n  %%> - the caller has access to the Node and no competing write owns a selected manager on this Node\n  %%> - admit all eligible Tools, or only the selected memberships, and the apt repositories they reference; one on another repository's URI and suite is refused\n  alt no registered Tools\n    GW-->>CLI: Complete · changed 0\n  else registered Tools\n    GW->>SW: Start Tools-only Operation\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted\n    SW->>N: Publish apt repositories<br/>over pinned SSH\n    %%> Repositories:\n    %%> - each one is that repository's own add: hold its preferences to its owned Tools, verify the key, write keyring and source, read its index\n    %%> - its outcome makes it ready or failed; a failed one stops only its own Tools\n    SW->>N: Reconcile Tools<br/>over pinned SSH\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - install missing packages and apply exact desired versions; leave existing unpinned packages alone\n    %%> - do not run core, role, network, or host Conn phases\n    %%> - add a Tool to its repository's preference only after it is verified installed\n    SW-->>GW: Report changes, versions<br/>and repository outcomes\n    CLI->>GW: Follow the Operation\n    GW-->>CLI: Terminal receipt\n  end\n"
  },
  "tool/install": {
   "kind": "tool.install",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% docs/reference/tools.md#install-and-update-a-tool\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: tool:install\n  %% kind: tool.install\n  %% Pinned Debian downloads use native SSH curl exit codes for transport-only fallback, then verify the pin before publication.\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Node 12<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: tool:install ripgrep<br/>--node=12 --manager=apt\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit tool:install ripgrep --node=12 --manager=apt`\n  %%> - syntax: `[package] --node=ID [--manager=MANAGER] [--tool-version=VERSION] [--constraint=CONSTRAINT] [--repository=NAME] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  Note over GW: Validate, record the Tool<br/>Tool: installing · task #5133\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12; the manager runs on its platform\n  %%> - the package is not a recipe package of Node 12's roles\n  %%> - file mode requires a catalog entry and refuses overrides; database mode accepts an exact desired version\n  %%> - `--repository` names a ready apt repository of Node 12 (`tool.repository_missing`, `tool.repository_not_ready`); the install never publishes it\n  %%> - `conn-server` on a configured host records membership and the desired release without a Swamp run; an explicit rollout applies it\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-tool-write\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5133\n  SW->>SW: Prepare native SSH<br/>admitted intent · pinned host key\n  SW->>N: Apply the package<br/>native SSH\n  %%> Scope:\n  %%> - direct writes share admission and candidate policy with convergence, but use the native SSH workflow, not an Ansible phase\n  %%> - only this Tool can change\n  %%> - a repository's preference holds only owned Tools until this Tool is verified installed from it; then it adds this Tool\n  %%> Version:\n  %%> - select the exact admitted version, or the manager's candidate for an unpinned database Tool; check the constraint before writing\n  SW->>N: Read resulting state<br/>native SSH\n  alt check passes\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded\n    Note over GW: Record the version<br/>Tool: installed 14.1.0\n  else the install fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - a Tool that needs another version of a held recipe package fails with `tool.recipe_conflict`; role software stays as tested\n    Note over GW: Mark the Tool failed\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5133\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "tool/remove": {
   "kind": "tool.remove",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% docs/reference/tools.md#remove-a-tool. R1: a record-only change starts no run.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: tool:remove\n  %% kind: tool.remove\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Node 12<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: tool:remove 31\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit tool:remove 31 --yes`\n  %%> - syntax: `<tool> [--yes] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  Note over GW: Validate, lock the Tool<br/>task #5135 · ripgrep on Node 12\n  alt protected host software, a Tool Orbit does not own outside an apt repository, or host Conn membership\n    Note over GW: Delete only the record<br/>no run, package stays\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the recipe owns the package; removing it would break the role\n    %%> - a Tool owns its package only when Orbit recorded it: a successful install, an adoption or a convergence that installed it; no failure, retry, cleanup or sibling changes that\n    %%> - withdrawing a host Conn membership leaves the service running and excludes it from future release rollouts\n    GW-->>CLI: Tool removed<br/>package or service kept\n  else Orbit owns the package, or the Tool's apt repository needs a repin\n    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-tool-write\n    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5135\n    SW->>SW: Prepare native SSH<br/>admitted intent · pinned host key\n    SW->>N: Remove the Tool<br/>native SSH\n    %%> Scope:\n    %%> - repin the owned remaining packages of the Tool's apt repository; the repository stays, even after its last Tool\n    %%> - `package_owned: false` skips every package read and plan, keeps the package and only repins\n    %%> - a removal that would also remove a held recipe package is refused\n    SW->>N: Read resulting state<br/>native SSH\n    alt check passes\n      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded\n      Note over GW: Delete the Tool record\n    else the removal fails\n      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code\n      Note over GW: Mark the Tool failed\n      %%> Retry:\n      %%> - a failed repin keeps the Tool failed with `tool.repository_failed`\n      %%> - run the same command again after a fix\n      %%> - a timed-out removal, its start reply lost included, keeps no uninstall authority until its receipt or Swamp shows the package was never reached\n      %%> - until Swamp answers whether an unbound timed-out start became a run, the retry refuses with `tool.removal_unsettled`\n      %%> - a package step that ran, a nonzero exit included, ends uninstall authority unless the check after it finds the package still present, whatever its version\n    end\n    loop every 2 s until terminal\n      CLI->>GW: operation:show #5135\n      GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "tool/update": {
   "kind": "tool.update",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% docs/reference/tools.md#install-and-update-a-tool\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: tool:update\n  %% kind: tool.update\n  %% Pinned Debian downloads use native SSH curl exit codes for transport-only fallback, then verify the pin before publication.\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant N as Node 12<br/>reached over ssh\n  CLI->>GW: tool:update 31\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit tool:update 31`\n  %%> - syntax: `<tool> [--tool-version=VERSION] [--repository=NAME] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  Note over GW: Validate, lock the Tool<br/>task #5134 · ripgrep on Node 12\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12\n  %%> - file mode requires its declared coordinate without overrides; database mode retains an existing pin unless the caller supplies a new one\n  %%> - a host `conn-server` update records the desired release without a Swamp run; an explicit rollout applies it\n  %%> - an apt Tool's repository must be ready (`tool.repository_not_ready`); `--repository` may only repeat the one it references (`tool.repository_conflict`)\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-tool-write\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5134\n  SW->>SW: Prepare native SSH<br/>admitted intent · pinned host key\n  SW->>N: Apply the package<br/>native SSH\n  %%> Scope:\n  %%> - update uses the direct install/update workflow, limited to this Tool; it shares admission and candidate policy, not the Ansible executor, with convergence\n  %%> - a repository's preference holds only owned Tools until this Tool is verified installed from it; then it adds this Tool\n  %%> Version:\n  %%> - select the exact admitted version, or the manager's candidate for an unpinned database Tool; check the constraint before writing\n  SW->>N: Read resulting state<br/>native SSH\n  alt check passes\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded\n    Note over GW: Record the version<br/>applied · unchanged\n  else the update fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - the Tool keeps its old version when the package manager did not change it\n    Note over GW: Mark the Tool failed\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5134\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n  end\n"
  },
  "vault/destroy": {
   "kind": "vault.teardown",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1 (--keep-items is record-only), R2-R4, swamp-vault-design. Same shape as tool.remove. docs/reference/vaultwarden.md.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rule 22 (plain pinned CLI primitives, native fan-out, vault-backed credentials and native end check).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: vault:destroy\n  %% kind: vault.teardown\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane VW icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant VW as Vaultwarden<br/>vault.orbit Route\n  CLI->>GW: vault:destroy\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit vault:destroy --yes`\n  %%> - syntax: `[--keep-items] [--yes] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Confirmation:\n  %%> - in a terminal the CLI asks a default-No question; without a terminal, or with `--json`, `--yes` is required\n  Note over GW: Validate, lock the vault<br/>task #5169 · 14 items\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the `vault` extension is on and Vaultwarden is set up, else `vault.not_configured`\n  %%> - no other setup, sync or destruction runs, else `vault.sync_busy`\n  %%> Decides:\n  %%> - whether the published items must leave Vaultwarden, or only the setup goes\n  opt --keep-items\n    Note over GW: Delete setup and ledger<br/>R1: no run, items stay\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the stored account, organization, collection and publishing ledger are deleted\n    %%> - the items stay in Vaultwarden; a later setup on the same collection can adopt them\n    %%> - no Swamp run starts (R1); Vaultwarden is not contacted\n    GW-->>CLI: Destroyed<br/>items kept in Vaultwarden\n    %%> What you get:\n    %%> - the final result at once; the command ends here without the deletion flow below\n  end\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-vault-teardown\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - without `--keep-items`; the opt-in path above has already returned\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - pushes the account secrets to the Swamp vault as `vault-teardown-5169` after commit (R3), then starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - the setup and the ledger of published item IDs and sources, and the vault key names\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5169\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  Note over SW: Prepare public data\n  Note over SW: Create CLI session\n  Note over SW: Prepare session file\n  SW->>VW: Configure the server\n  SW->>VW: Log in\n  SW->>VW: Unlock the vault\n  Note over SW: Resolve the session\n  SW->>VW: Sync the vault\n  SW->>VW: Read organizations\n  Note over SW: Select the organization\n  SW->>VW: Read private inventory\n  Note over SW: inventory\n  SW->>VW: Read private trash\n  Note over SW: trash\n  Note over SW: Select published items\n  SW->>VW: Delete the items\n  SW->>VW: Log out\n  Note over SW: Remove CLI session\n  Note over SW: Confirm teardown\n  %% Organization membership and collection inventory run in parallel.\n  %% Each CLI step is one bounded command; typed methods only shape private data.\n  %% Delete the items fans out over the admitted ledger and skips non-matching IDs and sources.\n  %% All shared-item refusals precede deletion; cleanup also follows skipped dependencies.\n  alt every item deleted\n    VW-->>SW: 14 items deleted\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the deleted item IDs\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Delete setup and ledger<br/>task succeeded\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the stored account, organization, collection and publishing ledger are deleted\n    %%> - task #5169 succeeds\n  else a step fails\n    VW--xSW: refused or no answer\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - login fails, Vaultwarden is down, an item is shared, or a delete fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its safe error code\n    Note over GW: Keep the setup<br/>ledger minus deleted items\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the setup stays; the ledger drops only the items that were deleted; task #5169 fails\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - safe: run the same command again; it deletes only what is still there\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5169\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "vault/setup": {
   "kind": "vault.setup",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R2-R4, swamp-vault-design (Vaultwarden = human copy). docs/reference/vaultwarden.md.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rule 22 (no script-read step; the Bitwarden CLI calls run on 209).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: vault:setup\n  %% kind: vault.setup\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane VW icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant VW as Vaultwarden<br/>vault.orbit Route\n  CLI->>GW: vault:setup<br/>--url=… --collection=… …\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit vault:setup --url=https://vault.orbit --client-id=user.3f0c… --client-secret-file=./client-secret --master-password-file=./master-password --organization=Orbit --collection=Databases`\n  %%> - syntax: `--url=ORIGIN --client-id=ID --client-secret-file=PATH --master-password-file=PATH --organization=NAME|ID --collection=NAME|ID`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the Vaultwarden origin, the Gateway account's API key and master password, and the organization and collection\n  %%> - the CLI reads the secrets from files, never from arguments\n  Note over GW: Validate, store the intent<br/>task #5167 · secrets to vault\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the `vault` extension is on; your Node has an access grant to the Gateway\n  %%> - the URL is HTTPS on a `.orbit` host, else `vault.url_invalid`\n  %%> - no other setup, sync or teardown runs, else `vault.sync_busy`\n  %%> - with items published, the organization and collection stay the same, else `vault.items_published`\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - stores the setup intent encrypted, then pushes the client secret and master password to the Swamp vault under a setup key after commit (R3)\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5167 recorded; the current setup, if any, stays in force\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-vault-setup\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209); no Node is a target\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - the URL, client ID, organization and collection, and the vault key names; never the secrets\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5167\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`, then prints `vault:status`\n  Note over SW: Prepare public data\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - compares the frozen public and private configuration, then stores credentials as native sensitive fields\n  Note over SW: workspace\n  Note over SW: Prepare session file\n  SW->>VW: Configure the server\n  SW->>VW: Log in\n  SW->>VW: Unlock the vault\n  Note over SW: Resolve the session\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - shapes private CLI data; credentials stay in native vault storage and cipher stdin stays in the 0700 scratch directory\n  SW->>VW: Read account status\n  SW->>VW: Read organizations\n  SW->>VW: Read collections\n  Note over SW: Check account KDF\n  Note over SW: Select the organization\n  Note over SW: Select the collection\n  SW->>VW: Probe write access\n  Note over SW: Read probe identity\n  Note over SW: Verify the account\n  SW->>VW: Clear the session\n  SW->>VW: Log out\n  Note over SW: Remove CLI session\n  Note over SW: Confirm the setup\n  %% Each command step runs one pinned CLI command. Typed shapers expose only bounded public metadata.\n  alt the account can write\n    VW-->>SW: collection writable\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the account email and the organization and collection IDs\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event · IDs\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Store the setup<br/>configured · admit vault.sync\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the setup is stored encrypted and `vault:status` shows `configured`\n    %%> - task #5167 succeeds, and the Gateway admits a first `vault.sync`\n  else login, unlock or write fails\n    VW--xSW: refused or no answer\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a wrong API key or master password, an unknown organization or collection, no write access, or TLS fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its safe error code\n    Note over GW: Keep the old setup<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the new setup is not stored; an earlier setup stays in force; task #5167 fails\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again with fixed values; it is a new task\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5167\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "vault/sync": {
   "kind": "vault.sync",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R2-R4, swamp-vault-design (Vaultwarden = human copy). docs/reference/vaultwarden.md#sync.\n  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rule 22 (direct pinned CLI primitives, private environment and stdin; no shipped scripts).\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: vault:sync\n  %% kind: vault.sync\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane VW icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209\n  participant VW as Vaultwarden<br/>vault.orbit Route\n  CLI->>GW: vault:sync\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit vault:sync`\n  %%> - syntax: `[--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - the Gateway admits the same sync itself when a database credential changes, and once an hour to repair drift\n  %%> - run the command to see the result at once\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - no per-minute poll; nothing runs while nothing changed, except the hourly check\n  Note over GW: Build the wanted items<br/>task #5168 · items to vault\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the `vault` extension is on and Vaultwarden is set up, else `vault.not_configured`\n  %%> - no other setup, sync or teardown runs; a busy sync waits briefly, then returns `vault.sync_busy`\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - builds one Database item per MySQL and PostgreSQL connection and Database user with a stored password; others are listed as skipped\n  %%> - pushes the wanted items and the account secrets to the Swamp vault as `vault-sync-5168` after commit (R3)\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - task #5168 recorded with the publishing ledger as input\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-vault-sync\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209); no Node is a target\n  %%> Inputs:\n  %%> - the setup, the ledger of published item IDs and sources, and the vault key names; never a password\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5168\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`, then prints `vault:status`\n  %% Worker-side actions use the pinned official CLI directly. No scripts are shipped or copied.\n  Note over SW: Prepare public data\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - compares the frozen public and private configuration, then stores credentials as native sensitive fields\n  Note over SW: workspace\n  Note over SW: Prepare session file\n  SW->>VW: configure\n  SW->>VW: login\n  SW->>VW: unlock\n  Note over SW: Resolve the session\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - shapes private CLI data; credentials stay in native vault storage and cipher stdin stays in the 0700 scratch directory\n  SW->>VW: sync\n  SW->>VW: Read private inventory\n  Note over SW: inventory\n  SW->>VW: Read private trash\n  Note over SW: trash\n  Note over SW: plan\n  SW->>VW: delete\n  %%> For each:\n  %%> - one action per admitted cipher; native receipts preserve completed work on resume\n  Note over SW: encode\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - shapes private CLI data; credentials stay in native vault storage and cipher stdin stays in the 0700 scratch directory\n  %%> For each:\n  %%> - one action per admitted cipher; native receipts preserve completed work on resume\n  SW->>VW: Apply cipher\n  %%> For each:\n  %%> - one action per admitted cipher; native receipts preserve completed work on resume\n  Note over SW: write\n  %%> For each:\n  %%> - one action per admitted cipher; native receipts preserve completed work on resume\n  SW->>VW: logout\n  Note over SW: remove-cache\n  Note over SW: result\n  Note over SW: Confirm the sync\n  %% Each command step runs one pinned CLI command. Typed shapers expose only bounded public metadata.\n  alt every change made\n    VW-->>SW: items match\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - each item's ID and source\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event · item IDs\n    %%> How the Gateway learns it:\n    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway\n    Note over GW: Update the ledger<br/>last_synced_at · task done\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the publishing ledger records each item ID; `vault:status` shows the new `last_synced_at`\n    %%> - task #5168 succeeds\n  else a change fails\n    VW--xSW: refused or no answer\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - login or unlock fails, Vaultwarden is down, or a create, edit or delete fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step\n    %%> The Gateway reads:\n    %%> - which step failed and its safe error code\n    Note over GW: Keep the ledger<br/>last_error · vault.sync_failed\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the ledger stays unchanged; `vault:status` shows `last_error`; task #5168 fails\n    %%> In Vaultwarden:\n    %%> - changes made before the failure stay\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - safe: the next sync reads the collection again and adopts items a lost reply created\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5168\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "websocket/app-create": {
   "kind": "websocket.app_create",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task; DECISIONS R1 + round 2 websocket.sync (no Swamp run, the Gateway closes the task from Reverb's own reports). docs/reference/websocket-apps.md.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: websocket:app:create\n  %% kind: websocket.app_create\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant N as Reverb<br/>on websocket Node\n  CLI->>GW: websocket:app:create<br/>hauzer\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit websocket:app:create hauzer`\n  %%> - syntax: `<name> [--operation-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - the app name: a lowercase kebab slug of at most 63 characters\n  %%> - optionally an operation key, to retry the same request after a lost response\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Save the app, revision 19<br/>R1: no run · task #5150\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - a Node has the `websocket` role\n  %%> - the name is free and is not `gateway`; otherwise `validation.failed`\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - generates the app ID, key and secret; the secret is stored encrypted and never returned\n  %%> - saves the app and a new registry revision 19 in one transaction\n  %%> - records task #5150, which waits for revision 19\n  %%> Why no Swamp run:\n  %%> - nothing has to be done on a Node; Reverb pulls the registry itself (R1)\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the app exists in the Gateway; Reverb does not serve it yet\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5150 · app ID, key\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, the app ID and the key, at once; never the secret\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  loop each Reverb reconcile, 30 s\n    N->>GW: Pull the registry<br/>full snapshot, revision 19\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - every Reverb process fetches the full registry snapshot on its own 30-second reconcile\n    %%> - it adds the app without a restart; existing connections stay open\n    %%> Not included:\n    %%> - no Swamp step, no SSH and no Reverb restart\n    N->>GW: Report applied revision<br/>per process and boot\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - each process reports the revision it applied, with its boot identity\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - a report from an old boot cannot confirm a replacement process\n  end\n  alt every process applied 19\n    Note over GW: Close the task<br/>succeeded · app served\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - every serving Reverb process has loaded the app\n    %%> - task #5150 succeeds\n  else a later revision replaced it\n    Note over GW: Close the task, failed<br/>websocket.registry_superseded\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - the app was destroyed or changed before every process applied revision 19\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - task #5150 fails; the registry keeps its latest state\n  else no fresh report in 5 min\n    Note over GW: Close the task, failed<br/>websocket.registry_pending\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a serving process is stale, missing or keeps failing to load the revision\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - task #5150 fails; the saved app stays, and Reverb loads it when it recovers\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run `websocket:sync` after the fix; it waits for the current revision\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5150\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "websocket/app-destroy": {
   "kind": "websocket.app_destroy",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task; DECISIONS R1 + round 2 websocket.sync (no Swamp run, the Gateway closes the task from Reverb's own reports). docs/reference/websocket-apps.md.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: websocket:app:destroy\n  %% kind: websocket.app_destroy\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant N as Reverb<br/>on websocket Node\n  CLI->>GW: websocket:app:destroy<br/>hauzer\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit websocket:app:destroy hauzer --force`\n  %%> - syntax: `<name> [--force] [--operation-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Confirmation:\n  %%> - without `--force`, the CLI asks first; the default is No\n  %%> - without a terminal, or with `--json`, `--force` is required (`websocket.confirmation_required`)\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: Delete app, revision 20<br/>R1: no run · task #5151\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the app is not `gateway` (`websocket.app_reserved`)\n  %%> - no Instance uses the app (`websocket.app_in_use`); remove it with `instance:websocket:remove` first\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - deletes the app and its encrypted secret, and saves registry revision 20, in one transaction\n  %%> - records task #5151, which waits for revision 20\n  %%> Why no Swamp run:\n  %%> - nothing has to be done on a Node; Reverb pulls the registry itself (R1)\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5151\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  loop each Reverb reconcile, 30 s\n    N->>GW: Pull the registry<br/>full snapshot, revision 20\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - every Reverb process fetches the full snapshot on its own 30-second reconcile\n    %%> - it stops accepting the app and closes only that app's sockets with code `4001`\n    %%> - other apps keep their connections; Reverb does not restart\n    N->>GW: Report applied revision<br/>per process and boot\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - each process reports the revision it applied, with its boot identity\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - the app only counts as gone when no serving process accepts it anymore\n  end\n  alt every process applied 20\n    Note over GW: Close the task<br/>succeeded · app revoked\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - no serving Reverb process accepts the app\n    %%> - task #5151 succeeds\n  else no fresh report in 5 min\n    Note over GW: Close the task, failed<br/>websocket.registry_pending\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a serving process is stale or missing\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - task #5151 fails; the app stays deleted in the Gateway\n    %%> - a process that still runs an older snapshot may accept the app until it reconciles; status reports show the pending revocation\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run `websocket:sync` after the fix\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5151\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "websocket/app-rotate": {
   "kind": "websocket.app_rotate",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task; DECISIONS R1 + round 2 websocket.sync (no Swamp run, the Gateway closes the task from Reverb's own reports). docs/reference/websocket-apps.md.\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: websocket:app:rotate\n  %% kind: websocket.app_rotate\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant N as Reverb<br/>on websocket Node\n  CLI->>GW: websocket:app:rotate<br/>hauzer\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit websocket:app:rotate hauzer --force`\n  %%> - syntax: `<name> [--force] [--operation-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Confirmation:\n  %%> - without `--force`, the CLI asks first; the default is No\n  %%> Who can call it:\n  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request\n  Note over GW: New secret, revision 21<br/>R1: no run · task #5152\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the app exists and is not `gateway`\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - keeps the app key, generates a new secret and advances the credential generation\n  %%> - updates the stored environment of every Instance that uses the app\n  %%> - saves registry revision 21; all of this in one transaction\n  %%> - records task #5152, which waits for revision 21\n  %%> Why no Swamp run:\n  %%> - nothing has to be done on a Node; Reverb pulls the registry itself (R1)\n  %%> Not included:\n  %%> - writing the Instances' `.env` files and reloading them; run `env:sync` per Instance (`input.app.instance_rollout` lists them)\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5152\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once; never the secret\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`\n  loop each Reverb reconcile, 30 s\n    N->>GW: Pull the registry<br/>full snapshot, revision 21\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - every Reverb process fetches the full snapshot on its own 30-second reconcile\n    %%> - it then accepts only the new secret and closes that app's sockets with code `4201`\n    %%> - other apps keep their connections; Reverb does not restart\n    N->>GW: Report applied revision<br/>per process and boot\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - each process reports the revision it applied, with its boot identity; that revision fixes the credential generation\n  end\n  alt every process applied 21\n    Note over GW: Close the task<br/>succeeded · old secret revoked\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - no serving Reverb process accepts the old secret\n    %%> - task #5152 succeeds\n  else a later revision replaced it\n    Note over GW: Close the task, failed<br/>websocket.registry_superseded\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - another rotation or a destroy of the app came first\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - task #5152 fails; the registry keeps its latest state\n  else no fresh report in 5 min\n    Note over GW: Close the task, failed<br/>websocket.registry_pending\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a serving process is stale or missing\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - task #5152 fails; the new secret stays stored, and an offline Instance cannot delay the revocation\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run `websocket:sync` after the fix\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5152\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "websocket/sync": {
   "kind": "websocket.sync",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task; DECISIONS R1 + round 2 websocket.sync (no polling step, the Gateway closes the task from Reverb's own reports, no run).\n  %% status: migrated\n  %% command: websocket:sync\n  %% kind: websocket.sync\n  autonumber\n  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant N as Reverb<br/>on websocket Node\n  CLI->>GW: websocket:sync\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - `orbit websocket:sync --wait=60`\n  %%> - syntax: `[--wait=SECONDS] [--operation-key=KEY] [--json]`\n  %%> Input:\n  %%> - `--wait`: how long the CLI follows the task, 0 to 3600 seconds; default 60\n  %%> - optionally an operation key, to retry the same sync\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - waits until every serving Reverb process has applied the current registry, without a restart\n  Note over GW: Pin current registry<br/>revision 18 · R1, no run\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - a Node has the `websocket` role\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - records task #5125 for the current registry revision 18\n  %%> - a pending app change does not block it, and separate syncs do not replace each other\n  %%> Why no Swamp run:\n  %%> - nothing has to be done on a Node; Reverb pulls and reports itself (R1)\n  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5125\n  %%> What you get:\n  %%> - the task number, at once\n  %%> Next:\n  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show` until `--wait` ends\n  loop each Reverb reconcile, 30 s\n    N->>GW: Pull the registry<br/>full snapshot\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - every Reverb process fetches the full snapshot on its own 30-second reconcile\n    %%> - an unchanged revision answers `304`\n    N->>GW: Report applied revision<br/>per process and boot\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - each process reports the revision it applied, with its boot identity\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - a report from an old boot cannot confirm a replacement process\n  end\n  alt every process applied 18\n    Note over GW: Close the task<br/>succeeded · backend applied\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - every serving Reverb process runs revision 18 or later with the same apps\n    %%> - task #5125 succeeds\n  else no fresh report in 5 min\n    Note over GW: Close the task, failed<br/>websocket.registry_pending\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - a serving process is stale or missing after 5 minutes\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - task #5125 fails; the registry stays as saved\n    %%> Retry:\n    %%> - run the same command again after the fix\n  end\n  loop every 2 s until terminal or --wait\n    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5125\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended or `--wait` has passed\n    %%> When `--wait` passes first:\n    %%> - the CLI exits non-zero; the task keeps waiting, and nothing is undone\n    GW-->>CLI: status + result\n    %%> Returns:\n    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended\n  end\n"
  },
  "reference/pipeline/1-prep": {
   "kind": "pipeline.1-prep",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: stage 1 of the command development pipeline. Prepare the chart, page and cases as the first commit on the feature branch. Build and prove the feature in stages 2 and 3 before stage 4 opens its one PR. This is the contributor workflow, not an automated prep operation.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: development pipeline stage 1 · spec first, before code or PR\n  %% kind: pipeline.1-prep\n  autonumber\n  %% lane AG icon=terminal pill=author tone=gray\n  %% lane CP icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  %% lane RV icon=terminal pill=reviewer tone=amber\n  participant AG as Author<br/>agent or person\n  participant CP as Task copy<br/>feature branch\n  participant RV as Reviewer<br/>independent agent\n  AG->>CP: Read the owning contracts<br/>brief · chart · page · code\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - settle the supported inputs, outcomes, refusals, repeat behavior and failure recovery from the brief and current decisions\n  %%> - read code to identify the gap, not to narrow the agreed behavior\n  AG->>CP: Draft the chart<br/>flow-tighten · chart rules\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - follows docs/cli/README.md and the flow-tighten skill; the chart states the intended flow before implementation\n  %%> - shows meaningful alternate outcomes and what remains after failure\n  AG->>CP: Write the page<br/>usage · behavior · errors\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - follows the chart and links shared rules instead of repeating them\n  AG->>CP: Design the proof cases<br/>chart paths · batches\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - derives cases from the chart and page; checks real effects, repeats, refusals and recovery where applicable\n  %%> - keeps existing runnable cases unless their behavior is removed; records runner gaps under uncovered instead of inventing steps\n  %%> - fails_today records a runnable case that current code fails; fails_on_base metadata does not cause replay in copy proofs\n  AG->>CP: Check the spec<br/>flows · docs · runner plan\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - bin/check-flows, composer docs-build and composer docs-lint pass\n  %%> - the runner plan accepts the cases file, and the runner tests pass; a plan is not execution proof\n  AG->>RV: Review the spec<br/>chart · page · cases\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - a fresh read-only reviewer checks the intended behavior and case coverage against their owning rules\n  loop findings remain\n    RV-->>AG: Findings<br/>file · reason\n    AG->>CP: Correct the spec<br/>repeat checks and review\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - unresolved product choices go to the owner before implementation\n  end\n  AG->>CP: Commit the spec first<br/>chart · page · cases only\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - the first commit on the feature branch holds the spec, with no implementation code\n  %%> - a changed chart stays draft until the owner approves it; authoring or a clean agent review does not mark it reviewed\n  Note over AG,CP: Build, then prove<br/>no PR opens yet\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - stages 2 and 3 build and prove the feature end to end in this task copy and its nested fleet before any PR, draft or ready, opens\n"
  },
  "reference/pipeline/2-development-environment": {
   "kind": "pipeline.2-development-environment",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Current flow: stage 2 of the command development pipeline. A task runs in a copy of its Project's default container Instance. Acquire it before prep when prep runs there, then reuse that copy for build and proof. A nested-fleet Project gives it its own Gateway, app-dev and app-prod containers. Task start and completion details belong to docs/cli/task/start.mmd.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: development pipeline stage 2 · task copy and nested fleet\n  %% kind: pipeline.2-development-environment\n  autonumber\n  %% lane AG icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane CP icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  %% lane NT icon=server pill=fleet tone=amber\n  participant AG as Author<br/>agent or person\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>create · complete\n  participant CP as Task copy<br/>container Instance\n  participant NT as Nested fleet<br/>Gateway · 2 Nodes\n  opt the task has not started\n    AG->>GW: Start the task<br/>task:start GROUP\n    %%> Requirements:\n    %%> - the task is todo and its Project's default Instance is a container; Orbit command development uses a Project with a nested fleet\n    Note over GW: Record the task copy<br/>branch · owner · create\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - binds the copy to the task and admits instance.create in one transaction; task copies take no CI place\n    GW->>SW: Create and complete<br/>operation-instance-create\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - starts the normal Instance clone flow after the transaction commits; instance.complete finishes setup after create\n    SW->>CP: Clone the default seed<br/>workspace · task branch\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - copies the container and its inner volume when present; the copy owns its workspace and fleet\n    SW->>CP: Complete the copy<br/>database · Processes · fleet\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - makes the copy active only after its setup and nested fleet are ready\n  end\n  alt the copy is active\n    SW-->>GW: Copy ready<br/>task running\n    GW-->>AG: Development environment<br/>copy active\n    loop build the whole feature\n      AG->>CP: Edit and test<br/>one feature branch\n      CP->>NT: Exercise the real path<br/>CLI · API · effect\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - nested Nodes mount the copy's checkout; apply migrations and service changes before API checks\n      %%> - codify manual changes and test the full end result here, not through a sequence of releases\n    end\n    Note over AG,NT: Run stage 3 in this copy<br/>before opening the PR\n  else create or completion fails\n    SW--xGW: Setup failed<br/>failed step · Operation\n    GW--xAG: Environment not ready<br/>follow task:start recovery\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - no build or proof starts on an incomplete copy; task:start owns create retries, safe removal and completion recovery\n  end\n"
  },
  "reference/pipeline/3-proof": {
   "kind": "pipeline.3-proof",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Current flow: stage 3 of the command development pipeline. The author runs the designed proof in the task copy where the feature was built, using that copy's nested fleet. Every required case must pass before a PR opens. The command contract is docs/cli/proof/proof.mmd; copy proofs do not replay cases on the merge base.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: development pipeline stage 3 · end-to-end proof before PR\n  %% kind: pipeline.3-proof\n  autonumber\n  %% lane AG icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane CP icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  %% lane NT icon=server pill=fleet tone=amber\n  participant AG as Author<br/>Gateway access\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>verify workflow\n  participant CP as Task copy<br/>candidate checkout\n  participant NT as Nested fleet<br/>Gateway · 2 Nodes\n  AG->>GW: Prove the command<br/>orbit proof COMMAND --group=GROUP\n  %%> Command:\n  %%> - orbit proof COMMAND --group=GROUP\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - call from a Node with Gateway access; the named group's active copy must keep its own nested fleet\n  %%> - the proof uses the copy's current checkout, committed or not; no new copy is created\n  Note over GW: Admit the proof<br/>group · copy · cases\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - refuses a missing group, an unavailable copy, a missing fleet or competing work; records intent without replay inputs\n  GW->>SW: Start the run<br/>proof-fleet-verify\n  SW->>CP: Plan the cases<br/>runner plan --lines\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - loads the cases from the copy's checkout before changing the fleet; invalid definitions fail the proof\n  loop each batch in declared order\n    SW->>CP: Reset the inner fleet<br/>restore base · apply · ready\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - resets before each batch, the first included; starts the containers and migrates the nested Gateway before readiness checks\n    %%> - a failed reset stops the run; later cases are unrun with cause infrastructure\n    SW->>CP: Run the batch<br/>setup · cases\n    CP->>NT: Exercise and check<br/>real CLI · real effects\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the shared runner prepares the candidate prerequisites and callers, applies batch setup and checks each case's observable outcome\n    %%> - cases use their own resources and undo nothing; shared-state cases run alone under the runner's rules\n  end\n  CP-->>SW: Case verdicts<br/>full result artifact\n  Note over SW: Join the verdicts<br/>no missing case is a pass\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - case receipts remain in the task copy; no reset follows the final batch, so the author can inspect the resulting state\n  %%> - fails_on_base remains case metadata: merge-base replay is not ported to copies (ProofMergeBaseReplay.php)\n  alt every required case passed\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>passed verdicts\n    GW-->>AG: Proof passed<br/>continue to stage 4\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - record the cases, verdicts and tested checkout for the PR; run affected proofs again after changes\n  else a case failed or did not run\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed or unrun cases\n    GW--xAG: Fix in the same copy<br/>repeat the affected proof\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - no PR opens; a runner gap or infrastructure failure is not evidence that the feature works\n  end\n"
  },
  "reference/pipeline/4-pr-and-ci": {
   "kind": "pipeline.4-pr-and-ci",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Current workflow and delivery gate: stage 4 of the command development pipeline. Open one PR only after stages 1 to 3 have produced the spec-first branch, working feature and passing end-to-end proof. CI confirms that result. CI proof is deliberately disabled by ORBIT_PROOF_ENABLED until commands have been checked systematically; a green CI result while it is off is not proof coverage.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: development pipeline stage 4 · complete PR, CI and gate\n  %% kind: pipeline.4-pr-and-ci\n  autonumber\n  %% lane AG icon=terminal pill=author tone=gray\n  %% lane RV icon=terminal pill=reviewer tone=amber\n  %% lane GH icon=server pill=PR tone=purple\n  %% lane CI icon=workflow pill=checks tone=green\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  participant AG as Author<br/>proven feature\n  participant RV as Reviewer<br/>fresh subagent\n  participant GH as GitHub<br/>PR · main\n  participant CI as CI<br/>selected checks\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>delivery gate\n  AG->>RV: Review the complete diff<br/>spec · code · proof evidence\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - the branch's first commit holds the chart, page and cases; implementation and required end-to-end proof have passed in one task copy with its nested fleet\n  %%> - use the plug-in point checklist where applicable; fix findings before opening or updating the PR\n  RV-->>AG: Findings or clean review<br/>independent assessment\n  AG->>GH: Push and open one PR<br/>complete feature · into main\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - no draft or ready PR opened before the feature and its proof passed\n  %%> - the PR body states the independent review ran, the proof result and any limits; a draft can hold back already-proven work, not replace pre-PR proof\n  GH->>CI: Run selected checks<br/>immutable PR merge tree\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - scopes checks by changed inputs; Required checks aggregates every selected required result\n  %%> - the pre-check: it plans the fixture shards and starts none; the merge queue run starts them\n  opt CI proof enabled and selected\n    Note over CI: Check proof trust<br/>ORBIT_PROOF_ENABLED=true\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - deliberately off until commands have been checked systematically; this documentation does not enable it\n    %%> - when enabled, selection.txt selects commands; outside PRs also need maintainer approval of the exact head and the run-proofs label\n    CI->>GW: Confirm the proven head<br/>proof:run COMMIT\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the trusted CI caller supplies the SHA; proof:run selects cases and uses copies of the proof Project's default container Instance, each with its nested fleet\n    %%> - each copy brings in the commit and checks its workspace; assigned batches reset the fleet before the first batch and after a batch that can change it\n    %%> - cases and runner are the same as the author's proof; no merge-base replay runs in copies\n    %%> - a new push cancels the previous PR proof; each run end requests destruction of its copy\n    GW-->>CI: Verdict per case<br/>complete selected coverage\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - a selected proof that runs blocks Required checks; failed, unrun, missing or interrupted coverage cannot pass\n  end\n  alt every required check passes\n    CI-->>GH: Required checks<br/>green on the head\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - CI confirms an already-proven feature; with CI proof off, green does not establish proof coverage\n    AG->>GH: Request the merge gate<br/>review of exact head\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - task-copy publication does not automatically merge; follow the Project's review and merge policy\n    %%> - task:pr-merge is unavailable for task copies; its older gate applies only to groups it supports\n    %%> - a new head needs affected local checks and proofs, CI and the gate again\n    AG->>GH: Queue the pull request<br/>gh pr merge --squash --auto\n    %%> Requirements:\n    %%> - green CI and the authorized review on the exact head; publication or a green result alone does not authorize a merge\n    %%> - --match-head-commit names the reviewed head, so GitHub refuses a head that moved; no branch update because main moved\n    GH->>CI: Run the merge queue run<br/>the commit main receives\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - GitHub builds one squash commit on main and any pull request still ahead; this run adds the selected fixture shards\n    %%> - the Docs gate reads the pull request body again\n    %%> - the plan covers changes since the newest commit that the queue tests or main delivered\n    alt the queue run passes\n      CI-->>GH: Required checks<br/>green on the queue commit\n      Note over GH: Fast-forward main<br/>to the tested commit\n      GH->>CI: Check main delivery<br/>queue run · release inputs\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - finds the green queue run on the exact main commit and promotes its web build; it compares no trees\n      %%> - a red main is fixed forward and never released; stages 5 and 6 confirm and refresh the working result\n    else the queue run fails\n      CI--xGH: Required checks<br/>red · leaves the queue\n      GH--xAG: Fix and queue again<br/>same branch · same PR\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - the next queued pull request is tested on main without it\n    end\n  else a required check fails\n    CI--xGH: Required checks<br/>red · no merge\n    GH--xAG: Fix and prove again<br/>same branch · same PR\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - fix failures caused by the change; report unrelated main failures without treating them as a passing check\n  end\n"
  },
  "reference/pipeline/5-automatic-release": {
   "kind": "pipeline.5-automatic-release",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Target design: stage 5 of the command development pipeline (feature-flow section 6, gateway-recovery \"Automatic releases\"). A merged green commit gets an automatic Swamp release run when automatic releases are enabled and unpaused; a failed check switches back. The steps of the run itself are in the chart gateway:release:deploy. No fleet test follows a release.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: development pipeline stage 5 · automatic Swamp release\n  %% kind: pipeline.5-automatic-release\n  autonumber\n  %% lane GH icon=server pill=CI tone=blue\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane GN icon=server pill=target tone=amber\n  participant GH as GitHub<br/>main · CI\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>release run\n  participant GN as Gateway Node<br/>code · services\n  Note over GW,GH: Find newest green commit<br/>on main · CI\n  %%> Built:\n  %%> - the Gateway looks every five minutes while automatic releases are on, and reads CI through its GitHub App; the merges of those five minutes go out in one release of the newest green commit\n  %%> What it accepts:\n  %%> - a run of `ci.yml` on `main`, with `Main delivery checks`, its main `Required checks` result and `Gateway release inputs` green; the delivery receipt names the green merge queue run of the commit and the promotion of its web artifact\n  %%> - main delivery requires the green merge queue run on the exact commit after verifying repository, workflow, queue branch, jobs and artifact digest; it republishes the web artifact under the main SHA and keeps the same check names\n  %%> - the release reader still accepts only the main CI result and main artifact; a fork, PR or other workflow cannot directly authorize a release\n  Note over GW: Admit the release<br/>one at a time\n  %%> Requirements:\n  %%> - no other release queued or running, and automatic releases not paused\n  %%> - the agent pin passes its Sigstore check; CI published the web build\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - several merges can land during a release; the next run takes only the newest commit\n  %%> Result:\n  %%> - a running release record; the pin, artifact and targets are frozen as inputs\n  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-gateway-release\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the Gateway records intent only; Swamp executes. Detail of every step: chart `gateway:release:deploy`\n  SW->>GN: Build the release<br/>composer · web build\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - refuses a commit that does not descend from the current release\n  %%> - installs dependencies and the web build, and seals the release\n  SW->>GN: Snapshot and migrate<br/>expand and contract\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - writes a database snapshot, then migrates while the old release still serves\n  %%> - old code must work with the new schema\n  SW->>GN: Wait for active Operations<br/>before Swamp load\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - checks release-blocking Operations while the old release and Horizon serve; force does not bypass it\n  %%> - a busy timeout refuses with `gateway.release_busy`; no Swamp load or activation follows\n  SW->>GN: Load the new workflows<br/>Swamp reloads, no restart\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - the switch then runs the new commit's own steps\n  SW->>GN: Drain Horizon<br/>loaded activation child\n  %%> On failure:\n  %%> - no switch follows a failed drain; Start Horizon still runs\n  SW->>GN: Switch to the new release<br/>gateway-rollback script\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - switches the links, reloads PHP-FPM, restarts the running services; a failed switch still reaches Start Horizon\n  SW->>GN: Check the Gateway<br/>health · version\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - reads the health endpoint and the status version of the Gateway\n  alt the check fails\n    SW->>GN: Switch back<br/>gateway-rollback previous\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - returns to the previous release without Swamp; a failed switch-back reports its own error\n  end\n  SW->>GN: Start Horizon<br/>on the live release\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - always runs after activation paths, including failed drain, switch, verification or switch-back; skips only when no Horizon unit is configured\n  %%> - a failed start admits no refresh\n  alt verification and Horizon pass\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result\n    Note over GW: Record the release<br/>verified\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - preserve every eligible Node’s prior host baseline before admitting any rollout work\n    %%> - the existing fleet admission job delivers the agent and runs the Node converge on each Node, one Node at a time\n    %%> - once every Node is current, the Gateway admits the nested-fleet refresh (stage 6); the daily fleet pass rechecks membership, pins and pending work even after fleet current\n  else verification failed\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>switch-back result · alert\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Gateway records the failed verification and switch-back outcome; no refresh is admitted\n  else a step from migrate on fails\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step\n    %%> On failure:\n    %%> - from migrate on, the old release may be live with a changed schema, so the run stops and names the snapshot\n    Note over GW: Pause automatic releases<br/>snapshot named · alert\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - restoring the snapshot is an operator action; `gateway:auto-release:resume` clears the pause\n  end\n"
  },
  "reference/pipeline/6-task-image-seed-refresh": {
   "kind": "pipeline.6-task-image-seed-refresh",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Current flow: stage 6 of the command development pipeline. Each Node with the nested-fleet switch keeps the nested-fleet Project's default Instance, an Incus container that holds the inner fleet. After each verified Gateway release, when refresh is enabled, Orbit deploys that default to the release commit, refreshes its fleet and takes the seed snapshot that a task or a new Instance of the Project clones. There is no image publish and no daily run. The per-Node run: docs/cli/instance/container/fleet-refresh.mmd.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: development pipeline stage 6 · per-Node seed refresh\n  %% kind: pipeline.6-task-image-seed-refresh\n  autonumber\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane N icon=server pill=each Node tone=amber\n  %% lane C icon=server pill=default tone=purple\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>one run per Node\n  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>switched on\n  participant C as Default<br/>with inner fleet\n  Note over GW: Wait for fleet current<br/>record each Operation\n  %%> Triggers:\n  %%> - fleet current, when refresh is enabled; the five-minute release tick redrives a missed continuation\n  %%> When:\n  %%> - a default with a clone copy in flight refuses with `instance.clone_in_flight`; the same release retries\n  %%> - a default with a deployment open refuses by its Instance lock; nothing queues behind it\n  GW->>SW: Start one run per Node<br/>instance-deploy-direct\n  %%> Why:\n  %%> - each Node keeps its own seed; nothing is copied between Nodes\n  loop each switched-on Node\n    SW->>N: Prepare the container<br/>start · fleet check · seed · fetch\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - one play: starts the default, checks its running fleet once, keeps the current seed, fetches the release commit\n    %%> Why:\n    %%> - a later failure then restores a seed that holds the fleet; a failure here changes nothing and restores nothing\n    SW->>C: Deploy the release commit<br/>checkout · deploy steps\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - the same steps as every container default deployment\n    SW->>C: Finish the container<br/>processes · refresh · seed · check\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - one play: installs the Processes, refreshes the inner fleet (migrate, converge, Doctor, and `base` retaken for the containers whose disk changed), takes the seed snapshot with the Processes stopped, and checks the default\n    %%> - refuses the seed when the Club key is a file on the default's disk; the key lives outside it\n    %%> - a default with an inner volume: also snapshots the volume under the same name, right after a flush of the volume (`sync -f`); the two snapshots are the seed, and older pairs go after both new halves exist\n  end\n  alt the run passes on a Node\n    SW-->>GW: Node refreshed<br/>terminal event per Node\n    Note over GW: Record the seed commit<br/>clones copy it\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - one slow Node does not hold the others; existing clones keep their fleet\n    %%> - proof Nodes publish the exact seed commit and timestamp through instance.seed_copy; the release retains those Operation IDs\n    %%> - a new copy of a default with an inner volume copies both halves: the volume to its own `inner-<id>`, then the container; while the run is open such a copy refuses with `instance.default_seed_in_flight`\n  else a step fails on a Node\n    SW->>N: Restore the newest seed<br/>then start the default\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - a default with an inner volume needs both halves of the seed before it restores anything, then restores the container and the volume\n    SW--xGW: Node not refreshed<br/>failed step\n    Note over GW: Keep the old seed<br/>record failure and retry\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - the Operation names the failed step; a terminal failure remains visible and can be fixed forward\n  end\n"
  },
  "reference/pipeline/capacity-model": {
   "kind": "pipeline.capacity",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Current capacity flow: task copies take no CI place. proof:run shares CI places with runner jobs and uses copies of the proof default, including available warm copies. The capacity and cleanup contract belongs to docs/cli/proof/run.mdx.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: development pipeline · copy capacity\n  %% kind: pipeline.capacity\n  autonumber\n  %% lane T icon=server pill=task tone=gray\n  %% lane PR icon=server pill=PR tone=purple\n  %% lane Q icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane CP icon=server pill=capacity tone=amber\n  %% lane PC icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  participant T as Task copy<br/>own nested fleet\n  participant PR as Pull request<br/>proven feature\n  participant Q as Proof queue<br/>Operation order\n  participant CP as CI Nodes<br/>jobs and copies\n  participant PC as Proof copies<br/>assigned batches\n  Note over T: Build and prove first<br/>no CI place held\n  T->>PR: Publish the feature<br/>after local proof passes\n  opt CI proof enabled and selected\n    PR->>Q: Confirm the PR head<br/>proof:run COMMIT\n    %%> When:\n    %%> - ORBIT_PROOF_ENABLED is deliberately off until commands have been checked systematically\n    Q->>CP: Read free CI places<br/>runner state · memory · seed\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - takes no busy runner's place; available warm copies already hold their place and memory\n    alt capacity is available\n      CP-->>Q: Available copies<br/>warm or new from seed\n      Q->>PC: Assign the batches<br/>balance by measured time\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - prepares the tested commit; each copy has its own nested fleet\n      PC->>PC: Reset and run cases<br/>per assigned batch\n      PC-->>Q: Verdict per case<br/>complete reports\n      Q->>CP: Destroy the copies<br/>release holds on success\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - every run end requests cleanup, including failures and cancellation; failed destruction keeps its hold until recovery succeeds\n      Q-->>PR: One merged result<br/>all selected cases required\n    else no free place\n      Note over Q: Wait within the allowance<br/>120 minutes from creation\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - capacity timeout fails; waiting or running no selected case never counts as proof\n    end\n  end\n"
  },
  "reference/pipeline/3-proof-runner": {
   "kind": "pipeline.3-proof-runner",
   "src": "sequenceDiagram\n  %% Current runner flow inside stage 3. Swamp reaches the task copy through its Node and runs as agent in the copy. One shared runner checks the real CLI and effects on the nested fleet. The verification-cases reference owns scheduling and case syntax; proof and proof:run own reset and cleanup policy.\n  %% status: draft\n  %% command: development pipeline stage 3 · proof runner\n  %% kind: pipeline.3-proof-runner\n  autonumber\n  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue\n  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green\n  %% lane CP icon=server pill=target tone=purple\n  %% lane NT icon=server pill=fleet tone=amber\n  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API\n  participant SW as Swamp<br/>verify workflow\n  participant CP as Task copy<br/>runner as agent\n  participant NT as Nested fleet<br/>Gateway · 2 Nodes\n  GW->>SW: Start the verify run<br/>copy · cases · assignments\n  SW->>CP: Select the cases<br/>runner plan --lines\n  %%> What it does:\n  %%> - validates definitions and emits batches in declared order; a plan is not a runtime verdict\n  CP-->>SW: Ordered plan<br/>batches · cases\n  loop each assigned batch\n    opt the batch requires a reset\n      SW->>CP: Reset the inner fleet<br/>base · apply · readiness\n      %%> When:\n      %%> - orbit proof resets every batch; proof:run resets its first batch and each batch after one that can change the fleet\n      %%> On failure:\n      %%> - no cases start; missing cases are unrun with cause infrastructure\n    end\n    SW->>CP: Run setup and batch<br/>shared runner\n    CP->>NT: Prepare prerequisites<br/>Gateway · observers · callers\n    %%> What it does:\n    %%> - uses the candidate's pinned Ansible collections and scoped grants; records the tested revision and prepares the proof-only static CLI catalog\n    %%> - prepares observer versions and fresh reports, then caller checkout mounts, home, CA and profile before scheduling cases\n    %%> - batch and direct case calls require preparation even if setup already ran; failure starts no case\n    opt declared batch setup\n      CP->>NT: Set the batch start state<br/>declared setup steps\n    end\n    loop cases under runner locks\n      CP->>NT: Set up the case<br/>declared resources\n      %%> What it does:\n      %%> - readers may share a Node; a writer holds its target through the case; shared-fleet and unresolved write scopes run alone\n      %%> - cases use their own resources and undo nothing; no reset follows a failed case within a batch\n      CP->>NT: Run the real CLI<br/>candidate checkout\n      CP->>NT: Check the effect<br/>typed result · real state\n      %%> Result:\n      %%> - assertions check the case's observable outcome, not just exit zero or an Operation status\n    end\n    CP-->>SW: Verdict per case<br/>full result artifact\n    %%> Result:\n    %%> - passed, failed or unrun, with cause, checks and timing; the 4 KiB history tail is not the report\n  end\n  alt every selected case passed\n    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>complete verdicts\n  else a case failed or did not run\n    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed or unrun cases\n  end\n"
  }
 }
}
